var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"HILT","text":"<p>HILT (Hardware-In-the-Loop Testing) is Python HIL testing with a pit of success: composable tests, guardrails that make a bad test hard to write, and an engine that won't let an unhandled failure pass silently. A Floor Technician categorizes a failure and starts the right recovery protocol without reading suite source; a developer writes the suite that makes that possible.</p> <ul> <li>Floor technicians: the station page is the   Floor Technician guide. That is the URL that belongs   on a station label.</li> <li>Suite authors: start with getting started, then the   overview, the lifecycle, idioms, and   contracts.</li> <li>Public surface: API stability is the policy;   the API reference is generated from the public names' docstrings.</li> <li>Inspector contributors: the target specification carries the   terminal workflow that <code>hilt inspect</code> opens.</li> </ul> <p>Build this site locally with <code>make docs</code>, or run <code>make docs-serve</code> to read it in a browser while you edit. The header toggle switches between light and dark. A broken cross-reference fails the gate. Nothing hosts it: GitLab renders every page from <code>docs/</code> on <code>main</code>, and a release branch carries the built site under <code>docs/site/</code> (<code>make docs-release</code>) so a station can open it from a clone without a network (#138).</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html","title":"API Stability","text":"<p>1.0.0 is a promise. This document states what the promise covers, how version numbers carry it, how a name leaves the surface, and where every change is announced. The developer reads it to know which names are safe to build a station on and how much warning a change carries. Suites written for 1.0 keep running on every 1.x, so the Floor Technician's stations do not break on an upgrade.</p> <p>The self-tests hold the framework to this document (<code>tests/test_public_surface.py</code>): the reference table below and <code>hilt.__all__</code> must name the same set, every example and every documented code block imports only that set from the package root, and <code>hilt.__version__</code> reports the installed distribution.</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#the-public-surface","title":"The public surface","text":"<p><code>hilt.__all__</code> is the public surface. Everything else is private.</p> <ul> <li>A name is public if and only if it appears in <code>hilt.__all__</code>. Import it from   <code>hilt</code>; the module a name is defined in is an implementation detail and may move   without notice. <code>from hilt import TestComponent</code> is a promise; <code>from hilt.base   import TestComponent</code> is not.</li> <li>The documented attributes, methods, parameters, and raised exceptions of a public   name are part of the promise. \"Documented\" means named in this document, in   <code>docs/contracts.md</code>, <code>docs/idioms.md</code>, <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>,   <code>docs/junit-xml.md</code>, or <code>docs/hilt-check.md</code>, or in the name's own docstring.</li> <li>A public object has no attribute of a private type. Every value a suite can read   from a public object carries a public type, so the reader can follow every name back   to its meaning. <code>ScopedLogger</code> is promised as its <code>logging.LoggerAdapter</code> base plus   the documented <code>scope</code> and <code>bread_crumbs</code> additions.</li> <li><code>hilt.__version__</code> is public: the installed distribution's version string.</li> <li>The invariants in <code>docs/contracts.md</code> are promises in their own right. A change that   weakens one is a breaking change even when no name moves.</li> <li>The records the framework emits (<code>result.json</code>, <code>timeline.jsonl</code>, the point-in-time   artifact envelope) are promised through their published JSON Schemas and   <code>format_version</code>, under the policy in <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>.</li> <li>Three environment variables are public configuration, and the framework reads no   others: <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, the artifact root when <code>TestUtils</code> is given none;   <code>environment=DEBUG</code>, which publishes every variable despite an <code>env_allow</code> or   <code>env_deny</code> list and logs a warning saying so (<code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>); and   <code>SSH_AUTH_SOCK</code>, the SSH agent <code>get_shell</code> and <code>use_shell</code> fall back to when no   credential is given. The run environment as a whole is published as evidence under   <code>env_allow</code> / <code>env_deny</code>, not read as configuration.</li> <li>The <code>hilt run</code> command line is public in two respects: its exit status contract   (<code>0</code> Pass, <code>1</code> Fail, <code>2</code> Aborted, <code>3</code> usage error) and its <code>--json</code> record, which is   the <code>result.json</code> record under the same <code>format_version</code> policy   (<code>docs/hilt-run.md</code>). The <code>hilt.cli</code> module behind it is not.</li> <li>The <code>hilt new</code> command line is public in two respects: the files it writes (a   gated-PASS suite and a matching self-test stub) and its exit status (<code>0</code> wrote the   files, <code>3</code> usage error). The <code>hilt.scaffold</code> module behind it is not   (<code>docs/hilt-new.md</code>).</li> <li>The <code>hilt inspect</code> command line is public in three respects: its selection rule   (PATH is a run directory or its <code>result.json</code>; no PATH opens the most recently   modified <code>result.json</code> under <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, default <code>artifacts/</code>), its   exit status (<code>0</code> the Inspector was closed, <code>3</code> nothing to inspect, not a saved   version-1 run, or a <code>--suite</code> that cannot be loaded), and the rule that the Re-Run   tab executes only the suite the run recorded as <code>suite_target</code> or the one <code>--suite</code>   names, never one derived from evidence. The screen behind it,   <code>hilt.inspector_app</code>, and the re-run adapter, <code>hilt.inspector_rerun</code>, are not   (<code>docs/inspector.md</code>).</li> <li>The <code>hilt-check</code> command line is public in two respects: its exit status contract   and the rule codes it ships (<code>docs/hilt-check.md</code>). The Python API of the   <code>hilt_check</code> package is not.</li> </ul> <p>Private, with no promise attached:</p> <ul> <li>Any name beginning with an underscore, at any depth.</li> <li>Every submodule path. <code>hilt.lifecycle</code>, <code>hilt.routing</code>, <code>hilt.executor</code>,   <code>hilt.timeline</code>, <code>hilt.mock</code>, and the rest are the framework's organs; the   self-tests import from them, suites do not.</li> <li>Annotation-only aliases that appear in signatures but are not exported   (<code>WaitPredicate</code>, <code>HaltReason</code>, <code>ExecFn</code>). They are documented by their expansion.</li> <li>Directory and file names beneath the artifact root. <code>TestComponent.log_path</code> and the   <code>result</code> pointer in the timeline are the API; the layout that produces them is not.</li> <li>The transports, <code>RemoteInterface</code>, <code>SshInterface</code>, and <code>SerialInterface</code>. A suite   author never constructs a transport: <code>TestUtils.use_shell</code> and <code>TestUtils.get_shell</code>   open an SSH connection and hand back a <code>Shell</code>; <code>TestUtils.use_serial</code> and   <code>TestUtils.get_serial</code> do the same for a console by device path, and their   documented parameters are the promise the serial transport is reached through.   Transport authors and the self-tests import <code>hilt.remote</code>, <code>hilt.ssh</code>, and   <code>hilt.serial</code> directly; a transport contract for third parties is a design note   of its own (issues #91, #93\u2013#97).</li> <li>The Device API backends, <code>DeviceApiInterface</code>, <code>HttpInterface</code>, <code>CoapInterface</code>,   and <code>MockDeviceApi</code>. A suite author never constructs one: <code>TestUtils.get_http</code>   and <code>TestUtils.get_coap</code> open the session and hand back a <code>DeviceApi</code>. Self-tests   import <code>hilt.device_api</code> directly.</li> <li><code>hilt.junit.JunitXml</code>. The runner owns the reporter: <code>hilt run --junit PATH</code>   is the promised way to a JUnit report. A harness adapter that imports   <code>hilt.junit</code> directly does so without the promise, as <code>docs/junit-xml.md</code> says.</li> <li>The self-test fixtures, the loopback sshd, and <code>MockTransport</code>. A transport double   for suite authors is a feature to be designed, not a side door into the test kit.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#version-numbers","title":"Version numbers","text":"<p>The distribution version follows Semantic Versioning 2.0.0: <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>.</p> <p>Before 1.0.0 (<code>0.y.z</code>) nothing is promised. A name may be renamed or removed in any release, without a deprecation period; the composition vocabulary ruling on issue #110 (<code>case</code> and <code>group</code> removed outright, a <code>hilt-check</code> rule naming the replacement) is the precedent. Every such change still lands in <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> under the release that makes it, so the trail exists before the promise does.</p> <p>From 1.0.0 a release bumps exactly one component:</p> Component Bumped when Examples MAJOR A promise is broken. A name leaves <code>__all__</code>; a documented parameter, attribute, or return type changes incompatibly; a contract in <code>docs/contracts.md</code> weakens; a <code>format_version</code> bumps; <code>requires-python</code> rises; a <code>hilt-check</code> rule code is removed or renamed. MINOR The surface grows or a deprecation is announced. A new name in <code>__all__</code>; a new optional keyword parameter; a new call form of an existing method that leaves every existing call valid; a new field on a record within its <code>format_version</code>; a new <code>hilt-check</code> rule; a name marked deprecated. PATCH Behaviour is corrected without changing the surface. A bug fix that brings behaviour in line with a documented contract; a dependency bump that changes nothing a suite can observe; documentation. <p>Two consequences the developer can rely on:</p> <ul> <li>A fix is not a break. When a documented contract and the code disagree, the   document is the promise and the code is the bug. Correcting it is a PATCH even if a   suite depended on the wrong behaviour.</li> <li>A <code>format_version</code> bump is a MAJOR. The Inspector and the harness that read a   station's records are part of the station. A new record version that an installed   consumer refuses is a break, whatever the Python surface did.</li> </ul> <p>Dependencies are private. <code>asyncssh</code> and anything else the framework imports may change major version in a MINOR or PATCH release as long as nothing a suite can observe changes; a dependency that becomes observable (a type in a public signature) becomes part of the promise at that moment and is documented as such.</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#deprecation","title":"Deprecation","text":"<p>A public name is never removed in the MAJOR line that introduced it. It is deprecated first, and removed no sooner than the next MAJOR release and no sooner than two MINOR releases after the deprecation was announced. Both conditions hold at once.</p> <p>Announcing a deprecation is one MR that does all of the following:</p> <ol> <li>The old spelling keeps working, with the documented behaviour, for the whole    deprecation period.</li> <li>Calling it emits <code>DeprecationWarning</code> through <code>warnings.warn</code> with    <code>stacklevel=2</code>, so the warning points at the suite's line, not the framework's. The    message names the replacement and the release that will remove the old spelling.</li> <li>The reference table marks it with <code>Deprecated in x.y, removed in z.0</code> and the    replacement, and the section that documents the name says the same.</li> <li><code>CHANGELOG.md</code> records it under Deprecated for the release that announces it,    and under Removed for the release that removes it.</li> <li><code>hilt-check</code> gains a rule that flags the deprecated spelling and names the    replacement, in the pattern of <code>hilt/retired-names</code>, so the finding reaches the    developer's CI before it reaches a station log.</li> </ol> <p>The developer sees the warning in the self-tests of their own suite (pytest surfaces <code>DeprecationWarning</code> by default) and in <code>hilt-check</code> output; <code>hilt run</code> routes Python warnings through the run log so the station's console carries the notice as well.</p> <p>A removal at the next MAJOR deletes the code and the rule, moves the table row to the changelog's Removed list, and stops there: a removed name is not reused for something else within the following MAJOR line.</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#the-changelog","title":"The changelog","text":"<p><code>CHANGELOG.md</code> at the repository root follows Keep a Changelog: an Unreleased section at the top, one section per release below it, entries grouped under Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, and Security.</p> <p>Every MR that changes a public name, a documented contract, a record format, or a <code>hilt-check</code> rule adds its line to Unreleased in the same MR. A release renames Unreleased to the version and date and opens a fresh Unreleased above it. The changelog is the announcement; the GitLab issue is the ruling behind it.</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#changing-the-surface","title":"Changing the surface","text":"<p>A change to the public surface is a change to a promise, and the workflow in <code>STANDARDS.md</code> section 7 applies: it is raised as an issue for Ty's sign-off before its MR opens, stating how the change helps the developer help the Floor Technician. The MR then updates <code>hilt.__all__</code>, the reference table below, the section that documents the name, and <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> together. The public-surface self-test refuses an MR that updates one without the others.</p>"},{"location":"api-stability.html#reference","title":"Reference","text":"<p>Every name in <code>hilt.__all__</code>, what it is, and where its contract is written. Kind is checked against the object by the self-tests.</p> Name Kind What it is Described in <code>PASS</code> constant The verdict. <code>return PASS</code> passes with no evidence; <code>return PASS({...})</code> passes and carries the readings that prove it. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Why HILT Rejects \"Pass by Default\"), <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Evidence Travels With the Verdict), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Terminal State Contract) <code>AfterEachContext</code> class The narrow integration-hook context after a node settles: <code>BeforeEachContext</code> plus <code>result</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Integration Hooks), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Integration Hook Contract) <code>AfterEachHook</code> type alias <code>Callable[[AfterEachContext], Awaitable[None]]</code>; the <code>after_each</code> parameter of <code>TestComponent.run</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Integration Hooks) <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> class <code>TestFailure</code> raised when the resolver cannot map a MAC address to an IP. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (ArpResolver \u2014 MAC to IP) <code>ArpResolver</code> class Resolve a MAC address to an IP through the node's current exec route; <code>mac</code>, <code>resolve</code>, <code>reacquire</code>, <code>ping</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (ArpResolver \u2014 MAC to IP, ArpResolver with Via-Routing) <code>Artifact</code> class Immutable point-in-time evidence (<code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>timestamp</code>) for <code>TestUtils.capture_artifact</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Point-in-Time Artifacts), <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> <code>BeforeEachContext</code> class The narrow integration-hook context before a node runs: <code>name</code>, <code>ancestor_names</code>, <code>bread_crumbs</code>, <code>logger</code>, <code>env</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Integration Hooks), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Integration Hook Contract) <code>BeforeEachHook</code> type alias <code>Callable[[BeforeEachContext], Awaitable[None]]</code>; the <code>before_each</code> parameter of <code>TestComponent.run</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Integration Hooks) <code>CoapCode</code> class Named CoAP response codes as dotted strings (<code>CONTENT</code> is <code>2.05</code>, <code>CHANGED</code> is <code>2.04</code>, <code>NOT_FOUND</code> is <code>4.04</code>). A CoAP <code>DeviceResponse.status</code> is one of these strings; <code>from_wire</code> renders a code byte the same way. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP), signed <code>docs/hil/device-api.md</code> <code>ContractViolation</code> class A developer bug: suite code did something the verdict contract forbids. Records as Fail with <code>bug: true</code>. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>DeviceApi</code> class The session handle returned by <code>TestUtils.get_http</code> and <code>TestUtils.get_coap</code>; <code>request</code>, <code>scheme</code>, <code>base_url</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP), signed <code>docs/hil/device-api.md</code> <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> class <code>HiltError</code> raised when a device-API transfer does not complete. Not a DUT verdict; HTTP 4xx/5xx is data on <code>DeviceResponse</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP), signed <code>docs/hil/device-api.md</code> <code>DeviceRequest</code> class One resource operation: <code>method</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>payload</code> bytes or keyword-only <code>json=</code>, <code>headers</code>, retry declaration, <code>evidence_headers</code>. <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Device API Contract), <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP), signed <code>docs/hil/device-api.md</code> <code>DeviceResponse</code> class What a transfer returned: <code>status</code>, <code>body</code> bytes, <code>headers</code>; <code>json()</code> and <code>parse(response_type=\"json\" or \"yaml\")</code> decode explicitly. Status is data: an HTTP integer, or a CoAP dotted code (<code>2.05</code>). <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Device API Contract), <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP), signed <code>docs/hil/device-api.md</code> <code>EvidenceViolation</code> class <code>ContractViolation</code> raised by the child boundary when a verdict's evidence is not JSON, shadows a ctx key, or lacks a key <code>@evidence</code> declared; <code>key</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Evidence Travels With the Verdict), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Evidence Contract) <code>ExecResult</code> class What a command produced: <code>stdout</code>, <code>stderr</code>, <code>exit_code</code>, <code>truncated</code>, <code>captured_bytes</code>. <code>exec</code> never throws. <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Utils Contract) <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> class A harness-level halt: an integration hook threw. Aborts the run. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Integration Hook Contract) <code>Failure</code> type alias The union of <code>TestFailure</code>, <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>, and <code>MissingVerdict</code>; the type of <code>RunResult.failure</code>. <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>FrameworkContractViolation</code> class A framework bug: the second layer of the guard, unreachable by design. Exported so a log line can be followed. <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>HiltError</code> class Root of the framework's exception taxonomy. <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>JsonObject</code> type alias <code>dict[str, JsonValue]</code>; the shape of <code>run_data</code>, of a node's <code>evidence</code>, and of <code>Artifact.data</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (run_data \u2014 Automatic Structured Evidence) <code>Measurement</code> class One <code>utils.measure</code> call as recorded: <code>name</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>unit</code>, <code>limits</code>, <code>in_limits</code>, <code>timestamp</code>. Frozen; <code>in_limits</code> is evidence, never a verdict. <code>value</code> is <code>None</code> for a reading not taken, and such a reading is never inside its window. <code>docs/measurements.md</code> (The call) <code>MissingVerdict</code> class No verdict was produced: the test method returned something other than <code>PASS</code>. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Terminal State Contract) <code>NodeType</code> class <code>StrEnum</code> discriminator on <code>RunResult.type</code> and <code>SummaryTreeNode.type</code>: <code>SUITE</code>, <code>TEST</code>, <code>PARALLEL</code>, <code>SEQUENCE</code>; the wire values are fixed by the result schema. <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> (Version 1 result) <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> class A harness-level halt: the operator interrupted the run. Aborts the run. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>Phase</code> class Ordered <code>StrEnum</code> of the six survey dispatch points, <code>PRE_CONFIGURE</code> through <code>POST_CLEANUP</code>; the <code>phases=</code> selection of <code>use_survey</code> and the artifact-name prefix. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Constructor-Only Hooks), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Evidence Contract) <code>RunEnv</code> class The run environment on <code>TestUtils.env</code>: a read-only <code>Mapping[str, str]</code> plus <code>text</code>, <code>require</code>, <code>port</code>, <code>int</code>, and <code>float</code>, readers that Fail the run naming the bench variable to set. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (TestUtils Injection), <code>docs/hilt-run.md</code> (Selecting the suite) <code>RunResult</code> class Self-contained evidence for one settled node: <code>state</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>ancestor_names</code>, <code>bread_crumbs</code>, <code>summary_tree</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>run_data</code>, <code>to_json</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (RunResult Navigation), <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>, <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Evidence Contract) <code>RunSignal</code> class Read-only view of the halt signals affecting a node: <code>fired</code>, <code>reason</code>. <code>TestUtils.signal</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (TestUtils Injection) <code>ScopedLogger</code> class The per-node logger on <code>TestUtils.logger</code> and the hook contexts; a <code>logging.LoggerAdapter</code> with <code>scope</code> and <code>bread_crumbs</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Logging), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Evidence Contract) <code>Shell</code> class The scoped exec handle returned by <code>TestUtils.use_shell</code>, <code>TestUtils.get_shell</code>, <code>TestUtils.use_serial</code>, and <code>TestUtils.get_serial</code>; the <code>via</code> argument to all four. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (SSH Device, SSH Tunneling), <code>docs/utilities.md</code> (Serial console), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Exec Routing Contract) <code>Slot</code> class <code>StrEnum</code> of the lifecycle positions the framework calls suite code from; <code>RunResult.slot</code>. <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> (Version 1 result), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Lifecycle Contract) <code>State</code> class <code>StrEnum</code> of the three terminal states, <code>PASS</code>, <code>FAIL</code>, <code>ABORTED</code>; <code>RunResult.state</code>. There is no fourth. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (The Three Terminal States), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Terminal State Contract) <code>SummaryTreeNode</code> class Plain recursive snapshot of the run tree on <code>RunResult.summary_tree</code>; <code>evidence</code>, <code>evidence_declared</code>, <code>to_json</code>. <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> (Summary tree) <code>TestComponent</code> class The atom: a claim about hardware and the procedure that produces its evidence. <code>configure</code>, <code>build_suite</code>, <code>cleanup</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>sequence</code>, <code>parallel</code>, <code>use_survey</code>, <code>use_audit</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>log_path</code>, <code>run_data</code>, and the <code>redact_ctx</code> class attribute. <code>sequence</code> and <code>parallel</code> each have two call forms: <code>(children)</code> for the root of <code>build_suite()</code>, which takes the component's name, and <code>(name, children)</code>. Optionally parameterized as <code>TestComponent[CtxT]</code> to type the suite's own context; the bare name keeps today's meaning. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (The Atom), <code>docs/idioms.md</code>, <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Lifecycle Contract) <code>TestFailure</code> class A verdict of Fail: the hardware did not meet the spec. The exception a test method raises; <code>evidence=</code> carries the readings taken before it failed. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Failure Taxonomy Contract) <code>TestUtils</code> class The scoped service object handed to every lifecycle method: <code>logger</code>, <code>ctx</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>signal</code>, <code>artifact_root</code>, <code>exec_route</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>wait_for</code>, <code>capture_artifact</code>, <code>get_arp_resolver</code>, <code>use_shell</code>, <code>get_shell</code>, <code>use_serial</code>, <code>get_serial</code>, <code>get_http</code>, <code>get_coap</code>, <code>measure</code>. Optionally parameterized as <code>TestUtils[CtxT]</code> so <code>ctx</code> returns the suite's declared shape. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (TestUtils Injection), <code>docs/utilities.md</code> (Serial console), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Utils Contract) <code>TimeoutEvidence</code> class Structured evidence when a time budget cut a subtree: <code>reason</code>, <code>declared</code>, <code>budget_seconds</code>, <code>elapsed_seconds</code>, <code>clock</code>; <code>RunResult.timeout</code>. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Time Budgets), <code>docs/contracts.md</code> (Time Budget Contract) <code>Verdict</code> class The return type of a test method: <code>PASS</code> or <code>PASS(evidence)</code>; <code>evidence</code>. Generic in the evidence shape, so <code>-&gt; Verdict[MyEvidence]</code> lets pyright check the payload. <code>docs/overview.md</code> (Why HILT Rejects \"Pass by Default\"), <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Evidence Travels With the Verdict), <code>docs/hilt-check.md</code> (<code>hilt/unannotated-interface</code>) <code>evidence</code> function Decorator naming the keys a test method's <code>PASS</code> must carry: <code>@evidence(\"k\", ...)</code> or <code>@evidence(MyTypedDict)</code>. Static at class definition. <code>docs/idioms.md</code> (Evidence Travels With the Verdict), <code>docs/hilt-check.md</code> (<code>hilt/evidence-fulfilled</code>, <code>hilt/evidence-shadows-ctx</code>) <code>measure</code> function Package-root spelling of <code>utils.measure</code>, resolved to the node whose <code>configure()</code> or test method is running and refused outside one. <code>utils.measure</code> is the documented home. <code>docs/measurements.md</code> (The package-root alias)"},{"location":"api.html","title":"API reference","text":"<p>The public surface is <code>hilt.__all__</code>. Import these names from <code>hilt</code>; the module a name is defined in is an implementation detail. The policy is API stability.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.PASS","title":"PASS  <code>module-attribute</code>","text":"<pre><code>PASS: Verdict[Never] = Verdict()\n</code></pre> <p>The verdict. <code>return PASS</code> passes with no evidence; <code>return PASS({...})</code> passes and carries the readings that prove it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.AfterEachContext","title":"AfterEachContext  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>AfterEachContext(\n    name: str,\n    ancestor_names: tuple[str, ...],\n    bread_crumbs: str,\n    logger: LoggerAdapter[Logger],\n    env: Mapping[str, str],\n    result: RunResult,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>BeforeEachContext</code></p> <p>The narrow integration context plus this node's completed result.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.AfterEachHook","title":"AfterEachHook","text":"<pre><code>AfterEachHook = Callable[\n    [AfterEachContext], Awaitable[None]\n]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolutionFailure","title":"ArpResolutionFailure","text":"<pre><code>ArpResolutionFailure(message: str, *, mac: str, host: str)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>The resolver could not map a MAC address to an IP.</p> <p>This is not a <code>TestFailure</code>. That is deliberate:</p> <ul> <li>Uncaught in a test method or <code>configure</code>, the child boundary classifies it   as a developer bug. The suite did not model this failure mode at all.</li> <li>Caught and re-raised as <code>TestFailure</code>, the author acknowledged the mode   and chose the verdict shape: the device did not appear on the network.</li> </ul> <p>Catching this does not tell you why the ARP entry is missing \u2014 dead NIC, unplugged cable, hungry switch, DHCP failure, or a gateway that has not seen traffic from the target yet. No amount of code can distinguish those. Hardware versus infrastructure is the human triager's call from logs, survey artifacts, and the bench. Do not write code that pretends to know.</p> <p>If this extended <code>TestFailure</code>, an uncaught miss would land in Fail and look like a modeled hardware verdict, hiding that the author never handled the mode.</p> <p>Record the MAC that could not be resolved and the host that was asked.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>message</code> <code>str</code> <p>What failed, in the FT's language.</p> required <code>mac</code> <code>str</code> <p>The normalised MAC that was missing from the table.</p> required <code>host</code> <code>str</code> <p>Routing label of the host whose ARP table was read (<code>\"local\"</code>, <code>\"via 10.0.0.1\"</code>, \u2026).</p> required"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver","title":"ArpResolver","text":"<pre><code>ArpResolver(\n    *,\n    mac: str,\n    ping_count: int = 1,\n    exec_fn: ExecFn | None = None,\n    logger: ScopedLogger | None = None,\n    cache_key: str | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Resolve a MAC address to an IP through the node's current exec route.</p> <p><code>resolve()</code> returns a plain dict suitable for spreading into <code>configure()</code>'s return value. <code>ping(utils)</code> is a gated reachability claim. <code>reacquire(utils)</code> forces a fresh table read after a reboot, flash, or DHCP churn and returns the new IP; the resolver remembers it, so a later <code>ping(utils)</code> dials the address it last resolved. Ctx is read-only after <code>configure()</code>, so the address that moves lives in the resolver, and a test that wants it on the record carries it as evidence: <code>PASS({\"ip\": ip})</code>.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> <p>The MAC is missing from the table, or <code>/proc/net/arp</code> is unreadable. This is not a <code>TestFailure</code>. Uncaught it is a developer bug \u2014 the suite did not model the failure mode. Catch it and re-raise as <code>TestFailure</code> to make \"the device did not appear on the network\" a verdict. Hardware versus infrastructure remains the triager's call.</p> <code>RuntimeError</code> <p><code>resolve()</code> was called on an instance that was not created through <code>utils.get_arp_resolver()</code>.</p> <code>ValueError</code> <p><code>mac</code> does not contain 12 hex digits, or <code>ping_count</code> &lt; 1.</p> <p>Bind a MAC. Authors go through <code>utils.get_arp_resolver()</code>, not this.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>mac</code> <code>str</code> <p>Device MAC in any usual form (colons, dashes, or bare hex).</p> required <code>ping_count</code> <code>int</code> <p>ICMP packets per <code>ping()</code>. Must be at least 1.</p> <code>1</code> <code>exec_fn</code> <code>ExecFn | None</code> <p>Route used to read the ARP table. Injected by <code>get_arp_resolver</code>.</p> <code>None</code> <code>logger</code> <code>ScopedLogger | None</code> <p>Scoped logger for the resolve/reacquire lines.</p> <code>None</code> <code>cache_key</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Routing label identifying which host's table this is.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver.mac","title":"mac  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>mac: str\n</code></pre> <p>The normalised colon-separated lowercase MAC this resolver owns.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver.reset_caches","title":"reset_caches  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>reset_caches() -&gt; None\n</code></pre> <p>Drop every ARP table cache. Self-tests call this between cases.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver.resolve","title":"resolve  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>resolve() -&gt; dict[str, str]\n</code></pre> <p>Refresh a stale cache and return <code>{\"ip\": ...}</code> for this MAC.</p> <p>Zero-arg: uses the exec and cache key <code>get_arp_resolver</code> bound. Raises if this instance was constructed without that scope.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver.reacquire","title":"reacquire  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>reacquire(utils: TestUtils) -&gt; str\n</code></pre> <p>Force a fresh ARP read and return the new IP.</p> <p>Call from a test method after anything that may have changed the IP. The cache key stays the resolver's \u2014 that is the host whose table holds this MAC \u2014 while the command runs through the child's <code>utils.exec</code>. The resolver's shared table now holds the new address, so <code>ping(utils)</code> on any resolver for this MAC dials it; carry it as evidence on the verdict if the Floor Technician should see it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ArpResolver.ping","title":"ping  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>ping(utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict\n</code></pre> <p>Reachability claim against this MAC's last resolved IP.</p> <p>The address is read from the resolver's table for this MAC, which <code>resolve()</code> and <code>reacquire()</code> both refresh, and falls back to <code>utils.ctx[\"ip\"]</code> for a context that was seeded without a resolve. Uses the child's exec and logger. Packet loss and a missing stats line are <code>TestFailure</code>. Duplicate replies (<code>received &gt; transmitted</code>) still count as success \u2014 the host answered. A missing IP (resolve never ran) is <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Artifact","title":"Artifact  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>Artifact(\n    name: str,\n    type: str,\n    data: object,\n    timestamp: datetime = (lambda: now(UTC))(),\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Describe point-in-time evidence without exposing a file-writing operation.</p> <p>Persistence belongs exclusively to :meth:<code>TestUtils.capture_artifact</code>; keeping this value immutable and write-free makes it impossible for the public framework surface to create an artifact that lacks its chronological log pointer.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Artifact.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, object]\n</code></pre> <p>Return the versioned JSON shape persisted by the framework.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.BeforeEachContext","title":"BeforeEachContext  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>BeforeEachContext(\n    name: str,\n    ancestor_names: tuple[str, ...],\n    bread_crumbs: str,\n    logger: LoggerAdapter[Logger],\n    env: Mapping[str, str],\n)\n</code></pre> <p>External-coordination context that cannot exercise the target hardware.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.BeforeEachHook","title":"BeforeEachHook","text":"<pre><code>BeforeEachHook = Callable[\n    [BeforeEachContext], Awaitable[None]\n]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.CoapCode","title":"CoapCode","text":"<p>Dotted CoAP response codes, the form a record can carry.</p> <p>A CoAP <code>DeviceResponse.status</code> is one of these strings (<code>\"2.05\"</code>), never the wire integer <code>class &lt;&lt; 5 | detail</code>. HTTP status stays an <code>int</code>. Compare with <code>response.status == CoapCode.CONTENT</code> and put <code>response.status</code> on the evidence as-is: it is already the text a reader sees. <code>from_wire</code> is how the transport, and a claim that still holds a code byte, spells that integer.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.CoapCode.from_wire","title":"from_wire  <code>staticmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>from_wire(code: int) -&gt; str\n</code></pre> <p>Render a CoAP code byte as <code>class.detail</code> (<code>69</code> becomes <code>\"2.05\"</code>).</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>ValueError</code> <p><code>code</code> is not an integer in 0-255. Booleans are refused.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ContractViolation","title":"ContractViolation","text":"<pre><code>ContractViolation(\n    node: str,\n    detail: str | None = None,\n    cause: BaseException | None = None,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>TestFailure</code></p> <p>A developer bug: suite code did something the verdict contract forbids.</p> <p>Raised by the framework, never by suite code. It is a <code>TestFailure</code> subclass on purpose \u2014 the node lands in Fail \u2014 but it carries a banner so nobody mistakes a broken test for a broken unit.</p> <p>Banner the violation with the node it happened in.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>node</code> <code>str</code> <p>Name of the node whose contract was violated.</p> required <code>detail</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>What specifically went wrong. Defaults to the general form.</p> <code>None</code> <code>cause</code> <code>BaseException | None</code> <p>The original exception, preserved verbatim for the artifact.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ContractViolation.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>A contract violation is always a developer bug, never a device fault.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ContractViolation.escaped","title":"escaped  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>escaped(\n    node: str, error: BaseException\n) -&gt; ContractViolation\n</code></pre> <p>Banner an exception that is not a verdict and escaped suite code.</p> <p>The author neither returned PASS nor raised <code>TestFailure</code>, so the default \"return PASS or raise TestFailure\" advice would misdescribe what happened. The detail names the exception type and message \u2014 the thing the reader has to fix \u2014 and the original stays on <code>cause</code> for the artifact.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ContractViolation.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Carry banner, node, and cause so the record names who is at fault and where.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceApi","title":"DeviceApi","text":"<pre><code>DeviceApi(backend: DeviceApiInterface)\n</code></pre> <p>Author-facing handle from <code>get_http</code> / <code>get_coap</code>.</p> <p>Suite authors do not construct this. <code>request</code> returns a typed response; HTTP 4xx is data, not an exception. The framework owns connect, retry, and the closed-session halt. One handle carries one request at a time: <code>parallel</code> leaves sharing it queue in call order, so a latency measured around <code>request</code> on a shared handle includes the wait for siblings.</p> <p>Wrap an already-connected backend. Framework and self-tests only.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceApi.scheme","title":"scheme  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>scheme: str\n</code></pre> <p>Scheme this handle was bound to.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceApi.base_url","title":"base_url  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>base_url: str\n</code></pre> <p>Declared origin.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceApi.request","title":"request  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>request(req: DeviceRequest) -&gt; DeviceResponse\n</code></pre> <p>Perform <code>req</code> on this session. See :class:<code>DeviceRequest</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceApiOperationFailure","title":"DeviceApiOperationFailure","text":"<pre><code>DeviceApiOperationFailure(\n    message: str,\n    *,\n    handle: str,\n    operation: str,\n    method: str,\n    path: str,\n    ceiling: float | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>A device-API transfer did not complete. This is not a DUT verdict.</p> <p>Uncaught in a test method or <code>configure</code>, the child boundary classifies it as a developer bug: the suite did not model this failure mode. Caught and re-raised as <code>TestFailure</code>, the author chose the verdict. A well-formed HTTP 4xx/5xx is not this error \u2014 that status is data on <code>DeviceResponse</code>.</p> <p>Record the handle, operation, and ceiling that did not complete.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>message</code> <code>str</code> <p>What failed, in the FT's language.</p> required <code>handle</code> <code>str</code> <p>Scheme plus base URL of the session.</p> required <code>operation</code> <code>str</code> <p><code>request</code>, <code>connect</code>, or <code>close</code>.</p> required <code>method</code> <code>str</code> <p>HTTP/CoAP method, or empty when the operation has none.</p> required <code>path</code> <code>str</code> <p>Resource path, or empty when the operation has none.</p> required <code>ceiling</code> <code>float | None</code> <p>Backend-declared seconds bound, when one expired.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceRequest","title":"DeviceRequest  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>DeviceRequest(\n    method: DeviceMethod,\n    path: str,\n    payload: bytes | None = None,\n    headers: Mapping[str, str] = MappingProxyType({}),\n    retries: int = 0,\n    retry_delay: float = 0.05,\n    retry_non_idempotent: bool = False,\n    timeout: float | None = None,\n    evidence_headers: frozenset[str] = frozenset(),\n    *,\n    json: InitVar[Any] = _NO_JSON\n)\n</code></pre> <p>One resource operation: method, path, optional payload, optional headers.</p> <p>Path is relative to the handle's base URL. Use <code>payload</code> for raw bytes or keyword-only <code>json</code> for a JSON value, including <code>None</code> (JSON null). JSON is encoded once as UTF-8 and adds <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> unless a content type was supplied. Supplying both bodies raises <code>ValueError</code>; unserializable or non-finite JSON is refused at construction. <code>retries</code> counts extra application-level attempts after the first; the default is zero. Attempt logs withhold every header value unless the name is in <code>evidence_headers</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceResponse","title":"DeviceResponse  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>DeviceResponse(\n    status: int | str,\n    body: bytes,\n    headers: Mapping[str, str] = MappingProxyType({}),\n)\n</code></pre> <p>The bytes and status a transfer actually returned.</p> <p>HTTP 4xx/5xx and CoAP 4.xx/5.xx are fields here, not exceptions. An HTTP <code>status</code> is the integer. A CoAP <code>status</code> is the dotted code (<code>\"2.05\"</code>, :class:<code>CoapCode</code>). <code>headers</code> carries HTTP headers or CoAP options as strings. <code>body</code> is the payload bytes. Parsing is opt-in through <code>json()</code> or <code>parse(response_type=...)</code>; a parsing error never changes the transfer's status or raw bytes.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceResponse.json","title":"json","text":"<pre><code>json() -&gt; Any\n</code></pre> <p>Decode the body as JSON, independent of status and content type.</p> <p>Raises <code>json.JSONDecodeError</code> for malformed JSON and <code>UnicodeDecodeError</code> for invalid encoding. No fallback value is invented.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.DeviceResponse.parse","title":"parse","text":"<pre><code>parse(\n    response_type: Literal[\"json\", \"yaml\"] = \"json\",\n) -&gt; Any\n</code></pre> <p>Decode the body in the explicitly selected format; default is JSON.</p> <p>YAML uses <code>yaml.safe_load</code> and requires the <code>open-hilt[yaml]</code> extra. Parser errors propagate unchanged; an unknown <code>response_type</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. Parsing never sends a request or changes the response.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.EvidenceViolation","title":"EvidenceViolation","text":"<pre><code>EvidenceViolation(node: str, *, key: str, reason: str)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>ContractViolation</code></p> <p>The evidence a verdict carried broke the evidence contract.</p> <p>Three reasons, one class, so the Floor Technician reads one banner and the message names the key: the value cannot be carried as JSON, the key shadows a ctx entry or the reserved <code>measurements</code> slot, or <code>@evidence</code> declared the key and <code>PASS</code> did not carry it. A pass without its declared evidence is not a pass: the node lands in Fail under the contract-violation banner and no hardware verdict is forged (issue #118).</p> <p>Name the offending key and why it offends.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>node</code> <code>str</code> <p>Name of the leaf whose verdict carried the evidence.</p> required <code>key</code> <code>str</code> <p>The evidence key at fault.</p> required <code>reason</code> <code>str</code> <p>One of the three sentences above, in the FT's language.</p> required"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.EvidenceViolation.not_carried","title":"not_carried  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>not_carried(node: str, key: str) -&gt; EvidenceViolation\n</code></pre> <p><code>@evidence</code> declared <code>key</code> and <code>PASS</code> did not carry it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.EvidenceViolation.not_serializable","title":"not_serializable  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>not_serializable(node: str, key: str) -&gt; EvidenceViolation\n</code></pre> <p>The value under <code>key</code> cannot be carried as JSON.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.EvidenceViolation.shadows_context","title":"shadows_context  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>shadows_context(node: str, key: str) -&gt; EvidenceViolation\n</code></pre> <p><code>key</code> is already a ctx entry or the reserved measurements slot.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.EvidenceViolation.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Carry the offending key so the record names the collision, not just the class.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ExecResult","title":"ExecResult  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>ExecResult(\n    stdout: str,\n    stderr: str,\n    exit_code: int,\n    truncated: bool = False,\n    captured_bytes: int = 0,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>What a host command produced.</p> <p>A non-zero <code>exit_code</code> is evidence, not an exception. <code>-1</code> means the process was killed by a signal, or was aborted before an exit status existed. <code>truncated</code> means stdout+stderr together hit the output budget; <code>captured_bytes</code> is how much was kept. Existing callers that omit both still construct a result.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ExecResult.settled","title":"settled  <code>classmethod</code>","text":"<pre><code>settled(\n    stdout: str | bytes | None,\n    stderr: str | bytes | None,\n    exit_status: int | None,\n    *,\n    truncated: bool = False,\n    captured_bytes: int | None = None\n) -&gt; ExecResult\n</code></pre> <p>Settle raw transport output into a result.</p> <p>Output arrives as whatever the transport produced \u2014 <code>bytes</code> (decoded UTF-8 with replacement), <code>str</code>, or nothing (empty). An <code>exit_status</code> of <code>None</code> or below zero means no real status exists \u2014 signal kill, or abort before an exit status \u2014 and settles as <code>-1</code>. Every transport builds its results here, so the <code>-1</code> contract cannot drift between them. Truncation is evidence, not an exception: the marker and byte count ride on the same result.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ExternalIntegrationFailure","title":"ExternalIntegrationFailure","text":"<pre><code>ExternalIntegrationFailure(\n    hook: HookName, cause: BaseException\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>A harness-level halt: an integration hook threw.</p> <p>One instance plays three structurally identical roles \u2014 the failure recorded on the Aborted node, the reason carried by the run-level abort, and the exception raised at the boundary that noticed. One class, three roles, zero wrapper layers. Do not introduce a wrapper: this type is never rewrapped by an ancestor, because the integrator must see their own error, not the framework's opinion of it.</p> <p>The original thrown value survives verbatim on <code>cause</code> so a DB driver error or a network rejection crosses the framework boundary unrebranded.</p> <p>Carry the integrator's error across the framework without rebranding it.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>hook</code> <code>HookName</code> <p>Which hook threw \u2014 <code>before_each</code> or <code>after_each</code>.</p> required <code>cause</code> <code>BaseException</code> <p>The integrator's original exception, preserved verbatim.</p> required"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ExternalIntegrationFailure.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>False: the integrator's system failed, not the suite and not the framework.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ExternalIntegrationFailure.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>JSON-safe snapshot for the run artifact. Never raises.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Failure","title":"Failure","text":"<pre><code>Failure = TestFailure | HaltReason | MissingVerdict\n</code></pre> <p>Everything that can be recorded on a settled node. Pass records nothing.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.FrameworkContractViolation","title":"FrameworkContractViolation","text":"<pre><code>FrameworkContractViolation(\n    node: str,\n    detail: str | None = None,\n    cause: BaseException | None = None,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>ContractViolation</code></p> <p>A framework bug \u2014 the second layer of the guard, and unreachable by design.</p> <p>If this ever appears in a log, hilt itself is wrong. Its banner is distinct from the developer layer so the FT knows to escalate to the maintainers rather than to the suite author.</p> <p>Banner a framework bug and name the maintainer escalation.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>node</code> <code>str</code> <p>Name of the node whose contract was violated.</p> required <code>detail</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>What specifically went wrong. A rule-only detail is completed with the maintainer escalation so the author-facing string names the fix. Defaults to the general form.</p> <code>None</code> <code>cause</code> <code>BaseException | None</code> <p>The original exception, preserved verbatim for the artifact.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.HiltError","title":"HiltError","text":"<p>               Bases: <code>Exception</code></p> <p>Root of the framework's own exception taxonomy.</p> <p>Catching this catches everything hilt raises deliberately and nothing a suite author raises by accident.</p> <p>A failure recorded at the child boundary is bound to the run's redactions (issue #154): from then on <code>str()</code>, <code>repr()</code>, and <code>to_json()</code> mask every secret the suite declared through <code>redact_ctx</code>, so a <code>TestFailure</code> that was handed a session credential as its <code>received</code> value renders it as <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code> on every surface. Every <code>to_json</code> in the taxonomy is wrapped at class creation, so a subclass need not know the masking exists.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.JsonObject","title":"JsonObject","text":"<pre><code>JsonObject = dict[str, JsonValue]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Measurement","title":"Measurement  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>Measurement(\n    name: str,\n    value: int | float | None,\n    unit: str | None,\n    limits: Limits | None,\n    in_limits: bool | None,\n    timestamp: datetime,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>One <code>utils.measure</code> call, exactly as recorded.</p> <p>Frozen because the record is sealed with the node: a consumer keys a time series on <code>name</code> together with the node path, so a value that could change after the call would corrupt the series it belongs to. <code>in_limits</code> is <code>None</code> when the reading was recorded without a window, <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> otherwise; it is a recorded fact, and the verdict is decided elsewhere.</p> <p>A reading that was not taken has <code>value=None</code>. It is never inside its window, so <code>in_limits</code> is <code>False</code> when limits were given: a gate written on the window fails the claim however it is spelled, and the claim decides what a missing reading means. Without limits it is <code>None</code>, as for any trend reading.</p> <p>Attributes:</p> Name Type Description <code>name</code> <code>str</code> <p>The identity of the quantity, unique within the node.</p> <code>value</code> <code>int | float | None</code> <p>The reading, an <code>int</code> or a finite <code>float</code>; <code>None</code> when the suite tried to take it and could not.</p> <code>unit</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>The developer's label for the reading, or <code>None</code> for a dimensionless count. Never validated, never converted.</p> <code>limits</code> <code>Limits | None</code> <p>The inclusive <code>(low, high)</code> window, or <code>None</code> when none was given.</p> <code>in_limits</code> <code>bool | None</code> <p>Whether <code>value</code> sits inside <code>limits</code>; <code>False</code> for a reading not taken against a window; <code>None</code> without limits.</p> <code>timestamp</code> <code>datetime</code> <p>When the reading was recorded, timezone-aware UTC.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Measurement.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; JsonObject\n</code></pre> <p>Return the version-1 record shape from <code>docs/measurements.md</code>.</p> <p><code>limits</code> becomes an object with explicit <code>low</code> and <code>high</code> keys rather than a bare array so that an open side reads as <code>null</code> by name, and the timestamp takes the timeline's own UTC <code>Z</code> form so one clock reads across both records.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.MissingVerdict","title":"MissingVerdict","text":"<pre><code>MissingVerdict(node: str, returned: object)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>No verdict was produced: the node returned something other than the sentinel.</p> <p>A <code>None</code> fall-through or an explicit wrong value. Still a developer contract violation, logged as loudly as one \u2014 but not a failure, and deliberately not a <code>TestFailure</code>. The node rendered no verdict, and the framework will not forge Fail or Pass on its behalf. Renders node-local Aborted; siblings are unaffected, because one author's missing <code>return</code> must not take down a seventy-DUT run.</p> <p>Record the non-verdict a node returned.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>node</code> <code>str</code> <p>Name of the node that returned without a verdict.</p> required <code>returned</code> <code>object</code> <p>The value it returned instead of PASS.</p> required"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.MissingVerdict.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>True: the absence of a verdict is always broken code, never broken hardware.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.MissingVerdict.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>JSON-safe snapshot for the run artifact. Never raises.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.NodeType","title":"NodeType","text":"<p>               Bases: <code>StrEnum</code></p> <p>Inspector discriminator for each framework-owned node shape.</p> <p>The wire values are the composition vocabulary: the word the developer wrote in <code>build_suite</code> is the word the Floor Technician reads in the tree (issue #111).</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.OperatorInterrupt","title":"OperatorInterrupt","text":"<pre><code>OperatorInterrupt(signum: int)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>A harness-level halt: the operator interrupted the run.</p> <p>One instance is the failure recorded on every Aborted node and the reason carried by the run-level abort. It is not a <code>TestFailure</code> and not an integration-hook failure: the recovery is \"the operator stopped the rack.\"</p> <p>Name the operator and the signal that stopped the run.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>signum</code> <code>int</code> <p>The POSIX signal number (<code>SIGINT</code>, <code>SIGTERM</code>).</p> required"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.OperatorInterrupt.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>False: the operator stopped the run, not the suite and not the hardware.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.OperatorInterrupt.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>JSON-safe snapshot for the run artifact. Never raises.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Phase","title":"Phase","text":"<p>               Bases: <code>StrEnum</code></p> <p>The six survey dispatch points of one node lifecycle, in the order they occur.</p> <p>The values are the artifact-name prefixes the Evidence Contract already publishes (<code>pre-configure-1</code>), so naming a phase in code names it in the run directory. Members order by declaration, not by string: <code>Phase.PRE_TEST &lt; Phase.POST_TEST</code> holds. Order is not reach. After a failed <code>configure</code> the engine skips post-configure, pre-test, and post-test and still fires pre-cleanup and post-cleanup, so a survey scheduled \"post-configure onward\" guards the device with <code>utils.exec_route</code>, not with the phase.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv","title":"RunEnv","text":"<pre><code>RunEnv(values: Mapping[str, str])\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>Mapping[str, str]</code></p> <p>The run environment: a read-only mapping with readers that Fail the run.</p> Contract <ul> <li>It is the filtered, immutable snapshot <code>TestUtils.env</code> publishes on every   <code>RunResult</code>. <code>get</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>[]</code>, and iteration behave as on any mapping.</li> <li><code>text(name)</code> trims the value and treats blank as unset. A bench script that   exports <code>TARGET_PASSWORD=\"\"</code> means \"no password\", not \"the empty password\".</li> <li><code>require(name)</code> raises <code>TestFailure</code> naming the variable to set. Called from   <code>configure</code>, that is the \"did not reach a testable state\" verdict: the run   Fails before any claim runs, and the message is the operator's instruction.</li> <li><code>port(name, default=22)</code> parses an integer in 1-65535 and raises   <code>TestFailure</code> for anything else, so a bench typo is reported as the variable   rather than surfacing later as a transport error.</li> <li><code>int(name, default=)</code> and <code>float(name, default=)</code> do the same for a   number. Unset or blank uses <code>default</code> when one was passed, and otherwise   Fails naming the variable. A value that is not a base-10 integer, or not a   finite float, Fails naming the variable and the value.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv.text","title":"text","text":"<pre><code>text(name: str) -&gt; str | None\n</code></pre> <p>Return the trimmed value of <code>name</code>, or <code>None</code> when it is unset or blank.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv.require","title":"require","text":"<pre><code>require(name: str) -&gt; str\n</code></pre> <p>Return the trimmed value of <code>name</code>, or Fail the run naming the variable.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TestFailure</code> <p><code>name</code> is unset or blank.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv.port","title":"port","text":"<pre><code>port(name: str, *, default: int = 22) -&gt; int\n</code></pre> <p>Return <code>name</code> as a TCP port, or <code>default</code> when it is unset or blank.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TestFailure</code> <p>the value is not an integer in 1-65535.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv.int","title":"int","text":"<pre><code>int(name: str, *, default: int | None = None) -&gt; int\n</code></pre> <p>Return <code>name</code> as a base-10 integer, or <code>default</code> when unset or blank.</p> <p>A missing variable with no <code>default</code> Fails naming the variable, the same promise as <code>require</code>. A value that is not a base-10 integer Fails naming the variable and the value, even when a default was passed. <code>0x10</code> is refused; <code>010</code> is ten.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TestFailure</code> <p>unset or blank with no default, or not a base-10 integer.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunEnv.float","title":"float","text":"<pre><code>float(name: str, *, default: float | None = None) -&gt; float\n</code></pre> <p>Return <code>name</code> as a finite float, or <code>default</code> when unset or blank.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TestFailure</code> <p>unset or blank with no default, or not a finite number. The message names the variable and the value.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult","title":"RunResult  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>RunResult(\n    slot: Slot,\n    node: str,\n    state: State,\n    ancestor_names: list[str],\n    bread_crumbs: str,\n    summary_tree: SummaryTreeNode,\n    type: NodeType,\n    failure: Failure | None = None,\n    timeout: TimeoutEvidence | None = None,\n    value: object = None,\n    swallowed: BaseException | None = None,\n    env: Mapping[str, str] = _empty_env(),\n    run_data: JsonObject = default_run_data(),\n    suite_target: str | None = None,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Self-contained evidence produced when one test node reaches a terminal state.</p> <p><code>slot</code>, <code>value</code>, and <code>swallowed</code> preserve the existing internal <code>Outcome</code> navigation surface. They are deliberately absent from :meth:<code>to_json</code>: raw return values are lifecycle machinery, while the serialized form is an evidence contract.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult.suite_target","title":"suite_target  <code>class-attribute</code> <code>instance-attribute</code>","text":"<pre><code>suite_target: str | None = None\n</code></pre> <p>The loader target that produced the run, <code>path.py::SuiteClass</code>, when known.</p> <p><code>hilt run</code> records it so <code>hilt inspect</code> can offer the same suite for the Re-Run tab instead of deriving one from evidence; a run started from a developer's own harness leaves it <code>None</code> and the Inspector says so (issue #89, 2026-09-10).</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult.test_case","title":"test_case  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>test_case: str\n</code></pre> <p>Machine-facing alias for the node name used by the JSON contract.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult.physical_location","title":"physical_location  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>physical_location: str\n</code></pre> <p>Station identity captured with the run environment.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>Whether broken code, rather than hardware, produced this result.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunResult.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; JsonObject\n</code></pre> <p>Return the complete, versioned Inspector payload accepted by <code>json.dumps</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunSignal","title":"RunSignal","text":"<pre><code>RunSignal(\n    run_abort: RunAbort,\n    audit_signal: AuditSignal | None = None,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Read-only view of the cancellation signals affecting one test node.</p> <p>Expose observation without exposing either framework writer.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunSignal.fired","title":"fired  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>fired: bool\n</code></pre> <p>Whether integration or audit control flow has asked this node to stop.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.RunSignal.reason","title":"reason  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>reason: HaltReason | TestFailure | None\n</code></pre> <p>The failure that asked this node to stop, if one exists.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ScopedLogger","title":"ScopedLogger","text":"<pre><code>ScopedLogger(\n    logger: Logger,\n    scope: tuple[str, ...],\n    *,\n    timeline: RunTimeline | None = None,\n    timeline_paths: TimelinePaths | None = None,\n    redactions: Redactions | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>LoggerAdapter[Logger]</code></p> <p>A standard logger carrying the exact node path on every record.</p> <p>Bind <code>logger</code> records to a root-to-leaf node path.</p> <p><code>redactions</code> is the run's registry of secret values (issue #154). Every record is masked against it before it reaches the timeline or the process logging policy, so a suite that logs a redacted ctx value by hand still leaves no trace of it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ScopedLogger.scope","title":"scope  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>scope: tuple[str, ...]\n</code></pre> <p>Root-to-current-node path attached to every emitted record.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ScopedLogger.bread_crumbs","title":"bread_crumbs  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>bread_crumbs: str\n</code></pre> <p>Display form of :attr:<code>scope</code>, suitable for logs and artifact keys.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ScopedLogger.isEnabledFor","title":"isEnabledFor","text":"<pre><code>isEnabledFor(level: int) -&gt; bool\n</code></pre> <p>Keep every level live when the durable timeline is attached.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.ScopedLogger.log","title":"log","text":"<pre><code>log(\n    level: int, msg: object, *args: object, **kwargs: Any\n) -&gt; None\n</code></pre> <p>Persist the record before offering it to the process logging policy.</p> <p>The record is rendered once here, masked against the run's redactions (issue #154), and that one rendering goes to both the durable timeline and the process logger: a secret the suite declared is on neither.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Shell","title":"Shell","text":"<pre><code>Shell(exec_fn: ExecFn, ssh: RemoteInterface | None)\n</code></pre> <p>A scoped exec handle.</p> <p><code>ssh</code> is unset when the handle wraps a local route. Passing a local Shell as <code>via</code> is a direct connection from the test host \u2014 standalone behavior.</p> <p>Wrap an already-resolved exec function and optional hop.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Shell.ssh","title":"ssh  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>ssh: RemoteInterface | None\n</code></pre> <p>The hop this handle tunnels through, or <code>None</code> when the route is local.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Shell.exec","title":"exec  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>exec(command: str) -&gt; ExecResult\n</code></pre> <p>Run <code>command</code> on this handle's route. Never raises for the exit status.</p> <p>A closed or aborted transport settles as <code>exit_code == -1</code>, same as <code>TestUtils.exec</code>. <code>RemoteInterface.exec</code> still raises <code>ConnectionError</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Slot","title":"Slot","text":"<p>               Bases: <code>StrEnum</code></p> <p>The lifecycle positions the framework calls suite code from.</p> <p>The slot is what the boundary needs to know to apply the right policy; it is also what the FT reads in the log to know which of their functions misbehaved.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.State","title":"State","text":"<p>               Bases: <code>StrEnum</code></p> <p>The three terminal states. Peers \u2014 Aborted is not a flavor of failure.</p> <p>There is no fourth. A call that settles without producing a terminal state (a successful <code>configure</code>, a completed <code>cleanup</code>) reports <code>Outcome.state is None</code>, which says \"nothing terminal happened here\" rather than inventing a state to say it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.SummaryTreeNode","title":"SummaryTreeNode  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>SummaryTreeNode(\n    name: str,\n    type: NodeType,\n    state: State | None,\n    failure: Failure | None = None,\n    timeout: TimeoutEvidence | None = None,\n    log_path: str | None = None,\n    children: tuple[SummaryTreeNode, ...] = (),\n    evidence: JsonObject | None = None,\n    evidence_declared: tuple[str, ...] | None = None,\n    measurements: tuple[JsonObject, ...] | None = None,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Plain recursive snapshot consumed by a future Inspector.</p> <p><code>state is None</code> means the node was declared but did not run, as can happen to later steps in a failed sequence. It is not a fourth terminal state.</p> <p><code>log_path</code> links this node to its persisted timeline. It is the POSIX path of the node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> relative to the run directory, so a copied run directory keeps every link intact. It is <code>None</code> for a node that never ran and for a node whose records rolled up into an ancestor; an Inspector resolves the latter by walking to the nearest ancestor that carries a path.</p> <p><code>evidence</code> is what a leaf's verdict carried, <code>PASS(evidence)</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence=...)</code>, already validated and detached. <code>evidence_declared</code> is the leaf's <code>@evidence</code> declaration. A declared key absent from <code>evidence</code> is a reading that was not taken; an Inspector shows it by name. Both are <code>None</code> on a node that is not a leaf, that never ran, or that declared nothing (issue #118).</p> <p><code>measurements</code> is every <code>utils.measure</code> call this node recorded, in call order, as version-1 measurement objects. It sits on the tree node rather than only in the node's own <code>run_data</code> because <code>result.json</code> is the root's record: a leaf's <code>run_data</code> never reaches the file the Floor Technician and the SPC consumer open (issue #86). An empty tuple means the node ran and measured nothing; <code>None</code> means the node never ran, matching <code>state</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.SummaryTreeNode.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; JsonObject\n</code></pre> <p>Return the version-independent node shape accepted by <code>json.dumps</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent","title":"TestComponent","text":"<pre><code>TestComponent(\n    *,\n    name: str,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>A claim about hardware and the procedure that produces its evidence.</p> <p>Instances accept configuration during construction and are immutable afterwards. Calling :meth:<code>main</code> consumes the instance: a second call raises :class:<code>ContractViolation</code>, preserving the first run as evidence.</p> <p><code>CtxT</code> is opt-in sugar (issue #75): a suite that narrows <code>configure</code>'s return to a local <code>TypedDict</code> and declares <code>class MySuite(TestComponent[MyCtx])</code> gets <code>utils: TestUtils[MyCtx]</code> in <code>configure</code>, <code>cleanup</code>, and any test method that asks for it. Composition and internal plumbing (sequence/parallel children, the leaf/sequence/parallel specializations) hold <code>TestComponent[Any]</code> instead of the bare, defaulted alias: they compose nodes of any <code>CtxT</code> and must not force every child onto one suite's context shape.</p> <p>Create a named, not-yet-run test node.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>name</code> <code>str</code> <p>Stable node name used by logs, artifacts, and failures.</p> required <code>discrete_log_file</code> <code>bool</code> <p>Own a per-node timeline. Set false only when this composed node should roll its records into its parent.</p> <code>True</code> <code>fail_after</code> <code>float | None</code> <p>Positive finite seconds before this subtree renders Fail.</p> <code>None</code> <code>abort_after</code> <code>float | None</code> <p>Positive finite seconds before this subtree renders Aborted.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.redact_ctx","title":"redact_ctx  <code>class-attribute</code>","text":"<pre><code>redact_ctx: Sequence[str | Pattern[str]] = ()\n</code></pre> <p>Ctx keys whose values are not for the record (issue #154).</p> <p>Each entry names a key <code>configure()</code> returns, exactly or as a regular expression that the key matches whole; a compiled pattern is taken as written. A subclass adds to its bases' entries and never replaces them. The claims read the real value from <code>utils.ctx</code> as usual; the record does not carry it. <code>run_data</code> shows the key with the value <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code>, and a string under a redacted key is masked wherever it appears: the scoped log, a failure's message and comparison, evidence on a verdict, <code>RunResult.to_json()</code>, and every file in the run directory. This is the same standard the framework holds an SSH credential to (<code>docs/contracts.md</code>, Evidence Contract). An invalid declaration is a <code>TypeError</code> at class definition.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.name","title":"name  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>name: str\n</code></pre> <p>The stable node name used by logs, artifacts, and failures.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.discrete_log_file","title":"discrete_log_file  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>discrete_log_file: bool\n</code></pre> <p>Whether this node owns a timeline instead of rolling into its parent.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.log_path","title":"log_path  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>log_path: Path | None\n</code></pre> <p>Authoritative JSON Lines path, or <code>None</code> for a rolled-up/pending node.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.run_data","title":"run_data  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>run_data: JsonObject\n</code></pre> <p>A detached JSON-safe snapshot of this node's serializable context.</p> <p>This is the ctx view: what <code>configure()</code> provided, minus anything that is not JSON. A leaf's <code>RunResult.run_data</code> is this view plus the evidence its verdict carried; the merge happens when the leaf settles, so an override here sees only the context (issue #118). A key the suite's <code>redact_ctx</code> claims appears as <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code> (issue #154).</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.test","title":"test","text":"<pre><code>test(\n    name: str,\n    fn: _TestFunction,\n    /,\n    *args: object,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None,\n    **kwargs: object,\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Create the leaf node: a name, a verdict method, and that method's arguments.</p> <p>The framework calls <code>fn(utils, *args, **kwargs)</code>. A suite stamps out one leaf per core, rail, or channel from a single method by naming each leaf and passing the value that varies. <code>fail_after</code> and <code>abort_after</code> are the leaf's budget and never reach <code>fn</code>; a test method cannot take parameters of those names.</p> <p>The arguments are checked against <code>fn</code>'s signature here, at composition time, so a leaf that could never be called fails <code>build_suite</code> before any hardware is touched. Static checkers cannot see through this call (PEP 612 forbids keyword parameters between <code>*args</code> and <code>**kwargs</code> of a <code>ParamSpec</code>).</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.parallel","title":"parallel","text":"<pre><code>parallel(\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    /,\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None,\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre><pre><code>parallel(\n    name: str,\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <pre><code>parallel(\n    name: str | Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]] | None = None,\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Compose independent children under one structured-concurrency owner.</p> <p><code>self.parallel(children)</code> is the root of <code>build_suite()</code> and takes this component's name; <code>self.parallel(name, children)</code> names a level of its own. See <code>sequence</code> for the rule the two forms share.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.sequence","title":"sequence","text":"<pre><code>sequence(\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    /,\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None,\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre><pre><code>sequence(\n    name: str,\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <pre><code>sequence(\n    name: str | Sequence[TestComponent[Any]],\n    children: Sequence[TestComponent[Any]] | None = None,\n    *,\n    discrete_log_file: bool = True,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None\n) -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Compose dependent children that stop at the first non-pass verdict.</p> <p>Two call forms. <code>self.sequence(children)</code> is the root of <code>build_suite()</code>: the component already has a name, and its root takes that name instead of being given it a second time. <code>self.sequence(name, children)</code> names a level of its own, at the root or below it. A nameless composition is the root only: placing one inside another composition is a <code>ContractViolation</code> where it is nested, because two levels would then carry one name and siblings could share it.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.use_survey","title":"use_survey","text":"<pre><code>use_survey(\n    fn: HookFunction,\n    *,\n    phases: Iterable[Phase] | None = None\n) -&gt; None\n</code></pre> <p>Register a survey as immutable test identity during construction.</p> <p><code>phases</code> names the dispatch points the survey observes; <code>None</code> means all six. An empty selection is a <code>ValueError</code> here, not a survey that never fires. The selection is the schedule, not a guarantee of reach: a boundary the lifecycle never arrives at is never dispatched, and a boundary it arrives at without a device route still is (see <code>Phase</code>).</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.use_audit","title":"use_audit","text":"<pre><code>use_audit(*, interval: float, fn: HookFunction) -&gt; None\n</code></pre> <p>Register an interval audit as immutable test identity during construction.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.configure","title":"configure  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>configure(utils: TestUtils[CtxT]) -&gt; CtxT\n</code></pre> <p>Prepare the test and return context for its suite and cleanup.</p> <p>The default returns an empty mapping typed as <code>CtxT</code> for the unparameterized case; the cast is the framework's, not a suite author's \u2014 see issue #75.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.build_suite","title":"build_suite","text":"<pre><code>build_suite() -&gt; TestComponent[Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Return this node's authored composition.</p> <p>Subclasses provide a pure table of contents assembled with <code>self.test()</code>, <code>self.sequence()</code>, and <code>self.parallel()</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.cleanup","title":"cleanup  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>cleanup(utils: TestUtils[CtxT]) -&gt; None\n</code></pre> <p>Restore state after the suite settles, regardless of its outcome.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.run","title":"run  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>run(\n    *,\n    env: Mapping[str, str] | None = None,\n    env_allow: Iterable[str] | None = None,\n    env_deny: Iterable[str] | None = None,\n    before_each: BeforeEachHook | None = None,\n    after_each: AfterEachHook | None = None,\n    allow_unpaired_hooks: bool = False,\n    fail_after: float | None = None,\n    abort_after: float | None = None,\n    output_budget: int = DEFAULT_EXEC_OUTPUT_BUDGET,\n    subtree: Sequence[int] | None = None,\n    suite_target: str | None = None\n) -&gt; RunResult\n</code></pre> <p>Execute once, fanning root-owned integration hooks across every node.</p> <p><code>env_allow</code> / <code>env_deny</code> select which environment variables the run publishes; see <code>TestUtils</code> for the contract. Raises <code>ValueError</code> when both are given. <code>output_budget</code> is the combined stdout+stderr ceiling for every <code>exec</code> this run issues, local or SSH.</p> <p><code>subtree</code> confines execution to one declared node: child positions from the root, so <code>(0, 1)</code> is the second child of the root's suite. Every node on the path to it still runs its own configure and cleanup, because that is their lifecycle and the only way the selected node meets the hop its ancestors own; every leaf outside the subtree is left pending, never executed. This is what the Inspector's Re-Run tab calls (Ty's ruling of 2026-09-10, <code>docs/inspector.md</code>); it is not ancestor-configure replay, which stays prohibited. A position the declared tree does not have raises <code>ContractViolation</code> before anything runs.</p> <p><code>suite_target</code> is recorded in <code>result.json</code> as the loader target that produced the run (<code>path.py::SuiteClass</code>), so <code>hilt inspect</code> can offer the same suite for re-running without deriving one from evidence. <code>hilt run</code> supplies it; a run started from a developer's own harness may leave it unset.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestComponent.main","title":"main  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>main(utils: TestUtils | None = None) -&gt; RunResult\n</code></pre> <p>Execute the sealed lifecycle with an optional framework test seed.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestFailure","title":"TestFailure","text":"<pre><code>TestFailure(\n    fail_message: str,\n    expected: object = MISSING,\n    received: object = MISSING,\n    *,\n    evidence: Mapping[str, object] | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>               Bases: <code>HiltError</code></p> <p>A verdict of Fail: the hardware did not meet the spec.</p> <p>Raised by suite code. Caught and recorded at the child boundary, which is what makes a failing DUT data rather than control flow \u2014 server 12 failing POST does not disturb the other 69.</p> <p>Raised from <code>configure()</code> it means \"did not reach a testable state\". Whether the cause was hardware or infrastructure is not knowable from code; the human triager decides and the framework does not pretend otherwise.</p> <p>Record what failed and, when given, the comparison that proves it.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>fail_message</code> <code>str</code> <p>What failed, in the FT's language.</p> required <code>expected</code> <code>object</code> <p>The spec-defined value, when there is one.</p> <code>MISSING</code> <code>received</code> <code>object</code> <p>The value actually observed \u2014 unknown, which is why we test.</p> <code>MISSING</code> <code>evidence</code> <code>Mapping[str, object] | None</code> <p>Readings taken before the failure, carried into the leaf's <code>run_data</code> beside the verdict. A failure is not held to the method's <code>@evidence</code> declaration; it records whatever it is handed.</p> <code>None</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestFailure.has_comparison","title":"has_comparison  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>has_comparison: bool\n</code></pre> <p>True if this failure carries structured expected/received values.</p> <p>Check before rendering the comparison, so a failure that is self-evident does not print two empty columns.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestFailure.is_bug","title":"is_bug  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>is_bug: bool\n</code></pre> <p>True if this Fail was caused by broken code rather than broken hardware.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestFailure.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; dict[str, Any]\n</code></pre> <p>JSON-safe snapshot for the run artifact. Never raises.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils","title":"TestUtils","text":"<pre><code>TestUtils(\n    *,\n    ctx: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\n    env: Mapping[str, str] | None = None,\n    env_allow: Iterable[str] | None = None,\n    env_deny: Iterable[str] | None = None,\n    logger: Logger | None = None,\n    artifact_root: str | Path | None = None,\n    output_budget: int = DEFAULT_EXEC_OUTPUT_BUDGET,\n    _integration_hooks: IntegrationHooks | None = None,\n    _runtime: NodeRuntime | None = None,\n    _suite_target: str | None = None\n)\n</code></pre> <p>The single fully scoped service object passed to every lifecycle method.</p> <p>A node owns the object, logger, and routing policy. Its authored composition receives distinct <code>TestUtils</code> objects which deliberately share that authored instance's mutable <code>ctx</code>. A composed <code>TestComponent</code> subclass instead begins with a fresh context, preserving instance locality.</p> <p><code>CtxT</code> is opt-in sugar (issue #75): the runtime object is always a :class:<code>RunContext</code>, a framework-owned <code>dict[str, Any]</code>. A suite that narrows <code>configure</code>'s return to a local <code>TypedDict</code> and declares <code>class MySuite(TestComponent[MyCtx])</code> gets that type back from :attr:<code>ctx</code> in every method it types as <code>utils: TestUtils[MyCtx]</code> \u2014 pyright then catches a typo in a ctx key at the call site. Nothing forces a suite to parameterize; the default <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code> reproduces today's un-parameterized behavior exactly.</p> <p>Everything the lifecycle drives lives on the :class:<code>NodeRuntime</code> this object holds; the methods here are the author's reads and writes of that same state.</p> <p>Create an unscoped run seed; lifecycle execution creates node views.</p> <p>Raises <code>ValueError</code> when both <code>env_allow</code> and <code>env_deny</code> are given.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>ctx</code> <code>dict[str, Any] | None</code> <p>Initial root context, copied into the run's context; read it back through :attr:<code>ctx</code>. The framework mutates that copy in place.</p> <code>None</code> <code>env</code> <code>Mapping[str, str] | None</code> <p>Environment source. Defaults to the process environment. The published snapshot is an immutable copy, filtered per the lists below.</p> <code>None</code> <code>env_allow</code> <code>Iterable[str] | None</code> <p>Regular expressions; only variables whose name matches one (<code>re.search</code>) are published. Exclusive with <code>env_deny</code>.</p> <code>None</code> <code>env_deny</code> <code>Iterable[str] | None</code> <p>Regular expressions; variables whose name matches one are withheld. Exclusive with <code>env_allow</code>. With either list, <code>environment=DEBUG</code> in the source publishes every variable and logs a warning saying so.</p> <code>None</code> <code>logger</code> <code>Logger | None</code> <p>Logging sink beneath the per-node scope adapter.</p> <code>None</code> <code>artifact_root</code> <code>str | Path | None</code> <p>Directory for point-in-time evidence. Defaults to the <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code> environment snapshot, then <code>./artifacts</code>.</p> <code>None</code> <code>output_budget</code> <code>int</code> <p>Combined stdout+stderr ceiling in bytes for every <code>exec</code> this run issues, local or SSH. Defaults to <code>DEFAULT_EXEC_OUTPUT_BUDGET</code> (4 MiB).</p> <code>DEFAULT_EXEC_OUTPUT_BUDGET</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.logger","title":"logger  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>logger: ScopedLogger\n</code></pre> <p>Logger whose records are tagged with this node's full scope.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.ctx","title":"ctx  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>ctx: CtxT\n</code></pre> <p>This authored instance's context: what <code>configure()</code> provided, read-only after.</p> <p>Always a :class:<code>RunContext</code> at runtime regardless of <code>CtxT</code>; the cast here is the framework's, narrowing to the suite's declared shape for an author who typed <code>utils: TestUtils[MyCtx]</code> (issue #75). An un-parameterized caller sees the same <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code> as before.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.env","title":"env  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>env: RunEnv\n</code></pre> <p>Immutable snapshot of the run environment, as published in every <code>RunResult</code>.</p> <p>This is the filtered view when the run declared <code>env_allow</code> or <code>env_deny</code>; a variable the filter withheld is absent here too, so <code>PHYSICAL_LOCATION</code> and <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code> must survive an allow list to keep their effect. Beyond the mapping, <code>RunEnv.require</code>, <code>text</code>, and <code>port</code> read a bench variable and Fail the run naming it when it is missing or malformed.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.signal","title":"signal  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>signal: RunSignal\n</code></pre> <p>Read-only view of the run-level integration halt signal.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.artifact_root","title":"artifact_root  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>artifact_root: Path\n</code></pre> <p>Absolute directory where this run's captured evidence is stored.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.exec_route","title":"exec_route  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>exec_route: str\n</code></pre> <p>The host <code>utils.exec</code> lands on right now: <code>\"local\"</code> or the hop's endpoint.</p> <p><code>\"local\"</code> is the test host, before any <code>use_shell</code> and after <code>use_shell(inherit=False)</code>. Otherwise it is the endpoint of the hop this node's route attaches to, the last name in the <code>exec routing:</code> evidence line (<code>\"10.10.10.10\"</code> for a route <code>bastion -&gt; 10.10.10.10</code>). A survey reads this to know whether there is a device on the other end of <code>exec</code> at the phase it fires, which the phase alone cannot say.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.capture_artifact","title":"capture_artifact  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>capture_artifact(artifact: Artifact) -&gt; Path\n</code></pre> <p>Persist evidence and append its structured pointer to the scoped timeline.</p> <p>The file is durably published before the pointer is logged. Serialization or persistence failures therefore produce neither a misleading pointer nor a partially published artifact.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.exec","title":"exec  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>exec(command: str) -&gt; ExecResult\n</code></pre> <p>Run <code>command</code> through this node's active route.</p> <p>Never raises for the command's own exit status. A non-zero <code>exit_code</code> is evidence for the test method to judge, not an exception. Signal-killed and aborted commands settle as <code>exit_code == -1</code>. A closed transport (including after <code>cancel_in_flight</code>) settles the same way, with the protocol error on stderr \u2014 <code>RemoteInterface.exec</code> still raises <code>ConnectionError</code> internally; this method is the author-facing surface and stays total. Hitting the output budget terminates the command and settles with <code>truncated=True</code>; that is evidence, not an exception. <code>CancelledError</code> still propagates: that is TaskGroup teardown, not a command outcome.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.wait_for","title":"wait_for  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>wait_for(\n    predicate: Callable[[], Awaitable[T]],\n    *,\n    looking_for: str,\n    interval: float = ...,\n    timeout_seconds: float = ...\n) -&gt; T\n</code></pre><pre><code>wait_for(\n    predicate: Callable[[], T],\n    *,\n    looking_for: str,\n    interval: float = ...,\n    timeout_seconds: float = ...\n) -&gt; T\n</code></pre> <pre><code>wait_for(\n    predicate: WaitPredicate[T],\n    *,\n    looking_for: str,\n    interval: float = 1.0,\n    timeout_seconds: float = 30.0\n) -&gt; T\n</code></pre> <p>Poll until <code>predicate</code> returns a truthy value, and hand that value back.</p> <p>A predicate that returns <code>True</code> still yields <code>True</code>. Any other truthy value \u2014 the entries a query log just found, a parsed reading \u2014 is what the caller receives, so the observation does not have to live in a closure (issue #158). Falsy results keep polling. A timeout raises <code>TestFailure</code> with <code>looking_for</code> as the expected value and <code>\"timeout\"</code> as the received one.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>predicate</code> <code>WaitPredicate[T]</code> <p>Sync or async callable, polled until it returns a truthy value.</p> required <code>looking_for</code> <code>str</code> <p>Plain-language name of the condition. It is the Floor Technician's primary diagnostic when the wait times out.</p> required <code>interval</code> <code>float</code> <p>Seconds between polls. Must be greater than zero.</p> <code>1.0</code> <code>timeout_seconds</code> <code>float</code> <p>Seconds to keep polling. Zero polls once, then times out if that look was falsy.</p> <code>30.0</code> <p>Returns:</p> Type Description <code>T</code> <p>The predicate's last truthy value.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TestFailure</code> <p>The window closed before a truthy value.</p> <code>ValueError</code> <p><code>interval</code> is not positive, or <code>timeout_seconds</code> is negative.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.get_arp_resolver","title":"get_arp_resolver","text":"<pre><code>get_arp_resolver(\n    *, mac: str, ping_count: int = 1\n) -&gt; ArpResolver\n</code></pre> <p>Return a MAC-to-IP resolver scoped to this node's current exec route.</p> <p>ARP table reads and pings go through <code>utils.exec</code>, so they land on the same host as the rest of this node's commands \u2014 local, or through a via hop. The routing label is the cache key: devices behind different gateways keep independent ARP state.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>mac</code> <code>str</code> <p>Device MAC in any usual form (colons, dashes, or bare hex).</p> required <code>ping_count</code> <code>int</code> <p>ICMP packets per <code>ping()</code>. Defaults to 1.</p> <code>1</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.use_shell","title":"use_shell  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>use_shell(\n    *,\n    host: str | None = None,\n    username: str | None = None,\n    port: int = 22,\n    password: str | None = None,\n    private_key: str | bytes | None = None,\n    private_key_path: str | Path | None = None,\n    passphrase: str | None = None,\n    agent: str | None = None,\n    skip_host_verification: bool = False,\n    via: Shell | None = None,\n    inherit: bool = True,\n    propagate: bool = True,\n    max_sessions: int | None = None\n) -&gt; Shell\n</code></pre> <p>Declare this node's exec route from <code>configure()</code>, or read the active one.</p> <p>With no arguments this returns the active <code>Shell</code> and may be called from any slot. A route-shaping argument (<code>host</code>, <code>via</code>, <code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) declares the route, and the framework admits that only while this node's <code>configure()</code> is running: the <code>exec routing:</code> line is written when configure returns and the FT reads it as true for the whole node (Exec Routing Contract). From any other slot the declaring call raises <code>ContractViolation</code> before the router is touched.</p> <p><code>max_sessions</code> is the most commands the opened connection runs at once. Every <code>exec</code> under this route opens its own SSH session, and an sshd refuses a session past its <code>MaxSessions</code> (10 by default), so a wide <code>parallel</code> under one hop can Fail a healthy device with <code>open failed</code>. Set it at or below the device's limit and the commands past it wait their turn, in call order, instead of being refused; the wait counts against the waiting leaf's time budget. The default, <code>None</code>, does not bound them. It is accepted only with <code>host</code>: it belongs to the node that opens the hop, and every node that inherits the route shares the one bound.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.get_shell","title":"get_shell  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>get_shell(\n    *,\n    host: str,\n    username: str,\n    port: int = 22,\n    password: str | None = None,\n    private_key: str | bytes | None = None,\n    private_key_path: str | Path | None = None,\n    passphrase: str | None = None,\n    agent: str | None = None,\n    skip_host_verification: bool = False,\n    via: Shell | None = None,\n    inherit: bool = True,\n    propagate: bool = True,\n    max_sessions: int | None = None\n) -&gt; Shell\n</code></pre> <p>Open an auxiliary endpoint without changing this node's exec route.</p> <p><code>max_sessions</code> bounds the commands in flight on the opened connection, as it does for <code>use_shell</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.use_serial","title":"use_serial  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>use_serial(\n    *,\n    device: str | None = None,\n    baud: int = 115200,\n    prompt: str | None = None,\n    login_prompt: str | None = None,\n    password_prompt: str | None = None,\n    username: str | None = None,\n    password: str | None = None,\n    login_timeout: float = 10.0,\n    prompt_timeout: float = 30.0,\n    bridge_command: str | None = None,\n    output_budget: int | None = None,\n    via: Shell | None = None,\n    inherit: bool = True,\n    propagate: bool = True\n) -&gt; Shell\n</code></pre> <p>Declare this node's exec route through a console from <code>configure()</code>, or read it.</p> <p><code>use_serial(device=...)</code> is <code>use_shell(host=...)</code> for a console reached by its device path (<code>/dev/ttyUSB0</code>): the port is opened, <code>utils.exec</code> is bound to it for this node and its descendants, and the framework closes it after the post-cleanup survey and on abort. With no arguments this returns the active <code>Shell</code> and may be called from any slot. The declaring form is admitted only while this node's <code>configure()</code> is running, for the reason <code>use_shell</code> gives: the <code>exec routing:</code> line is written when configure returns and the FT reads it as true for the whole node (Exec Routing Contract). From any other slot the declaring call raises <code>ContractViolation</code> before the router is touched.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>device</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>The device node, on the test host or, with <code>via</code> or an inherited SSH route, on the hop box.</p> <code>None</code> <code>baud</code> <code>int</code> <p>Line rate requested at open.</p> <code>115200</code> <code>prompt</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Regex matched against the tail of decoded output; <code>None</code> is a bare POSIX prompt. Supply it for anything else \u2014 a prompt guessed at runtime is how a stuck read gets misread as a fast command.</p> <code>None</code> <code>login_prompt</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Regex answered with <code>username</code> during connect, if the console logs in.</p> <code>None</code> <code>password_prompt</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Regex answered with <code>password</code> during connect.</p> <code>None</code> <code>username</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Login name; required when <code>login_prompt</code> is given.</p> <code>None</code> <code>password</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Login secret. Never rendered in evidence.</p> <code>None</code> <code>login_timeout</code> <code>float</code> <p>Seconds to resolve login and the first prompt at connect.</p> <code>10.0</code> <code>prompt_timeout</code> <code>float</code> <p>Seconds one <code>exec</code> waits for the prompt before settling <code>exit_code == -1</code> with the text so far as evidence.</p> <code>30.0</code> <code>bridge_command</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Remote command whose stdin/stdout is the UART, for a console behind a hop. Defaults to a <code>stty</code> + <code>cat</code> bridge.</p> <code>None</code> <code>output_budget</code> <code>int | None</code> <p>Per-exec output ceiling for this console; <code>None</code> is the run's shared ceiling.</p> <code>None</code> <code>via</code> <code>Shell | None</code> <p>An SSH <code>Shell</code> to bridge through. A serial console accepts no other kind of hop.</p> <code>None</code> <code>inherit</code> <code>bool</code> <p>Bridge through an inherited SSH route when <code>via</code> is omitted.</p> <code>True</code> <code>propagate</code> <code>bool</code> <p><code>False</code> keeps this route from flowing to children.</p> <code>True</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.get_serial","title":"get_serial  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>get_serial(\n    *,\n    device: str,\n    baud: int = 115200,\n    prompt: str | None = None,\n    login_prompt: str | None = None,\n    password_prompt: str | None = None,\n    username: str | None = None,\n    password: str | None = None,\n    login_timeout: float = 10.0,\n    prompt_timeout: float = 30.0,\n    bridge_command: str | None = None,\n    output_budget: int | None = None,\n    via: Shell | None = None,\n    inherit: bool = True,\n    propagate: bool = True\n) -&gt; Shell\n</code></pre> <p>Open a console as an auxiliary endpoint without changing this node's exec route.</p> <p>The arguments are those of <code>use_serial</code>. The port is framework-managed the same way: closed after the post-cleanup survey and on abort.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.get_http","title":"get_http  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>get_http(\n    *,\n    base_url: str,\n    headers: Mapping[str, str] | None = None,\n    token: str | None = None,\n    verify: bool = True,\n    follow_redirects: bool = False,\n    request_timeout: float = 20.0\n) -&gt; DeviceApi\n</code></pre> <p>Open an HTTP session. Does not change this node's exec route.</p> <p>Status on the response is data, including 4xx/5xx. Credentials in <code>token</code> never reach evidence. Abort closes the session; it does not invent a shutdown request.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>base_url</code> <code>str</code> <p>Origin, including <code>http</code> or <code>https</code>.</p> required <code>headers</code> <code>Mapping[str, str] | None</code> <p>Default headers for every request on this handle.</p> <code>None</code> <code>token</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Optional Bearer token. Named as a path in logs, never the value.</p> <code>None</code> <code>verify</code> <code>bool</code> <p>TLS verification. <code>False</code> is lab-only and logged as such.</p> <code>True</code> <code>follow_redirects</code> <code>bool</code> <p>Same-origin follow. Cross-host Location is data.</p> <code>False</code> <code>request_timeout</code> <code>float</code> <p>Positive finite seconds per attempt.</p> <code>20.0</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.get_coap","title":"get_coap  <code>async</code>","text":"<pre><code>get_coap(\n    *,\n    base_url: str,\n    headers: Mapping[str, str] | None = None,\n    request_timeout: float = 20.0\n) -&gt; DeviceApi\n</code></pre> <p>Open a CoAP session. Does not change this node's exec route.</p> <p>Scheme identity is <code>coap</code> (UDP CON/ACK). <code>coaps</code> / <code>coaps+tcp</code> are not this backend. Status is the CoAP code; 4.xx is data.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>base_url</code> <code>str</code> <p>Origin, including the <code>coap</code> scheme.</p> required <code>headers</code> <code>Mapping[str, str] | None</code> <p>Reserved for CoAP options on a later backend; unused here.</p> <code>None</code> <code>request_timeout</code> <code>float</code> <p>Positive finite seconds per attempt.</p> <code>20.0</code>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TestUtils.measure","title":"measure","text":"<pre><code>measure(\n    name: str,\n    value: int | float | None,\n    *,\n    unit: str | None = None,\n    limits: Limits | None = None\n) -&gt; Measurement\n</code></pre> <p>Record one parametric reading on this node, beside the verdict.</p> <p>A limit is evidence, never a verdict: the reading is recorded with <code>in_limits</code> decided and nothing about the node's state changes. When the window is a pass criterion, gate on the returned record in plain sight so the verdict's predicate is visible to the reader and to <code>hilt/gated-pass</code>::</p> <pre><code>rail = utils.measure(\"output_voltage_v\", volts, unit=\"V\", limits=(4.75, 5.25))\nif rail.in_limits:\n    return PASS({\"output_voltage_v\": volts})\nraise TestFailure(\"rail outside window\", \"4.75-5.25 V\", f\"{volts} V\")\n</code></pre> <p>A reading the suite could not take is <code>value=None</code>, recorded as not taken (issue #159). It is never inside its window, so the gate above fails the claim on a missing reading without a <code>try</code> around every read over a shell; without a window it is a trend entry with a hole, and the verdict stays where the claim put it.</p> <p>Synchronous and free of I/O, so no cancellation can land between the reading and its record. Allowed from <code>configure()</code> and the test method only, the two slots that produce the node's record; the record is sealed when the node settles. <code>hilt.measure</code> is the same call resolved to the running node.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>name</code> <code>str</code> <p>Non-empty identity of the quantity, unique within this node. For the life of the suite it names the same quantity in the same unit; a consumer keys its series on it.</p> required <code>value</code> <code>int | float | None</code> <p>An <code>int</code> or finite <code>float</code>, or <code>None</code> when the reading could not be taken. A textual observation is evidence on the verdict, not a measurement; <code>NaN</code> is a bug in the suite, not a missing reading.</p> required <code>unit</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Free label such as <code>\"V\"</code> or <code>\"ms\"</code>, or <code>None</code> for a dimensionless count. Never validated, never converted.</p> <code>None</code> <code>limits</code> <code>Limits | None</code> <p>Inclusive <code>(low, high)</code> window with <code>None</code> on an open side, or <code>None</code> to record for trend with <code>in_limits</code> unset.</p> <code>None</code> <p>Returns:</p> Type Description <code>Measurement</code> <p>The frozen <code>Measurement</code> exactly as recorded.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>ContractViolation</code> <p>When an argument is refused, when the name was already recorded in this node, or when the call comes from outside <code>configure()</code> or the test method.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TimeoutEvidence","title":"TimeoutEvidence  <code>dataclass</code>","text":"<pre><code>TimeoutEvidence(\n    reason: Literal[\"timeout\"],\n    declared: TimeBudgetName,\n    budget_seconds: float,\n    elapsed_seconds: float,\n    clock: str,\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Structured evidence emitted when a time-budget clock cuts a subtree.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.TimeoutEvidence.to_json","title":"to_json","text":"<pre><code>to_json() -&gt; JsonObject\n</code></pre> <p>Return the stable Inspector representation.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Verdict","title":"Verdict","text":"<p>The value that means \"this node passed\", with or without evidence.</p> Contract <ul> <li>Exactly one bare instance exists per interpreter. <code>Verdict()</code> returns it;   copying, deep-copying, and unpickling all return it. It is the value bound to   <code>PASS</code>.</li> <li><code>PASS(evidence)</code> mints a verdict that carries a detached copy of <code>evidence</code>.   It is a distinct object, so identity with <code>PASS</code> is never the check: the   child boundary asks <code>isinstance(result, Verdict)</code>.</li> <li><code>evidence</code> is read-only. The boundary copies it into <code>run_data</code> after checking   every entry; a carrying verdict cannot be edited after it is returned.</li> <li><code>repr()</code> is the bare word <code>PASS</code>, or <code>PASS({...})</code> for a carrying verdict, so   logs and artifacts read as the FT expects rather than showing an address.</li> </ul> <p>The type parameter is the evidence shape. A method annotated <code>-&gt; Verdict[MyEvidence]</code> with a <code>TypedDict</code> has its <code>PASS({...})</code> payload checked against that shape by pyright; the bare <code>-&gt; Verdict</code> accepts any mapping.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.Verdict.evidence","title":"evidence  <code>property</code>","text":"<pre><code>evidence: Mapping[str, object] | None\n</code></pre> <p>The readings this verdict carries, or <code>None</code> for the bare <code>PASS</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.evidence","title":"evidence","text":"<p><code>@evidence</code>: the readings a test method promises to carry on <code>PASS</code>.</p> <p>A test method is a function from <code>utils.ctx</code> to a verdict. The decorator is the method's return signature: it names the keys a pass must carry, beside the code that keeps the promise. It annotates and registers nothing; <code>build_suite</code> still names and binds the method. The declaration is fixed at class definition and never computed at run time: pass criteria that vary by SKU or fixture are a leaf argument or a different leaf, decided before <code>build_suite</code> runs (issue #118).</p> <p>The child boundary holds <code>PASS</code> to the declaration: a declared key the payload does not carry is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the leaf and the key. Extra keys are allowed; the declaration is a floor. <code>TestFailure</code> is not held to it, since a failure may fire before every reading was taken. An undecorated method may still return <code>PASS(payload)</code>; the payload is recorded unchecked.</p> <p>Two spellings::</p> <pre><code>@evidence(\"output_voltage_v\")\nasync def _check_voltage(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict: ...\n\nclass VoltageEvidence(TypedDict):\n    output_voltage_v: float\n\n@evidence(VoltageEvidence)\nasync def _check_voltage(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict[VoltageEvidence]: ...\n</code></pre> <p>The <code>TypedDict</code> form declares its required keys at run time and lets pyright check the <code>PASS({...})</code> payload against the shape at write time.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.evidence.evidence","title":"evidence","text":"<pre><code>evidence(\n    *keys: str,\n) -&gt; Callable[[_Method[P, R]], _Method[P, R]]\n</code></pre><pre><code>evidence(\n    shape: type[E],\n) -&gt; Callable[\n    [_Method[P, Verdict[E]]], _Method[P, Verdict[E]]\n]\n</code></pre> <pre><code>evidence(\n    *keys: str | type[Mapping[str, object]]\n) -&gt; Callable[[Any], Any]\n</code></pre> <p>Declare the evidence keys a <code>PASS</code> from the decorated test method must carry.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>*keys</code> <code>str | type[Mapping[str, object]]</code> <p>String keys, or exactly one <code>TypedDict</code> class whose required keys are the declaration.</p> <code>()</code> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>TypeError</code> <p>The declaration is empty, mixes the two spellings, repeats a key, or names a class that is not a <code>TypedDict</code>.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.evidence.declared_evidence","title":"declared_evidence","text":"<pre><code>declared_evidence(fn: object) -&gt; tuple[str, ...] | None\n</code></pre> <p>The keys <code>fn</code> was decorated to carry on <code>PASS</code>, or <code>None</code> when undeclared.</p>"},{"location":"api.html#hilt.measure","title":"measure","text":"<pre><code>measure(\n    name: str,\n    value: int | float | None,\n    *,\n    unit: str | None = None,\n    limits: Limits | None = None\n) -&gt; Measurement\n</code></pre> <p>Record a measurement on the node whose <code>configure()</code> or test method is running.</p> <p>The package-root spelling of <code>utils.measure</code> (issue #85, Ty's condition): the same signature, the same rules, the same record. It exists so <code>from hilt import measure</code> reads naturally in a suite; <code>utils.measure</code> remains the documented home. Outside a running slot there is no node to record against, so the call is refused rather than recorded somewhere surprising.</p> <p>Parameters:</p> Name Type Description Default <code>name</code> <code>str</code> <p>Non-empty identity of the quantity, unique within the node.</p> required <code>value</code> <code>int | float | None</code> <p>An <code>int</code> or finite <code>float</code>, or <code>None</code> for a reading not taken.</p> required <code>unit</code> <code>str | None</code> <p>Free label such as <code>\"V\"</code> or <code>\"ms\"</code>, or <code>None</code> for a count.</p> <code>None</code> <code>limits</code> <code>Limits | None</code> <p>Inclusive <code>(low, high)</code> window, <code>None</code> on an open side, or <code>None</code>.</p> <code>None</code> <p>Returns:</p> Type Description <code>Measurement</code> <p>The frozen <code>Measurement</code>, with <code>in_limits</code> already decided.</p> <p>Raises:</p> Type Description <code>MeasurementUnbound</code> <p>When no node's <code>configure()</code> or test method is executing in the current task.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html","title":"Developer-Framework Contracts","text":"<p>The contracts below define the mutual obligations between the developer and the framework. These are non-negotiable invariants. Any API change must preserve every contract listed here.</p> <p>In the Python port, tier-1 enforcement of these contracts lives in the child-boundary wrapper (<code>src/hilt/lifecycle.py</code>, <code>ChildBoundary</code>): every call the framework makes into suite code passes through it, and per-slot policy (<code>SLOT_POLICIES</code>) is declared once and read nowhere else. Nothing that protects hardware or verdict integrity may live only in static analysis.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#lifecycle-contract","title":"Lifecycle Contract","text":"<p>Developer obligation: Implement <code>configure()</code>, <code>build_suite()</code>, and <code>cleanup()</code> with the correct intent. - <code>configure()</code> \u2014 put the test in a known state and return what the tests will read. Readiness gates and polling live here. The return is a plain dict (or a local <code>TypedDict</code> under pyright strict; the framework annotation is <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code>) whose entries become <code>utils.ctx</code> for test methods, hooks, <code>cleanup()</code>, and all descendant tests. Ctx does not change after that: a <code>[]</code> read of a key <code>configure()</code> did not provide, and any write once it has returned, is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the test and the key, not a hardware Fail. <code>.get()</code> and <code>in</code> read an optional key (issues #112, #118). - <code>build_suite()</code> \u2014 pure table-of-contents composition. Return a <code>TestComponent</code> built from <code>self.test()</code> / <code>self.sequence()</code> / <code>self.parallel()</code>. The composition it returns may be written without a name, <code>self.sequence([...])</code> or <code>self.parallel([...])</code>, and then takes this component's name; a nameless composition nested inside another one is a <code>ContractViolation</code> raised where it is nested (issue #165). Test methods (private class methods passed to <code>self.test()</code>) carry the actual verdict logic \u2014 return <code>PASS</code>, or <code>PASS(evidence)</code> with the readings, to pass; raise <code>TestFailure</code> (optionally with <code>evidence=</code>) to fail intentionally. - <code>cleanup()</code> \u2014 restore state. Must not throw (errors are swallowed and logged). It reads ctx and does not write it; a value it must restore was captured in <code>configure()</code>.</p> <p>Framework obligation: Execute configure \u2192 build_suite \u2192 cleanup in order, guaranteed. - If <code>configure()</code> raises a <code>TestFailure</code>: cleanup runs, test methods never run, the test lands in <code>Fail</code>. The verdict means \"the device did not reach a testable state\" \u2014 whether the underlying cause is hardware or infrastructure is not knowable from code and is the human triager's call. - If <code>configure()</code> raises anything else: wrapped as a <code>ContractViolation</code> (\"CONTRACT VIOLATION IN : ... escaped the node\", recorded as Fail with <code>bug: true</code>) \u2014 configure must signal verdicts via <code>TestFailure</code>. - If a test method raises a plain exception: wrapped as a <code>ContractViolation</code> the same way \u2014 a developer contract violation, not a hardware failure. - An exception that is not a verdict is logged with its traceback (issue #167). The banner names the exception's type and message; the traceback follows it in the same node's log at <code>error</code>, one entry per line, and names the file and line that raised. It starts at the author's code: the framework's frames between the child boundary and the author's function are left out, unless every frame is the framework's. A chained exception keeps its chain. The same holds where the exception is swallowed, under the <code>cleanup failed (verdict unaffected)</code> or <code>survey failed (verdict unaffected)</code> line, and under the halt line of a <code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> hook that threw. A <code>TestFailure</code> is a verdict and has no traceback. <code>result.json</code> does not carry the traceback; its <code>failure.cause</code> holds the type and message as before. - A cancellation already delivered to suite code outranks any return value or different   exception produced after a pathological catch. The child boundary restores the   cancellation before classifying that settlement, so an aborted run cannot forge a   hardware or bug verdict. - <code>cleanup()</code> always runs regardless of any prior outcome \u2014 after configure failure and after abort. Framework finalization shields it from caller cancellation within its own bounded timeout; repeated cancellation cannot abandon that wait, and the original cancellation is re-raised after cleanup settles or exhausts the evidence budget. - The evidence budget is 30 seconds, granted per node, not per run. Each node's cleanup draws its own allowance, so a deep tree tearing down after an abort can spend up to N \u00d7 30 seconds before the abort reaches the operator \u2014 plan teardown depth with that ceiling in mind. A cleanup that exhausts its allowance is a <code>ContractViolation</code> charged to that node. - Lifecycle execution order is enforced at runtime by the child boundary \u2014 the type system is not the enforcer."},{"location":"contracts.html#terminal-state-contract","title":"Terminal State Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Every run of a <code>TestComponent</code> resolves to exactly one of three peer terminal states. The states are distinct categories with distinct recovery protocols \u2014 the framework does not collapse them. There is no hidden fourth: a call that settles without producing a terminal state (a successful <code>configure</code>, a completed <code>cleanup</code>) reports no state at all rather than inventing one.</p> <ol> <li>Pass \u2014 a test method returned a <code>Verdict</code>: the bare <code>PASS</code>, or <code>PASS(evidence)</code> carrying its readings. The check is <code>isinstance(result, Verdict)</code>; a <code>Verdict</code> is obtained only by importing <code>PASS</code> or calling it, so nothing a suite constructs can forge one. A pass without its declared evidence is not a pass: when the method carries <code>@evidence(...)</code>, a <code>PASS</code> that does not carry every declared key is a <code>ContractViolation</code> (developer-bug Fail), never a green run (issue #118).</li> <li>Fail \u2014 a test method raised a <code>TestFailure</code> (or subclass): the DUT produced a verdict and the verdict was negative. OR the developer didn't model a failure mode: a plain exception \u2192 <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (<code>bug: true</code>).</li> <li>Aborted \u2014 no verdict was produced. Peer to Pass/Fail, NOT a flavor of failure. Three causes:</li> <li>Harness-level (integration): a <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> integration hook threw; the error is wrapped as <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> and the run halts before a verdict could be produced.</li> <li>Harness-level (operator): SIGINT or SIGTERM arrived while a run was live; the failure is <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> so the Floor Technician reads that the operator stopped the rack. The same run-abort path fires as a hook throw: Aborted everywhere, transports hard-closed, <code>RunResult</code> returned.</li> <li>Node-local: a test method returned anything other than the <code>PASS</code> sentinel \u2014 <code>None</code> fall-through or an explicit wrong value (<code>MissingVerdict</code>). No verdict exists, so the framework refuses to forge Fail (or Pass) on its behalf. Siblings are unaffected. (Deliberate deviation from the TypeScript original, which folded this into the contract-violation/Fail path.)</li> </ol> <p>Developer obligation: Recognize the taxonomy. A <code>TestFailure</code> means \"the DUT failed a check\"; an <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> means \"the harness around the test broke\"; an <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> means \"the operator stopped the rack.\" Never conflate them \u2014 the Floor Technician's recovery protocol depends on knowing which. A <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> raised from suite code is not an operator signal: the child boundary still passes it through unrecorded.</p> <p>Framework obligation: When a descendant's hook throws, the <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> propagates up through every ancestor class-intact. Sequences and parallel nodes do not rewrap it. Every ancestor's state cascades to Aborted.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#time-budget-contract","title":"Time Budget Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Any node may declare a time budget by naming the terminal state it produces on expiry: <code>fail_after=</code> (seconds) or <code>abort_after=</code> (seconds). Exactly one name, or neither. Both on one node is a <code>ContractViolation</code> at composition time. A node with neither has no deadline of its own and is bounded only by its ancestors. Durations must be positive, finite numbers; booleans, zero, negatives, infinities, NaN, and non-numbers are rejected before execution.</p> <ul> <li>The pair is part of the <code>TestComponent</code> fractal atom. It is accepted by authored-node   construction, <code>self.test()</code>, <code>self.parallel()</code>, <code>self.sequence()</code>, and root <code>run()</code>.   Every node is a suite, so every placement reads the same way: this suite completes   within the budget or the named state is raised. Construction and <code>run()</code> are two   ways to declare the root's one budget, not a way to attach two clocks to one node.</li> <li>The window opens when the node enters <code>configure</code> and closes when its test method, or   its last child, settles. Leaves, parallel nodes, and sequences open the equivalent   window at their test/child boundary. Cleanup runs after the cut on its own   finalization budget. Root-owned <code>before_each</code> and <code>after_each</code> integration hooks are   outside every declared node window; time spent in them cannot spend an ancestor's   hardware-verdict budget.</li> <li>A budget bounds the whole subtree. Every declared clock remains active while its   subtree does test work. Descendants inherit all ancestor bounds and may only tighten   them. The clock that fires first decides the state; a descendant's longer declaration   can never loosen an ancestor's remaining time. A <code>wait_for</code> timeout longer than the   effective enclosing budget never fires\u2014the budget wins. Concurrent descendants are   separate active lineages: one sibling entering an excluded hook or cleanup cannot   pause an ancestor clock while another sibling is still doing timed work. The clock   stalls only while no descendant lineage is inside its timed window.</li> <li>Expiry settles every descendant still running with the winning clock's declared   state. <code>fail_after</code> renders Fail with a structured <code>TestFailure</code> naming the budget.   <code>abort_after</code> renders node-local Aborted without forging a failure verdict. Descendants   already settled retain their verdicts, and a composite follows the ordinary terminal   aggregation rule: any Aborted outranks Fail.</li> <li>Deadline Aborted is node-local. It never fires the shared run halt and never cascades   beyond the bounded subtree. Siblings outside that subtree are untouched under the   Group Isolation Contract. Expiry is never classified as a BUG.</li> <li>Expiry cancels the coroutine and hard-cancels at the resource every transport the cut   node itself opened. An inherited route is never in that owned set and is not touched;   it remains the parent's, available to the parent and to every sibling behind it.</li> <li>A parent whose <code>configure()</code> opens the hop owns its own configure window. If that   window expires, the Fail or Aborted state and its timeout evidence are the parent's,   and the children it never composed render as the Lifecycle Contract says for a parent   that never reached a testable state.</li> <li>Every cut node carries structured <code>timeout</code> evidence with <code>reason=\"timeout\"</code>, the   declared name, the declared budget in seconds, active elapsed seconds, and the   root-to-node path of the clock that fired. This evidence appears on <code>RunResult</code>, in   every affected <code>summary_tree</code> node, and in their JSON forms.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Choose the name for what \u201ctook too long\u201d means at that step. <code>fail_after</code> says the hardware did not do its job in time; <code>abort_after</code> says the harness gave up and no verdict exists. Never swallow <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code>; doing so would obstruct every deadline declared above the await.</p> <p>Consequence: Fail at a deadline sends the Floor Technician to the hardware. Aborted at a deadline sends them to the harness. The composition names that distinction, and the record identifies the exact clock and budget without requiring the suite source.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#failure-taxonomy-contract","title":"Failure Taxonomy Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Every failure must be traceable to one of the following root causes, made obvious by signal: 1. DUT failed a check \u2014 <code>TestFailure</code> from a test method or from <code>configure()</code>. State = Fail. A <code>TestFailure</code> from <code>configure()</code> carries an extra ambiguity: the device did not reach a testable state, but whether the cause is hardware or infrastructure cannot be determined from code. The framework surfaces the <code>looking_for</code> / failure message; the human triager decides. 2. Hardware lying \u2014 survey artifacts expose mismatch between claimed and actual state. 3. Test method bug \u2014 a plain exception from a test method or from <code>configure()</code> \u2192 all failure modes modeled by the developer must raise <code>TestFailure</code>. Anything else raised is a bug. Full stop. A missing verdict (non-PASS return) is likewise a contract violation, rendered as node-local Aborted because no verdict exists to report. 4. External integration failure \u2014 <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> from a <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> hook. State = Aborted. 5. Operator interrupt \u2014 <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> from SIGINT/SIGTERM during a live <code>run()</code>. State = Aborted. The failure names the operator and the signal.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Use the right signal for the right category. - Raise <code>TestFailure</code> for any verdict \u2014 from a test method or from <code>configure()</code> \u2014 never a plain exception. - Use survey hooks to capture device-reported state at phase boundaries. - Use <code>configure()</code> for environment/readiness gating. A timeout there is a <code>TestFailure</code> whose cause (hardware vs. infrastructure) is for the human to determine \u2014 the framework will not pretend to know. - Let the framework raise <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> \u2014 developers do not construct or raise it directly. It is raised automatically when a <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> hook throws anything.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#evidence-contract","title":"Evidence Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Every run produces scoped, structured evidence automatically. - Logging is scoped to the current leaf test. - Survey and audit hooks share the same automatic artifact contract: every non-<code>None</code>   return is faithfully recorded as an Artifact, including empty dicts, lists, and   strings. Python resolves an explicit <code>return None</code>, a delegated call that returns   <code>None</code>, and a fall-through to the same runtime value, so <code>None</code> writes only an   informational log entry and no automatic artifact. When <code>None</code> itself is meaningful   evidence, the SE must make that intent explicit with   <code>await utils.capture_artifact(Artifact(..., data=None))</code> inside the hook.   - Once a hook returns a non-<code>None</code> observation, its automatic artifact publication is     shielded from shutdown within its own 30-second evidence budget. Repeated     cancellation cannot orphan an accepted observation after the hardware was read.   - Survey dispatch points are the six members of <code>Phase</code>, in order: <code>pre-configure</code>,     <code>post-configure</code>, <code>pre-test</code>, <code>post-test</code>, <code>pre-cleanup</code>, and <code>post-cleanup</code>,     limited to the phases the lifecycle reaches. The artifact name identifies the     dispatch point and registered-hook index; audit names identify both the hook and     interval dispatch. Both use artifact type <code>survey</code>, keeping one Inspector evidence     contract.   - <code>use_survey(fn, phases=...)</code> schedules a survey at a subset of those points; the     default is all six, and an empty selection is a <code>ValueError</code> at registration. A     boundary the survey is not scheduled at writes one debug line and no artifact, so     the run directory holds one artifact per boundary the survey was meant to observe.   - Order is not reach. The phases are totally ordered, but after a failed <code>configure</code>     the engine skips <code>post-configure</code>, <code>pre-test</code>, and <code>post-test</code> and still fires     <code>pre-cleanup</code> and <code>post-cleanup</code>, with <code>utils.exec</code> on the test host if <code>use_shell</code>     never returned. A survey that reads the device therefore guards on     <code>utils.exec_route</code>, not on the phase; the phase says when, the route says where. - Log artifacts are co-located with their node. - Evidence travels with the verdict (issue #118). A leaf's readings leave its test method as <code>PASS(evidence)</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence=...)</code>. The child boundary validates the payload entry by entry before it is recorded: a value that is not JSON, a key that shadows a ctx entry or the framework-owned <code>measurements</code> slot, or a key <code>@evidence</code> declared that <code>PASS</code> did not carry is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the leaf and the key. Nothing is stripped silently. A failure is not held to the declaration; it records what it was handed. - A leaf's <code>RunResult.run_data</code> is the JSON-safe view of its ctx plus the evidence its verdict carried, flat. A non-leaf node's <code>run_data</code> is the ctx view alone. Every <code>summary_tree</code> node carries <code>evidence</code> (what the leaf's verdict carried, or null) and <code>evidence_declared</code> (the <code>@evidence</code> declaration, or null); a declared key absent from <code>evidence</code> is a reading that was not taken, shown by name. - All serializable values (primitives, plain dicts, lists) <code>configure()</code> returned appear in <code>run_data</code>, except under a key the suite's <code>redact_ctx</code> claims (issue #154): that key appears with the value <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code>, whatever it holds. The claims read the real value from <code>utils.ctx</code>; the record does not carry it.   - Functions, class instances, and live handles (e.g. SSH connections) are stripped from the ctx view; they are the objects a run works through, not evidence.   - Context annotations do not bypass this filter. A <code>TypedDict</code> is an ordinary dict at runtime, and each value still has to satisfy the same serialization rules.   - Filtering is all-or-nothing for each top-level ctx entry. A nested instance, handle,     function, cycle, non-string mapping key, or non-finite float strips that complete     entry; the framework never emits partial evidence or silently coerces it.   - <code>run_data</code> is a detached snapshot taken when the node settles. An aborted leaf     contributes no evidence; readings in hand when a time budget fires are lost by     design, and the scoped log and point-in-time artifacts remain for in-flight     observation.   - <code>run_data[\"measurements\"]</code> is framework-owned: the readings <code>utils.measure</code>     recorded on this node, in call order, written when the node settles. An authored     ctx entry with that name cannot populate or replace the slot, and a <code>run_data</code>     override cannot drop it. Aggregation, SPC, and Cpk are the consumer's, not the     record's. - Measurement Contract (issue #86, <code>docs/measurements.md</code>). A limit is evidence,   never a verdict: <code>utils.measure</code> records <code>in_limits</code> and touches neither the node's   state nor its failure. A <code>None</code> value is admitted as a reading not taken (issue   #159): recorded with <code>value: null</code>, never inside its window, so a claim gated on the   window fails through its own gate. The framework refuses, as a <code>ContractViolation</code>   naming the node and the measurement: an empty name, a <code>bool</code>, <code>NaN</code>, or infinite   value, a non-text unit, or a window that bounds nothing or runs backwards; the same name   recorded twice in one node; a call from outside <code>configure()</code> or the test method   (<code>cleanup</code>, surveys, audits, hooks, or after the node settled); and <code>hilt.measure</code>   where no node's slot is executing. The record is sealed on every terminal state,   Aborted included, and published on the node's <code>run_data</code> and on every <code>summary_tree</code>   node (<code>null</code> when the node never ran). A measurement does not satisfy an <code>@evidence</code>   declaration. - Every <code>RunResult</code> includes <code>ancestor_names: list[str]</code> (path from root) and <code>bread_crumbs: str</code> (joined for display), plus a <code>summary_tree</code> snapshot of the node's subtree state at completion. Downstream integrators can reconstruct the full Inspector tree from the root's RunResult alone. - <code>TestFailure</code>, <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, and <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> all implement <code>to_json()</code>. Serializing a <code>RunResult</code> produces clean JSON for downstream integrators without any custom serializer. - Provenance. Every executed node records one <code>exec routing: &lt;where&gt; (&lt;why&gt;)</code> line \u2014 <code>where</code> is <code>local</code> or <code>via &lt;chain&gt;</code>, <code>why</code> names the call that put it there (<code>declared via use_shell</code>, <code>inherited from parent</code>, <code>declared via use_shell(inherit=False)</code>). A node that never declared a route renders <code>where</code> alone. The line is emitted after <code>configure</code> returns, since <code>use_shell</code> lives there and the route is not final until it does. - Every transport the router opens records its provenance lines as it comes up. SSH renders <code>ssh connect: &lt;user&gt;@&lt;host&gt;:&lt;port&gt; auth=&lt;label&gt; via=&lt;chain&gt;</code>; <code>via=direct</code> means the connection dials out from the test host itself. When host-key verification is skipped, SSH records a second line, <code>ssh host-key verification skipped: &lt;host&gt;:&lt;port&gt;</code>, so the Floor Technician can see that verification was off without opening the suite. - Via chains are rendered in full and test-host-first (<code>bastion -&gt; edge -&gt; dut</code>), never summarized to the last hop. - The credential path is named exactly \u2014 <code>key-file(/path)</code>, <code>agent(/socket)</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>none</code> \u2014 and the credential's value never appears. What is logged is what was dialed: the resolution order is declared once, in <code>SshInterface._auth_choice</code>, and both the connect options and the provenance line read it. A password, private-key bytes, and the contents of a key file never reach the scoped log, <code>run_data</code>, the env snapshot, <code>RunResult.to_json()</code>, or a failure repr. - A suite's own secret is held to the same standard (issue #154). <code>redact_ctx</code> on the suite class names the ctx keys that are not for the record, exactly or as regular expressions matched against the whole key; a subclass adds to its bases' entries and never replaces them. A string under a redacted key is masked as <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code> wherever it appears, run-wide: every scoped-log record, including one the author writes by hand; a failure's <code>str()</code>, <code>repr()</code>, and <code>to_json()</code>, down its cause chain, once the child boundary has recorded it; evidence on a verdict; captured artifacts; <code>run_data</code>; and therefore <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> and every file in the run directory. Only a string is a needle; a number or a handle under a redacted key is shown as <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code> in the ctx view and nothing more. The env snapshot stays verbatim (issue #53): a secret that is also in the environment is kept off the record with <code>env_deny</code>. Masking begins when the value enters ctx, by any write path (<code>configure()</code>'s return, <code>[]=</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>setdefault</code>, <code>|=</code>), so a value logged inside <code>configure()</code> before it enters ctx is the author's to keep quiet. Masking is plain substring replacement, so a very short secret (a two-character token, a numeric sid such as <code>200</code>) also masks unrelated text that happens to contain it; that fails closed and is accepted. - Consequence: the Floor Technician reconstructs the run's topology and its auth decisions from the artifacts alone, without opening the suite source.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Instrument appropriately. - Use <code>utils.logger</code> (never <code>print</code>) so evidence is captured in artifacts. - Use <code>self.use_survey()</code> for periodic state snapshots; use   <code>await utils.capture_artifact(artifact)</code> for point-in-time captures. <code>Artifact</code> is a   write-free value on the public surface: the utility method is the only framework API   that persists it, and it always emits the chronological pointer after publication. - Return structured data from <code>use_survey</code> / <code>use_audit</code> hooks to capture it as an artifact. - Hand readings back with the verdict: <code>PASS({...})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={...})</code>. Declare with <code>@evidence(...)</code> what a pass must carry, beside the method that keeps the promise.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#temporal-backbone-contract","title":"Temporal Backbone Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Every full <code>run()</code> creates one timestamped run directory beneath the configured artifact root before the root lifecycle begins. Point-in-time artifacts and every per-node timeline live beneath that directory, so a run's evidence is self-contained and cannot overwrite a neighboring run.</p> <ul> <li>Every executed node that owns a discrete log streams the same records into two files:   <code>timeline.log</code>, the human line view, and <code>timeline.jsonl</code>, the versioned Inspector   record. Both files are flushed after every entry; an interrupted process therefore   preserves every complete entry already accepted by the logger.</li> <li>The JSON Lines record carries one complete object per physical line with   <code>format_version</code>, UTC <code>timestamp</code>, root-to-leaf <code>scope</code>, <code>level</code>, and <code>message</code>.   Artifact-pointer records also carry <code>artifact_name</code>, <code>artifact_path</code>,   <code>artifact_type</code>, and <code>artifact_timestamp</code>.</li> <li>A logging node opens its files lazily on its first record and closes them when that   node reaches its terminal boundary. A declared node that never executes opens   nothing.</li> <li>Authored suites, sequences, and parallel nodes own a log by default. Leaves roll up   to the nearest owning ancestor. Sequences, parallel nodes, and composed suites may opt   into the same roll-up with <code>discrete_log_file=False</code>; a root still owns the run log.</li> <li><code>TestComponent.log_path</code> exposes the owning node's absolute <code>timeline.jsonl</code> path after   execution. It remains <code>None</code> for pending nodes and nodes whose records roll up.</li> <li>Every <code>summary_tree</code> node carries the same link as <code>log_path</code>, rendered as a POSIX   path relative to the run directory, so the structural record reaches the temporal   record without the live objects. It is <code>null</code> for pending nodes and nodes whose   records roll up; the nearest ancestor with a link holds their chronology.</li> <li>On Pass, Fail, and Aborted, root <code>run()</code> atomically publishes the root   <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> as <code>result.json</code> at the run-directory root before returning.   The root timeline's final entry points to that file; a failed publication cannot   expose a partial replacement or a pointer to evidence that was not published.</li> </ul> <p>The persisted chronology and root result are framework-owned. Developer code continues to use only <code>utils.logger</code> and <code>utils.capture_artifact()</code>; it never opens or edits timeline or result files.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Do not write directly into the run directory or depend on its timestamped directory name. Treat <code>log_path</code> and captured artifact paths as opaque evidence references.</p> <p>The private <code>_run_lifecycle()</code> entry point is not a full run and therefore does not create a timestamped run directory. Framework self-tests that invoke it directly may leave point-in-time artifacts at the configured base root; authored suites use <code>run()</code>.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#audit-contract","title":"Audit Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: When <code>self.use_audit(interval=..., fn=...)</code> is called in the constructor, the framework runs <code>fn</code> concurrently with the test methods on the specified interval. - Multiple audit hooks are supported \u2014 each runs on its own interval. - Every audit interval is validated during construction as positive and finite. An   invalid registration cannot survive long enough to touch hardware. - Audit start/stop is framework-controlled, not caller-controlled: the loop begins immediately before the first test method runs and ends immediately after the last test method returns. No delayed start, no manual trigger. The lifecycle contract (test in runnable state before <code>configure()</code> returns) guarantees the audit's preconditions are met by start. - If the audit <code>fn</code> raises a <code>TestFailure</code>, the running test method is immediately cancelled and the test fails with the audit's failure. - Audit errors are not swallowed \u2014 unlike survey hooks, a throwing audit is a real failure. Survey and cleanup errors are swallowed to protect the hardware verdict; <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> is exempt from swallowing everywhere \u2014 swallowing it would kill the halt signal. - Audit follows the same artifact contract as survey (see Evidence Contract above). - Cancellation is cooperative and asyncio-native. The run-level abort (<code>RunAbort</code> in <code>lifecycle.py</code>) fires when an integration hook aborts the run; a throwing audit cancels the in-flight test method; test-method completion stops the audit loop. Suite code must let <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code> propagate \u2014 swallowing it breaks the wind-down. - The read-only <code>utils.signal</code> combines the run-level integration halt with the nearest   authored audit scope. Audit scope is inherited only by that node's composed suite: an   audit failure cancels its own in-flight work and produces Fail without cancelling an   independent parallel sibling. If an audit verdict and suite completion occur in the same   event-loop turn, the audit verdict wins; evidence is not discarded by timing luck. - Every started descendant cancelled by an audit still seals its result and dispatches   its paired <code>after_each</code>. An unsettled descendant carries the audit's <code>TestFailure</code>   class-intact; a descendant already dispatching <code>after_each</code> retains the verdict that   hook received. Audit cancellation never becomes a run-wide integration halt. - <code>utils.exec</code> threads cancellation to the underlying child process. When a cancellation fires, in-flight shell commands are killed (SIGTERM) immediately \u2014 no zombie processes outlive the test.</p> <p>Developer obligation: - Call <code>self.use_audit()</code> in the constructor with both <code>interval</code> and <code>fn</code> \u2014 both are required parameters. - Yield to the event loop (<code>await</code>) inside any long-running loop in a test method so cancellation can be delivered, and never suppress <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code>. - Use <code>use_audit</code> for active monitoring that can fail the test (e.g. thermal runaway, watchdog). Use <code>use_survey</code> for passive state snapshots at phase boundaries that never affect the verdict. - Raise <code>TestFailure</code> from the audit <code>fn</code> to fail the test. Return data to continue \u2014 it is always recorded as evidence.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#integration-hook-contract","title":"Integration Hook Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>before_each</code> and <code>after_each</code> are optional integration hooks registered at the root <code>run()</code> call. The framework threads them down to every executed descendant and dispatches them around every node's lifecycle.</p> <ul> <li><code>before_each</code> fires immediately before a node's lifecycle begins.</li> <li><code>after_each</code> fires immediately after a node's lifecycle completes, receiving the node's <code>RunResult</code>.</li> <li>Pair invariant: <code>after_each</code> fires EVEN IF <code>before_each</code> threw. An integrator writing a \"started\" row in <code>before_each</code> always gets a \"finished\" row in <code>after_each</code> \u2014 no orphan records in the external system.</li> <li>Throw from either hook \u2192 framework wraps as <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> \u2192 the shared run-abort fires \u2192 every in-flight framework\u2192user await across the tree unwinds at its own framework boundary \u2192 every in-flight transport is closed via <code>cancel_in_flight()</code> \u2192 enclosing node transitions to Aborted. The integrator's own error class arrives intact as the failure's cause. An operator SIGINT/SIGTERM during a live run takes this same halt path, with <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> as the reason.</li> <li>Hooks receive a deliberately narrow context (<code>BeforeEachContext</code> / <code>AfterEachContext</code>): node name, ancestor chain, breadcrumbs, scoped logger, env snapshot. NOT <code>exec</code>, <code>get_shell</code>, or <code>capture_artifact</code>. Integration hooks are for external coordination, not for exercising the hardware.</li> <li>Unpaired registration (only <code>before_each</code> or only <code>after_each</code>) emits a loud warning at run start unless <code>allow_unpaired_hooks=True</code> is passed. The pairing enforces the started/finished symmetry.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Do not register <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> inside your <code>TestComponent</code> subclasses. These hooks are the integrator's contract with the outside world, not part of the test's own lifecycle. Use <code>use_survey</code> / <code>use_audit</code> for per-test observation; use <code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> only when composing HILT into a larger harness.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#in-flight-termination-contract","title":"In-Flight Termination Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: When the run-level abort fires (an integration hook throws anywhere in the tree, or the operator sends SIGINT/SIGTERM to a live run), every framework-managed transport is closed HARD immediately \u2014 not at the next polling boundary. Cancelling the coroutine is not enough; the framework cancels the RESOURCE.</p> <p>The operator-signal handler is installed by <code>run()</code>/<code>main()</code> only while that run is live, on the running loop, and removed on the way out so a library caller never leaks it. The first signal fires <code>RunAbort</code> with an <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> and hard-closes transports; a second signal inside the finalization window exits the process immediately.</p> <ul> <li><code>SshInterface</code> destroys its underlying asyncssh connection, closing every open channel and failing every pending <code>exec</code> stream at the socket level.</li> <li>Recursively propagates through <code>via</code> chains \u2014 a jump-host tunnel and everything tunneled through it collapse together.</li> <li><code>RemoteInterface</code> subclasses expose <code>cancel_in_flight()</code> for any transport type that needs aggressive close semantics.</li> <li>Long-running user operations (firmware flashes, long-polling requests, multi-step SSH scripts) in flight when the abort fires receive a dead resource, causing their own awaitables to fail and settle harmlessly in the event loop.</li> </ul> <p>Consequence: A 30-minute firmware flash in progress when <code>before_each</code> throws does NOT continue to mutate the hardware. This is load-bearing for manufacturing test integrity \u2014 the halt signal means \"stop touching the hardware,\" and the framework enforces that at the transport layer.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#group-isolation-contract","title":"Group Isolation Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>TestGroup</code> runs its children concurrently under <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code>, and the child boundary guarantees the TaskGroup semantics are correct: Fail and node-local Aborted are returned by the boundary, never raised \u2014 one child's failure cannot escape into the TaskGroup and cancel healthy siblings. Only a harness-level halt propagates, and the TaskGroup then cancelling everything is exactly right, because that is what Aborted means.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#utils-contract","title":"Utils Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>TestUtils</code> is the single, fully-scoped utility surface passed to every lifecycle method.</p> <ul> <li><code>utils.exec</code> never throws. It returns an <code>ExecResult(stdout, stderr, exit_code, truncated, captured_bytes)</code> \u2014 a non-zero exit code is data for the test method to judge, not an exception. Signal-killed, aborted, and closed-transport commands settle as <code>exit_code=-1</code>. A <code>ConnectionError</code> from <code>RemoteInterface.exec</code> is absorbed here; the protocol stays loud, the author-facing surface stays total. <code>CancelledError</code> still propagates.</li> <li>Stdout and stderr together are bounded by one named <code>output_budget</code> (default 4 MiB), applied identically by the local executor and <code>SshInterface</code>. The budget is configured at the run seed (<code>TestComponent.run(output_budget=...)</code> / <code>TestUtils(output_budget=...)</code>) or at <code>LocalExec</code> / <code>SshInterface</code> construction \u2014 never as a process-global. Exceeding it terminates the command the same way abort does (SIGTERM the host process group; SIGTERM then close that SSH channel, session stays up) and settles with the bytes actually received, <code>truncated=True</code>, and <code>captured_bytes</code> equal to what was kept. Truncation is evidence, never an exception. The scoped log names it (<code>exec output truncated: captured N bytes (budget B)</code>). Streaming reads preserve interrupted output: decode happens only at settlement, with replacement for a cut in the middle of a UTF-8 sequence.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Use only <code>utils.*</code> inside lifecycle methods \u2014 never import framework internals directly into test logic.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#exec-routing-contract","title":"Exec Routing Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>use_shell</code> is the only writer of the node's exec router. <code>get_shell</code> may open extra endpoints and may read <code>inherit</code> to pick a tunnel; it never assigns the router or the propagate flag.</p> <ul> <li>Four <code>use_shell</code> shapes: connection (<code>host</code> + <code>username</code>), connection with explicit <code>via</code>, policy-only <code>inherit=False</code> (force local), and bare <code>use_shell()</code> (return the active handle).</li> <li>Explicit <code>via</code> always wins. <code>inherit=False</code> on a connection form skips the parent's hop.</li> <li>Declared in <code>configure()</code>, nowhere else. A declaring <code>use_shell</code> (<code>host</code>, <code>via</code>, <code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) is admitted only while this node's own <code>configure()</code> is running; from a test method, <code>cleanup</code>, a survey, an audit, or a helper they call, it raises <code>ContractViolation</code> before the router is touched. The <code>exec routing:</code> line is written when <code>configure</code> returns and the FT reads it as the truth for the node's whole run; a route moved later would make that line a lie for every command after the move. The bare <code>use_shell()</code> reads the active handle and may be called from any slot.</li> <li>A second declaration. <code>configure()</code> may declare more than once, since no command has yet run under the route being replaced: open a jump host, then route through it. The last declaration is the route; every shell opened on the way stays <code>opened</code> on this node and is not torn down early.</li> <li>Ownership. The node whose <code>configure()</code> opened a shell, through <code>use_shell</code> or <code>get_shell</code>, owns it. The framework closes it at that node's cleanup (after the post-cleanup survey, before the hop it tunnels through), never earlier and never from another node.</li> <li>Hooks and audits observe the route; they do not set it. They read <code>utils.exec_route</code> and exec through what <code>configure()</code> committed to. A survey runs at phases before <code>configure()</code> and after <code>cleanup()</code>, and an audit runs concurrently with the test method, so neither has a moment at which one route could be declared once. <code>get_shell</code> stays open to them: an auxiliary endpoint changes nothing the routing line states.</li> <li><code>propagate=False</code> on <code>use_shell</code> stops this node's router from flowing to children; they start local. <code>propagate</code> on <code>get_shell</code> is a documented no-op.</li> <li>The parent owns the route. A route is owned by the node whose <code>configure()</code> declared it and is inherited by every node that node composes, down the tree, until a descendant declares <code>inherit=False</code> (or the owner declared <code>propagate=False</code>). A device class never takes a routing argument: a gateway is a parent whose <code>configure()</code> opens the hop and whose <code>build_suite()</code> composes the devices behind it, and those devices are routed by being its children. The <code>TestComponent</code> constructor has no <code>via=</code>; the only <code>via</code> is the tunnel argument to <code>use_shell</code> / <code>get_shell</code>, declared inside <code>configure()</code> by the node that owns the route (issue #137).</li> <li>Children inherit the route through run params (<code>RouterRunParams</code>), never through ctx. ctx is instance-local domain data, not infrastructure.</li> <li> <p>Routed tunnels are borrowed; the router owns hop lifecycle. Every hop the router resolves \u2014 an explicit <code>via=</code> Shell or an inherited route \u2014 is already live, so the connector threads it through the transport's borrowed <code>tunnel=</code> channel, never the owned <code>via=</code> one. The opened interface must not disconnect, cancel, or collapse its hop. What a node opened is recorded on the node (<code>opened</code>), stays connected through the node's post-cleanup survey, and closes after that survey and before the hop it tunnels through \u2014 the parent's hop closes at the same point of the parent's own lifecycle, after every child is done with it (issue #115), so a survey that execs at post-cleanup reads the device, not a closed transport; a hard abort on the hop kills everything tunneled through it at the socket (In-Flight Termination Contract). A dead hop at open time is an error, not something the router silently brings up.</p> </li> <li> <p>Local exec and every SSH session this router opens share the run's <code>output_budget</code> (see the Utils Contract). A fat command is truncated and killed at the resource; it does not take the rest of the rack with it.</p> </li> <li>A connection carries a bounded number of sessions. Every <code>exec</code> over SSH opens its own session channel, and an sshd refuses a channel past its <code>MaxSessions</code> (10 by default); the refused command settles at <code>exit_code == -1</code> with <code>transport closed: open failed</code>. <code>use_shell(..., max_sessions=N)</code> and <code>get_shell(..., max_sessions=N)</code> bound the commands in flight on the connection the call opens: a command past the bound waits for a session to end and then runs, in call order, and nothing is refused. The bound is the hop owner's to declare, is shared by every node that inherits the route, and is stated on the <code>ssh connect:</code> line. The wait counts against the waiting leaf's time budget (Time Budget Contract). A connection tunnelled through the hop has its own bound, and its tunnel channel is not a session on the hop. The default, <code>None</code>, does not bound them. <code>max_sessions</code> without <code>host</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>, and a value below 1 raises <code>ValueError</code>.</li> <li>A transport's address is fixed while it is live. <code>connect(host, port)</code> re-points an addressed transport only through a closed handle; asking a live one for a different address is refused, never silently replaced. After a flash or DHCP churn the flow is <code>disconnect()</code>, reacquire, <code>connect(new_ip)</code>. There is no self-healing: a socket the framework killed stays dead (In-Flight Termination Contract), and an unexpected drop reaches the author as a <code>ConnectionError</code> on the next <code>exec</code>, not as a quiet redial.</li> <li><code>utils.exec_route</code> states where <code>utils.exec</code> lands at the moment it is read: <code>local</code> for the test host, otherwise the endpoint of the hop the route attaches to (the last name in the <code>exec routing:</code> chain). It is derived from the router, never stored, so it is right in a survey at every phase, including the pre-configure dispatch before <code>use_shell</code> has run and the pre-cleanup dispatch after a <code>configure</code> that failed before it.</li> <li>The router logs what it opens and where every node's exec lands (see the Evidence Contract). A transport that hides its hop hides the topology: <code>endpoint</code>, <code>hop</code>, and <code>route_label</code> on <code>RemoteInterface</code> are how a transport states its place in the chain, and <code>provenance_lines</code> (starting with <code>connect_provenance</code>) are the lines the router records for it.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare in <code>configure()</code>, and only there, where this node's <code>utils.exec</code> lands when it should be somewhere other than the test host; a test method, survey, or audit that needs a different endpoint reads <code>use_shell()</code> or opens one with <code>get_shell</code>. Do not stuff SSH handles into ctx and expect routing to notice.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#device-api-contract","title":"Device API Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> is a contract, not an <code>exec</code> subclass (HIL ruling 1). The guarantees live in the signed Device API design note; this page does not restate them.</p> <p><code>DeviceRequest(..., json=value)</code> is the JSON body convenience (issue #155): it serializes once to UTF-8 <code>payload</code> bytes and supplies <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> unless the request already declares a content type, matched case-insensitively. <code>json=None</code> sends JSON null; omitting <code>json</code> leaves the raw-payload API unchanged. Combining <code>json</code> with a non-<code>None</code> <code>payload</code>, even empty bytes, is a <code>ValueError</code> before any transfer. Non-serializable values and non-finite numbers are refused at construction. The request never mutates the caller's headers or retains its JSON object.</p> <p><code>DeviceResponse.json()</code> decodes the returned bytes. <code>parse(response_type=\"json\")</code> is the format-selecting form; <code>response_type=\"yaml\"</code> uses <code>yaml.safe_load</code> from the optional <code>open-hilt[yaml]</code> extra. Unknown formats raise <code>ValueError</code>, a missing YAML dependency raises <code>ImportError</code>, and parser errors propagate without a fallback. Parsing is explicit, independent of response status and headers; it preserves <code>body</code> and <code>status</code>, makes no request, and supplies no verdict. A malformed body remains a completed transfer for the suite to judge. Body contents never enter attempt logs. A CoAP <code>status</code> is the dotted code (<code>\"2.05\"</code>, a <code>CoapCode</code> constant). An HTTP <code>status</code> stays an integer (issue #156).</p> <p>Developer obligation: Implement <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> as that note specifies.</p>"},{"location":"contracts.html#composability-contract","title":"Composability Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Any <code>TestComponent</code> or subclass thereof can be composed indefinitely in any shape the SE deems appropriate.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Leaves must be self-contained \u2014 declare their own readiness preconditions in <code>configure()</code>, not in the parent sequence.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html","title":"Floor Technician guide \u2014 reading a result without reading source","text":"<p>This is the station page. You opened a run because something stopped the line. You do not need the suite source, a Slack thread, or a software engineer to categorize what happened. You need <code>result.json</code>, the scoped log next to it, and the recovery protocol below.</p> <p><code>hilt inspect</code> puts both records on one screen; the section below says how that screen is laid out. Without it you read the same two records the Inspector does: the structural tree in <code>result.json</code>, and the temporal log at each node's <code>log_path</code>. Every field below is the field in <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>, explained from this side of the bench.</p> <p>Clock rule. A station whose system clock is not on NTP cannot produce trustworthy evidence. Do not execute tests on it. The log is used as chronology in customer disputes.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#where-to-look","title":"Where to look","text":"<p>A completed run always writes a timestamped directory under <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code> (or <code>./artifacts</code> if that variable was not set). Inside that directory:</p> You want File The verdict and the tree <code>result.json</code> at the run-directory root What happened, in order, for a node that node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> (the Inspector record) and <code>timeline.log</code> (the same events as readable lines) A captured screenshot, dump, or similar the path in an artifact-pointer log line A CI-flavored projection of the same tree the JUnit XML the runner wrote with <code>--junit</code>, if anyone asked for one. It is not a second record. <p><code>result.json</code> is present for Pass, Fail, and Aborted. Absence of the file means the invocation never reached a run (usage error, exit 3) \u2014 there is no hardware story to read.</p> <p>The last entry in the root node's timeline points at <code>result.json</code> with artifact name <code>result</code> and type <code>run-result</code>. You do not have to guess the run directory name from the clock.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#sixty-second-recovery-protocol","title":"Sixty-second recovery protocol","text":"<p>Open <code>result.json</code>. Read <code>state</code> at the root, then <code>failure</code>. That pair is the category. Then follow the protocol. A human still makes the final call; the framework's job is to make the call obvious.</p> Root <code>state</code> What <code>failure</code> looks like Category What you do <code>Pass</code> <code>failure</code> is <code>null</code> The claim held Ship. You are done. <code>Fail</code> <code>name</code> is <code>TestFailure</code> (or a suite-authored subclass), <code>bug</code> absent or <code>false</code> Hardware. The unit did not meet a check the suite named. Pull the unit. Follow the station protocol for that failure name. The <code>fail_message</code> is written in your language; <code>expected</code> / <code>received</code> are the comparison when there is one. <code>Fail</code> <code>banner</code> is <code>CONTRACT VIOLATION</code>, or <code>bug</code> is <code>true</code> Author. The suite broke its contract. This is not the unit. Do not scrap the unit on this evidence. Escalate to the suite author. The message names the node and the fix. Where an exception escaped the suite's code, the lines under the banner in the node's log are its traceback: send them along, they name the file and line. <code>Fail</code> <code>banner</code> is <code>FRAMEWORK CONTRACT VIOLATION</code> Framework. Should be unreachable. Escalate to the framework maintainers with the full run directory. Not the unit, not the suite author. <code>Aborted</code> <code>name</code> is <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> Integration / harness. The shop-floor DB, the coordinator, the hook around the suite. Fix the harness. Re-run. The unit was not judged. <code>Aborted</code> <code>name</code> is <code>MissingVerdict</code>, or <code>bug</code> is <code>true</code> with <code>state</code> <code>Aborted</code> Author. The test method returned nothing the framework will accept as a verdict. Escalate to the suite author. Do not treat this as a hardware Fail. <code>Aborted</code> <code>name</code> is <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>, <code>operator</code> is <code>true</code> Operator. Someone sent SIGINT or SIGTERM. The rack was stopped on purpose. Not a Fail, not a harness outage. Re-run when the line is ready. <code>signal</code> names which one. <code>Aborted</code> <code>timeout.declared</code> is <code>abort_after</code> Budget. The node's abort clock fired. The framework refused to forge a Fail. Read <code>timeout.clock</code> for which node declared the budget, then decide with the log whether the unit hung or the budget was tight. <p>A <code>TestFailure</code> raised from <code>configure()</code> means the device did not reach a testable state. Hardware vs infrastructure is not knowable from the file. You decide that, using the message, the survey artifacts, and what you can see on the bench.</p> <p>Do not collapse Fail and Aborted. A Fail says check the hardware. An Aborted says no verdict was produced \u2014 check the harness, the author, or the operator, not the unit, unless you have other evidence.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#how-to-find-the-node-that-matters","title":"How to find the node that matters","text":"<p>The root object is one node. The tree of everything that ran is <code>summary_tree</code>. Walk <code>children</code> in declaration order.</p> <p>Use <code>ancestor_names</code> as the node's identity. It is the root-to-this path as an array of names. Names may contain <code>/</code>, so the array is the durable form.</p> <p>Use <code>bread_crumbs</code> only to display that path. It is exactly <code>ancestor_names</code> joined with <code>/</code>. Do not parse it back into names.</p> <p>A component is two nodes: itself (<code>type</code> <code>suite</code>) and, as its only child, the sequence or parallel it built, which usually carries the same name. So a path often reads <code>rack</code>, <code>rack</code>, <code>sata</code>, <code>sata</code>, <code>present</code>. That is the record, not a mistake. The component's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> holds its configure and cleanup; its child's holds the tests. The Inspector draws such a pair as one row and shows both timelines as one log, merged in time order (<code>docs/inspector.md</code>, The fold).</p> <p>A node whose <code>summary_tree</code> <code>state</code> is <code>null</code> never ran (a sequence stopped before it). That is not a fourth terminal state. It has no log and no failure. The declared tree is still complete, so you can see what was skipped.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#the-same-records-in-the-inspector","title":"The same records in the Inspector","text":"<p><code>hilt inspect</code> opens the latest run with the tree above and the highlighted node's log below. Every key is in the Inspector guide. Three things about the screen are worth knowing before you need them:</p> <ul> <li>The log has the bottom quarter of the screen, and never fewer than six   lines. Press <code>l</code> to give it the whole screen, and <code>l</code> again to get the tree   back. Your node, your errors-only filter, and your place in the log are where   you left them. The arrows still move through the tree while it is hidden; the   first line of the log names the node you are on.</li> <li>Parents stay in view. When a deep tree scrolls, the rows above the first   one shown stay pinned at the top, above a rule, so you can always see which   rack, device, and step the highlighted check belongs to. On a short screen   only the nearest parents are pinned; the first line of the log has the full   path.</li> <li><code>Shift-\u2191</code> and <code>Shift-\u2193</code> move between the rows under one parent. From one   device they go to the next device, without stepping through its checks. They   stop at the parent's first and last row and never leave it. <code>Alt-\u2191</code> and <code>Alt-\u2193</code>   do the same, in a terminal that does not keep those keys for itself.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#every-field-from-this-side-of-the-bench","title":"Every field, from this side of the bench","text":""},{"location":"floor-technician.html#the-root-result-resultjson","title":"The root result (<code>result.json</code>)","text":"Field What you do with it <code>format_version</code> Read this first. Version <code>1</code> is this page. A number you do not know is not a result you can guess at \u2014 refuse it and get a decoder that names the versions it accepts. <code>state</code> The root verdict: <code>Pass</code>, <code>Fail</code>, or <code>Aborted</code>. This is the first word of the recovery protocol. <code>failure</code> Why this node is not Pass, or <code>null</code>. Shape below. <code>timeout</code> <code>null</code> unless a time budget determined or contributed to this state. Shape below. <code>test_case</code> Stable name of this node. The root's name is usually the suite class name. <code>type</code> The node's shape in the suite: <code>suite</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>parallel</code>, or <code>sequence</code>. A <code>test</code> is one claim. A <code>sequence</code> stops at the first failure. A <code>parallel</code> runs children together and keeps the worst state. <code>ancestor_names</code> Identity. Root-to-this, inclusive. <code>bread_crumbs</code> Display path only. <code>summary_tree</code> The whole declared tree, rooted here. This is the structural view. <code>physical_location</code> Station / slot name from <code>PHYSICAL_LOCATION</code>, or <code>unknown</code> if that variable was not in the run environment. <code>env</code> The run environment as the run declared it. Secrets in an unfiltered environment are in this object verbatim \u2014 that is an operator obligation, not a framework rewrite. With <code>environment=DEBUG</code> the allow/deny list is ignored and a warning is in the log. <code>run_data</code> Suite-authored evidence snapshot: what <code>configure()</code> provided, minus anything that is not JSON. Version 1 always has a <code>measurements</code> array: the numbers this node recorded with <code>utils.measure</code>, or empty. Other keys are the suite's. The readings a claim took, and the numbers it measured, are on that claim's node in <code>summary_tree</code>, not here. <code>suite_target</code> Which suite produced this run, as <code>hilt run</code> was told it: the file or module, <code>::</code>, the class. This is what the Inspector's Re-Run tab executes when you press Enter on a node, so the check you run at the bench is the code that ran in production. <code>null</code> means the run was not started by <code>hilt run</code>; open the Inspector with <code>--suite</code> if you need to re-run from it."},{"location":"floor-technician.html#a-summary-tree-node","title":"A summary-tree node","text":"Field What you do with it <code>name</code> This node's name. Combine with parent names via <code>ancestor_names</code> on the matching <code>RunResult</code> when you have one; in the tree, the path is the walk from the root. <code>type</code> Same discriminator as the root: <code>suite</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>parallel</code>, <code>sequence</code>. <code>state</code> Terminal state, or <code>null</code> if this step did not run. <code>failure</code> Failure for this node, or <code>null</code>. A parent can be Fail because a child is; look at the child that actually failed. <code>timeout</code> Time-budget evidence for this node, or <code>null</code>. <code>log_path</code> POSIX path of this node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code>, relative to the run directory. <code>null</code> means the records rolled up into an ancestor, or the node never ran. Resolve it against the run directory you opened. The root always has one. <code>children</code> Ordered children. Groups keep declaration order after settlement. <code>evidence</code> The readings this claim handed back with its verdict, or <code>null</code>. Read it beside the failure: a Fail with <code>evidence</code> shows you what the unit measured. <code>evidence_declared</code> The readings this claim promised to take on a pass, or <code>null</code>. A name listed here that is missing from <code>evidence</code> is a reading that was not taken: the claim failed, aborted, or never ran before it got there. It is not a reading of zero. <code>measurements</code> The numbers this claim recorded, with their windows, or <code>null</code> if it never ran. An entry with <code>in_limits: false</code> is a reading outside its window. An entry with <code>value: null</code> is a reading the claim tried to take and the device did not give; it is not a reading of zero. Read <code>state</code> beside either: it is not a Fail unless <code>state</code> says Fail. The cases are in <code>docs/measurements.md</code>, \"What the Floor Technician reads\"."},{"location":"floor-technician.html#the-failure-object","title":"The failure object","text":"<p>Failures keep their framework name so you can tell the cases apart. Unknown fields are additive; read what you know.</p> What you see Meaning <code>name</code> The failure class: <code>TestFailure</code>, a suite subclass (<code>DimmFailure</code>, \u2026), <code>ContractViolation</code>, <code>FrameworkContractViolation</code>, <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, <code>MissingVerdict</code>, <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>, <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code>. <code>fail_message</code> The sentence you read out loud. Hardware copy for a <code>TestFailure</code>; a banner plus a fix for a contract break; \"operator interrupted\u2026\" for a Ctrl-C. <code>state</code> <code>Fail</code> or <code>Aborted</code>, matching the taxonomy. <code>expected</code> / <code>received</code> Present only when the suite supplied a comparison. Omitted, not nulled, when the failure is self-evident. <code>received</code> is what the suite observed. <code>banner</code> <code>CONTRACT VIOLATION</code> (author) or <code>FRAMEWORK CONTRACT VIOLATION</code> (maintainers). <code>bug</code> <code>true</code> means broken software, not broken hardware. <code>node</code> Which node broke the contract. <code>cause</code> Nested failure or a <code>{name, message}</code> for a plain exception. Follow it; do not stop at the wrapper. <code>hook</code> <code>before_each</code> or <code>after_each</code> on an <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>. That is the harness hook that threw. <code>operator</code> <code>true</code> on <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>. The operator stopped the rack. <code>signal</code> <code>SIGINT</code>, <code>SIGTERM</code>, or <code>signal N</code>. <code>returned</code> What a <code>MissingVerdict</code> node actually returned instead of PASS. <code>key</code> / <code>owner</code> On a context contract break: the ctx key that was never published, or that a claim tried to write after <code>configure()</code>, and which <code>configure()</code> owns it. <code>key</code> On an evidence contract break: the reading a <code>PASS</code> did not carry, could not be written as JSON, or that collided with a ctx key. The claim is broken software, not a bad unit. <code>evidence</code> On a <code>TestFailure</code>: the readings the claim took before it failed, when it handed them over. <code>mac</code> / <code>host</code> On <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code>: the MAC that was not in the table, and which host was asked. <p>Live handles (an open SSH session, a dict with a weird key) degrade to a string rather than making the file unreadable. If <code>received</code> looks like <code>&lt;SshInterface host=dut-12&gt;</code>, that is the degraded form, not a missing value.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#the-timeout-object","title":"The timeout object","text":"<p><code>timeout</code> is <code>null</code> when no deadline contributed. Otherwise:</p> Field What you do with it <code>reason</code> Always <code>timeout</code>. This object exists because a clock fired. <code>declared</code> Which clock won: <code>fail_after</code> or <code>abort_after</code>. <code>budget_seconds</code> How long that clock was willing to wait. <code>elapsed_seconds</code> How long the node had actually been active when it fired. <code>clock</code> Root-to-declaring-node path of the winning clock. The budget may have been declared on a parent. <p><code>fail_after</code> also puts a hardware-facing <code>TestFailure</code> in <code>failure</code>. <code>abort_after</code> may leave <code>failure</code> null \u2014 the timeout record is the reason the node is Aborted. Do not report those two the same way.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#the-timeline-timelinejsonl","title":"The timeline (<code>timeline.jsonl</code>)","text":"<p>One complete JSON object per line. You may read it while a run is still live; keep every complete line after an interrupt. <code>timeline.log</code> is the same events as escaped human lines.</p> Field What you do with it <code>format_version</code> Timeline schema version; currently <code>1</code>. Independent of the result's version. <code>timestamp</code> Timezone-aware UTC, ending in <code>Z</code>. This is the chronology. <code>scope</code> Root-to-emitting-node path. Same idea as <code>ancestor_names</code>. <code>level</code> Logging level (<code>info</code>, <code>warning</code>, <code>error</code>, \u2026). <code>message</code> The line. Newlines stay escaped inside the JSON. <code>artifact_name</code> Present only on pointer lines. The captured artifact's name, or <code>result</code> for the root result. <code>artifact_path</code> Absolute path of that file. Open it. <code>artifact_type</code> What kind of capture, or <code>run-result</code> for <code>result.json</code>. <code>artifact_timestamp</code> When that evidence was taken, UTC. <p>Ordinary log lines omit the four <code>artifact_*</code> fields rather than setting them to null. A pointer line has all four.</p> <p>Pending nodes create no files. Leaves roll their records into the nearest ancestor that owns a log. If a node has a terminal <code>state</code> and <code>log_path</code> is null, read the nearest ancestor that has a <code>log_path</code>.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#operator-interrupt-in-the-file","title":"Operator interrupt, in the file","text":"<p>A Ctrl-C or a SIGTERM during a live run is not a Fail and not a harness outage.</p> <p>In <code>result.json</code>:</p> <ul> <li>root <code>state</code> is <code>Aborted</code></li> <li><code>failure.name</code> is <code>OperatorInterrupt</code></li> <li><code>failure.operator</code> is <code>true</code></li> <li><code>failure.signal</code> is <code>SIGINT</code> or <code>SIGTERM</code></li> <li><code>failure.fail_message</code> contains <code>operator</code></li> </ul> <p>In the timeline: the same story in order, including any artifacts flushed before the signal. The run directory is still the record; <code>result.json</code> is still written.</p> <p>If <code>operator</code> is missing or false, it was not the operator. Do not guess.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#two-records-two-version-numbers","title":"Two records, two version numbers","text":"<p><code>result.json</code> and <code>timeline.jsonl</code> each carry their own <code>format_version</code>. They move independently. The tree points at the log through <code>log_path</code>.</p> <p>Within version 1, new fields may appear. Ignore fields you do not know; do not fail the read. A changed meaning, a renamed field, or a moved file would bump the number. From 1.0.0 that bump is a contract change. Before 1.0.0 the record may still change in place under version 1.</p> <p>The published schemas live next to this guide:</p> <ul> <li><code>docs/schema/result-v1.schema.json</code></li> <li><code>docs/schema/timeline-v1.schema.json</code></li> </ul> <p>You do not have to validate against them on the station. They exist so a decoder can refuse a version it was not written for, loudly.</p>"},{"location":"floor-technician.html#what-this-page-is-not","title":"What this page is not","text":"<p>It is not the authoring guide (<code>docs/idioms.md</code>, <code>docs/contracts.md</code>). It is not the runner's exit-code table (<code>docs/hilt-run.md</code>) \u2014 though exit <code>0</code>/<code>1</code>/<code>2</code> match Pass/Fail/Aborted, and exit <code>3</code> means there is no <code>result.json</code> because no run happened. It is not the Inspector guide (<code>docs/inspector.md</code>); the Inspector consumes exactly these files.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html","title":"Getting started","text":"<p>This page takes you from a fresh clone to a saved run open in the Inspector, then through the examples in the order they teach. For why HILT is shaped the way it is, read the overview; for the rules your suite is held to, the contracts.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#install","title":"Install","text":"<p>HILT needs Python 3.14 and uv.</p> <pre><code>make install   # uv sync + git hooks\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#your-first-run","title":"Your first run","text":"<pre><code>uv run hilt run examples/hello_world.py\nuv run hilt inspect\n</code></pre> <p><code>hilt run</code> loads a suite file or module and runs the one <code>TestComponent</code> subclass it defines, or the one named after <code>::</code>. Its exit status is the verdict:</p> Exit Meaning 0 Pass 1 Fail 2 Aborted 3 The suite could not be run at all <p><code>hilt inspect [PATH]</code> opens a saved run in the Inspector, the Floor Technician's terminal surface. Without <code>PATH</code> it opens the latest run under <code>artifacts/</code>.</p> <p>Three flags shape what a run leaves behind (hilt run):</p> <ul> <li><code>--artifact-root DIR</code> puts the run's evidence somewhere specific.</li> <li><code>--json</code> prints the <code>format_version</code> 1 result record to stdout   (Inspector JSON contract).</li> <li><code>--junit PATH</code> writes the JUnit XML projection beside it, for CI.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#write-your-own-suite","title":"Write your own suite","text":"<pre><code>uv run hilt new WidgetSuite\n</code></pre> <p><code>hilt new</code> writes a gated-<code>PASS</code> suite and a matching self-test, so a new suite starts in the right shape instead of as a copy of an example (hilt new). <code>hilt-check</code> is the optional tier-3 linter over authored suites (hilt-check); idioms collects the authoring patterns.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#walk-the-examples","title":"Walk the examples","text":"<p>The locally executable examples build from one explicit claim, through composition, to polling, lifecycle hooks, and the evidence surface:</p> <pre><code>uv run hilt run examples/hello_world.py\nuv run hilt run examples/test_composition.py::SequencedDevice\nuv run hilt run examples/test_composition.py::ParallelDevice   # exits 1 by design\nuv run hilt run examples/wait_for_patterns.py::ConfigureGatedDevice\nuv run hilt run examples/wait_for_patterns.py::MainAssertionDevice\nuv run hilt run examples/hardware_device_lifecycle.py\nuv run hilt run examples/run_data_evidence.py\n</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>wait_for_patterns.py</code> shows readiness polling in <code>configure()</code> and verdict polling   in a test method.</li> <li><code>hardware_device_lifecycle.py</code> registers survey and audit hooks around a rail that   comes up, is watched, and is dropped in cleanup.</li> <li><code>run_data_evidence.py</code> shows limits flowing in through ctx and readings travelling   out with the verdict.</li> </ul> <p>Every example carries comments explaining the reasoning behind its pattern, not only what the code does. Read them as the authoring guide.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#examples-against-real-devices","title":"Examples against real devices","text":"<p>The SSH-backed examples take their target from <code>utils.env</code> in <code>configure()</code>. A missing variable Fails the suite (exit 1): the device did not reach a testable state, and the message names the variable to set. Optional password and port variables (<code>SSH_PASSWORD</code> / <code>SSH_PORT</code>, <code>JUMP_*</code> / <code>TARGET_*</code>, <code>GATEWAY_*</code>) default the port to 22 when unset.</p> <pre><code>SSH_HOST=dut.lab SSH_USER=root uv run hilt run examples/remote_device_ssh.py\nJUMP_HOST=jump.lab JUMP_USER=ops TARGET_HOST=dut.lab TARGET_USER=root uv run hilt run examples/ssh_tunneling.py\nDUT_MAC=aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff uv run hilt run examples/networked_device.py::DutDevice\nTARGET_ADDRESS=pihole.lab TARGET_USER=root uv run hilt run examples/pi_hole_server.py\n</code></pre> <p><code>networked_device.py</code> defines <code>Gateway</code> and <code>DutDevice</code>; pick the station suite with <code>::DutDevice</code>. <code>GATEWAY_HOST</code> and <code>GATEWAY_USER</code> (both required together) resolve ARP through a hop instead of the test host's table.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#deploying-to-a-station","title":"Deploying to a station","text":"<p>Every test host must synchronize its system clock with NTP before HILT runs. The scoped log is the authoritative chronology for a run, including structured pointers to captured artifacts, and may be used to establish event order in customer disputes. A station with an unsynchronized clock cannot produce trustworthy evidence and must not execute tests.</p> <p>Set <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code> to the run-specific evidence directory. Point-in-time evidence is an immutable <code>Artifact</code> value with no public file-writing method; persist it only with <code>await utils.capture_artifact(artifact)</code>, which writes the file and then appends its pointer to the scoped log timeline.</p> <p>The Floor Technician guide is the station page: how to read a result without reading source. Its rendered page, https://gitlab.com/Tyler-Meador/HILT/-/blob/main/docs/floor-technician.md, is the URL that belongs on a station label.</p>"},{"location":"getting-started.html#contributing","title":"Contributing","text":"<p>Read <code>STANDARDS.md</code> before committing.</p> <pre><code>make fix           # auto-format + auto-fix lint\nmake check         # the gate (there is no CI; the bench run is it): format check, lint, pyright strict, import cycles, pytest under the coverage floors, hilt-check, wheel smoke install, docs, pip-audit\nmake docs          # documentation site (strict: a broken cross-reference is red)\nmake docs-release  # the same build into docs/site/, committed on a release branch only\n</code></pre> <p>API stability defines the public surface, semantic versioning from 1.0.0, and the deprecation procedure. <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> at the repository root records every change to that surface. For a release MR, select the Release template (<code>.gitlab/merge_request_templates/Release.md</code>): it records the candidate's checks, changelog cut, and the handoff to Zavala for review.</p> <p>Nothing hosts the docs site. The tree is the documentation: GitLab renders every page under https://gitlab.com/Tyler-Meador/HILT/-/tree/main/docs, and each release branch (<code>release/MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>) also carries the built site under <code>docs/site/</code> (<code>make docs-release</code>; open <code>docs/site/index.html</code> from a clone, no network needed).</p> <p>The port design lives in the workspace plan (<code>../PLAN.md</code>); the TypeScript original is the <code>../hilt-test-legacy/</code> design reference, and its language server is <code>../hilt-language-server-legacy/</code>. Both are read-only \u2014 parity with them is explicitly not a goal. <code>PLAN.md</code> section 0 maps the workspace.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html","title":"hilt-check \u2014 Tier 3 Static Analysis","text":"<p><code>hilt-check</code> is the third tier of the enforcement stack described in overview.md. It reads authored suite files as Python ASTs and reports departures from the declarative idiom.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#skippable-by-design","title":"Skippable by design","text":"Tier Mechanism Guarantees Skippable? 1 Runtime IoC wrapping Verdict contract, failure taxonomy, lifecycle order, immutable-self freeze Never 2 <code>__init_subclass__</code> at import Structural contract: sealed internals, lifecycle signatures, hook shape Never 3 <code>hilt-check</code> Declarative syntax: pure <code>build_suite</code>, gated <code>PASS</code>, immutable-self idiom, constructor-only hooks, live composition names, ctx provenance, fulfilled evidence, routes declared in <code>configure</code>, measurements named once and recorded where a node runs, one source per <code>configure()</code> value Yes <p>Nothing that protects hardware or verdict integrity lives only here. Every rule below either has a tier-1 or tier-2 counterpart that holds whether or not the linter ever runs, or guards a style alone and says so in its row. A suite that skips <code>hilt-check</code> loses early, readable feedback at edit time; it does not lose a guard. That is the whole reason this tier may be skipped and the other two may not.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#usage","title":"Usage","text":"<pre><code>uv run hilt-check suites/                 # check a directory (recursive)\nuv run hilt-check suites/blade.py other/  # files and directories mix freely\nuv run hilt-check --list-rules            # the rule catalogue\nuv run hilt-check --select hilt/gated-pass suites/\nuv run hilt-check --ignore hilt/pure-build-suite suites/\nuv run hilt-check --format json suites/   # machine-readable report\npython -m hilt_check suites/              # same command without the script\n</code></pre> <p>Output is one line per finding, <code>path:line:col: code message</code>, followed by a count.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#exit-status-the-ci-contract","title":"Exit status \u2014 the CI contract","text":"Status Meaning <code>0</code> Every checked file is clean. <code>1</code> At least one rule violation. <code>2</code> A file could not be checked (missing path, unreadable, invalid Python), or the invocation was wrong (unknown rule code, no paths). <p>A path that cannot be parsed is an error, never silence: a suite that did not parse was not checked. Unknown rule codes fail the same way so a typo in a CI job cannot quietly disable a rule. <code>make check</code> runs the tool over <code>examples/</code>, which is the scaffolding suite authors copy from, so the examples always demonstrate the idiom.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#rules","title":"Rules","text":"Code Idiom Runtime counterpart (the guarantee) <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> Every <code>return PASS</code> sits inside an <code>if</code> branch or <code>match</code> arm; the pass predicate is visible where the PASS is. Tier 1: the child boundary accepts only the <code>PASS</code> identity and refuses to forge a verdict. <code>hilt/pure-build-suite</code> <code>build_suite</code> is one <code>return</code> of composition calls: no other statements, no conditionals, filtered comprehensions, lambdas, <code>await</code>, or walrus inside; an unfiltered comprehension enumerates leaves (one per core, rail, or channel) and is allowed. Tier 2: <code>__init_subclass__</code> requires <code>build_suite</code> with the right shape; tier 1 refuses anything but a <code>TestComponent</code> from it. <code>hilt/immutable-self</code> <code>self.&lt;attr&gt;</code> is assigned or deleted only in <code>__init__</code>; runtime state returns from <code>configure()</code> into <code>utils.ctx</code>. Tier 1: the instance freezes after the most-derived <code>__init__</code>; assignment raises <code>AttributeError</code>. <code>hilt/constructor-hooks</code> <code>use_survey</code> and <code>use_audit</code> are called only in <code>__init__</code>; hooks are identity, not runtime state. Tier 1: both registrars raise <code>ContractViolation</code> on a frozen instance. <code>hilt/unannotated-interface</code> Functions and methods inside suite classes annotate every explicit parameter and return; methods passed to <code>self.test</code> declare <code>-&gt; Verdict</code>. Tier 1 for the <code>-&gt; Verdict</code> half: the child boundary accepts only the <code>PASS</code> identity and refuses to forge a verdict. The parameter half is authoring clarity only; an annotation guards nothing at runtime. <code>hilt/raise-test-failure</code> A test method raises <code>TestFailure</code>, not a plain <code>ValueError</code> / <code>RuntimeError</code> / <code>Exception</code>. Tier 1: the failure taxonomy classifies any non-<code>TestFailure</code> exception escaping a test method as a developer-bug Fail. <code>hilt/method-reference</code> <code>self.test</code> takes a method reference, not a <code>lambda</code> or nested def. Tier 2: method references passed to <code>test</code> are auto-bound; the wrapper is unnecessary. <code>hilt/retired-names</code> <code>self.case</code> and <code>self.group</code> are gone; compose with <code>self.test</code>, <code>self.sequence</code>, and <code>self.parallel</code> (issue #110). Tier 1: the attributes no longer exist, so <code>build_suite</code> raises <code>AttributeError</code> before any hardware is touched; the rule names the replacement instead. <code>hilt/no-verdict-in-cleanup</code> <code>cleanup</code> does not <code>return PASS</code> or <code>raise TestFailure</code>; it restores hardware state. Tier 1: cleanup's return is discarded and its exceptions are swallowed. <code>hilt/ctx-provenance</code> Every <code>utils.ctx[\"k\"]</code> read in a test method, hook, <code>configure</code>, or <code>cleanup</code> names a key that <code>configure()</code> returns (its dict literal or <code>TypedDict</code>), and ctx is never written: no subscript assignment, <code>update</code>, <code>setdefault</code>, <code>pop</code>, or <code>clear</code> anywhere in the suite. A reading travels with the verdict, and <code>.get()</code> is how an optional key is read (issues #112, #118). Tier 1: a <code>[]</code> read of a key <code>configure()</code> did not provide, and any write after <code>configure()</code> returns, raise <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the test and the key; <code>get</code> and <code>in</code> never do. <code>hilt/shell-in-configure</code> <code>utils.use_shell(...)</code> or <code>utils.use_serial(...)</code> with arguments, the call that declares where this node's <code>utils.exec</code> lands, appears only in <code>configure()</code>; the bare <code>use_shell()</code> / <code>use_serial()</code> read and <code>get_shell</code> / <code>get_serial</code> may appear anywhere (issue #76). Tier 1: a declaring call (<code>host</code>, <code>via</code>, <code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) from any slot but <code>configure</code> raises <code>ContractViolation</code> before the router is touched, so the <code>exec routing:</code> line written after <code>configure</code> is the route every command of the node ran under. <code>hilt/evidence-fulfilled</code> Every <code>return PASS({...})</code> in a method decorated <code>@evidence(...)</code> carries every declared key, and such a method never returns bare <code>PASS</code> (issue #118). Tier 1: the child boundary holds every <code>PASS</code> to the declaration; a missing key is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the leaf and the key, a developer-bug Fail. <code>hilt/evidence-shadows-ctx</code> No key <code>@evidence</code> declares (a literal, or a same-module <code>TypedDict</code> field) is a key the class's <code>configure()</code> returns (its dict literal, <code>dict(k=...)</code>, a local literal it builds, its return <code>TypedDict</code>, or a same-module base's keys) or the framework-owned <code>measurements</code> slot. Ctx and evidence land on one record, so the reading cannot reuse the name of the limit or target it was judged against; the finding sits on the declaring literal, where the rename happens (bench finding F14, issue #161). A clash with a key only an enclosing suite's ctx provides is out of a single file's reach and left to tier 1. Tier 1: the child boundary refuses a payload key that is in the node's ctx or is <code>measurements</code> as a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the leaf and the key; nothing is stripped silently. <code>hilt/measurement-name-unique</code> Two <code>measure(...)</code> calls, either spelling, with the same literal name inside one <code>configure</code> or test method; a measurement's series is keyed on its name, so the second call would overwrite or fork it (issue #132). Tier 1: the duplicate-name guard in <code>TestUtils.measure</code> and <code>hilt.measure</code> raises <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the node and the measurement. <code>hilt/measurement-in-node</code> A <code>measure(...)</code> call, either spelling, inside <code>cleanup</code>, a method registered with <code>use_survey</code>/<code>use_audit</code>, a <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> hook (by name, or passed under either keyword), or outside any function; a reading belongs to the node whose <code>configure()</code> or test method is running (issue #133). Tier 1: the outside-a-node guard raises <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the node and the measurement for <code>hilt.measure</code> with nothing bound and for either spelling from <code>cleanup</code>, a survey, an audit, a hook, or after the node settled. <code>hilt/no-input-fallback</code> Inside <code>configure()</code>, a value is identity from the constructor or discovered through <code>utils</code>, never one with the other as fallback: no <code>self._ip or await utils.get_arp_resolver(...).resolve()</code>, no conditional expression choosing between them, no local name bound from a <code>self</code> attribute by one assignment and from <code>get_arp_resolver</code> / <code>use_shell</code> / <code>get_shell</code> / <code>use_serial</code> / <code>get_serial</code> / <code>get_http</code> / <code>get_coap</code> by another. Identity fed into discovery and identity read from <code>utils.env</code> stay (issues #142, #144, #147). None, by design: a fallback is a style the ruling forbids, not a guard on hardware or a verdict. The runtime records which utility provided a value either way; the rule moves the ruling to the editor. <code>hilt/env-via-utils</code> Suite code reads the environment through <code>utils.env</code> (<code>utils.env[\"K\"]</code>, <code>.get</code>, <code>.require</code>), never <code>os.environ</code> or <code>os.getenv</code>, under any import alias; <code>utils.env</code> is the run's recorded, injectable snapshot and <code>os.environ</code> is invisible to the evidence. None can exist: <code>os.environ</code> is the interpreter's. The rule is the only gate, so it stays narrow and names the replacement. <code>hilt/logger-not-print</code> Suite code logs through <code>utils.logger</code>, whose entries land in the run's timeline; a builtin <code>print</code> inside a <code>TestComponent</code> subclass reaches only the process's stdout, which the framework does not capture. None: the framework cannot intercept the builtin without changing what <code>print</code> means everywhere, so the rule is the gate. <code>hilt/model-resolution-failure</code> An awaited <code>.resolve()</code> / <code>.reacquire(...)</code> / <code>.request(...)</code> inside a suite class sits in a <code>try</code> with a handler that raises <code>TestFailure</code>; <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> and <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> are not verdicts, and the suite models the miss instead of leaking it. In <code>cleanup</code>, which gives no verdict and must not throw, the handler instead raises nothing and logs the miss, the shape <code>hilt/no-verdict-in-cleanup</code> also accepts (issue #153). Tier 1: the failure taxonomy classifies the escaping exception as a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (\"CONTRACT VIOLATION IN ...\"), which is correct and unhelpful; the rule moves the fix to the editor and names the exception to catch. From <code>cleanup</code> the exception is logged and swallowed, and the rest of the teardown does not run. <code>hilt/no-swallowed-cancel</code> Inside an <code>async</code> method, a <code>try</code> whose body awaits is not guarded by a bare <code>except</code>, <code>except BaseException</code>, or <code>except CancelledError</code> that fails to re-raise with a bare <code>raise</code>; <code>contextlib.suppress</code> never names either. <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code> is the abort signal, and a handler that swallows it mints a verdict after the harness gave up. Catch <code>Exception</code> for the failure you modeled. Tier 1: the cancellation discipline of issue #38 shields evidence collection and expects suite code to propagate; this rule is the editor-time half. <p>Run <code>hilt-check --list-rules</code> for the catalogue shipped in the installed version. Each rule's module docstring under <code>tools/hilt_check/rules/</code> states exactly what it flags, what it leaves alone, and why.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#adding-a-rule","title":"Adding a rule","text":"<p>The registry is extensible without touching the CLI:</p> <ol> <li>Add a module under <code>tools/hilt_check/rules/</code>.</li> <li>Define a class deriving from <code>hilt_check.Rule</code> with a stable <code>code</code>    (<code>hilt/&lt;kebab-name&gt;</code>), a one-line <code>summary</code>, and a <code>check(module)</code> method that    yields <code>Violation</code> values via <code>module.violation(node, code, message)</code>.</li> <li>Land its tests in <code>tests/</code> alongside the others, with a seeded fixture under    <code>tests/fixtures/hilt_check/</code> so the CLI is proven end to end.</li> </ol> <p><code>RuleRegistry.discover()</code> imports every module in the package and instantiates every concrete <code>Rule</code> subclass it finds. <code>SuiteModule</code> carries the structural queries a rule needs \u2014 enclosing function, method, and class, the methods a class registers by reference (<code>use_survey</code>, <code>use_audit</code>, <code>test</code>), and a static answer to \"is this class a <code>TestComponent</code>\" \u2014 so a rule is only its own idiom.</p> <p>A rule's <code>code</code> is a contract with suite authors' <code>--ignore</code> flags; rename it only with a note in the changelog of the MR that does so.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#editor-the-pylsp-adapter","title":"Editor \u2014 the pylsp adapter","text":"<p>The same registry the CLI runs is a <code>python-lsp-server</code> plugin. Install the extra so the core package stays dependency-light:</p> <pre><code>uv pip install 'open-hilt[pylsp]'\n# or, from a checkout: uv sync --extra pylsp\n</code></pre> <p><code>python-lsp-server</code> discovers the plugin via the <code>pylsp</code> entry point. Every rule in <code>hilt-check --list-rules</code> is published as a diagnostic on the open buffer (unsaved contents included). Two rules offer a quick-fix:</p> Code Action <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> Wrap the <code>return PASS</code> in an explicit pass predicate. Preceding <code>if ...: raise</code> guards are inverted into that predicate only when they sit flush against the return in its own block (a return in a loop's or <code>try</code>'s <code>else:</code> or in <code>finally:</code> never borrows the body's guards), load no name bound at the insertion point, and contain no call. Every other shape gets <code>if False:</code> plus a <code>TestFailure</code> raise \u2014 a leftover scaffold cannot return PASS. <code>hilt/method-reference</code> Replace a trivial <code>lambda</code> (<code>lambda u: self._claim(u)</code>) with the method reference. A lambda that carries extra logic is left alone \u2014 that logic belongs in a real method. <p>Disable the plugin in pylsp config with <code>plugins.hilt_check.enabled: false</code>.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-check.html#what-this-tier-is-not","title":"What this tier is not","text":"<ul> <li>It is not a type checker. Suites are held to the same annotation standard as the   framework (STANDARDS.md \u00a72); <code>hilt/unannotated-interface</code> makes the interface   explicit, and pyright strict remains the authority on whether those types agree.</li> <li>It is not a second checker. The editor plugin is the CLI's registry with an LSP   surface; a finding in the editor is the same finding <code>hilt-check</code> would print.</li> <li>It is not a guard. See the table above.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"hilt-new.html","title":"hilt new \u2014 the suite generator","text":"<p><code>hilt new</code> writes a gated-PASS suite and a matching self-test so the author starts from a shape that is already correct, annotated, and gated. Examples stay the lessons; this is the scaffolding they used to be copied from.</p> <pre><code>uv run hilt new WidgetSuite\nuv run hilt new PowerRail --directory suites/\nuv run hilt new WidgetSuite --force\n</code></pre> <p>The command writes two files next to each other:</p> File What it is <code>widget_suite.py</code> One <code>TestComponent</code> subclass named <code>WidgetSuite</code> <code>test_widget_suite.py</code> A pytest stub that constructs it from its name and asserts Pass"},{"location":"hilt-new.html#what-is-public","title":"What is public","text":"<p>The command line is public (<code>docs/api-stability.md</code>): the class name, the two files, <code>--directory</code>, <code>--force</code>, and the exit status (<code>0</code> wrote the files, <code>3</code> usage error). The Python module behind it is not.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-new.html#the-shape-it-writes","title":"The shape it writes","text":"<p>The generated suite is the single source for this shape. Every file <code>hilt new</code> emits has:</p> <ul> <li>an annotated <code>__init__</code> whose extra parameters carry defaults, so <code>hilt run</code>   can construct the class from its name alone</li> <li><code>configure</code> returning a narrowed <code>TypedDict</code> ctx</li> <li>a pure <code>build_suite</code> that returns <code>self.sequence([self.test(...)])</code>: the root   sequence takes the suite's name, and the next claim is one more entry in its list</li> <li>one test method whose <code>return PASS</code> sits inside an explicit pass predicate</li> <li><code>cleanup</code></li> </ul> <p>A missing target after construction is <code>TestFailure</code> from <code>configure()</code>: \"did not reach a testable state\". That is the ruled meaning of a failure there (<code>docs/contracts.md</code>).</p> <p>The generated files pass <code>hilt-check</code>, ruff, and pyright strict unmodified on the day they are written. Teaching examples in <code>examples/</code> are verified against the core of this shape (annotated <code>__init__</code>, <code>build_suite</code>, gated <code>return PASS</code>) rather than regenerated from it \u2014 they carry lessons the template does not.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-new.html#exit-status","title":"Exit status","text":"Status Meaning <code>0</code> Both files were written. <code>3</code> Usage error. The name is not a suite class name, the directory does not exist, or the files already exist and <code>--force</code> was not given. Nothing was overwritten. <p>A name must be a Python identifier, not a keyword, and start with an uppercase letter (<code>WidgetSuite</code>, not <code>widget_suite</code> and not <code>class</code>). Existing files are refused unless <code>--force</code> is passed, so a second invocation cannot clobber a suite the author has started editing.</p>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html","title":"hilt run \u2014 the runner","text":"<p><code>hilt run</code> is the one command the developer documents and the Floor Technician types. It loads a suite, runs it through the sealed lifecycle, and exits with a code the station script branches on without parsing output.</p> <pre><code>uv run hilt run examples/hello_world.py\nuv run hilt run examples/test_composition.py::ParallelDevice\nuv run hilt run station.suites.psu --artifact-root /var/lib/hilt/psu-0042\nuv run hilt run suite.py --json &gt; result.json\nuv run hilt run suite.py --junit report.xml\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html#what-is-public","title":"What is public","text":"<p>Two things are promised (<code>docs/api-stability.md</code>): the exit status contract below, and the <code>--json</code> record, which is the same <code>format_version</code> 1 result the framework writes as <code>result.json</code>, governed by the policy in <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>. The Python module behind the command is private.</p> <p>The record names the suite that produced it. <code>hilt run</code> resolves <code>TARGET</code> to an absolute file or module name and the class it selected, and writes that as <code>suite_target</code>, so <code>hilt inspect</code> can offer the same suite on its Re-Run tab without being told again (<code>docs/inspector.md</code>).</p>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html#exit-status","title":"Exit status","text":"Status Meaning <code>0</code> The root settled Pass. <code>1</code> The root settled Fail. <code>2</code> The root settled Aborted. <code>3</code> Usage error. The invocation was wrong, or the suite could not be loaded, selected, or constructed. No run took place, no evidence was written. <p>There is no fourth terminal state and no fourth run-related code. A station script treats <code>0</code> as ship, <code>1</code> as reject, <code>2</code> as re-run after the operator looks, and <code>3</code> as a suite-authoring or configuration fault that never reached hardware.</p> <pre><code>uv run hilt run suites/psu.py --artifact-root \"$RUN_DIR\"\ncase $? in\n  0) echo ship ;;\n  1) echo reject ;;\n  2) echo \"aborted; see $RUN_DIR\" ;;\n  *) echo \"suite could not run\" &gt;&amp;2 ;;\nesac\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html#selecting-the-suite","title":"Selecting the suite","text":"<p><code>TARGET</code> is <code>&lt;path-or-module&gt;[::SuiteClass]</code>.</p> <ul> <li>A source ending in <code>.py</code>, or naming an existing file, is loaded from disk,   standalone. Anything else is imported by dotted name through the normal import   path (<code>examples.hello_world</code>, <code>station.suites.psu</code>).</li> <li>With <code>::SuiteClass</code>, the named attribute may be any <code>TestComponent</code> subclass bound   in the module, imported or defined. A station module may therefore re-export the   suite it runs.</li> <li>Without <code>::SuiteClass</code>, a module-level <code>__suite__</code> bound to a <code>TestComponent</code>   subclass is the default: a file that holds a gateway and the device behind it   (<code>examples/networked_device.py</code>) can say <code>__suite__ = Gateway</code> and be run by path   alone. <code>::SuiteClass</code> still overrides it. A <code>__suite__</code> bound to anything else is a   usage error naming what it found.</li> <li>Without either, the module must define exactly one <code>TestComponent</code> subclass. A   module that defines none, or more than one, is a usage error whose message lists   what it found.</li> </ul> <p>The run record always names the class (<code>&lt;file&gt;::SuiteClass</code>), whichever rule picked it, so <code>hilt inspect --rerun</code> selects the same suite after another is added beside it.</p> <p>The runner constructs the selected class as <code>SuiteClass(name=SuiteClass.__name__)</code>. Every other <code>__init__</code> parameter must carry a default; a constructor that needs more is a usage error naming the parameter. A suite that reads its target from the environment does so in <code>configure()</code> through <code>utils.env</code>, where the value is published as evidence in the same motion (<code>docs/idioms.md</code>, TestUtils Injection).</p>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html#evidence","title":"Evidence","text":"<ul> <li><code>--artifact-root DIR</code> sets the evidence root for this run and overrides   <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>. Without it, the variable applies, then <code>./artifacts</code>.   The run directory, the per-node timelines, and <code>result.json</code> land beneath it as   <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> describes.</li> <li><code>--json</code> prints the <code>format_version</code> 1 result record to stdout and nothing   else goes there, so <code>&gt; result.json</code> captures exactly the record.</li> <li><code>--junit PATH</code> also writes the run as JUnit XML through <code>hilt.junit.JunitXml</code>   (<code>docs/junit-xml.md</code>). The runner owns the reporter; a harness that imports the   module directly does so off the promised surface. A <code>PATH</code> that cannot be written   is an error line on stderr; the exit status stays the verdict's, because the run   settled and <code>result.json</code> under the artifact root is the record.</li> <li>The verdict line and the run log go to stderr:   <code>hilt run: &lt;node&gt;: &lt;state&gt;</code> with the failure class and message when there is one.   The log is the scoped logger at <code>INFO</code>, the same lines the per-node <code>timeline.log</code>   records, so the person at the station watches the run as it happens. Python   warnings, including <code>DeprecationWarning</code>, are routed through the same log.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"hilt-run.html#operator-interrupt","title":"Operator interrupt","text":"<p><code>SIGINT</code> or <code>SIGTERM</code> during a run is an operator run-abort (<code>docs/contracts.md</code>, Operator Interrupt): transports close hard, every unsettled node records the operator's name, <code>result.json</code> is still written, and the exit status is <code>2</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html","title":"Framework Idioms &amp; Patterns","text":""},{"location":"idioms.html#the-golden-rule","title":"The Golden Rule","text":"<p>If implementing something feels awkward or \"ugly\", you are fighting the framework. Seek the idiomatic approach.</p> <p>Treat that friction as a design-review signal, not a style preference and not immediate evidence that the framework needs another feature. Ask:</p> <p>What established HILT idiom is this design fighting, and which problem-domain concept would let composition, lifecycle boundaries, or <code>utils</code> express the intent coherently?</p> <p>Resolve that question before expanding the public API. A new abstraction is warranted only when the existing idioms cannot express a real manufacturing-test concept without obscuring evidence, hardware ownership, or verdict integrity. Convenience alone is not sufficient.</p> <p>The most important application is the React boundary in the port plan's section 1. HILT borrows React's component model\u2014composition, inversion of control, hooks, and local state\u2014not its runtime model. Reconciliation, re-rendering, and reactivity are out of scope because a hardware test executes once and may produce irreversible physical side effects. If a proposal feels awkward because it tries to import that runtime model, the friction identifies a crossed boundary, not a missing HILT feature.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#immutable-self-convention","title":"Immutable-self Convention","text":"<p>After <code>__init__</code> runs, <code>self</code> is read-only. What the tests need flows through configure's return value into <code>utils.ctx</code>, and what they observed leaves with the verdict. This eliminates hidden state coupling and makes the data flow explicit.</p> <p>Rules: - <code>__init__</code> sets read-only config (MAC, credentials, site, etc.) - <code>configure()</code> returns what the tests will read, as a plain dict \u2014 the framework merges it into <code>utils.ctx</code> and closes it - test methods, <code>cleanup()</code>, and all child test bodies read from <code>utils.ctx</code>, not from <code>self</code>; every key they read with <code>[]</code> is one <code>configure()</code> returned, and a key it did not provide is read with <code>.get()</code> or not at all \u2014 a missing <code>[]</code> read is a <code>ContractViolation</code>, not a <code>KeyError</code> (issue #112) - <code>cleanup()</code> reads every key with <code>.get()</code> or <code>in</code>, never <code>[]</code>: it also runs when <code>configure()</code> raised before it returned, and then ctx holds nothing <code>configure()</code> would have provided. A <code>[]</code> read there logs a <code>ContractViolation</code> beside the real Fail (issue #152) - No <code>self.field = value</code> after construction, and no write to <code>utils.ctx</code> after <code>configure()</code> \u2014 a reading travels with the verdict, <code>PASS({...})</code>, and a thing that changes mid-run (an address, a connection) is an object in ctx that manages itself (issue #118)</p> <p>Why: Mutable state, on <code>self</code> or in ctx, creates hidden coupling between lifecycle phases. When inputs flow through ctx and readings flow out with the verdict, the data path is explicit and traceable. The Floor Technician benefits because each leaf's record carries exactly what that leaf handed back, beside the limits it judged against.</p> <p>Enforcement: This is a tier-1 runtime guard, not merely a lint convention. <code>TestComponent</code> freezes only after the most-derived <code>__init__</code> returns, so subclasses may finish declaring their identity after <code>super().__init__()</code>. Ordinary assignment and deletion then raise <code>AttributeError</code>. Framework-owned lifecycle and evidence transitions use <code>object.__setattr__</code> directly; suite code receives no framework escape-hatch method. The static no-self-mutation rule remains defense in depth.</p> <p>Scope note: ctx is strictly instance-local. A parent's ctx never flows into composed children \u2014 composition is the only coupling mechanism, and ctx inheritance would smuggle hidden state across it. One leaf never reads another's reading either: what a sibling observed is on that sibling's record, not in ctx.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#evidence-travels-with-the-verdict","title":"Evidence Travels With the Verdict","text":"<p>A test method is a function from <code>utils.ctx</code> to a verdict. Its readings leave with the verdict: <code>PASS({...})</code> on the pass path, <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={...})</code> on the fail path. <code>@evidence</code> is the method's return signature \u2014 it names the keys a pass must carry, beside the code that keeps the promise (issue #118).</p> <pre><code>@evidence(\"output_voltage_v\")\nasync def _check_voltage(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n    v = await self._read(utils, utils.ctx[\"ip\"])\n    reading = {\"output_voltage_v\": v}\n    if v &lt;= utils.ctx[\"voltage_high_v\"]:\n        return PASS(reading)\n    raise TestFailure(f\"{v} V exceeds limit\", evidence=reading)\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>The declaration is fixed at class definition and never computed at run time. Pass   criteria that vary by SKU or fixture are a leaf argument or a different leaf, decided   before <code>build_suite</code> runs.</li> <li>A pass without its declared evidence is not a pass: the child boundary refuses it as a   <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the leaf and the key. Extra keys are allowed; the   declaration is a floor. A failure is not held to it, since a failure may fire before   every reading was taken; it records what it is handed.</li> <li>The payload is validated entry by entry. A value that is not JSON, or a key that shadows   a ctx entry or the framework-owned <code>measurements</code> slot, is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming   the key. Nothing is stripped silently.</li> <li>Ctx and evidence are one namespace, because they land on one record. A key   <code>configure()</code> returns names the limit or the target (<code>kernel</code>, <code>domain</code>, <code>voltage_high_v</code>);   a key <code>@evidence</code> declares names the reading judged against it (<code>kernel_running</code>,   <code>queried</code>, <code>output_voltage_v</code>). The same word cannot do both, so pick the evidence name   with the ctx key in view. <code>hilt-check</code> flags the clash on the declaring literal as   <code>hilt/evidence-shadows-ctx</code> (bench finding F14, issue #161).</li> <li>A measurement is not evidence. <code>utils.measure</code> records a number beside the verdict   (see Measurements below); it does not satisfy an <code>@evidence</code> declaration, so a claim   that wants the reading in both records names it in both.</li> <li>An undecorated method may still return <code>PASS(payload)</code>; the payload is recorded   unchecked. Declare when you want to be held to it.</li> <li>The same decorator accepts a <code>TypedDict</code>. With <code>-&gt; Verdict[MyEvidence]</code> on the method,   pyright checks the <code>PASS({...})</code> payload against the shape at write time; at run time   the <code>TypedDict</code>'s required keys are the declaration.</li> </ul> <pre><code>class VoltageEvidence(TypedDict):\n    output_voltage_v: float\n\n\n@evidence(VoltageEvidence)\nasync def _check_voltage(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict[VoltageEvidence]: ...\n</code></pre> <p><code>hilt-check</code> enforces the declaration statically as <code>hilt/evidence-fulfilled</code>, the one namespace as <code>hilt/evidence-shadows-ctx</code>, and the read-only context as part of <code>hilt/ctx-provenance</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#the-parent-owns-the-route","title":"The Parent Owns the Route","text":"<p>The node that opens a hop owns it, and everything composed under it inherits it. A gateway is a parent node: its <code>configure()</code> calls <code>utils.use_shell(host=..., ...)</code>, and its <code>build_suite()</code> composes the devices behind the hop. The framework routes the gateway's own <code>utils.exec</code> through that hop and hands the route to every child, so a device's configure, surveys, tests, and audits all land on the far side without the device saying so. A device class never takes a routing argument; it is written once and reused on every bench.</p> <pre><code>class Gateway(TestComponent):\n    async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n        await utils.use_shell(host=\"10.0.0.1\", username=\"ops\")\n        return {}\n\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return MyDevice(name=\"target\", mac=\"aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\")\n</code></pre> <p><code>MyDevice</code> run on its own reads the test host; composed under <code>Gateway</code> it reads the gateway. Same bytes. A child that must run locally declares <code>use_shell(inherit=False)</code> in its own <code>configure()</code>; a parent that must not hand its route down declares <code>propagate=False</code>. The hop stays up until the parent's own teardown, after every child has cleaned up \u2014 a child that fails or expires loses only the transports it opened itself, never its parent's hop. A gateway that cannot come up Fails in its own <code>configure()</code>, and the children it never composed stay pending: the bench was not ready, so the device was never asked.</p> <p>The only <code>via</code> in the framework is the tunnel argument to <code>use_shell</code> / <code>get_shell</code> (see SSH Tunnel below). That is the parent's tool for reaching through a jump host inside its own <code>configure()</code>; it is never a child's burden.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#the-node-names-what-it-needs-the-framework-decides-where-it-comes-from","title":"The Node Names What It Needs; the Framework Decides Where It Comes From","text":"<p>Ruled on issue #142 (2026-09-12). A constructor argument is identity, fixed at composition time: a name, a MAC, the SKU-specific pass criteria. Runtime state comes from <code>utils</code> in <code>configure()</code>, as one call with no fallback: the IP, the shell, the tunnel, the resolver, the device API. When a parent and a child both could provide the same thing, that thing belongs in <code>TestUtils</code>, where the framework inherits it down the tree and records which way it went; it never travels through ctx or the constructor. A fallback in <code>configure()</code> is the smell that says a utility is missing.</p> <pre><code># Not allowed: two sources of truth for one ctx key, a branch the suite engineer\n# wrote, and a record that cannot say which path produced the value.\nip = self._ip or await utils.get_arp_resolver(mac=self._mac).resolve()\n</code></pre> <p>The framework already does this twice. The route flows down through <code>use_shell</code> and is inherited by every composed child; the ARP resolver is scoped to the node's current route and its cache is keyed by the routing label, so devices behind one gateway share ARP state without the suite engineer writing anything to arrange it.</p> <p>Identity reaches a root node through <code>utils.env</code> when no parent composed it: the <code>DUT_MAC</code> a bench-run device reads is the same MAC a rack parent passes to the constructor, and the run's environment is published on the record either way (see Environment Variables below). That is the run's declared input, not discovery, and it is what keeps the bench-run device and the composed one the same bytes.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#run_data-automatic-structured-evidence","title":"run_data \u2014 Automatic Structured Evidence","text":"<p>The default <code>run_data</code> returns a detached snapshot of all serializable values from <code>utils.ctx</code>: the ctx view. JSON primitives (with finite numbers), plain dicts with string keys, and lists survive; class instances (<code>SshInterface</code>, etc.), functions, live handles, non-finite floats, and cycles are stripped automatically. A leaf's <code>RunResult.run_data</code> is that view plus the evidence its verdict carried, flat, so the limit sits beside the reading; the leaf's <code>summary_tree</code> node carries the same evidence and its declaration. A non-leaf node's <code>run_data</code> is the ctx view alone.</p> <p>Stripping is deterministic and all-or-nothing at each top-level ctx entry. If a list or dict contains one invalid nested value, that complete entry is absent from <code>run_data</code>. HILT does not remove only the handle and leave a plausible-looking partial record.</p> <p>A value the claims need but the record must not carry, such as a session credential obtained in <code>configure()</code>, is named in <code>redact_ctx</code> on the suite class (issue #154). Each entry is a ctx key, exact or as a regular expression the key matches whole. The key appears in <code>run_data</code> as <code>&lt;REDACTED&gt;</code>, and the string under it is masked on every record surface, including a log line or a <code>TestFailure</code> the author hands it to. The record shows that the key was held; it never shows what. ctx itself is unchanged: the claims read the real value.</p> <pre><code>class BlocklistRoundTrip(TestComponent):\n    redact_ctx = (\"sid\", r\"api_.*\")\n\n    async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n        sid = await self._login(utils)\n        return {\"sid\": sid, \"api_token\": await self._token(utils, sid), \"domain\": \"x.test\"}\n\n\n# RunResult.run_data =&gt; {\n#     \"measurements\": [],\n#     \"sid\": \"&lt;REDACTED&gt;\",\n#     \"api_token\": \"&lt;REDACTED&gt;\",\n#     \"domain\": \"x.test\",\n# }\n</code></pre> <pre><code># configure returns data \u2014 it appears in run_data automatically\nasync def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n    ip = await self._resolve()\n    return {\"ip\": ip, \"hostname\": \"switch-01\"}\n\n\n# RunResult.run_data =&gt; {\n#     \"measurements\": [],\n#     \"ip\": \"10.0.0.1\",\n#     \"hostname\": \"switch-01\",\n# }\n</code></pre> <p>Override <code>run_data</code> only when you need computed or filtered output from the context. An override sees the ctx view, not the readings, which are merged when the leaf settles:</p> <pre><code>from hilt import JsonObject\n\n\n@property\ndef run_data(self) -&gt; JsonObject:\n    base = super().run_data\n    return {**base, \"within_spec\": self._check_spec(base)}\n</code></pre> <p>Note: non-JSON-safe objects (<code>datetime</code>, sets, arbitrary classes) are stripped by default. Override <code>run_data</code> if you need custom serialization for these types, and return only JSON-safe data from the override.</p> <p>Every snapshot includes <code>run_data[\"measurements\"]</code>, the list <code>utils.measure</code> fills. It is framework-owned: a ctx entry with that key does not populate or replace it, and it is written after any <code>run_data</code> override runs, so an override cannot drop it.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#measurements-utilsmeasure","title":"Measurements: <code>utils.measure</code>","text":"<p>A reading with a unit and a window that a process engineer will chart belongs in <code>utils.measure</code>, not only in the evidence payload. The call records and returns; the verdict stays whatever the gated <code>PASS</code> says, because a limit is evidence, never a verdict (<code>docs/measurements.md</code>):</p> <pre><code>from hilt import PASS, TestComponent, TestFailure, TestUtils, Verdict, evidence\n\n\nclass Rail(TestComponent):\n    @evidence(\"output_voltage_v\")\n    async def _output_rail(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n        volts = await self._read_rail(utils)\n        rail = utils.measure(\"output_voltage_v\", volts, unit=\"V\", limits=(4.75, 5.25))\n        if rail.in_limits:\n            return PASS({\"output_voltage_v\": volts})\n        raise TestFailure(\"rail outside window\", \"4.75-5.25 V\", f\"{volts} V\")\n</code></pre> <p>The gate reads in plain sight, so <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> sees the predicate and the next reader sees the criterion. Name the quantity once per node, in the unit it will always carry; the consumer keys its series on that name. Record for trend without a window by passing <code>limits=None</code>. Measure from <code>configure()</code> (a bring-up reading) or the test method only; <code>cleanup</code>, surveys, audits, and hooks are refused. <code>from hilt import measure</code> is the same call resolved to the running node when a helper does not have <code>utils</code> in hand.</p> <p>When the reading comes over a shell, parse it to <code>float | None</code> and hand <code>measure</code> the <code>None</code> instead of wrapping the parse in a <code>try</code>: a reading not taken is recorded as such, it is never inside its window, and the same gate fails the claim (issue #159). Keep <code>NaN</code> out of the call; that is a bug in the suite's arithmetic, not a blank from the device, and the framework refuses it.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#composition-factories-selftest-selfsequence-selfparallel","title":"Composition Factories: <code>self.test</code>, <code>self.sequence</code>, <code>self.parallel</code>","text":"<p><code>TestComponent</code> exposes three instance factories for building the suite. The names are the vocabulary a suite reads aloud in: a leaf is a test, dependent children run in sequence, independent children run in parallel. There is exactly one spelling for each; <code>case</code> and <code>group</code> are retired (issue #110) and <code>hilt/retired-names</code> flags them.</p> <ul> <li><code>self.test(name, fn, *, fail_after=None, abort_after=None)</code> \u2014 the leaf: one verdict   method, named here at composition time. Defaults to <code>discrete_log_file=False</code> so   entries roll up to the parent artifact.</li> <li><code>self.sequence(children, ...)</code> or <code>self.sequence(name, children, ...)</code> \u2014 serial   execution, bails on first failure, and owns a discrete timeline by default.</li> <li><code>self.parallel(children, ...)</code> or <code>self.parallel(name, children, ...)</code> \u2014 concurrent   execution via <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code>, collects all failures, and owns a discrete timeline   by default. The name says so: nobody misses that two children share a bus.</li> </ul> <p>The root of <code>build_suite()</code> takes no name. The component already has one, given at construction, and the composition <code>build_suite()</code> returns is that component's body, not a second thing to name. Write <code>self.sequence([...])</code> or <code>self.parallel([...])</code> there and the root takes the component's name; the Inspector then draws the component and its root as one row (the fold). Give a name when it says something the component's does not: every composition below the root, and a root that is a stage of its own, such as <code>self.sequence(\"burn-in\", [...])</code>.</p> <p>Only the root may go without. A nameless composition placed inside another one is a <code>ContractViolation</code> raised where it is nested, at composition and before any hardware is touched: two levels would carry one name, and siblings could share it. A name with no children, <code>self.sequence(\"stage\")</code>, is refused the same way, and the message names both forms. <code>self.sequence(self.name, [...])</code>, the spelling before the nameless form existed, still works and records exactly the same tree.</p> <p>Pass <code>discrete_log_file=False</code> to <code>self.sequence()</code> or <code>self.parallel()</code> when that composition is too small to merit a separate Inspector timeline. Its records then roll into the nearest owning ancestor, just as leaves do. Authored <code>TestComponent</code> subclasses also default to a discrete timeline and may opt into roll-up through <code>super().__init__(..., discrete_log_file=False)</code>. The root always owns a log so every run has an authoritative temporal backbone.</p> <p>Private methods pass as references directly \u2014 Python bound methods carry <code>self</code> for free.</p> <pre><code>def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n    return self.sequence(\n        [\n            self.test(\"check reachable\", self._check_reachable),\n            self.test(\"check firmware\", self._check_firmware),\n        ]\n    )\n\n\nasync def _check_reachable(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n    # `self` is preserved \u2014 bound at attribute access, no wrapping needed\n    result = await utils.exec(\"true\")\n    if result.exit_code == 0:\n        return PASS\n    raise TestFailure(\"device unreachable\", \"exit 0\", str(result.exit_code))\n</code></pre> <p>Every <code>return PASS</code> sits under the predicate that earns it; <code>hilt-check</code> (the <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> rule) flags a bare one, and the examples show the gated shape.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#enumerate-leaves-at-runtime","title":"Enumerate Leaves at Runtime","text":"<p>A leaf's name is decided where the tree is composed, not where the method is defined. One method, many leaves: pass the value that varies straight to <code>self.test()</code> after the method, and name each leaf in a comprehension. The framework calls <code>fn(utils, *args, **kwargs)</code>; <code>utils</code> is always first, and the developer's arguments follow. <code>fail_after</code> and <code>abort_after</code> are the leaf's budget, never the method's parameters. The arguments are checked against the method's signature when the tree is built, so a leaf that could never be called fails <code>build_suite</code> with a <code>ContractViolation</code> before any hardware is touched.</p> <pre><code>class Compute(TestComponent):\n    def __init__(self, *, name: str, cpu_count: int) -&gt; None:\n        super().__init__(name=name)\n        self._cpu_count = cpu_count\n\n    async def _cpu_stress(self, utils: TestUtils, *, cpu: int) -&gt; Verdict: ...\n\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return self.parallel(\n            [\n                self.test(f\"cpu{i}_stress\", self._cpu_stress, cpu=i, fail_after=120.0)\n                for i in range(self._cpu_count)\n            ]\n        )\n</code></pre> <p>The comprehension maps a known population (every core, every rail, every channel) onto leaves; it does not choose among them. A comprehension with an <code>if</code> filter decides at build time which claims exist, and <code>hilt/pure-build-suite</code> flags it. The population is identity, like a hook: it comes in through <code>__init__</code>, never out of <code>configure()</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#selftest-or-configure","title":"<code>self.test()</code> or <code>configure()</code>?","text":"<p>A long operation such as a firmware flash or a provisioning step is a <code>self.test(...)</code> when the Floor Technician should see it in the report with its own verdict and its own budget. When it is preparation the report should not itemize, it belongs in <code>configure()</code>, where a <code>TestFailure</code> means \"the device did not reach a testable state\" (see overview.md) and the node reads Fail without a phantom leaf. Ask whether the Floor Technician acts differently when this step fails on its own line. If so, it is a leaf.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#time-budgets-name-the-meaning-of-too-long","title":"Time Budgets: Name the Meaning of \u201cToo Long\u201d","text":"<p>Declare a budget on the composition line that owns the work. Use <code>fail_after</code> when missing the time limit is a negative hardware verdict, and <code>abort_after</code> when the harness should stop waiting without claiming the hardware failed.</p> <pre><code>def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n    return self.parallel(\n        [\n            self.test(\"BMC ready\", self._bmc_ready, fail_after=45.0),\n            self.test(\"optional telemetry\", self._telemetry, abort_after=10.0),\n        ],\n        fail_after=120.0,\n    )\n\n\nresult = await rack.run(abort_after=300.0)\n</code></pre> <p>The run budget bounds the whole rack. The parallel node and its leaves may tighten that bound; they cannot lengthen it. If the 45-second leaf is reached with only 8 seconds left on the run clock, the run clock fires after those 8 seconds and its <code>abort_after</code> state applies. The resulting <code>timeout</code> evidence names <code>reason=\"timeout\"</code>, the declared budget, active elapsed time, and the root-to-node path of the winning clock.</p> <p>The timed window is configure through the test method or last child. Cleanup retains its separate finalization allowance. Root-owned <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> hooks are excluded from every time-budget clock, so a slow shop-floor database cannot manufacture a hardware Fail. In a parallel node, however, a sibling still inside its timed window keeps the shared ancestor clock moving; another sibling's hook or cleanup cannot lend it extra time. Do not catch or suppress <code>asyncio.CancelledError</code> around awaited work.</p> <p>A deadline closes only transports opened by the cut node. An inherited route remains the parent's resource; this is what keeps a timed-out DUT from taking down a healthy sibling behind the same gateway.</p> <p>A gateway parent may declare the same budget pair. Its clock begins when it enters <code>configure</code>; if the hop is not open by then, the Fail or Aborted state and its timeout evidence are the gateway's own, and the devices behind it are never asked.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#passfailaborted-contract","title":"Pass/Fail/Aborted Contract","text":"<ul> <li>A test method returning <code>PASS</code>, or <code>PASS(evidence)</code> with its readings \u2192 test passes (state <code>Pass</code>). The check is <code>isinstance(result, Verdict)</code>, and only <code>PASS</code> mints one. The string <code>\"PASS\"</code> does not pass, and a <code>PASS</code> missing a key its <code>@evidence</code> declared is a contract violation, not a pass.</li> <li>A test method raising <code>TestFailure</code> (or subclass) \u2192 test fails with structured data (state <code>Fail</code>)</li> <li>A test method raising any other exception \u2192 <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (banner <code>CONTRACT VIOLATION</code>, <code>bug: true</code>): the developer didn't model this failure mode. The node's log carries the traceback under the banner, starting at the suite's own code</li> <li>A test method returning anything other than the <code>PASS</code> sentinel (including falling through with <code>None</code>) \u2192 node-local <code>Aborted</code>. No verdict was produced, so the framework refuses to forge one. Siblings are unaffected.</li> <li>An integration hook (<code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code>) throwing anywhere in the tree \u2192 state <code>Aborted</code> on the enclosing node AND every ancestor</li> </ul> <p><code>Aborted</code> is a peer state to <code>Pass</code> and <code>Fail</code>, not a flavor of failure. It signals that no verdict was produced \u2014 either the harness around the test broke, or the test method finished without rendering one. The Floor Technician's recovery protocol is \"check the integration layer / the test method,\" not \"check the hardware.\"</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#constructor-only-hooks-use_survey-and-use_audit","title":"Constructor-Only Hooks: <code>use_survey</code> and <code>use_audit</code>","text":"<p>Survey and audit hooks are registered in <code>__init__</code>, not in <code>configure()</code>. They are stackable \u2014 multiple calls register multiple independent hooks, each firing on its own schedule.</p> <pre><code>class ComputeBlade(TestComponent):\n    def __init__(self, params: TestParams) -&gt; None:\n        super().__init__(params)\n\n        # Passive snapshot at every lifecycle phase boundary\n        self.use_survey(self._dmesg_snapshot)\n\n        # Active monitoring that can fail the test\n        self.use_audit(interval=5.0, fn=self._check_thermals)\n</code></pre> <p>Why constructor-registration, not configure-registration? Survey and audit are part of the test's identity \u2014 stable across runs, visible on the class \u2014 not runtime-configured state. Registering them in the constructor makes them discoverable and keeps <code>configure()</code> focused on runtime wiring.</p> <p>Survey errors are swallowed (never affect the verdict). Audit errors are real failures \u2014 a throwing audit cancels in-flight test methods immediately and fails the test.</p> <p>Scheduling a survey. A survey fires at all six <code>Phase</code> boundaries unless the constructor says otherwise. A survey that reads the device through <code>utils.exec</code> has no device to read at <code>pre-configure</code>, before <code>use_shell</code> has run; its dispatch there execs on the test host and records the developer's machine as device evidence. Say which boundaries the survey observes, and guard the route:</p> <pre><code>from hilt import TestComponent, Phase, TestUtils\n\n\nclass PiHole(TestComponent):\n    def __init__(self, *, name: str) -&gt; None:\n        super().__init__(name=name)\n        self.use_survey(\n            self._ftl_tail,\n            phases=[phase for phase in Phase if phase &gt;= Phase.POST_CONFIGURE],\n        )\n\n    async def _ftl_tail(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; object:\n        if utils.exec_route == \"local\":\n            return None  # no device on the other end of exec at this boundary\n        return (await utils.exec(\"tail -n 50 /var/log/pihole/FTL.log\")).stdout\n</code></pre> <p>Both halves are needed. The phase list is the schedule; the route check is the guard. Order is not reach: when <code>configure</code> fails before <code>use_shell</code> returns, the engine skips <code>post-configure</code>, <code>pre-test</code>, and <code>post-test</code> and still fires <code>pre-cleanup</code> and <code>post-cleanup</code>, and at those two <code>utils.exec</code> is on the test host. <code>utils.exec_route</code> answers <code>\"local\"</code> there and the hop's endpoint (<code>\"10.10.10.10\"</code>) once the route is up, so a survey that compares against it never tails the wrong machine. An empty <code>phases=</code> is a <code>ValueError</code> at registration, not a survey that silently never fires.</p> <p>Survey artifacts are named for the lifecycle dispatch point and hook index. Audit artifacts use the same <code>survey</code> artifact type and add the audit hook and dispatch indexes to the name. Every non-<code>None</code> return is captured, including empty containers. Python cannot distinguish an explicit or delegated <code>None</code> return from fall-through at runtime, so <code>None</code> writes only an informational log entry. A hook that needs to preserve a deliberate null observation calls <code>await utils.capture_artifact(Artifact(..., data=None))</code> directly. Once a hook produces an observation, framework shutdown waits for its artifact publication within the bounded evidence budget, even under repeated cancellation. Audit intervals must be positive and finite, and the framework stops every audit loop as soon as the authored suite settles.</p> <p>See <code>examples/hardware_device_lifecycle.py</code> for survey and audit around a device with real state transitions \u2014 rail up in <code>configure</code>, thermal audit during the claim, rail down in <code>cleanup</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#integration-hooks-before_each-after_each-on-the-run-params","title":"Integration Hooks: <code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> on the Run Params","text":"<p>For composing HILT into a larger harness (shop-floor DB, lab coordinator, audit pipeline), pass <code>before_each</code> and <code>after_each</code> to the root <code>run()</code> call:</p> <pre><code>await root.run(\n    before_each=lambda ctx: shopfloor_db.record_started(\n        fn=ctx.bread_crumbs,\n        station=ctx.env.get(\"STATION_ID\"),\n        at=datetime.now(UTC),\n    ),\n    after_each=lambda ctx: shopfloor_db.record_finished(\n        fn=ctx.bread_crumbs,\n        state=ctx.result.state,\n        failure=ctx.result.failure.fail_message if ctx.result.failure else None,\n        at=datetime.now(UTC),\n    ),\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Register once at the root \u2014 the framework fires them around every executed test in the tree. The hook context is deliberately narrow (node name, <code>ancestor_names</code>, <code>bread_crumbs</code>, <code>logger</code>, <code>env</code>) \u2014 NOT <code>exec</code> or <code>get_shell</code>. Integration hooks are for external coordination, not for exercising the hardware.</p> <p>Pair invariant: <code>after_each</code> fires even when <code>before_each</code> threw. Register them as a pair or pass <code>allow_unpaired_hooks=True</code> to opt out of the warning.</p> <p>The exception-path behavior is: throw from either hook \u2192 run halts \u2192 every enclosing node transitions to <code>Aborted</code> \u2192 every in-flight SSH session and subprocess is killed immediately. A 30-minute firmware flash mid-abort must NOT continue to mutate the hardware.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#configure-returns-context-pattern","title":"Configure-Returns-Context Pattern","text":"<p><code>configure()</code> returns a plain dict; the framework merges it into <code>utils.ctx</code> for test methods, survey hooks, <code>cleanup()</code>, and all descendant leaves, then closes it. No instance fields are needed for configure-phase data.</p> <p>The return value is the contract with the test methods: it names every key they read with <code>[]</code>, and it is all they will ever find there. Seed no placeholders \u2014 a reading belongs on the verdict, not in ctx \u2014 and nothing writes ctx after configure returns. <code>hilt-check</code> enforces both statically as <code>hilt/ctx-provenance</code>; at run time a <code>[]</code> read of a missing key, or any later write, is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the test and the key, while <code>.get()</code> and <code>in</code> read an optional key (issues #112, #118).</p> <p>The framework annotation is <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code>; a plain <code>dict[str, Any]</code> return is the default and satisfies it. When a suite benefits from precise key types, define a local <code>TypedDict</code> and use it as the covariant return annotation \u2014 pyright strict accepts the override because a <code>TypedDict</code> is a <code>Mapping</code>, which <code>dict[str, Any]</code> as the base annotation would not allow. This is static information only: a <code>TypedDict</code> is still an ordinary mutable dict at runtime, and the framework copies it into <code>utils.ctx</code>. A context dataclass is deliberately out of contract because it would fight dict merge semantics and require special handling in the default <code>run_data</code> path.</p> <pre><code>class DeviceContext(TypedDict):\n    ssh: RemoteInterface\n    token: str\n\n\nasync def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; DeviceContext: ...\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"idioms.html#carrying-the-same-type-into-the-test-methods","title":"Carrying the same type into the test methods","text":"<p>A <code>TypedDict</code> return tells the reader the shape, but <code>utils.ctx</code> still hands the test methods a <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code>, so a misspelled key survives pyright and reaches the floor as a <code>ContextInsufficient</code> contract violation \u2014 a Fail under the bug banner, where the author meant to take a reading (issue #112). Naming the type once on the class closes that gap: declare <code>class MySuite(TestComponent[MyCtx])</code> and annotate the lifecycle methods <code>utils: TestUtils[MyCtx]</code>, and <code>utils.ctx[\"volts\"]</code> is a <code>float</code> to the editor while <code>utils.ctx[\"volt\"]</code> is a type error before it is ever run (issue #75).</p> <pre><code>class PowerCtx(TypedDict):\n    rail: str\n    volts: float\n\n\nclass PowerSuite(TestComponent[PowerCtx]):\n    async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils[PowerCtx]) -&gt; PowerCtx:\n        return {\"rail\": \"3v3\", \"volts\": 3.3}\n\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return self.test(\"rail\", self._rail_is_up)\n\n    async def _rail_is_up(self, utils: TestUtils[PowerCtx]) -&gt; Verdict:\n        if utils.ctx[\"volts\"] &gt; 3.0:  # a float here, not Any\n            return PASS\n        raise TestFailure(\"rail low\", \"&gt; 3.0V\", f\"{utils.ctx['volts']}V\")\n</code></pre> <p>This is sugar, and it is opt-in in both directions. Writing <code>TestComponent</code> and <code>TestUtils</code> bare means what it always did \u2014 the parameter defaults to <code>Mapping[str, Any]</code> \u2014 so no existing suite has to change, and a suite that adopts the parameter is making a claim to its own reader, not to the framework. Nothing about the run moves: the context object is the framework's <code>RunContext</code>, a <code>dict[str, Any]</code> it owns; a key <code>configure()</code> never provided is still a runtime <code>ContractViolation</code> rather than a static one; and <code>run_data</code> still strips class instances, functions, and live handles value by value.</p> <p>Composition stays unparameterized on purpose. <code>self.test()</code>, <code>self.sequence()</code>, and <code>self.parallel()</code> speak of <code>TestComponent</code> nodes of any context shape, because a rack composes suites that do not share one context \u2014 pinning them to a single <code>CtxT</code> would make the parameter contagious rather than opt-in.</p> <pre><code>class MyDevice(TestComponent):\n    # configure ALWAYS returns the context your test provides to its\n    # lifecycle methods and children. This tells future devs exactly what\n    # context your test requires and what it supplies \u2014 important, for\n    # example, for ssh chaining, which requires an \"ssh\" entry in ctx.\n    async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n        # utils.env.require raises TestFailure when the variable is unset or\n        # blank: a verdict from configure \u2014 the test lands in Fail without the\n        # suite ever running. The verdict means \"did not reach a testable\n        # state\"; whether the cause is config/infra or DUT is the human's call\n        # from logs and survey artifacts. Model as many failure modes as\n        # possible so they surface as verdicts rather than bugs. utils.env.port\n        # does the same for a port, defaulting to 22 and rejecting a typo.\n        # utils.env.int and utils.env.float do it for a number: unset uses the\n        # default when one was given, and a bad value Fails naming the variable\n        # and the value.\n        username = utils.env.require(\"TARGET_USER\")\n        password = utils.env.require(\"TARGET_PASSWORD\")\n        address = utils.env.require(\"TARGET_ADDRESS\")\n        shell = await utils.use_shell(host=address, username=username, password=password)\n        # use_shell wires utils.exec to route through SSH automatically\n        return {\"ssh\": shell.ssh, \"token\": await self._authenticate()}\n\n    # pass or fail, cleanup is GUARANTEED to run at the end of the lifecycle.\n    # Use it to close any opened connections, put the device into a neutral state, etc.\n    # It runs after a configure Fail too, so read ctx with .get(): \"token\" may never\n    # have been returned.\n    async def cleanup(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; None:\n        pass  # ssh auto-disconnected by framework, after the post-cleanup survey\n\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return self.sequence(\n            [\n                self.test(\"survey\", self._capture_survey),\n                self.test(\"reachable\", self._check_reachable),\n            ]\n        )\n\n    async def _check_reachable(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n        # utils.exec already routed through ssh\n        result = await utils.exec(\"true\")\n        if result.exit_code == 0:\n            return PASS\n        raise TestFailure(\"device unreachable\", \"exit 0\", str(result.exit_code))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"idioms.html#composability","title":"Composability","text":"<p>A device-level test can be dropped into a rack-level test without modification. \"Push state up\" to higher-level components; trust the framework lifecycle.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#environment-variables","title":"Environment Variables","text":"<p>Always use <code>utils.env</code> inside test code. Never <code>os.environ</code> directly. The env snapshot is stored in every <code>RunResult</code> for post-run auditing \u2014 filtered by the run's <code>env_allow</code> or <code>env_deny</code> list when one was declared, verbatim otherwise (see <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>). <code>text</code>, <code>require</code>, and <code>port</code> read a string, a required string, and a TCP port. <code>int</code> and <code>float</code> read a number the same way: a bad value Fails the run naming the variable and the value, and a missing one uses the <code>default</code> when the call passed one.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#testutils-injection","title":"TestUtils Injection","text":"<p>Call <code>await root.run()</code> to execute a suite. The framework constructs <code>TestUtils</code>; suite authors import it for lifecycle annotations, not as plumbing they must assemble.</p> <p>Every node receives a distinct <code>TestUtils</code> object with a logger scoped to that node's full root-to-leaf path. Leaves, sequences, and parallel nodes built by one authored instance share that instance's <code>utils.ctx</code> dict, while a composed <code>TestComponent</code> subclass starts with a fresh context. The environment snapshot is immutable and shared across the run; the halt signal is read-only from suite code.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#logging","title":"Logging","text":"<ul> <li><code>utils.logger</code> is scoped per node \u2014 never <code>print()</code>, so evidence is captured in artifacts</li> <li>Artifacts captured automatically</li> <li><code>discrete_log_file=False</code> rolls log entries into the parent logger. <code>discrete_log_file=True</code> (the default on class-based tests) gives the node its own log file. Inline <code>self.test()</code> bodies default to <code>False</code>.</li> <li>Every full run creates a timestamped directory under the configured artifact root.   A logging owner streams both <code>timeline.log</code> for humans and versioned   <code>timeline.jsonl</code> for the Inspector, flushing both on every entry. Treat   <code>TestComponent.log_path</code> as the authoritative path; directory names are not an API.</li> <li>While a durable timeline is attached, <code>utils.logger.isEnabledFor(...)</code> is true at   every level so developer-side guards cannot suppress the authoritative chronology.   Process logging handlers still apply their own level policy.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"idioms.html#point-in-time-artifacts","title":"Point-in-Time Artifacts","text":"<p>Construct an immutable <code>Artifact</code> and pass it to <code>utils.capture_artifact()</code>. The method persists the JSON artifact under <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, then emits a structured pointer through the same scoped logger that records the surrounding test events. <code>Artifact</code> deliberately has no <code>write()</code> or <code>write_file()</code> method: a file without its pointer would be absent from the authoritative chronology.</p> <pre><code>from hilt import Artifact\n\nsnapshot = Artifact(\n    name=\"pre-flash\",\n    type=\"hardware-survey\",\n    data={\"firmware\": \"2.4.1\", \"healthy\": True},\n)\nartifact_path = await utils.capture_artifact(snapshot)\n</code></pre> <p>Artifact names and types accept letters, numbers, dashes, and underscores. Payloads must be JSON serializable. Capture is async, never overwrites an existing artifact, and returns the absolute path recorded in the pointer entry.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#runresult-navigation-ancestor_names-and-bread_crumbs","title":"RunResult Navigation \u2014 <code>ancestor_names</code> and <code>bread_crumbs</code>","text":"<p>Every <code>RunResult</code> carries the node's position in the tree:</p> <ul> <li><code>ancestor_names: list[str]</code> \u2014 canonical form, root-to-this inclusive. Use for programmatic aggregation (DB keys, leaf identity).</li> <li><code>bread_crumbs: str</code> \u2014 <code>\"/\".join(ancestor_names)</code> precomputed. Use for display, log lines, error messages.</li> <li><code>summary_tree: SummaryTreeNode</code> \u2014 snapshot of this node's subtree at completion. Enables downstream integrators to reconstruct the full Inspector tree from the root's RunResult alone.</li> </ul> <p>The versioned payload and recursive node fields are defined in <code>inspector-json.md</code>.</p> <p>A full <code>run()</code> also persists the root payload automatically as <code>result.json</code> in that run's artifact directory. Authored suites should consume the returned <code>RunResult</code> when they need the live value; they never write the canonical Inspector file themselves.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#fractal-design-hilt-exposes-a-single-class","title":"Fractal Design: HILT exposes a single class","text":"Need Use Physical hardware component <code>TestComponent</code> Networked device (MAC-based) <code>TestComponent</code> + <code>utils.get_arp_resolver()</code> in configure Device reached over SSH, connection shared across tests <code>TestComponent</code> with configure returning <code>{\"ssh\": ...}</code> Device behind a jump host or OOB gateway The gateway is a <code>TestComponent</code> whose <code>configure()</code> opens the hop and whose <code>build_suite()</code> composes the device Standalone test procedure <code>TestComponent</code> <p>The Python <code>examples/</code> tree is the authoring reference. A broken example means the API changed and the docs need updating.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#selfsequence-vs-selfparallel","title":"<code>self.sequence()</code> vs <code>self.parallel()</code>","text":"<p><code>self.sequence()</code> \u2014 serial, stops on the first failure. Use when later tests depend on earlier ones. The Floor Technician sees exactly where the chain broke and nothing beyond it.</p> <p><code>self.parallel()</code> \u2014 concurrent via <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code>, collects all failures. Use when tests are fully independent. The Floor Technician sees everything broken at once. One child's Fail never cancels its siblings \u2014 Fail is returned, not raised, at the child boundary; only a harness-level abort cancels the parallel node, and then cancelling everything is exactly what Aborted means.</p> <p>Independent claims can still share one resource. A single <code>DeviceApi</code> handle opened in <code>configure()</code> and read from ctx by every leaf serialises their requests: the leaves run concurrently, their requests go out one at a time in call order, and a latency measured around <code>request</code> includes the wait for siblings. That is correct when the claim is about the bytes; when the claim is about the DUT's own latency, give each leaf its own handle.</p> <p>The same holds for a routed console. <code>use_serial(device=...)</code> in <code>configure()</code> binds <code>utils.exec</code> for the node and its descendants to one <code>SerialInterface</code>, and <code>parallel</code> leaves under it take turns on it in call order (<code>docs/hil/serial.md</code>, Serial Turn Contract). A console costs more to share than a handle does: the leaves share one shell's state, so a <code>cd</code> or an exported variable in one leaf is visible to the next. Claims that need isolation want one console each, via <code>get_serial(device=...)</code>.</p> <p>An SSH hop is shared differently: each <code>utils.exec</code> opens its own session on the one connection, so <code>parallel</code> leaves really do run at once on the device, up to the number of sessions its sshd grants per connection (<code>MaxSessions</code>, 10 by default). Past that the sshd refuses the session, and a healthy device reads Fail with <code>transport closed: open failed</code>. A hop with a wide tree under it declares the bound where it opens the connection, <code>use_shell(host=..., username=..., max_sessions=8)</code>, and commands past the bound take turns in call order. The leaves stay <code>parallel</code>: they are independent, and the bound is a property of the hop, not a dependency between claims. Do not reach for <code>sequence</code> to stay under the limit; it would stop at the first failure and hide the rest.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#arpresolver-mac-to-ip","title":"ArpResolver \u2014 MAC to IP","text":"<p><code>ArpResolver</code> is a composable utility for resolving a device's IP from its MAC. Create one via <code>utils.get_arp_resolver()</code> in <code>configure()</code>, call <code>resolve()</code>, and return both the resolved data and the resolver into ctx. Test methods read the IP and the resolver from <code>utils.ctx</code>. Call <code>arp.ping(utils)</code> for reachability. Call <code>arp.reacquire(utils)</code> after anything that may change the IP (reboot, firmware flash, DHCP churn): it returns the new address and the resolver remembers it, so a later <code>ping</code> dials the address it last resolved while ctx stays as <code>configure()</code> left it. Carry the new address as evidence on the verdict if the Floor Technician should see it.</p> <p><code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> is not a <code>TestFailure</code>. Catch it in <code>configure</code> and re-raise as <code>TestFailure</code> so a missing ARP entry is a verdict. Uncaught, it is a developer bug \u2014 the suite did not model the failure mode.</p> <pre><code># In configure():\narp = utils.get_arp_resolver(mac=self._mac)\ntry:\n    resolved = await arp.resolve()\nexcept ArpResolutionFailure as error:\n    raise TestFailure(\n        f\"{error.mac} did not appear on the network\",\n        error.mac,\n        error.host,\n    ) from error\nreturn {**resolved, \"arp\": arp}\n\n# In build_suite():\nself.test(\"reachability\", self._reachability)\n</code></pre> <p>See <code>examples/networked_device.py</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#arpresolver-behind-a-gateway","title":"ArpResolver Behind a Gateway","text":"<p>When the test host cannot reach the target network directly, the SE code is identical to the local case. Compose the device under a gateway parent whose <code>configure()</code> opened the hop, and the framework routes the lookup \u2014 the SE never touches SSH.</p> <pre><code># DutDevice.configure() \u2014 no SSH awareness, identical to local ArpResolver usage:\narp = utils.get_arp_resolver(mac=self._mac)\nresolved = await arp.resolve()\nreturn {**resolved, \"arp\": arp}\n\n# Gateway.build_suite() \u2014 the parent names what sits behind its hop:\nreturn self.sequence([self.test(\"gateway-up\", self._gateway_up), DutDevice(name=\"dut\")])\n</code></pre> <p>See <code>examples/networked_device.py</code>. <code>hilt run ...::DutDevice</code> reads the test host's ARP table; <code>hilt run ...::Gateway</code> runs the same DUT class through the hop.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#ssh-device-class-owned-connection","title":"SSH Device \u2014 Class-Owned Connection","text":"<p>Extend <code>TestComponent</code> when one SSH connection must be shared across all of the device's leaves. Open in <code>configure()</code> via <code>utils.use_shell()</code> and let the framework route <code>utils.exec</code> through it automatically.</p> <p>Returning <code>{\"ssh\": ...}</code> from <code>configure()</code> also makes the device eligible as a <code>via</code> target. Pass it as <code>via</code> in <code>TestParams</code> \u2014 the framework manages the lifecycle and scopes <code>utils.exec</code> automatically.</p> <p><code>use_shell</code> vs <code>get_shell</code>: <code>use_shell</code> answers \"where does <code>utils.exec</code> go\" \u2014 it rebinds the routing for this test and its descendants. <code>get_shell</code> hands you an additional interface without touching the routing.</p> <p>A console by device path is the same idiom: <code>await utils.use_serial(device=\"/dev/ttyUSB0\")</code> in <code>configure()</code> declares it as the node's exec route and <code>get_serial</code> opens one as an auxiliary endpoint; both hand back a <code>Shell</code>, the framework closes the port after the post-cleanup survey and from the abort sweep, and no transport is imported or carried in ctx. Behind a hop, pass an SSH <code>Shell</code> as <code>via</code> or let the console inherit the parent's <code>use_shell</code> route. The policy-only shapes (<code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) stay with <code>use_shell</code>.</p> <p>See <code>examples/remote_device_ssh.py</code>. The pi-hole dogfood suite (<code>examples/pi_hole_server.py</code>) is the same class-owned hop plus phase-boundary surveys that tail service logs for the Floor Technician.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#ssh-tunneling","title":"SSH Tunneling","text":"<p>Pass the <code>Shell</code> an earlier <code>utils.get_shell()</code> returned as the <code>via</code> argument to the next <code>utils.get_shell()</code> to tunnel the new connection through it. Both interfaces are framework-managed \u2014 no <code>cleanup()</code> override is needed.</p> <pre><code>jump = await utils.get_shell(host=jump_host, username=jump_user)\ntarget = await utils.get_shell(host=target_host, username=target_user, via=jump)\n</code></pre> <p>See <code>examples/ssh_tunneling.py</code>.</p>"},{"location":"idioms.html#device-api-coaphttp","title":"Device API \u2014 CoAP/HTTP","text":"<p>A device API is not <code>exec</code> with a URL. Open a session from <code>configure()</code> with <code>utils.get_http</code> or <code>utils.get_coap</code>, then <code>request</code> a typed <code>DeviceRequest</code>. The response's <code>status</code> is data \u2014 HTTP 4xx/5xx is not an exception, and neither is a CoAP 4.xx. On HTTP, <code>status</code> is the integer. On CoAP it is the dotted code (<code>CoapCode.CONTENT</code> is <code>\"2.05\"</code>), which is what the record stores when the claim puts <code>response.status</code> on the verdict. Catch <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> when the transfer itself did not complete (timeout, refused connection) and model it as a <code>TestFailure</code>.</p> <p>Retries default to zero. Each declared attempt is logged. POST refuses retry unless <code>retry_non_idempotent=True</code>. Attempt logs keep header names and withhold every value unless the author lists that name in <code>evidence_headers</code>. Abort closes the session; it does not invent a shutdown PUT.</p> <p>One handle carries one request at a time. Leaves under a <code>parallel</code> that share the handle <code>configure()</code> opened queue on it in call order \u2014 nothing collides and nothing is refused \u2014 so a round trip timed in the leaf includes the queue. A request still waiting when the handle is cancelled or closed is never sent; it fails with <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code>, the same class as any other transfer that did not complete. Measure the DUT alone with one handle per leaf.</p> <pre><code>api = await utils.get_http(base_url=\"https://dut.local\")\nresponse = await api.request(DeviceRequest(method=\"GET\", path=\"/whoami\"))\nif response.status != 200:\n    raise TestFailure(\"whoami rejected\", 200, response.status)\nreturn PASS({\"status\": response.status})\n</code></pre> <p>The backends stay off the public surface, the way <code>SshInterface</code> does.</p> <p>For a JSON body, declare <code>json=</code> instead of encoding bytes and setting a header by hand. Read it back with <code>.json()</code>, or select the parser with <code>.parse(response_type=\"json\")</code> / <code>.parse(response_type=\"yaml\")</code> (YAML needs <code>open-hilt[yaml]</code>). Both leave the original bytes and status available. Parser errors propagate, so model a malformed response as <code>TestFailure</code> when it is a DUT failure mode. Neither the transfer nor the parser chooses a verdict.</p> <pre><code>response = await api.request(DeviceRequest(method=\"PUT\", path=\"/settings\", json={\"enabled\": True}))\nif response.status == 200 and response.json().get(\"enabled\") is True:\n    return PASS({\"enabled\": True})\nraise TestFailure(\"device did not enable\", \"enabled\", response.status)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"idioms.html#wait_for-patterns","title":"wait_for Patterns","text":"<p>Where <code>wait_for()</code> is called determines what the timeout means, not what gets raised \u2014 both raise <code>TestFailure</code>:</p> <ul> <li>In <code>configure()</code> \u2014 timeout becomes a <code>TestFailure</code> that halts the lifecycle. The test lands in Fail; the suite never runs. The verdict means \"the device did not reach a testable state.\" Whether the cause is hardware or infrastructure is not knowable from code \u2014 the human triager makes that call from logs and survey artifacts.</li> <li>In a test method \u2014 timeout becomes a <code>TestFailure</code> that is the verdict itself. The device was reachable but failed to reach the expected state.</li> </ul> <p><code>looking_for</code> is required and must be plain language \u2014 it becomes the Floor Technician's primary diagnostic string when a wait times out. The call returns the predicate's truthy value (<code>True</code> stays <code>True</code>); a claim that needs what was observed returns it from the predicate instead of closing over a list.</p> <p>The runnable <code>examples/wait_for_patterns.py</code> ports both placements from the legacy example. It also shows how polling observations flow through scoped logs, phase-boundary surveys, point-in-time artifacts, <code>run_data</code>, and the root <code>summary_tree</code> without adding another evidence API.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html","title":"Inspector JSON Contract","text":"<p>Floor Technicians reading a run should start at <code>docs/floor-technician.md</code>. This page is the consumer contract those fields live in.</p> <p>The Inspector (<code>hilt inspect</code>, <code>docs/inspector.md</code>) reads the record this page specifies, and any other consumer may read it the same way. Every completed node produces a <code>RunResult</code>, and <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> returns a plain Python object that <code>json.dumps()</code> accepts without an encoder. The root result is self-contained: consumers do not need the live <code>TestComponent</code> objects to reconstruct the run tree. CI that already speaks JUnit XML consumes a projection of the same tree (<code>docs/junit-xml.md</code>); that reporter is not a second evidence record.</p> <p>Every full <code>TestComponent.run()</code> atomically writes the root <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> payload to <code>result.json</code> in the timestamped run directory beneath <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>. The file is present for Pass, Fail, and Aborted outcomes. Its absolute path is published by the final entry in the root node's timeline, with artifact name <code>result</code> and artifact type <code>run-result</code>; consumers do not need to derive the run directory name.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#version-1-result","title":"Version 1 result","text":"Field JSON type Meaning <code>format_version</code> integer Schema version; currently <code>1</code>. <code>state</code> string One terminal state: <code>Pass</code>, <code>Fail</code>, or <code>Aborted</code>. <code>failure</code> object or null JSON-safe framework failure, including nested causes. <code>timeout</code> object or null Structured time-budget evidence when a deadline determined or contributed to this state. <code>test_case</code> string Stable name of this node. <code>type</code> string <code>suite</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>parallel</code>, or <code>sequence</code>: the node shape, in the vocabulary of <code>build_suite</code>. <code>ancestor_names</code> array of strings Root-to-this path, inclusive. <code>bread_crumbs</code> string Display path, exactly <code>ancestor_names.join(\"/\")</code>. <code>summary_tree</code> object Recursive snapshot rooted at this node. <code>physical_location</code> string <code>PHYSICAL_LOCATION</code>, or <code>unknown</code> when absent. <code>env</code> object Environment snapshot taken at the start of the run, filtered per the run's allow or deny list; see below. <code>run_data</code> object Detached structured evidence snapshot: the JSON-safe view of the node's ctx, plus, on a leaf, the evidence its verdict carried. It always contains a framework-owned <code>measurements</code> array: one entry per <code>utils.measure</code> call on this node, in call order, empty when the node measured nothing. <code>suite_target</code> string or null The loader target that produced the run, as <code>hilt run</code> resolved it: an absolute suite file or module name, <code>::</code>, and the suite class name. <code>hilt inspect</code> loads this suite for its Re-Run tab, so the technician re-runs a node with the code that ran in production; <code>--suite</code> overrides it. <code>null</code> when the run was started without one, from a developer's own harness. Added within version 1 on 2026-09-10; a consumer written before it treats the field as additive. <p>A measurement entry is <code>{name, value, unit, limits, in_limits, timestamp}</code> as <code>docs/measurements.md</code> defines it: <code>value</code> is a number, or <code>null</code> when the suite tried to take the reading and could not (issue #159; a hole in the series, never a zero), <code>limits</code> is <code>{low, high}</code> with <code>null</code> for an open side or <code>null</code> altogether, <code>in_limits</code> is <code>true</code>, <code>false</code> (always <code>false</code> for a <code>null</code> value against a window), or <code>null</code> when no window was given, and <code>timestamp</code> is UTC ending in <code>Z</code>. <code>in_limits</code> is evidence, never a verdict; version 1 defines no aggregation, SPC, or Cpk, and an integrator must not infer them from the entries. Because <code>result.json</code> is the root's record, the entries a leaf recorded reach the file through <code>measurements</code> on its <code>summary_tree</code> node, not through this <code>run_data</code>.</p> <p>Python-only lifecycle details such as the internal slot and raw return value are not part of this payload. Consumers must treat unknown fields as additive and select a decoder by <code>format_version</code> (see Versioning policy).</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#the-env-snapshot-is-filtered-by-the-run-not-by-the-schema","title":"The <code>env</code> snapshot is filtered by the run, not by the schema","text":"<p>Version 1 publishes the run environment as the run declared it. <code>TestComponent.run()</code> and <code>TestUtils</code> accept either <code>env_allow</code> or <code>env_deny</code> (both is a <code>ValueError</code>): a list of regular expressions matched with <code>re.search</code> against each variable's name. <code>TOKEN</code> therefore matches <code>GITLAB_TOKEN</code>; anchor as <code>^HOME$</code> for an exact name. With an allow list only matching names are published; with a deny list matching names are withheld. Values are never hashed or rewritten \u2014 a variable is either present verbatim or absent.</p> <p>With neither list the snapshot is verbatim: every value present at run start, including tokens or credentials injected by CI or the shell, is serialized into the artifact. Keeping secrets out of an unfiltered run environment is the operator's obligation.</p> <p>DEBUG override. When the source environment carries <code>environment=DEBUG</code>, the declared list is ignored and every variable is published. The run logs a warning \u2014 <code>DEBUG mode detected, ignoring provided &lt;allow|deny&gt; list and including ALL environment variables</code> \u2014 so the artifact's extra content is explained in its own timeline.</p> <p>The filter is applied once, at the root, and every node and every <code>RunResult</code> sees the same filtered view. <code>PHYSICAL_LOCATION</code> and <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code> are read from that view, so an allow list must let them through to keep their effect. Decoders must not assume any particular variable is present (ruling on issue #53, 2026-09-03).</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#version-1-timeline","title":"Version 1 timeline","text":"<p>Each <code>run()</code> creates a timestamped directory directly beneath <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>. Point-in-time artifacts are stored at that run-directory root. Every executed node that owns a discrete log receives a child directory containing:</p> <ul> <li><code>timeline.log</code> \u2014 a streamed human view with one escaped line per entry;</li> <li><code>timeline.jsonl</code> \u2014 the authoritative Inspector record, with one complete JSON object   per physical line.</li> </ul> <p>Both files are flushed after each entry. Consumers may read the JSON Lines file while a run is active and retain every complete line after interruption. The child-directory name is framework-owned and must be treated as opaque; use <code>TestComponent.log_path</code> rather than reconstructing it.</p> <p>Every version-1 timeline object contains these fields:</p> Field JSON type Meaning <code>format_version</code> integer Timeline-record schema version; currently <code>1</code>. <code>timestamp</code> string Timezone-aware UTC timestamp ending in <code>Z</code>. <code>scope</code> array of strings Root-to-emitting-node path, inclusive. <code>level</code> string Lowercase Python logging level name. <code>message</code> string Fully rendered log message. Newlines remain escaped within the JSON line. <p>An artifact-pointer entry additionally contains all four fields below. They are absent from ordinary records rather than set to null.</p> Field JSON type Meaning <code>artifact_name</code> string Captured artifact name, or <code>result</code> for the root result. <code>artifact_path</code> string Absolute path of the published artifact or root <code>result.json</code>. <code>artifact_type</code> string Captured artifact type, or <code>run-result</code> for the root result. <code>artifact_timestamp</code> string The evidence timestamp in UTC; for the root result, its publication time. <p>Authored suites, sequences, and parallel nodes own a log by default. Leaves roll their records into the nearest owning ancestor. A sequence, parallel node, or composed suite constructed with <code>discrete_log_file=False</code> also rolls up. Pending nodes create no files.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#two-records-two-version-fields","title":"Two records, two version fields","text":"<p>A run therefore produces two versioned records, and each carries its own <code>format_version</code>:</p> Record Where it lives Version field Result <code>result.json</code> at the run-directory root; the serialized root <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> and structural view rooted at <code>summary_tree</code> <code>format_version</code> on the result object Timeline <code>timeline.jsonl</code> beneath the run directory; the temporal view <code>format_version</code> on every JSON Lines object <p>The two version numbers move independently. The summary tree links the structural view to the temporal view through each node's <code>log_path</code> (ruling on issue #106, 2026-09-04: the link is additive within result version 1). An Inspector selects a node in the tree, resolves <code>log_path</code> against the run directory, and reads that node's chronology.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#versioning-policy","title":"Versioning policy","text":"<p><code>format_version</code> is an integer on every record the framework emits: the result, every timeline line, and the envelope <code>Artifact.to_json()</code> writes for point-in-time evidence. One rule governs all of them.</p> <ol> <li>Additive within a version. Adding a field to a framework-owned object keeps the    number. So does adding a key that the record already declares open (<code>env</code>,    <code>run_data</code>, and the failure object). A consumer must accept fields it does not know    and must not fail on them; it reads what it was written for and ignores the rest.</li> <li>Breaking bumps the version. Removing or renaming a field, changing a field's JSON    type or nullability, changing the meaning of an existing value, adding a member to a    closed set (<code>state</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>declared</code>, the failure <code>state</code>), moving or renaming    <code>result.json</code> or <code>timeline.jsonl</code>, or changing the one-object-per-line rule bumps the    number. A bump is a contract change and follows the workflow in <code>STANDARDS.md</code>    section 7: it is raised as an issue and ruled on before its MR opens.</li> <li>Consumers declare what they accept. A decoder reads <code>format_version</code> before any    other field, accepts the versions it was written for, and refuses every other version    loudly, with an error that names the version found and the versions accepted. A    best-effort read of an unknown version is a silent misreading and is not permitted.    Within an accepted version the decoder validates against the published schema for    that version.</li> <li>The records version independently. Bumping the result does not bump the    timeline, and the reverse. The per-node <code>log_path</code> link is additive within result    version 1 (ruling on issue #106, 2026-09-04).</li> <li>Every change amends the schema in the same MR. A field added within version 1    appears in the version-1 schema and in this document before the framework emits it.    The self-tests close every framework-owned object against its schema, so an emitted    field the schema does not name is a red gate, not a silent contract change. A bump    publishes a new schema file and leaves the previous one in place: a run directory    written under version 1 stays readable by a version-1 consumer for as long as the    record exists.</li> </ol> <p>Before 1.0.0 the record may change in place under version 1, because no consumer exists to break; the <code>type</code> vocabulary was renamed this way to match the composition vocabulary (ruling on issue #111, 2026-09-06), and <code>measurements[].value</code> became nullable this way for a reading not taken (ruling on issue #159, 2026-10-20). From 1.0.0 rule 2 applies as written.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#published-schemas","title":"Published schemas","text":"Record Schema Draft Result <code>docs/schema/result-v1.schema.json</code> JSON Schema 2020-12 Timeline <code>docs/schema/timeline-v1.schema.json</code> JSON Schema 2020-12 Artifact envelope <code>docs/schema/artifact-v1.schema.json</code> JSON Schema 2020-12 <p>The published files are the consumer contract, and they are open: framework-owned objects do not forbid additional properties, so a consumer validating with the file shipped in one release accepts the additive fields of a later release within the same version. Three objects are open by design and stay open under the self-tests as well: the failure object (a <code>TestFailure</code> subclass may add fields), <code>env</code> (station-defined names), and <code>run_data</code> (suite evidence beyond the framework-owned <code>measurements</code> slot). The measurement object itself is open as well, so a field added to it within version 1 is accepted by a consumer validating with this file.</p> <p><code>tests/test_inspector_schema.py</code> validates every emitted <code>result.json</code> and every emitted timeline line against these files on each gate run, first as published and then with every framework-owned object closed. It also validates emitted point-in-time captures against the artifact-envelope schema with the envelope closed. That schema describes the existing <code>Artifact.to_json()</code> shape: <code>format_version</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, and <code>data</code>. The observation may be any JSON value, including explicit null. Additive envelope fields remain acceptable to consumers; the payload is never closed. The internal Inspector capture reader consumes this envelope under the same version policy. The root <code>run-result</code> pointer instead uses the result schema; it is not an envelope.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#summary-tree-node","title":"Summary tree node","text":"<p>Every <code>summary_tree</code> node contains exactly these version-1 fields:</p> Field JSON type Meaning <code>name</code> string Stable node name. <code>type</code> string The same node-type discriminator used by <code>RunResult</code>. <code>state</code> string or null Terminal state, or null when a declared sequence step did not run. <code>failure</code> object or null JSON-safe failure for this exact node. <code>timeout</code> object or null Structured time-budget evidence for this exact node. <code>log_path</code> string or null POSIX path of this node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code>, relative to the run directory. Null when the node never ran or when its records rolled up into an ancestor. <code>children</code> array Ordered child nodes. Groups retain declaration order after settlement. <code>evidence</code> object or null The readings this leaf's verdict carried: <code>PASS({...})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={...})</code>, validated entry by entry and detached. Null on a node that is not a leaf, that never ran, or whose verdict carried none. <code>evidence_declared</code> array of string or null The keys the leaf's <code>@evidence</code> declaration promised on <code>PASS</code>, in declaration order. A declared key absent from <code>evidence</code> is a reading that was not taken; show it by name. Null when the node is not a leaf or declared nothing. <code>measurements</code> array or null Every <code>utils.measure</code> call this node recorded, in call order, as measurement objects (<code>docs/measurements.md</code>). An array on every node that ran, empty when it measured nothing; null when the node never ran, matching <code>state</code>. A measurement does not satisfy an <code>@evidence</code> declaration and <code>in_limits: false</code> is not a Fail. <p>A null tree state means pending/unreached; it is not a fourth terminal state. A stopped sequence retains its unexecuted descendants with null states, so the root snapshot still describes the full declared tree.</p> <p><code>log_path</code> is relative so that a run directory copied to another machine keeps every link intact. Consumers resolve it against the run directory they opened, the same directory that holds the point-in-time artifacts. A node with a terminal <code>state</code> and a null <code>log_path</code> rolled its records into an ancestor; the nearest ancestor with a non-null <code>log_path</code> holds them, and the root always carries one. A node whose <code>state</code> is null never ran and has no chronology anywhere.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#failure-shape","title":"Failure shape","text":"<p>Failures use their framework <code>to_json()</code> methods. <code>TestFailure</code> and its subclasses retain the failure name, message, state, comparisons, and bug metadata. An <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> retains its hook and JSON-safe cause. <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> retains <code>operator: true</code> and the signal name so a Ctrl-C is not mistaken for a hook failure. Arbitrary exception objects and live values degrade to descriptive JSON-safe forms rather than making serialization fail and masking the evidence.</p>"},{"location":"inspector-json.html#timeout-shape","title":"Timeout shape","text":"<p>When no deadline contributed to a node's state, <code>timeout</code> is null. Otherwise it carries these fields:</p> Field JSON type Meaning <code>reason</code> string Always <code>timeout</code>. <code>declared</code> string The winning declaration: <code>fail_after</code> or <code>abort_after</code>. <code>budget_seconds</code> number Positive finite duration declared on the winning clock. <code>elapsed_seconds</code> number Active window time consumed when that clock fired. <code>clock</code> string Root-to-declaring-node path of the winning clock. <p>For <code>fail_after</code>, <code>failure</code> also contains the hardware-facing <code>TestFailure</code>. For <code>abort_after</code>, <code>failure</code> may be null because the framework does not forge a verdict; the timeout record carries the reason for the node-local Aborted state.</p> <p>Integrators should use <code>ancestor_names</code> as the durable node identity and <code>bread_crumbs</code> only for display. Names may contain <code>/</code>; the array is therefore the canonical representation.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html","title":"HILT Inspector \u2014 target specification","text":"<p>This is the first deliverable of #89. It carries the legacy Floor Technician guide into the Python port, adjusted for the signed decisions on #88, #106, and #107. It specifies the terminal UI, which <code>hilt inspect</code> now opens. The Floor Technician guide remains the station page for reading the evidence by hand. The review rulings on !119 set the implementation order: Inspect first, Run as a separate increment behind its own decision. Ty ruled on that increment on 2026-09-10: the Re-Run tab ships, the Failures list is folded into Inspect as a key that hides passing rows, and the cut between the two tabs is the one the legacy tool drew. Inspect is the archive of the production run and executes nothing. Re-Run is the bench, where a node is executed again with the code that ran in production and nothing it produces persists.</p> <p>The internal timeline reader (<code>hilt.inspector_timeline.TimelineReader</code>) is implemented as a foundation for the log pane. It asynchronously reads a selected timeline path, validates each version-1 object against the packaged published schema, and returns records and line-numbered diagnostics in physical order. Additive fields and recorded scopes and pointers survive unchanged. A complete final JSON object is readable without a trailing newline; an unfinished final object or UTF-8 sequence produces a truncation diagnostic. Malformed complete records, duplicate keys, non-finite numbers, and unsupported versions are explicit diagnostics, and later valid records remain readable. Missing or unreadable logs produce a file-level unavailable diagnostic. These are reader observations, never test verdicts. The timeline reader does not open artifact pointers or resolve tree ownership. Its <code>read_reference(run_root, reference)</code> boundary opens a recorded POSIX <code>log_path</code> only beneath the selected run directory, preserving the complete relative path after a move. Empty, absolute, traversing, missing, non-file, and symlink-escaping references produce unavailable diagnostics. The read retains the recorded reference and the resolved source path; a reference that cannot resolve has no source path. It never searches another directory for a matching basename. Timeline and capture readers share one checked, cached loader for the packaged schemas; evidence is read afresh on every call. The operator's selected run directory may itself use symlinks or <code>..</code>; the reader resolves that directory before containing recorded links.</p> <p>The internal capture reader (<code>hilt.inspector_artifacts.ArtifactReader</code>) now reads absolute pointers beneath an opened run root. An explicit, established original root enables relocation while preserving the full relative tail; without that mapping a pointer outside the opened root is unavailable. The reader checks resolved containment, refuses non-file targets, and never falls back to the original run or a basename match. It validates the artifact envelope or root result against its packaged version-1 schema, retaining additive fields and explicit null observations. Missing files, malformed JSON, and unsupported versions are diagnostics that retain the recorded reference. JSON parsing is shared with the timeline reader, so duplicate keys and non-finite values are refused consistently. The original-root mapping remains an input to this internal component; the saved-run loader establishes it from publication evidence.</p> <p>The internal tree reader (<code>hilt.inspector_tree.TreeReader</code>) now opens a selected result through that validated result boundary. It resolves the operator's selection before deriving the run root, so a symlink such as <code>latest/result.json</code> or a path with <code>..</code> opens the same saved evidence as its direct path. References inside saved records still obey the capture reader's traversal and containment checks. <code>read_selected()</code> retains the resolved run directory alongside the validated tree; <code>read()</code> returns just that tree through the same selection boundary. Unresolvable operator paths produce unavailable diagnostics in either form. Its <code>InspectorTree</code> preserves every recorded node in declaration preorder, including pending descendants, and keeps full name components rather than splitting display breadcrumbs. Stable row indices keep duplicate names distinct. Inspect selection and the Failures projection share the same rows: previous/next failure includes Fail and Aborted, skips Pass and pending, and does not wrap. The model retains failure causes, timeout fields, and additive evidence; declared readings absent from evidence are exposed by name, while null and zero remain present observations. Beside those rows the tree exposes its drawn lines (<code>InspectorTree.lines</code>): the same nodes with each same-named root folded into its component, as the fold specifies. A line carries the node the cursor selects, the nodes drawn with it, and the depth, parent, and path of the drawing. The cursor and the failure projection move over lines; indices stay those of the rows.</p> <p>For an executed selection, the model identifies its own or nearest ancestor's recorded timeline link. A pending selection has no owner and must render not run. An owner identifies a recorded link, not proof the file is available: the model opens no timeline, and its caller must still enforce run-root containment and report unavailable files.</p> <p>The internal selection model (<code>hilt.inspector_selection.InspectorSelection</code>) joins that tree to contained timeline loading for both Inspect and Failures. Its caller supplies the validated tree and the opened directory containing that same result, then navigates through the selection model. Selection clears the previous chronology before I/O, keeps the claim and its recorded owner together, and reads only through <code>read_reference</code>. A folded line has more than one recorded owner behind it; the model reads each and publishes them as one chronology merged in time order, naming the further owners. Every request has its own identity: a delayed load cannot overwrite a later selection, even if the cursor returned to the same row. Pending rows render not run; executed rows with no owner or an unreadable file render log unavailable. Complete records, their scopes, partial reads, and decoding diagnostics remain intact. Reselection reads evidence afresh. Cancellation and reader defects propagate to the caller and return only the affected current load to idle; they never restore an earlier log or become verdicts.</p> <p>The internal saved-run loader (<code>hilt.inspector_run.SavedRunReader</code>) now opens a validated result, its contained root timeline, and a capture reader for that same run. It uses <code>TreeReader.read_selected()</code> so selection errors and the resolved run boundary have one owner. The tree's chronology ownership also governs this opening: a pending root supplies no timeline or relocation origin, even if a damaged record gives it a link. It establishes the original root only from one final root-scoped pointer with <code>artifact_type=\"run-result\"</code>, <code>artifact_name=\"result\"</code>, and an absolute, non-traversing path to <code>result.json</code>. The result's ancestor names must agree with its tree root; full name components are compared without splitting breadcrumbs, and a disagreement names the result defect instead of directing the technician to the log. A missing, damaged, ambiguous, or misplaced publication leaves the original root explicitly unavailable while preserving the saved tree and timeline diagnostics. Complete records without a trailing newline remain readable. The loader never guesses from a basename or timestamp, reads the original station, or edits saved pointers. Reopening reads the evidence afresh. Without a mapping, captures already beneath the opened root remain readable, but pointers outside it cannot relocate.</p> <p>The internal timeline presentation model (<code>hilt.inspector_timeline_view.TimelineView</code>) pages a completed selection's chronology without changing its recorded data. It opens at the tail with filtering off; toggling errors returns to the first page. The filter retains Python <code>error</code> and <code>critical</code> records (case-insensitively) and every reader diagnostic. Custom levels have no inferred severity and remain in the full view. Each page retains physical line numbers, scopes, timestamps, and additive fields. Resizing changes the number of records per page and clamps the current page; an empty projection has one empty page. The terminal supplies that size after reserving its header and controls. This model counts records, not wrapped terminal lines.</p> <p>Structured pointers receive one-based numbers across the complete loaded chronology, before paging or filtering. Lookup keeps the same target even when its row is hidden; message text never supplies a path. An empty recorded path remains numbered so the capture reader can diagnose it. All numbers remain addressable, including those above the <code>1</code>\u2013<code>9</code> direct shortcuts. The returned record supplies the pointer fields to the saved run's capture reader, including the distinct <code>run-result</code> decoding kind. A new completed selection read gets a fresh presentation model; selection/load state remains owned by <code>InspectorSelection</code>. The moved 70-DUT test exercises this composition without executing suite code or changing saved bytes.</p> <p>The internal artifact navigation model (<code>hilt.inspector_artifact_navigation.ArtifactNavigation</code>) now groups the complete timeline's numbered pointers by recorded type. Types follow first appearance; captures within a type retain chronology order. Opening selects exactly the requested number, including one above nine or hidden by a filter; an invalid number is rejected instead of opening another capture. Single-view type navigation wraps and selects the new type's first capture, while capture navigation clamps at either end.</p> <p>Diff selection requires at least two captures of the same type. It starts with the single-view capture on the left and the first other capture on the right. The bracket actions cycle the left target and the arrow actions cycle the right target within that type. When a move reaches the other target, the two sides swap; they never collapse onto one capture. Leaving diff restores the original single-view selection. Targets retain their timeline number, full pointer record, recorded reference, and decoding kind for the existing contained capture reader. Empty paths remain selectable and produce that reader's unavailable diagnostic. A moved 70-DUT test opens null and named captures and the root result without suite execution or saved-byte changes.</p> <p>The internal artifact modal model (<code>hilt.inspector_artifact_view.ArtifactModal</code>) joins that selection to the saved run's capture reader and pages what it reads. A capture is printed in its recorded key order, so two captures of one type line up; an unavailable or undecodable capture shows the reader's diagnostic with the recorded reference and is never replaced by another file. In diff mode the two captures are aligned row by row and one page cursor moves both sides together, as the artifact table above specifies; a singleton type stays single without a reload, and every change of selection returns to the first page because a capture is read from its top. A delayed read never replaces a newer selection, in the pattern of the selection model. The shared 70-DUT recording in <code>tests/inspector_rack.py</code> proves the composition without suite execution or saved-byte changes.</p> <p>The main view (<code>hilt.inspector_app.InspectorApp</code>) is the first terminal screen, built on Textual by Ty's ruling of 2026-09-09. It renders the tab row, the tree, and the chronology pane from the models above and forwards the quick-reference keys, one model call per key: Inspect and Re-Run share one cursor, the chronology loads on cursor movement without Enter and names its owner when the node's records rolled up, the owner's row carries the cyan arrow, arrows jump between Fail and Aborted rows without wrapping, <code>Shift-\u2191</code>/<code>Shift-\u2193</code> (or <code>Alt-\u2191</code>/<code>Alt-\u2193</code>) step between siblings, <code>h</code> hides passing rows, <code>n</code>/<code>p</code> page, <code>f</code> filters to errors while a truncation marker stays in place, and <code>l</code> gives the log the whole body and back. It draws the tree's lines, not its rows, so a component and its same-named root are one row. Tree depth is drawn with the legacy glyphs (<code>\u251c\u2500</code>, <code>\u2514\u2500</code>, <code>\u2502</code>), computed over the rows on screen so a hidden branch still closes on its last visible child. The split between the two panes and the ancestors pinned above a scrolled tree are each one function of the terminal's height and the selection, so the screen holds no scroll position that could disagree with the cursor. The plain text of every redraw is kept on the app so the self-tests drive it against the recorded runs in <code>tests/inspector_rack.py</code> and a screenshot can be read back against this guide.</p> <p>The re-run session (<code>hilt.inspector_rerun.RerunSession</code>) is the adapter behind the Re-Run tab. It receives a suite factory from the command line, never a suite derived from evidence; loads it on the tab's first opening, off the event loop; refuses a suite whose declared tree differs from the saved one, naming the first row that differs; executes the pressed node through <code>TestComponent.run(subtree=...)</code>, the sealed lifecycle with every leaf outside that subtree left pending; reads the result back through the same saved-run reader the archive uses, so the tab shows a re-run's states and chronology with the same code paths; and keeps that evidence under a scratch directory emptied per node on each re-run and removed when the Inspector closes. Interrupting a live re-run raises the operator signal, so the framework's own termination contract settles the node Aborted with bounded cleanup.</p> <p>The artifact modal screen (<code>hilt.inspector_app.ArtifactScreen</code>) opens from a digit in the log pane over the capture carrying that number and owns the artifact keys until <code>b</code> closes it: <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> move between types or the left side of a comparison, the arrows between captures of the type or the right side, <code>n</code> and <code>p</code> page both sides together, <code>d</code> enters and leaves the comparison. It renders the modal model only: one capture as its lines, a comparison as two aligned columns with differing rows highlighted, a diagnostic as the reader's words with the recorded reference.</p> <p>The entry point is <code>hilt inspect [PATH]</code> (<code>hilt.cli</code>): PATH is a run directory or its <code>result.json</code>; without PATH the most recently modified <code>result.json</code> under the artifact root opens (<code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, default <code>artifacts/</code>; <code>--artifact-root DIR</code> overrides), the legacy discovery rule carried over. Nothing under the root, or a selection that is not a saved version-1 run, is refused with the reader's diagnostic on stderr and exit status 3. Opening runs no suite code. <code>--suite TARGET</code> names the suite the Re-Run tab executes, in the form <code>hilt run</code> accepts, and is loaded at the prompt so a wrong target is exit 3 there; without it the tab uses the <code>suite_target</code> the run recorded, loaded only when the tab is first opened.</p> <p>The Inspector serves the person at the station when a test stops the line. Its test of success is concrete: find what happened, categorize it, and begin the station's recovery protocol within sixty seconds, without opening suite source.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#what-the-inspector-reads","title":"What the Inspector reads","text":"<p>Inspect mode replays saved evidence. Moving the cursor, paging a log, opening an artifact, or comparing two captures never touches the DUT and never executes suite code.</p> <p>The input is a run directory containing <code>result.json</code>, its per-node <code>timeline.jsonl</code> files, and the point-in-time artifacts referenced by those timelines. The structural tree comes entirely from <code>summary_tree</code>; the temporal view comes entirely from the linked JSON Lines records. A saved 70-DUT rack must be inspectable without live <code>TestComponent</code> objects, source discovery, an SSH connection, or the test station that produced it.</p> <p>The Inspector JSON contract owns the field definitions and version policy. This page owns their presentation. It introduces no new result fields or verdict rules. The result and each timeline record have independent <code>format_version</code> values; select and validate each decoder separately, accept additive fields, and report unsupported versions explicitly rather than guessing their meaning.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#opening-a-run","title":"Opening a run","text":"<p><code>hilt inspect PATH</code> opens a selected <code>result.json</code>, or the <code>result.json</code> inside a selected run directory, and treats its containing directory as the run root. <code>hilt inspect</code> alone opens the most recently modified <code>result.json</code> under the artifact root (<code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, default <code>artifacts/</code>; <code>--artifact-root DIR</code> overrides), the legacy convenience carried over by Ty's ruling of 2026-09-09 on #89. The legacy <code>npm run inspect</code> command and <code>summary-tree.artifact</code> input are not Python interfaces.</p> <p>If no result is available, explain that there is no saved tree to inspect. Do not start a test to manufacture one: <code>hilt inspect</code> with nothing under the root exits 3 saying so, and a selection that is not a saved version-1 run exits 3 with the reader's diagnostic, never another file opened in its place. Absence of <code>result.json</code> is not a verdict: an invocation may have failed before a run, or a process may have ended before result publication. Preserve any surviving timeline evidence for diagnosis.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#the-screen-layout","title":"The screen layout","text":"<p>The terminal has a tab bar, the tree above, and the highlighted node's timeline below. This illustration shows the arrangement, not a captured run. The tree is scrolled: its first rows have left the pane, and the ancestors of the first row still in view are pinned above the rule.</p> <pre><code>  [ Inspect ]   Re-Run\n  ---------------------------------------------------------\n    rack  Aborted\n    \u2514\u2500 servers  Aborted  \u2190\n    \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n       \u251c\u2500 server-07  Pass\n       \u2502  \u2514\u2500 thermal check  Pass\n       \u251c\u2500 server-08  Fail\n  &gt;    \u2502  \u2514\u2500 thermal check  Fail  not taken: fan_rpm\n       \u251c\u2500 server-09  Aborted\n       \u2502  \u2514\u2500 thermal check  Aborted\n       \u251c\u2500 server-10  Pass\n       \u2502  \u2514\u2500 thermal check  Pass\n       \u251c\u2500 server-11  Pass\n  ---------------------------------------------------------\n  log: rack/servers  (rolled up: thermal check has no own log)\n  page 9/14  errors only: off  captures: 1  (1-9 opens a capture)\n      14:02:11Z info     starting thermal ramp\n  [1] 14:02:45Z info     survey captured\n      14:03:02Z error    temperature 87 C exceeds 75 C\n  Tab Inspect / Re-Run  q Quit  \u2190 \u2192 Failures  h Hide passes  l Log full  \u23ce Re-run node  n p Page  f Errors only  shift+\u2191 shift+\u2193 Sibling\n</code></pre> <p>The footer lists the keys on one line, and a terminal too narrow for all of them cuts the list at its right edge. Quit, the tab key, and the two keys that change what the screen shows, <code>h</code> and <code>l</code>, come first and are whole at 100 columns. The quick reference is complete at any width.</p> <p>The split. The body is everything between the tab bar and the footer. The log has a quarter of it, rounded down, and never fewer than six lines: its two header lines and four records. The tree has the rest. On a terminal too short for both, the tree gives up its rows first, because the log is the authoritative record; when not one tree row would fit, the log has the whole body.</p> <p>The log full screen. <code>l</code> puts the tree away and gives the log the whole body; <code>l</code> again brings the tree back. The tab bar and the footer stay. Nothing else changes: the highlighted node, the errors-only filter, and the reader's place in the log are kept. The record that headed the page before the toggle is on the page after it, and toggling twice returns the same page. Every key works in both layouts. The arrows still move the selection while the tree is out of sight, and the log's first line names the node.</p> <p>Pinned ancestors. When the tree is scrolled, the ancestors of the first row in view stay pinned at the top of the pane: one line per level, root first, each drawn exactly as its own row, with its indentation and glyphs, its name, its state, and the <code>\u2190</code> owner mark when it owns the timeline. A dim rule separates the pinned lines from the rows that scroll beneath them. A header leaves when the last row of its subtree has scrolled past, and the next one takes its place. No row is drawn under the pinned lines, so the highlighted row is never hidden by them, whichever key moved it there. The pinned lines take at most a third of the tree pane. A deeper chain drops its outermost levels and keeps the nearest, because those are the context the highlighted row needs and the log's first line already names the full path. A tree pane under four rows pins nothing. Pinning holds with passing rows hidden, where a hidden ancestor is stepped over, and on the Re-Run tab, where a pinned line carries that tab's label. The ancestors are those of the drawn tree: a component folded with its root is pinned as the one row it is drawn on.</p> <p>The log updates on cursor movement. Enter is never required to read it. A delayed load for the previous selection must not overwrite the currently highlighted node's view. Show the selected node and, when different, the ancestor that owns its timeline: the pane names that owner, and the owner's row ends in the cyan <code>\u2190</code> so the eye finds the file the highlighted node's records went into.</p> <p>Depth is drawn with the legacy glyphs: <code>\u251c\u2500</code> for a child with siblings below it, <code>\u2514\u2500</code> for the last child, <code>\u2502</code> continuing a branch that has more children further down. They are computed over the rows on screen, so when passing rows are hidden each remaining branch still closes on its last visible child.</p> <p>Tree order is declaration order, including in parallel compositions. A parent marked Fail may summarize a failing child; keep the descendants visible so the technician can reach the actual claim. Use the root-to-node array of names as the path, not a split of the display breadcrumbs: names may themselves contain <code>/</code>.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#the-fold-one-row-per-component","title":"The fold: one row per component","text":"<p>The record holds two nodes for every component: the component itself (type <code>suite</code>, which owns <code>configure</code> and <code>cleanup</code>) and the composition its <code>build_suite()</code> returned. The root takes the component's name, so a tree drawn node for node prints nearly every name twice and spends half its depth doing it:</p> <pre><code>  \u251c\u2500 sata  Pass\n  \u2502  \u2514\u2500 sata  Pass\n  \u2502     \u251c\u2500 present  Pass\n  \u2502     \u2514\u2500 spins  Pass\n</code></pre> <p>The Inspector draws such a pair as one row (Ty, 2026-11-21, issue #165):</p> <pre><code>  \u251c\u2500 sata  Pass\n  \u2502  \u251c\u2500 present  Pass\n  \u2502  \u2514\u2500 spins  Pass\n</code></pre> <p>This is a rule of the drawing only. <code>result.json</code> keeps both nodes at <code>format_version</code> 1, a run saved before the rule opens and folds the same way, and every row index and Re-Run position still refers to the saved nodes. An author gets the pair by writing the root without a name, <code>self.sequence([...])</code> (idioms); <code>self.sequence(self.name, [...])</code> records the same two nodes and folds like it.</p> <p>The rule. A <code>suite</code> row is drawn together with its child when that child is its only child, carries exactly the same name (compared as written, case included), and recorded the same state. The rule applies again to that child, so a component whose root is a same-named component folds with that component's root as well. Nothing else folds:</p> <ul> <li>A root the author named for itself, <code>self.sequence(\"checks\", [...])</code> under <code>wifi</code>,   is a level they meant to show. It keeps its row.</li> <li>A pair whose states differ keeps both rows, so the row that failed is the row the   technician sees. The engine settles a component and its root alike in every run it   writes today: a <code>cleanup</code> failure is logged without changing the verdict, and a   component that fails in <code>configure</code> records no root at all. The rule is for a record   that says otherwise.</li> </ul> <p>What the one row shows.</p> <ul> <li>State and readings. The component's state, which is its root's too, and every   reading either node declared and did not take.</li> <li>Log. Highlighting the row loads both nodes' timelines as one chronology, merged   in time order, so <code>configure</code>, the claims, and <code>cleanup</code> read top to bottom as they   happened. The header says so: <code>log: rack/sata  (with its root's log, in time order)</code>.   Each file keeps its own order in the merge. A damaged line stays behind the record   it followed and is labelled with its file, because a line number alone no longer   names one. A root that rolled into its component is one file, read once.</li> <li>Owner arrow. A claim under a folded root rolled into the root's timeline. The   root has no row of its own, so the arrow sits on the folded row, and the pane shows   the root's file alone: <code>log: rack/sata  (rolled up: present has no own log)</code>.</li> <li>Paths. Wherever the screen names a node it uses the drawn path,   <code>rack/sata/present</code>, not the saved one, <code>rack/rack/sata/sata/present</code>.</li> <li>Cursor. Up, down, and the failure arrows move over drawn rows and never rest on   a folded root. <code>h</code> hides or keeps a folded row as the one row it is.</li> <li>Re-Run. Enter on a folded row re-runs the component, which runs its root and   everything beneath it; the root has no key of its own. The Re-Run tab keeps the rows   the archive drew, so the tree does not change shape under the cursor after a re-run:   the row shows the component's re-run state, and its log merges the same two nodes'   re-run timelines.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"inspector.html#the-tabs","title":"The tabs","text":""},{"location":"inspector.html#inspect-the-default","title":"Inspect \u2014 the default","text":"<p>Display every declared node, with its recorded state and failure message. Color and symbols help scanning, but the state must remain legible as text.</p> State Presentation and meaning <code>Pass</code> Green success mark. The framework accepted an explicit verdict and any required evidence. A string <code>\"PASS\"</code> is not a passing verdict. <code>Fail</code> Red failure mark. Read the failure object: this can be a modeled <code>TestFailure</code> or a software contract violation. <code>Aborted</code> Distinct, named state. No verdict was produced; the cause may be integration, operator interrupt, missing verdict, or an abort budget. <code>null</code> Dim Pending / unreached row. This declared step never ran; it is not a fourth terminal state. <p>Left and right arrows move backward and forward through nodes needing attention, skipping Pass and pending rows. Preserve the legacy implementation's inclusion of both Fail and Aborted in that navigation, while keeping their labels and recovery meanings distinct. At either end, leave the cursor where it is; do not wrap unexpectedly.</p> <p><code>Shift-\u2191</code> and <code>Shift-\u2193</code> move to the highlighted row's previous and next sibling: the row before or after it under the same parent, stepping over everything nested between. From one server to the next, without walking the first one's checks. The jump never leaves the parent (Ty, 2026-11-22): a row at the same depth under the next parent is a cousin and is not reached, so on a parent's first or last row, and on an only child, the cursor stays where it is. The parent is the drawn one: a component folded with its root is one parent. Hidden passes are stepped over. Both tabs have the key. <code>Alt-\u2191</code> and <code>Alt-\u2193</code> do the same. Two modifiers reach one action because a terminal may keep one for itself: Terminator moves between its own panes on Alt with an arrow and never passes the key on. The footer names Shift, the pair that arrives there too.</p> <p><code>h</code> hides every Pass row and shows them again. This replaces the legacy Failures list (Ty, 2026-09-10): the same recorded tree with the passing branches folded away, so a failure buried inside a large rack is found without leaving the tree or losing its place in it. Pending rows stay visible; they are not passes. If the cursor was on a row that just disappeared, it lands on the nearest visible row above it. When every node passed, the pane says so rather than showing nothing.</p> <p>Show the selected leaf's <code>evidence</code> beside its claim and failure. For every name in <code>evidence_declared</code> absent from <code>evidence</code>, show not taken by name. An absent reading is neither zero nor a successful measurement. Do not present the reserved empty <code>run_data[\"measurements\"]</code> slot as an implemented measurement or SPC feature.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#re-run-the-bench","title":"Re-Run \u2014 the bench","text":"<p>The same tree, unhydrated: every row reads not re-run until the technician executes it. Move to any node and press Enter to execute that node and everything beneath it with the code that ran in production. Switching tabs or highlighting a node never runs anything; Enter on the Inspect tab does nothing. The purpose is the floor's quick check: the ping failed, the cable is reseated, the one claim is re-run with the production code, and only then is the whole suite restarted through the station's normal procedure.</p> <p>What executes is the sealed lifecycle, confined to one subtree. <code>TestComponent.run</code> takes <code>subtree=</code>, the child positions from the root to the pressed node. Every node on the path to it runs its own configure and cleanup, because that is their lifecycle and the only way the pressed node meets the hop its ancestors own; every leaf outside the subtree is left pending, never executed. The framework continues to own configure and cleanup. This is not the legacy <code>\u2191ctx</code> ancestor-configure replay, which stays prohibited: no parent's configure is replayed outside the lifecycle to make a device runnable, and the instance-local context idiom is untouched.</p> <p>The suite is supplied, never derived. <code>hilt run</code> records the loader target it used in <code>result.json</code> as <code>suite_target</code> (absolute file or module name, <code>::</code>, class), and <code>hilt inspect</code> loads that suite the first time the tab is opened; <code>--suite TARGET</code> names another, and is loaded at the prompt. Nothing is reconstructed from <code>result.json</code>, artifact contents, or source found beside the run directory. Before any node runs, the supplied suite's declared tree is compared with the saved one, name and type in preorder; a difference is refused by path, and the pane says which suite to name instead. A run that recorded no target, one started from a developer's own harness, leaves the tab unavailable with that reason until <code>--suite</code> is given.</p> <p>Nothing the bench produces persists. Each re-run writes its evidence under a scratch directory, in a subdirectory per pressed node. Re-running the same node empties that subdirectory first, so the ninth re-run leaves nothing of the eighth. The whole scratch directory is removed when the Inspector closes. After a re-run the pressed node and its descendants that ran show their fresh states; rows the re-run never reached keep whatever an earlier re-run left, or not re-run. The log pane shows the highlighted node's re-run chronology, read by the same reader as the archive, with the same owner arrow; a node no re-run reached says so instead of borrowing the archive's log.</p> <p>A node above the pressed one reads partly re-run, never a verdict. Its lifecycle ran and its chronology is worth reading, but the state it rolled up covers only the child the bench was pointed at: every sibling was left pending and contributed nothing to it. Drawn as a verdict, that rollup turns a group whose other child failed in production into a Pass and carries the Pass to the root, which is the one reading the floor must never be given (issue #141). The bench answers for the branch it ran; the verdict on the whole node belongs to the archive on the Inspect tab, or to a re-run of that node, which runs every claim beneath it and so reads as a verdict again.</p> <p>The run's recorded environment seeds the re-run, so the station's variables travel with the record; the live environment overrides it, so a technician can change one deliberately before pressing Enter.</p> <p>A live re-run is live hardware. Tab is held while a node runs, as in the legacy UI, and Enter starts nothing else. <code>q</code> during a re-run is the operator interrupt: it takes the framework's termination contract, the node settles Aborted with bounded cleanup, and only then does the Inspector exit. A second <code>q</code> during that cleanup exits immediately, as a second signal does at the station. The legacy promise that Ctrl-C always exits immediately with nothing at stake applies to the archive, not to the bench.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#the-main-workflow-diagnosing-a-failure","title":"The main workflow: diagnosing a failure","text":"<ol> <li>Find the node needing attention. Use right/left to move forward/backward without    scrolling through passed leaves. Read the recorded state before choosing a recovery.</li> <li>Read the failure message and comparison. Show <code>fail_message</code>, and <code>expected</code> /    <code>received</code> when present. Preserve bug banners, nested causes, hook names, and operator    signals; a plain-language message alone does not establish hardware fault.</li> <li>Read the timeline already below the tree. <code>n</code> advances a page, <code>p</code> returns a    page, and <code>f</code> toggles the errors-only filter. <code>l</code> gives a long log the whole screen    and returns to the tree at the same place. Log severity does not replace the verdict.</li> <li>Open a referenced artifact. Press its displayed digit, <code>1</code>\u2013<code>9</code>. Survey captures    can show whether the device's reported health agrees with what actually happened.</li> <li>Choose the recovery protocol. Read the failure's cause and the available evidence.    The human makes the final determination; uncertainty is a reason to escalate.</li> </ol> <p>The five categories follow the current Failure Taxonomy Contract, which supersedes the legacy guide's separate, automatically assigned infrastructure category:</p> Category Evidence to read Recovery direction DUT failed a check Modeled <code>TestFailure</code>, comparisons, and captured readings. A configure failure means the device did not reach a testable state; hardware versus infrastructure remains a human determination. Follow the station protocol for the established cause. Hardware lying Survey evidence contradicts a claimed healthy state or the apparent verdict. Preserve the discrepancy and seek deeper inspection, even after Pass. Test method bug Contract-violation banner, <code>bug</code>, or <code>MissingVerdict</code>. The latter is Aborted; thrown bugs are Fail. A framework-contract banner identifies the framework maintainer as the owner. Escalate the software defect; do not condemn the unit from this signal. External integration failure <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, its hook, and its cause. Restore the harness or coordination service before retrying. Operator interrupt <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>, <code>operator: true</code>, and its signal. The rack was stopped deliberately; resume only under the station's procedure. <p>Time-budget evidence supplements this taxonomy. Display the winning <code>timeout.clock</code>, declaration, budget, and elapsed time. <code>fail_after</code> produces Fail; <code>abort_after</code> produces Aborted and may have no failure object at all. Do not relabel the latter as a hook failure just because both are Aborted. The station recovery table gives the full reading procedure.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#the-timeline-and-its-owner","title":"The timeline and its owner","text":"<p>Resolve a node's non-null <code>log_path</code> relative to the selected run directory. A terminal node with a null link rolled its records into the nearest ancestor with a link; indicate that ancestor with the legacy cyan owner arrow. Keep each entry's <code>scope</code> visible so sibling records in the shared timeline cannot be mistaken for this leaf's readings. A folded row has two nodes behind it, each with its own link; its chronology is both files merged in time order, and the header says so.</p> <p>A pending node has no chronology anywhere. Show not run, not its parent's timeline as though the pending node produced evidence. A missing file for a node that did run is a separate, explicit log unavailable condition. Neither condition changes state.</p> <p>Read JSON Lines incrementally, preserving file order and every complete valid record. For an interrupted final line, display the complete preceding records and a visible truncation marker; do not fill in the unfinished object. Report malformed complete records or unsupported versions as decoding errors rather than silently dropping them. An errors-only filter must not hide these reader diagnostics.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#artifacts-the-evidence-behind-the-verdict","title":"Artifacts \u2014 the evidence behind the verdict","text":"<p>Number structured artifact-pointer entries in the loaded timeline and show <code>[N]</code> on the corresponding row. Digits <code>1</code>\u2013<code>9</code> open the matching numbered capture in a modal. Take the link from the pointer fields, not from a path-looking substring in <code>message</code>. Retain numbering when paging or filtering so a digit never silently changes its target.</p> <p>The modal groups captures by <code>artifact_type</code> and labels each with its name and timestamp. Show the persisted content, including an explicit null observation; nothing in the viewer recomputes a verdict. The root <code>run-result</code> pointer opens <code>result.json</code>, whose shape differs from a point-in-time artifact envelope. The capture reader checks the envelope's own version and published schema as required by the JSON version policy.</p> <p>An unavailable or unreadable capture produces a visible diagnostic containing the reference, while the log and tree remain usable. The saved files are never edited. Version 1 stores absolute <code>artifact_path</code> pointers, whereas tree log links are relative. The reader resolves captures under the selected run root. For a moved run, try re-anchoring the recorded path's run-root-relative tail beneath the opened run root before declaring the capture unavailable. Preserve that full relative path: two nodes may capture different evidence with the same filename. Never search for or substitute a file merely because its basename matches.</p> <p>Re-anchoring requires an established original run root. If the reader cannot establish that mapping, or the mapped capture cannot be read under the opened root, show artifact unavailable with the recorded reference; do not guess a root or silently fall back to another run's evidence. The reader implementation must demonstrate that mapping and its unavailable cases in tests before claiming moved-run support.</p> <p>This is a reader rule. Saved pointers remain unchanged and absolute at version 1. Changing their stored representation to relative paths is a record-contract change under the version policy, requiring Ty's decision before an implementation MR.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#reading-and-comparing-artifacts","title":"Reading and comparing artifacts","text":"Key Single-artifact view Diff view <code>[</code> / <code>]</code> Previous / next artifact type. Change the left artifact within the current type. <code>\u2190</code> / <code>\u2192</code> Previous / next artifact within the current type. Change the right artifact within the current type. <code>n</code> / <code>p</code> Next / previous content page. Page both sides together. <code>d</code> Enter diff when the type has at least two artifacts. Return to the single-artifact view. <code>b</code> Close the modal and return to the log. Close the modal and return to the log. <p>Diff shows two captures of the same type side by side, highlighting changed lines and dimming unchanged lines. Preserve the legacy guide's intended swap when the two selected indices collide, so a two-capture comparison can reverse sides. This is a display of recorded differences, not an automatic diagnosis of hardware truth.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#key-bindings-quick-reference","title":"Key bindings quick reference","text":"Context Key Action Saved inspection, including a modal <code>q</code> / <code>Ctrl-C</code> Quit without changing evidence or touching hardware. Live re-run <code>q</code> / <code>Ctrl-C</code> Operator interrupt: the node settles Aborted through bounded cleanup, then the Inspector exits. A second press during cleanup exits at once. Main view <code>Tab</code> Switch Inspect / Re-Run. Held during a live re-run. Either tree <code>\u2191</code> / <code>\u2193</code> Move through the rows on screen; hidden passes are stepped over. Either tree <code>\u2190</code> / <code>\u2192</code> Previous / next Fail or Aborted node of the saved run; skip passed and pending rows. Either tree <code>Shift-\u2191</code> / <code>Shift-\u2193</code>, or <code>Alt-\u2191</code> / <code>Alt-\u2193</code> Previous / next sibling: the row on screen before or after the highlighted one under the same parent; stay put when there is none. Use Shift where the terminal keeps Alt with an arrow for itself. Inspect tree <code>h</code> Hide passing rows, or show them again. Re-Run tree <code>Enter</code> Execute the highlighted node and everything beneath it; nothing else runs anything. Log pane, no modal <code>n</code> / <code>p</code> Next / previous page. Log pane, no modal <code>f</code> Toggle errors-only filtering. Log pane, no modal <code>1</code>\u2013<code>9</code> Open the artifact carrying that number. Main view <code>l</code> Show the log full screen, or bring the tree back; the selection, the filter, and the place in the log are kept. Artifact modal <code>b</code>, <code>[</code>, <code>]</code>, <code>\u2190</code>, <code>\u2192</code>, <code>n</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>d</code> Use the artifact controls above; the modal owns these keys until it closes."},{"location":"inspector.html#things-that-need-careful-reading","title":"Things that need careful reading","text":"<ul> <li>An ancestor owns the log. The owner arrow explains roll-up; it says nothing about   context inheritance. The legacy <code>\u2191ctx</code> ancestor-configure replay is prohibited.</li> <li>One row, two records. A component and its same-named root are one row on the   screen and two nodes in <code>result.json</code>, each with its own timeline. The row's log is   both, merged. Reading the files by hand, look in both directories.</li> <li>An error appears in a passing test. Survey errors are logged but do not decide   the verdict. Read the state and the surrounding capture, not just the line's color.</li> <li>Many nodes are Aborted. An integration halt or operator interruption can affect   the whole in-flight tree. A missing verdict or an abort budget is node-local. Read   the cause instead of inferring a rack-wide hardware failure from repeated labels.</li> <li>The record ends abruptly. Retain the accepted chronology and mark the missing   tail. Neither incomplete evidence nor missing declared readings may become Pass.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"inspector.html#implementation-acceptance","title":"Implementation acceptance","text":"<p>The screen implementation (<code>hilt.inspector_app</code>) and the entry point (<code>hilt inspect</code>) follow this document. The view model is testable independently of the terminal and driven by a recorded 70-DUT rack:</p> <ul> <li>Reconstruct tree order, peer states, pending descendants, failure causes, timeout   evidence, and missing declared readings from version-1 records alone.</li> <li>Navigate failures and load the selected node's own or rolled-up timeline immediately,   with correct owner and scope; never show stale content after a cursor change.</li> <li>Draw a component and its same-named, same-state only child as one row on both tabs   and with passes hidden, with the merged chronology, the owner arrow, and the drawn   path; keep both rows for a differently named root and for a pair whose states differ.   The saved record is read, never rewritten.</li> <li>Open numbered pointers, group artifacts by type, and compare captures using the   specified keys, including the two-artifact index collision.</li> <li>Preserve complete lines under truncation and distinguish unavailable evidence,   malformed records, unsupported versions, and nodes that never ran.</li> <li>Keep the reader's place on a deep tree and a long log. The highlighted row is never   drawn under the pinned ancestors, including after a jump upward or a filter change;   the log has a quarter of the body and at least six lines; and showing the log full   screen and returning lands on the same first visible record.</li> <li>Make Inspect usable from saved evidence without source or hardware, hidden passes   included; it never waits for a suite. The saved-view command and discovery rule are   the ones under \"Opening a run\".</li> <li>Verify moved-run resolution preserves the complete run-relative path and remains   under the opened root. Exercise captures with identical basenames in different nodes,   an unestablished root mapping, and unavailable files; preserve the recorded reference   in every unavailable diagnostic and never substitute another capture.</li> <li>Hold Re-Run to its ruling. Its adapter accepts only an operator-supplied suite   through the command line (<code>suite_target</code> or <code>--suite</code>), never reconstruction from   evidence or neighboring source, and refuses a suite whose declared tree differs from   the saved one. Execution is <code>TestComponent.run(subtree=...)</code>: ancestors' own lifecycle,   the pressed subtree, every other leaf pending. Ancestor-configure replay stays   prohibited. A node above the pressed one shows partly re-run, never the state it   rolled up over the one child the bench ran. Scratch evidence is replaced per node and   removed on close. A live   re-run holds Tab, and quitting it goes through the operator-interrupt path to   Aborted with bounded cleanup before the Inspector exits.</li> </ul> <p>If the message, chronology, and captures do not establish a recovery category, keep the run directory and escalate with the selected node's path. The Inspector must make that uncertainty visible. Evidence earns a conclusion; the screen cannot supply one for it.</p>"},{"location":"inspector.html#migration-references","title":"Migration references","text":"<p>The behavioral reference is the read-only <code>hilt-test-legacy/docs/inspector.md</code>, with navigation checked against <code>src/inspector/InspectTab.mts</code>, <code>RunTab.mts</code>, <code>TreePanel.mts</code>, <code>DebugView.mts</code>, and <code>ArtifactModal.mts</code> in that repository. The guide's workflow, split panes, Run tab, tree glyphs, owner arrow, failure navigation, modal, and diff controls are retained; the Failures list became the <code>h</code> key. Python record names, the five current categories, instance-local ctx, explicit evidence, and interruption semantics follow this port's contracts. No TypeScript runtime or language-server behavior is required to specify this saved-evidence viewer.</p>"},{"location":"junit-xml.html","title":"JUnit XML reporter","text":"<p>Every CI system reads JUnit XML. <code>hilt.junit.JunitXml</code> maps a <code>RunResult</code> summary tree onto that schema so a hilt run shows up beside every other suite with no adapter. The Floor Technician's taxonomy survives the mapping: a red build says hardware, harness, or bug \u2014 not just red.</p> <p>The runner calls <code>JunitXml.write</code>: <code>hilt run --junit PATH</code> writes the report beside the run's evidence (<code>docs/hilt-run.md</code>). A harness adapter that imports <code>hilt.junit</code> directly does so off the promised surface (<code>docs/api-stability.md</code>).</p> <p>This is a projection of the Inspector JSON contract, not a second source of truth. <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> remains the evidence record. JUnit is what CI already knows how to paint.</p>"},{"location":"junit-xml.html#mapping-of-the-three-terminal-states","title":"Mapping of the three terminal states","text":"<p>There is no fourth terminal state. A declared node that did not run (<code>state</code> is <code>null</code> on the summary tree \u2014 a later step in a failed sequence) is <code>skipped</code>, which is a JUnit status, not a HILT state.</p> HILT JUnit element What the FT still sees <code>Pass</code> <code>testcase</code> with neither <code>failure</code> nor <code>error</code> A green row. <code>Fail</code> <code>failure</code> Taxonomy class on <code>@type</code>, <code>fail_message</code> on <code>@message</code>, full <code>to_json()</code> payload in the body. <code>bug: true</code> means broken code, not broken hardware. <code>Aborted</code> <code>error</code> Same payload shape. Aborted is not a flavor of Fail; CI must not count it as <code>failures</code>. unreached (<code>state</code> null) <code>skipped</code> The node was declared and never ran. <p><code>@type</code> is the taxonomy class name (<code>TestFailure</code>, <code>ContractViolation</code>, <code>MissingVerdict</code>, <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>). The body is the same JSON object <code>RunResult.to_json()</code> already publishes under <code>failure</code>, so a CI log and the Inspector agree.</p>"},{"location":"junit-xml.html#tree-testcases","title":"Tree \u2192 testcases","text":"<ul> <li>Every leaf (a summary-tree node with no children) is one <code>testcase</code>.</li> <li>Groups, sequences, and suites become <code>classname</code>: the root-to-parent path, joined   with <code>/</code>. <code>ancestor_names</code> is still the durable identity in the Inspector JSON;   classname is display.</li> <li>A composite that settled before any child ran (configure failed, abort during   bring-up) is itself a <code>testcase</code>. Otherwise CI would paint skip \u2014 or nothing \u2014 for   a Fail. Declared children that exist on the tree and never ran are <code>skipped</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Counts on <code>testsuites</code> / <code>testsuite</code> (<code>tests</code>, <code>failures</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>skipped</code>) are the counts of emitted testcases, not of composite nodes.</p>"},{"location":"junit-xml.html#artifacts","title":"Artifacts","text":"<p>Point-in-time evidence stays in the run directory. JUnit gets pointers, not copies:</p> <ul> <li><code>system-out</code> contains <code>timeline: &lt;log_path&gt;</code> when the node (or the nearest ancestor   that owns a log) has a <code>timeline.jsonl</code> relative to the run directory.</li> <li>A timeout that determined the state adds a <code>timeout: \u2026</code> line with the same fields   as the Inspector timeout object.</li> </ul> <p>Consumers resolve <code>log_path</code> against the run directory that holds <code>result.json</code>.</p>"},{"location":"junit-xml.html#document-shape","title":"Document shape","text":"<p>Jenkins-compatible JUnit (the schema GitLab CI, Jenkins, and pytest-junit share):</p> <pre><code>&lt;?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?&gt;\n&lt;testsuites name=\"rack\" children=\"3\" failures=\"1\" errors=\"1\" skipped=\"1\"&gt;\n  &lt;testsuite name=\"rack\" children=\"3\" failures=\"1\" errors=\"1\" skipped=\"1\"&gt;\n    &lt;testcase name=\"identity\" classname=\"rack/claims\"/&gt;\n    &lt;testcase name=\"firmware\" classname=\"rack/claims\"&gt;\n      &lt;failure type=\"TestFailure\" message=\"gate remained closed\"&gt;{taxonomy JSON}&lt;/failure&gt;\n      &lt;system-out&gt;timeline: rack/timeline.jsonl&lt;/system-out&gt;\n    &lt;/testcase&gt;\n    &lt;testcase name=\"never reached\" classname=\"rack/ordered claims\"&gt;\n      &lt;skipped/&gt;\n    &lt;/testcase&gt;\n  &lt;/testsuite&gt;\n&lt;/testsuites&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>No <code>time</code> attribute is emitted: <code>RunResult</code> does not carry per-node duration, and inventing <code>0</code> would lie.</p>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html","title":"The lifecycle","text":"<p>Every <code>TestComponent</code> has a lifecycle of three methods: <code>configure</code>, <code>build_suite</code>, and <code>cleanup</code>. HILT calls them in that order on every run and enforces the contract of each.</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD\n    init([\"`**Before the lifecycle**\n    The constructor\n    describes the test.\n    No hardware yet.`\"])\n\n    subgraph lifecycle[\"THE LIFECYCLE: HILT calls these three methods, in this order\"]\n        direction LR\n        configure[\"`**configure**\n        Step 1: SET UP\n        Get the device ready.\n        Return what the tests need.`\"]\n        build[[\"`**build_suite**\n        Step 2: LIST THE TESTS\n        Return the suite.\n        HILT then runs it.\n        Expanded in the next chart.`\"]]\n        cleanup[\"`**cleanup**\n        Step 3: TEAR DOWN\n        Put the device back.\n        Always runs.`\"]\n\n        configure ==&gt;|\"device ready\"| build\n        build ==&gt;|\"suite has run\"| cleanup\n        configure -.-&gt;|\"device not ready: skip to cleanup\"| cleanup\n    end\n\n    result([\"`**Result**\n    Pass, Fail, or Aborted`\"])\n\n    init --&gt; lifecycle\n    lifecycle --&gt; result\n\n    classDef step fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#1d4ed8,stroke-width:2px,color:#0b1f44\n    classDef outside fill:#f3f4f6,stroke:#6b7280,color:#111827\n    class configure,build,cleanup step\n    class init,result outside</code></pre> <p>If you have written a bench procedure, you know the shape: set up, run the checks, tear down. The difference is that HILT owns the order. You never call these methods yourself, and HILT runs the teardown after every outcome.</p> <p>Two things you write are not lifecycle steps:</p> <ul> <li><code>__init__</code>, the constructor, runs earlier, when the component is created.</li> <li>Test methods are never called by the lifecycle directly. <code>build_suite</code> hands   them over as part of the suite, which is the next chart.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#what-build_suite-returns","title":"What <code>build_suite</code> returns","text":"<p><code>build_suite</code> does not run tests. It returns the suite: a tree that names each test and says which method performs it. HILT runs that tree after <code>build_suite</code> and before <code>cleanup</code>.</p> <pre><code>flowchart LR\n    build[[\"`**build_suite**\n    Lifecycle step 2\n    returns the suite`\"]]\n\n    subgraph suite[\"THE SUITE: the tests HILT runs, in this order\"]\n        direction LR\n        t1[\"`**Test 1**\n        fan reaches minimum speed\n        HILT calls your method\n        _reaches_minimum_speed`\"]\n        t2[\"`**Test 2**\n        fan current is within limit\n        HILT calls your method\n        _current_within_limit`\"]\n        t1 ==&gt;|\"if it passed\"| t2\n    end\n\n    cleanup[\"`**cleanup**\n    Lifecycle step 3`\"]\n\n    build ==&gt; suite\n    suite ==&gt; cleanup\n\n    classDef step fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#1d4ed8,stroke-width:2px,color:#0b1f44\n    classDef test fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#b45309,color:#451a03\n    class build,cleanup step\n    class t1,t2 test</code></pre> <p>Each test in the suite pairs a name, which is what the technician reads in the report, with one of your test methods. A test method checks one thing and ends with <code>return PASS</code> or <code>raise TestFailure</code>.</p> <p>A suite is built from four kinds of block. The one above is a <code>self.sequence</code> holding two <code>self.test</code> blocks:</p> Block What HILT does with it <code>self.test(name, method)</code> Calls <code>method</code> once and records its verdict under <code>name</code>. <code>self.sequence([...])</code> Runs the children in order and stops at the first failure. <code>self.parallel([...])</code> Runs the children at the same time and collects every failure. Another component, such as <code>FanSuite(name=\"fan A\")</code> Runs that component's own lifecycle. See Components nest. <p>The sequence or parallel that <code>build_suite</code> returns needs no name. It takes the component's own, the one given as <code>FanSuite(name=\"fan A\")</code>. One placed inside another block is named first, as a test is: <code>self.parallel(\"rails\", [...])</code>. See Composition Factories.</p>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#the-same-thing-in-code","title":"The same thing in code","text":"<p>A fan test: power it on, check its speed and its current draw, power it off. This is the component both charts describe.</p> <pre><code>from typing import Any\n\nfrom hilt import PASS, TestComponent, TestFailure, TestUtils, Verdict, evidence\n\n\nclass _Fan:\n    \"\"\"A pretend fan so this file runs on any machine. Swap in your real device.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -&gt; None:\n        self.powered = False\n\n    def power_on(self) -&gt; None:\n        self.powered = True\n\n    def power_off(self) -&gt; None:\n        self.powered = False\n\n    def read_rpm(self) -&gt; int:\n        return 2000 if self.powered else 0\n\n    def read_current_ma(self) -&gt; int:\n        return 180 if self.powered else 0\n\n\nclass FanSuite(TestComponent):\n    # Before the lifecycle: describe the test. No hardware yet.\n    def __init__(self, *, name: str, min_rpm: int = 1800, max_current_ma: int = 250) -&gt; None:\n        super().__init__(name=name)\n        self._min_rpm = min_rpm\n        self._max_current_ma = max_current_ma\n\n    # LIFECYCLE 1 of 3: set up the device, then return what the tests will need.\n    async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n        fan = _Fan()\n        fan.power_on()\n        try:\n            await utils.wait_for(lambda: fan.read_rpm() &gt; 0, looking_for=\"fan spinning\")\n        except TestFailure:\n            fan.power_off()  # a failed configure hands cleanup nothing, so undo it here\n            raise\n        return {\"fan\": fan, \"min_rpm\": self._min_rpm, \"max_current_ma\": self._max_current_ma}\n\n    # LIFECYCLE 2 of 3: return the suite. Name each test and hand over its method.\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return self.sequence(\n            [\n                self.test(\"fan reaches minimum speed\", self._reaches_minimum_speed),\n                self.test(\"fan current is within limit\", self._current_within_limit),\n            ]\n        )\n\n    # LIFECYCLE 3 of 3: put the device back. Runs even if configure or a test failed.\n    async def cleanup(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; None:\n        fan = utils.ctx.get(\"fan\")\n        if fan is not None:\n            fan.power_off()\n\n    # Test methods are not lifecycle steps. build_suite hands each one to self.test(),\n    # and HILT calls it when it runs the suite.\n    @evidence(\"rpm\")\n    async def _reaches_minimum_speed(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n        rpm = utils.ctx[\"fan\"].read_rpm()\n        minimum = utils.ctx[\"min_rpm\"]\n        if rpm &gt;= minimum:\n            return PASS({\"rpm\": rpm})\n        raise TestFailure(\n            \"fan below minimum speed\", f\"&gt;= {minimum} rpm\", f\"{rpm} rpm\", evidence={\"rpm\": rpm}\n        )\n\n    @evidence(\"current_ma\")\n    async def _current_within_limit(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n        current_ma = utils.ctx[\"fan\"].read_current_ma()\n        limit = utils.ctx[\"max_current_ma\"]\n        if current_ma &lt;= limit:\n            return PASS({\"current_ma\": current_ma})\n        raise TestFailure(\n            \"fan draws too much current\",\n            f\"&lt;= {limit} mA\",\n            f\"{current_ma} mA\",\n            evidence={\"current_ma\": current_ma},\n        )\n</code></pre> <p>Save it as <code>fan_suite.py</code> and run <code>uv run hilt run fan_suite.py</code>. It ends with <code>hilt run: FanSuite: Pass</code>.</p>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#what-goes-where","title":"What goes where","text":"Method Put here Keep out Before the lifecycle <code>__init__</code> Name, limits, addresses. Anything known up front. Anything that touches the device. Lifecycle 1 <code>configure</code> Power-up, connections, waiting until ready. Return a dict of what the tests need. Checks that deserve their own line in the report. Lifecycle 2 <code>build_suite</code> The tests, by name, in order. Any work. It is a plain <code>def</code> and reads no hardware. Lifecycle 3 <code>cleanup</code> Power-down, restoring settings. Raising errors. Reading ctx with <code>[]</code>. Used by the suite Test method One measurement and the comparison that judges it. Setup or teardown for other tests. <p>Three rules cover most first-week mistakes:</p> <ul> <li><code>utils.ctx</code> holds what <code>configure</code> returned. That is how values reach the test   methods. It is read-only after <code>configure</code>; a test hands its readings back with   <code>PASS({...})</code>.</li> <li><code>self</code> is frozen after <code>__init__</code>. Setting <code>self.something</code> later raises an   error. Use ctx instead.</li> <li><code>cleanup</code> reads ctx with <code>.get()</code>. It also runs when <code>configure</code> failed, and   then ctx holds nothing <code>configure</code> would have returned. So a <code>configure</code> that fails   partway undoes what it started, the way the example powers the fan back off.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#components-nest","title":"Components nest","text":"<p>A <code>TestComponent</code> is a self-contained block: it knows how to set itself up, what to run, and how to clean up after itself. Bigger tests are built by putting blocks inside blocks, the way an assembly is built from subassemblies. A component placed in a suite runs its own complete lifecycle there.</p> <pre><code>class RackSuite(TestComponent):\n    def build_suite(self) -&gt; TestComponent:\n        return self.sequence([FanSuite(name=\"fan A\"), FanSuite(name=\"fan B\")])\n</code></pre> <pre><code>flowchart TD\n    rc[\"`**RackSuite: configure**`\"]\n    rb[[\"`**RackSuite: build_suite**\n    returns a sequence of two components`\"]]\n    subgraph a[\"Component: FanSuite, fan A\"]\n        direction LR\n        a1[\"configure\"] ==&gt; a2[[\"build_suite\"]] ==&gt; a3[\"cleanup\"]\n    end\n    subgraph b[\"Component: FanSuite, fan B\"]\n        direction LR\n        b1[\"configure\"] ==&gt; b2[[\"build_suite\"]] ==&gt; b3[\"cleanup\"]\n    end\n    rk[\"`**RackSuite: cleanup**`\"]\n\n    rc ==&gt; rb\n    rb ==&gt;|\"first\"| a\n    a ==&gt;|\"then\"| b\n    b ==&gt; rk\n\n    classDef step fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#1d4ed8,stroke-width:2px,color:#0b1f44\n    class rc,rb,rk,a1,a2,a3,b1,b2,b3 step</code></pre> <p><code>RackSuite</code> defines only <code>build_suite</code>: <code>configure</code> and <code>cleanup</code> are optional, for a component with nothing of its own to set up or put back.</p> <p>Each component has its own ctx. The tests inside one component share it; a component nested in another does not see what the outer one's <code>configure</code> returned.</p>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#when-something-goes-wrong","title":"When something goes wrong","text":"<p>For the fan example, whose suite is a sequence:</p> What happened Suite runs? <code>cleanup</code> runs? Result A test method raises <code>TestFailure</code> Up to the failure Yes <code>Fail</code>: the claim did not hold <code>configure</code> raises <code>TestFailure</code> (a <code>wait_for</code> timeout does) No Yes <code>Fail</code>: the device never became testable <code>configure</code> or a test method raises any other exception Up to the exception Yes <code>Fail</code>, flagged as a bug in the suite A test method ends without <code>return PASS</code> Up to that test Yes <code>Aborted</code>: no verdict was given <p>In a <code>self.parallel</code>, one test failing does not stop the others.</p> <p><code>TestFailure</code> is a verdict: the device did not pass. Whether the cause is the hardware or the bench around it is for the person at the station to work out. Any other exception means the suite itself has a bug. Catch what the device can do wrong and raise <code>TestFailure</code> for it.</p>"},{"location":"lifecycle.html#going-further","title":"Going further","text":"<ul> <li>Watching the device during a run. Surveys snapshot the device at each lifecycle   boundary, and audits check it on a timer while the suite runs. Both are registered   in <code>__init__</code>; see <code>examples/hardware_device_lifecycle.py</code> and idioms.</li> <li>The exact guarantees. Contracts is the precise statement of what   the framework promises, starting with the   Lifecycle Contract. This page is the short   version; where the two differ, the contract is right.</li> <li>Authoring patterns. Idioms covers time budgets, evidence, typed   ctx, and SSH.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"measurements.html","title":"Parametric Measurements","text":"<p>Design note for #85, signed off by Ty on the issue 2026-09-05 and implemented under</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#86-this-page-owns-the-protocol-docsinspector-jsonmd-owns-the-records-place-in","title":"86. This page owns the protocol; <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> owns the record's place in","text":"<p><code>result.json</code>.</p> <p>A test method already hands its readings back with the verdict (<code>PASS(evidence)</code>, <code>docs/idioms.md</code>). That record answers the Floor Technician's question: did the unit meet the claim. It does not answer the process engineer's question: where inside the window did this unit land, and where are the last five hundred landing. That question needs a number with a unit and limits, in a shape a statistical consumer can read without the suite source. <code>utils.measure</code> records that number, in one call, without touching the verdict.</p> <p>The rule this note is built on: a limit is evidence, never a verdict. A measurement outside its limits is recorded as such, beside the verdict; the verdict stays whatever the gated <code>PASS</code> says. A <code>Pass</code> keeps meaning exactly what it has always meant, and the framework forges nothing.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#the-call","title":"The call","text":"<p><code>utils.measure</code> is the documented home. It is a method on <code>TestUtils</code>, the scoped service object every lifecycle method already receives, so a measurement is scoped to its node the way a log line is.</p> <pre><code>def measure(\n    self,\n    name: str,\n    value: int | float | None,\n    *,\n    unit: str | None = None,\n    limits: tuple[float | None, float | None] | None = None,\n) -&gt; Measurement: ...\n</code></pre> <p>It is synchronous. It records and returns; it performs no I/O, so there is nothing to await and no place for a cancellation to land between the reading and the record.</p> <p><code>name</code> \u2014 a non-empty <code>str</code>, unique within the node. The name is the identity the downstream consumer keys on, together with the node path (see The export contract): for the life of the suite it names the same quantity in the same unit. Use the same spelling discipline as evidence keys (<code>output_voltage_v</code>, <code>boot_time_s</code>). Recording the same name twice in one node is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the node and the name; the second reading is not silently appended, because a consumer that sees two <code>output_voltage_v</code> rows for one node cannot tell a retry from a bug.</p> <p><code>value</code> \u2014 an <code>int</code> or a finite <code>float</code>, or <code>None</code> for a reading the suite tried to take and could not. <code>bool</code> is refused (it is an <code>int</code> to Python and a category to a process engineer), as are <code>NaN</code> and the infinities, for the same reason <code>run_data</code> refuses them: a JSON consumer cannot carry them. A textual observation (<code>ftl_state: \"running\"</code>) is evidence on the verdict, not a measurement; SPC and Cpk are defined on numbers, and this protocol does not pretend otherwise.</p> <p><code>None</code> and <code>NaN</code> are kept apart on purpose (issue #159, bench finding F10). A blank answer over a shell is a fact about the device, and the record is where the Floor Technician should find it; a <code>NaN</code> can only come from arithmetic in the suite, and that is a bug the <code>ContractViolation</code> names. Before #159 every reading parsed from a shell needed its own <code>try</code> to turn the blank into a failure; now the parse hands <code>measure</code> the <code>None</code> and the gate below does the rest.</p> <p><code>unit</code> \u2014 a free string the developer chooses (<code>\"V\"</code>, <code>\"ms\"</code>, <code>\"\u00b0C\"</code>), or <code>None</code> for a dimensionless count. The framework validates nothing about it and never converts; it is a label the consumer reads back. That is why the name must not change meaning: a name that was volts in one release and millivolts in the next breaks every chart built on it.</p> <p><code>limits</code> \u2014 <code>None</code>, or a <code>(low, high)</code> tuple, inclusive at both ends, with <code>None</code> for an open side: <code>(4.75, 5.25)</code>, <code>(None, 250.0)</code>, <code>(0, None)</code>. A tuple is enough: it reads as the window it is, and it puts no new type into a public signature. Both sides <code>None</code>, a bound that is not a finite number, or <code>low &gt; high</code> is a <code>ContractViolation</code> at the call \u2014 a limit that bounds nothing is a bug in the suite, not a wide window. With <code>limits=None</code> the measurement is recorded for trend and <code>in_limits</code> is <code>null</code>.</p> <p>The return is a frozen <code>Measurement</code> record: <code>name</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>unit</code>, <code>limits</code>, <code>in_limits</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, exactly what was recorded. It exists so the developer can gate on it in plain sight:</p> <pre><code>@evidence(\"output_voltage_v\")\nasync def _check_rail(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n    v = await self._read_rail(utils, utils.ctx[\"ip\"])\n    rail = utils.measure(\"output_voltage_v\", v, unit=\"V\", limits=(4.75, 5.25))\n    if rail.in_limits:\n        return PASS({\"output_voltage_v\": v})\n    raise TestFailure(\n        \"5V rail out of window\", \"4.75..5.25 V\", f\"{v} V\", evidence={\"output_voltage_v\": v}\n    )\n</code></pre> <p>The framework evaluated the limit once, the developer chose what to do about it, and <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> can see the predicate beside the <code>PASS</code>. Whether an out-of-window reading is a <code>TestFailure</code>, a warning to trend, or a reason to re-seat the probe and read again is the suite author's decision, taken in the suite.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#a-reading-not-taken","title":"A reading not taken","text":"<p><code>measure(name, None, ...)</code> records that the suite tried and the device gave nothing. The verdict rule for it follows from the one above and adds nothing to it:</p> <ul> <li>A missing reading is never inside its window. With <code>limits</code>, <code>in_limits</code> is   <code>false</code>, so the gate in the example fails the claim however it is spelled   (<code>if rail.in_limits:</code> or <code>if rail.in_limits is False:</code>). The claim then says what the   hole means, usually a <code>TestFailure</code> naming the command that answered blank. Without   <code>limits</code>, <code>in_limits</code> is <code>null</code>, as for any trend reading.</li> <li>The framework still changes nothing. A limit is evidence, never a verdict, and a   hole is no different: a claim that gates on the reading fails through its own gate; a   trend-only reading leaves the verdict where the claim put it. A <code>Pass</code> beside a   <code>value: null</code> means the claim did not gate on that reading, and that is the claim   author's decision, visible in the record.</li> <li>The record keeps the entry. The reading sits in <code>measurements</code> in call order with   <code>value: null</code>, so the consumer sees a hole in the series where there was one, and the   Floor Technician sees the name of what was not taken rather than a shorter array.</li> </ul> <pre><code>@evidence(\"root_used_pct\")\nasync def _root_filesystem(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; Verdict:\n    result = await utils.exec(\"df --output=pcent / | tail -n 1\")\n    used = _number(result.stdout.rstrip().rstrip(\"%\"))  # float | None; no try here\n    reading = utils.measure(\"root_used_pct\", used, unit=\"%\", limits=(None, 90))\n    if reading.value is None:\n        raise TestFailure(\"df gave no percentage\", \"N%\", result.stdout.strip())\n    if reading.in_limits:\n        return PASS({\"root_used_pct\": reading.value})\n    raise TestFailure(\"root filesystem too full\", \"&lt;= 90 %\", f\"{reading.value:g} %\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"measurements.html#where-it-may-be-called","title":"Where it may be called","text":"<p><code>configure</code> and the test method. Those are the two slots that produce the node's record: <code>configure</code> provides ctx, the test method produces the verdict, and both receive the node's own <code>TestUtils</code>. A bring-up reading (ambient at start, supply voltage before the DUT is powered) belongs to <code>configure</code>; a reading under test belongs to the test method. A composite node (<code>sequence</code>, <code>parallel</code>) has a <code>configure</code> and may measure there.</p> <p>Not from <code>cleanup</code>, which restores state and produces no evidence (<code>hilt/no-verdict-in-cleanup</code> is the same rule from the verdict's side). Not from surveys or audits: they already have an artifact channel for what they observe, and an audit's samples are a time series with its own semantics, which this protocol does not define. Not from integration hooks, which are deliberately denied <code>utils</code>. A call from any of those, or after the node has settled, is a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming the node, in the pattern of <code>ContextImmutable</code>: the record is sealed at the same moment <code>run_data</code> is snapshotted, and nothing writes into a sealed record.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#the-package-root-alias","title":"The package-root alias","text":"<p>Ty's condition on the approval: <code>from hilt import measure</code> and <code>hilt.measure(...)</code> must both work. They do, with the same signature and the same rules, and <code>utils.measure</code> remains the home the documentation teaches. The alias is convenience, not a second mechanism.</p> <p>The alias has to find the current node's <code>TestUtils</code> without a global, because a <code>parallel</code> node runs its children concurrently in one process and a global would hand one child's reading to another. The mechanism is a <code>contextvars.ContextVar</code> that the framework binds around each slot that admits measurements, at the seam in <code>src/hilt/base.py</code> where the child boundary is handed <code>fn(utils)</code> for <code>configure</code> and the test method: <code>set</code> before the boundary runs the slot, <code>reset</code> in <code>finally</code>. A <code>ContextVar</code> is task-local, and asyncio copies the context into every task it creates, so each child of a <code>parallel</code> node sees its own binding and a gateway parent sees its own inside its own slots. <code>hilt.measure</code> reads the variable and delegates to that <code>TestUtils.measure</code>; when nothing is bound \u2014 module scope, a hook, <code>cleanup</code>, a helper called from outside a run \u2014 it raises <code>ContractViolation</code> saying that <code>measure</code> was called outside a running node and naming <code>utils.measure</code> inside <code>configure</code> or a test method as the fix. The self-tests hold the alias to the method: every guard above fires identically through either spelling.</p> <p>A <code>TestUtils</code> is not otherwise reachable from the package root; the variable is private to the framework, is never exported, and is read by nothing else.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#the-recorded-shape","title":"The recorded shape","text":"<p>Each call appends one object to the node's <code>measurements</code> array, in call order:</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"name\": \"output_voltage_v\",\n  \"value\": 5.02,\n  \"unit\": \"V\",\n  \"limits\": { \"low\": 4.75, \"high\": 5.25 },\n  \"in_limits\": true,\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T21:14:03.118402Z\"\n}\n</code></pre> Field JSON type Meaning <code>name</code> string The measurement's identity within the node; unique in the array. <code>value</code> number or null The reading, exactly as passed; null when the suite tried to take it and could not. A consumer charting the series skips null: it is a hole, not a zero. <code>unit</code> string or null The developer's label. Never converted. <code>limits</code> object or null <code>{ \"low\": number or null, \"high\": number or null }</code>, inclusive; null when the call declared none. <code>in_limits</code> boolean or null <code>low &lt;= value &lt;= high</code> with an open side always satisfied; false when <code>value</code> is null and <code>limits</code> is not; null when <code>limits</code> is null. Evidence, not a verdict. <code>timestamp</code> string When the reading was recorded, UTC ending in <code>Z</code>, the same form the timeline uses. <p>The node path, the run, and the station are not repeated in every entry. They are properties of the node and the run directory the array sits in, and repeating them would invite a consumer to trust the copy over the tree.</p> <p>In two lines: <code>measurements</code> is an array, one object per <code>measure</code> call in call order, each <code>{name, value: number | null, unit, limits: {low, high} | null, in_limits: bool | null, timestamp}</code>. It appears on the node's <code>run_data</code> and on the node's <code>summary_tree</code> entry; node path comes from the tree, run id from the run directory.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#where-it-appears-in-resultjson","title":"Where it appears in <code>result.json</code>","text":"<p>Two places, additive within result <code>format_version</code> 1 \u2014 the number does not bump.</p> <ol> <li><code>run_data[\"measurements\"]</code> on the node's own <code>RunResult</code>, the slot reserved    since Phase 2, now filled. The existing guards stay: an authored ctx entry named    <code>measurements</code> cannot populate or replace it, and an evidence key of that name is    still an <code>EvidenceViolation</code>.</li> <li><code>measurements</code> on every <code>summary_tree</code> node, beside <code>evidence</code> and    <code>evidence_declared</code>. This placement is load-bearing, not decoration. <code>result.json</code>    is the root <code>RunResult.to_json()</code>, and its <code>run_data</code> is the root's ctx view; a    leaf's <code>run_data</code> reaches only that leaf's <code>RunResult</code>, which the root record does    not carry. Without the tree field a leaf's measurements would never reach the file    the Floor Technician and the SPC consumer open. The precedent is <code>evidence</code> on the    tree node (#118) and <code>log_path</code> before it (#106): a per-node fact is published on the    per-node tree entry.</li> </ol> <p><code>measurements</code> on a tree node is an array on every node that ran (empty when it    measured nothing) and <code>null</code> on a node that never ran, matching <code>state</code>.</p> <p>Adding a field to a framework-owned object keeps the version under rule 1 of the versioning policy in <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code>; filling a slot the record already declares open is likewise additive. The self-tests close every framework-owned object against the schema, so the schema amendment lands in the same MR that first emits a measurement, as rule 5 requires. The amendment to <code>docs/schema/result-v1.schema.json</code> is a new <code>$defs/measurement</code> and two references to it:</p> <pre><code>\"measurement\": {\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"name\", \"value\", \"unit\", \"limits\", \"in_limits\", \"timestamp\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"name\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1 },\n    \"value\": { \"type\": [\"number\", \"null\"] },\n    \"unit\": { \"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"] },\n    \"limits\": {\n      \"anyOf\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"object\",\n          \"required\": [\"low\", \"high\"],\n          \"properties\": {\n            \"low\": { \"type\": [\"number\", \"null\"] },\n            \"high\": { \"type\": [\"number\", \"null\"] }\n          },\n          \"additionalProperties\": false\n        },\n        { \"type\": \"null\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"in_limits\": { \"type\": [\"boolean\", \"null\"] },\n    \"timestamp\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"format\": \"date-time\",\n      \"description\": \"Timezone-aware UTC timestamp ending in 'Z', matching the timeline's own timestamp field.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"additionalProperties\": true,\n  \"description\": \"One utils.measure call. in_limits is evidence: it never decides the node's state.\"\n}\n</code></pre> <p><code>result-v1.schema.json</code> has no shared <code>utcTimestamp</code> <code>$def</code> of its own \u2014 that definition lives in <code>docs/schema/timeline-v1.schema.json</code>, and the two schemas are not cross- referenced. <code>measurement.timestamp</code> therefore states the same <code>date-time</code> shape inline rather than inventing a cross-file <code>$ref</code> the published-schema policy does not use elsewhere.</p> <p><code>run_data.properties.measurements</code> gains <code>\"items\": { \"$ref\": \"#/$defs/measurement\" }</code> and drops the \"empty in version 1\" wording; <code>summaryTreeNode</code> gains <code>\"measurements\": { \"anyOf\": [{ \"type\": \"array\", \"items\": { \"$ref\": \"#/$defs/measurement\" } }, { \"type\": \"null\" }] }</code> and adds it to <code>required</code>. The <code>measurement</code> object stays open, like every framework-owned object, so a field added later within version 1 is accepted by a consumer validating with this file.</p> <p><code>value</code> became nullable on 2026-10-20 (issue #159), in place and under version 1. Rule 2 of the versioning policy would bump the number for a nullability change; the policy's own pre-1.0.0 clause lets the record change in place because no consumer exists to break, and Ty ruled on the issue that this is the time to make such changes. From 1.0.0 the same change would be a version 2.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#evidence-never-a-verdict","title":"Evidence, never a verdict","text":"<p>The framework evaluates the limit and writes <code>in_limits</code>. It does not raise, does not change <code>state</code>, does not add a failure, and does not warn the boundary. The three terminal states keep their meanings (<code>docs/contracts.md</code>, Terminal State Contract): <code>Pass</code> means a test method returned a <code>Verdict</code> through a gate the developer wrote, and an <code>in_limits: false</code> beside a <code>Pass</code> means that gate did not include this limit. That can be right \u2014 a trend-only measurement, a limit still being characterised \u2014 and it can be a suite defect; either way it is the suite author's decision, visible in the record, and not something the framework second-guesses.</p> <p>Because a measurement is independent of the verdict, it is sealed whatever the state. An aborted leaf contributes no evidence, since evidence travels with a verdict that was never produced; the measurements it recorded before the cut were timestamped and held by the framework at the moment of the call, and they are published with the node. The consumer reads them beside <code>state</code> and decides what an Aborted node's numbers are worth to a capability index. Feeding them in blind is the consumer's mistake, not a gap in the record.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#what-the-floor-technician-reads","title":"What the Floor Technician reads","text":"<p>In <code>result.json</code>, walk <code>summary_tree</code> to the claim and read its <code>measurements</code> array. <code>docs/floor-technician.md</code> gains one row for it: The numbers this claim recorded, with their windows. An entry with <code>in_limits: false</code> is a reading outside its window. Read <code>state</code> beside it: it is not a Fail unless <code>state</code> says Fail.</p> <code>state</code> <code>in_limits</code> on an entry What it means What you do <code>Fail</code> <code>false</code> The claim named that window in its check and the unit missed it. <code>evidence</code> on the same node usually carries the same reading. Hardware. Follow the station protocol for the failure name; the entry tells you by how much. <code>Pass</code> <code>false</code> The claim recorded the number for trend and did not gate on it, or the window is not yet a pass criterion. Ship on the verdict. Do not scrap the unit on this line alone; route the reading to the process engineer. <code>Pass</code> or <code>Fail</code> <code>null</code> Recorded without a window. Trend only. Nothing to act on here. any <code>false</code> or <code>null</code>, with <code>value: null</code> The claim tried to take this reading and the device gave nothing. Not a reading of zero. Read <code>state</code> and <code>failure</code> beside it: a <code>Fail</code> names what answered blank. A <code>Pass</code> means the claim did not gate on it; route the hole to the process engineer, not to scrap. <code>Aborted</code> anything The number was taken before the run stopped; no verdict exists. Follow the Aborted protocol for the failure. The measurement is context, not a category. <p>In the timeline, every call writes one scoped line so the chronology carries the number next to what the suite was doing when it took it:</p> <pre><code>measurement: output_voltage_v = 5.02 V limits=[4.75, 5.25] in_limits=true\nmeasurement: output_voltage_v = 5.31 V limits=[4.75, 5.25] in_limits=false\nmeasurement: output_voltage_v not taken limits=[4.75, 5.25] in_limits=false\n</code></pre> <p>An in-window or unlimited reading logs at <code>info</code>; an out-of-window reading, or one not taken, logs at <code>warning</code>, so it stands out in <code>timeline.log</code> without the FT filtering for it. The not-taken line says so in words rather than printing <code>= null V</code>, which reads as a number missing its digits. The line is a log entry and nothing more: it is not the record (the array is), and it does not change the node's state.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#relationship-to-evidence-and-passevidence","title":"Relationship to <code>@evidence</code> and <code>PASS(evidence)</code>","text":"<p>A measurement is not an evidence key. The two records answer different questions, are validated by different contracts, and live in different fields:</p> <ul> <li><code>evidence</code> is what the verdict carried, validated at the child boundary against the   <code>@evidence</code> declaration and the reserved keys, and <code>null</code> when there was no verdict.   Its shape is the developer's mapping.</li> <li><code>measurements</code> is what the node recorded, validated at each call, sealed with the   node whatever its state. Its shape is fixed by this note.</li> </ul> <p>Recording a measurement therefore does not satisfy an <code>@evidence</code> declaration, and a declared key absent from <code>PASS</code> is still <code>EvidenceViolation.not_carried</code> no matter how many measurements share its name. A developer who wants a reading held to the declaration names it in both places, as the rail example above does: <code>utils.measure</code> for the process engineer, <code>PASS({...})</code> for the claim. That is one extra name in a dict literal, and it keeps each record honest on its own terms. The alternative \u2014 merging measurements into <code>evidence</code> automatically \u2014 was considered and rejected: it would make the boundary's evidence check depend on calls made earlier in <code>configure</code>, would let a measurement shadow a ctx key by another route, and would give the SPC consumer two shapes for one number.</p> <p>The one interaction that exists is the reserved name. <code>measurements</code> is not a legal evidence key and not a ctx key that reaches <code>run_data</code>; both guards predate this note and stay.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#the-export-contract","title":"The export contract","text":"<p>The downstream consumer is an SPC or Cpk tool reading a directory of run directories, or the after-each integrator forwarding each <code>RunResult</code> to a database. What it needs, and where each part lives in the record, is fixed here so the consumer can be written against this page and the schema alone:</p> The consumer needs Where it is Stability A stable name <code>measurements[].name</code>, unique within the node The developer's promise; a rename is a new series. A unit <code>measurements[].unit</code> A label, never converted. Changing it under a name breaks the series. A numeric value <code>measurements[].value</code> A JSON number, or <code>null</code> when the reading was not taken (#159); never a string, boolean, or non-finite. The consumer skips <code>null</code> in a series: a hole, not a zero. The limits it was judged against <code>measurements[].limits.low</code> / <code>.high</code>, or null Inclusive; an open side is null. Whether it was inside <code>measurements[].in_limits</code> Evidence; the consumer does not recompute the verdict from it. A timestamp <code>measurements[].timestamp</code> UTC, <code>Z</code>-terminated, same form as the timeline. Depends on the station clock rule in <code>docs/floor-technician.md</code>. The node path The walk from the root of <code>summary_tree</code> to the node; <code>ancestor_names</code> on a per-node <code>RunResult</code> Durable identity. Names may contain <code>/</code>; do not parse <code>bread_crumbs</code>. The run id The run directory name, <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT/&lt;timestamp&gt;[.n]</code>, published by the root timeline's <code>result</code> pointer as the directory of <code>artifact_path</code> One directory per <code>run()</code>, collision-safe; <code>result.json</code> does not repeat it because a consumer has already opened the directory to read the file. The station <code>physical_location</code> on the root <code>PHYSICAL_LOCATION</code>, or <code>unknown</code>. The node's verdict <code>state</code> on the same tree node The consumer keys on it before it aggregates. <p>A series is <code>(station, node path, name)</code>. Everything the consumer aggregates over is in <code>result.json</code> or in the directory name; it never needs the suite source, the live objects, or the timeline, though the timeline carries the same numbers in order for a human.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#tiers","title":"Tiers","text":"<p>The tier-1 guards live in the framework, in <code>TestUtils.measure</code> and the package-root alias, and every one of them is a <code>ContractViolation</code> (developer bug, <code>bug: true</code>, <code>banner: CONTRACT VIOLATION</code>) naming the node and, where there is one, the measurement:</p> Guard Fires when Duplicate name The node already recorded that name. Not numeric <code>value</code> is a <code>bool</code>, a <code>str</code>, <code>NaN</code>, an infinity, or anything that is not <code>int</code>/<code>float</code>. <code>None</code> is not refused: it is the reading not taken (#159). Invalid limits Both sides <code>None</code>, a non-finite bound, or <code>low &gt; high</code>. Outside a node <code>hilt.measure</code> with nothing bound, or either spelling from <code>cleanup</code>, a survey, an audit, a hook, or after the node settled. <p>They are raised by a <code>ContractViolation</code> subclass in <code>src/hilt/errors.py</code> that is not exported, on the precedent of <code>ContextInsufficient</code> and <code>ContextImmutable</code>: the FT reads the banner, <code>node</code>, and a <code>measurement</code> field naming the reading; the developer follows the class name from the log. Nothing protecting the record lives only in tier 3.</p> <p>Tier-3 rules for <code>hilt-check</code>, each mirroring a guard above so the finding reaches the developer's CI before it reaches a station log:</p> Rule Flags Mirrors Status <code>hilt/measurement-name-unique</code> Two <code>measure(...)</code> calls with the same literal name inside one <code>configure</code> or test method. Tier 1: duplicate name. Shipped (#132). <code>hilt/measurement-in-node</code> A <code>measure(...)</code> call, either spelling, inside <code>cleanup</code>, a survey or audit function, a <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code>, or at module scope. Tier 1: outside a node. Shipped (#133). <p>Neither was part of #86; each was raised as its own issue per <code>docs/hilt-check.md</code> (Adding a rule) and both now ship. The <code>-&gt; Verdict</code> and <code>hilt/gated-pass</code> rules already cover the gate a developer writes on <code>.in_limits</code>.</p>"},{"location":"measurements.html#what-86-delivers","title":"What #86 delivers","text":"<p>The acceptance criteria this note fixes for the implementation issue:</p> <ol> <li><code>TestUtils.measure</code> with the signature above, synchronous, returning a frozen    <code>Measurement</code>; <code>measure</code> and <code>Measurement</code> added to <code>hilt.__all__</code>, the reference    table in <code>docs/api-stability.md</code> (rows in order), <code>docs/api.md</code>, and <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>    together, as <code>docs/api-stability.md</code> (Changing the surface) requires. Two names,    not three: the violation class stays private.</li> <li><code>hilt.measure</code> at the package root, bound through a <code>ContextVar</code> around    <code>configure</code> and the test method, <code>ContractViolation</code> when nothing is bound; the    self-tests exercise every guard through both spellings and prove a <code>parallel</code> node's    children do not see each other's binding.</li> <li>The four tier-1 guards above, each a <code>ContractViolation</code> naming node and    measurement, with a self-test per guard.</li> <li>The recorded shape, exactly as the table above states it, in call order, sealed when    the node settles, on every state including Aborted.</li> <li><code>run_data[\"measurements\"]</code> filled on the node's <code>RunResult</code>; <code>measurements</code> on every    <code>summary_tree</code> node (<code>null</code> when the node never ran); both under result    <code>format_version</code> 1, unbumped.</li> <li><code>docs/schema/result-v1.schema.json</code> amended as above in the same MR;    <code>tests/test_inspector_schema.py</code> still closes every framework-owned object.</li> <li>The timeline line, <code>info</code> in window and <code>warning</code> out of it.</li> <li>Documentation reconciled in the same MR, replacing every \"reserved and empty\"    sentence: the <code>run_data</code> row and the \"Version 1 does not define measurement    entries\" paragraph in <code>docs/inspector-json.md</code> plus a <code>measurements</code> row in its    summary-tree table; the <code>run_data</code> and summary-tree rows in    <code>docs/floor-technician.md</code> and the table in What the Floor Technician reads    above; the reserved-slot bullets in the Evidence Contract of <code>docs/contracts.md</code>    and a Measurement Contract stating the guards; the evidence section of    <code>docs/idioms.md</code> and a short Measurements idiom; the <code>TestUtils</code> row of the    reference table. This page then drops its Status header and becomes the owning    doc.</li> <li>The pi-hole dogfood (<code>examples/pi_hole_server.py</code>) records at least one real    measurement with limits and gates on <code>.in_limits</code> in plain sight; <code>hilt-check    examples</code> stays clean.</li> <li>Coverage floors hold or rise; <code>make check</code> green locally.</li> </ol> <p>Out of scope for #86, each a future issue if wanted: audit-sample time series, aggregation of any kind, unit conversion, a <code>run_id</code> field on the root record, and the two tier-3 rules.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html","title":"HILT \u2014 Project Overview","text":"<p>Philosophy: \"The act of running a HILT Test is the act of producing irrefutable evidence.\"</p> <p>\"Happy path\" scripts work 99% of the time but cause 100% of the line stoppages because they fail ambiguously. HILT forces the SE to handle the \"Sad Path\" (Infra vs. Hardware vs. Integration) at design time.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#what-it-is","title":"What It Is","text":"<p>An opinionated manufacturing test framework built in Python (asyncio). It seeks to implement empathetic architecture by addressing SE \u2192 tech communication issues upstream via a pit-of-success model.</p> <p>This is the Python port of the TypeScript original (<code>../hilt-test-legacy/</code>, read-only reference). Parity with the original is explicitly not a goal; the contracts are carried forward, the enforcement is stronger.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#how-it-does-that","title":"How it does that","text":"<p>HILT gives the SEs a framework that allows them to focus on getting the test right and logging meaningful evidence. It does this by reducing cognitive load on the SE as much as it possibly can. The SE declares their intent and HILT collects and organizes structured evidence. Much like a real hilt test, the act of executing the test is the act of producing evidence. full stop. The SEs capture meaningful information, and the hilt inspector guarantees that a tech is able to turn that data into information at 2am.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-hilt-success-metric","title":"The HILT Success Metric","text":"<p>A Floor Technician (the \"FT\" of the source docstrings) can accurately categorize a failure and initiate the correct recovery protocol within 60 seconds of opening the Inspector, without needing to Slack the Software Engineer.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-core-purpose","title":"The Core Purpose","text":"<p>\"We are giving developers the tools they need to give floor operators the tools THEY need, and doing so without additional cognitive load on the developer.\"</p> <p>Two-level service contract: 1. Framework \u2192 Developer: lifecycle guarantees, automatic evidence collection, structured failure signals \u2014 developer focuses on test logic, not plumbing. 2. Developer \u2192 Floor Technician: clear names, hardware-domain failure messages, scoped artifacts in the inspector tree \u2014 tech makes confident judgment calls without escalating to a software engineer.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-primary-beneficiary-floor-technician","title":"The Primary Beneficiary: Floor Technician","text":""},{"location":"overview.html#floor-technicians-simultaneously-have-the-biggest-impact-on-the-success-of-our-business-and-the-least-control-over-how-it-operates","title":"\"Floor technicians simultaneously have the biggest impact on the success of our business, and the least control over how it operates\"","text":"<p>Typically early-career technology enthusiast. Passionate, capable, but may be operating at the edge of their knowledge. They are the first person to see failures and to make failure category judgment calls. They are the experts on how to handle any given failure mode. The inspector is their primary tool \u2014 and also a containment layer preventing them from going places they shouldn't. The station page is <code>docs/floor-technician.md</code> (Floor Technician guide): how to read <code>result.json</code> and what to do next, without opening the suite source.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-atom","title":"The Atom","text":"<p><code>TestComponent</code> is the irreducible unit of the manufacturing test problem domain.</p> <ul> <li>Does not know or care what the DUT is</li> <li>Does not know what stage it's running at</li> <li>Does not know what surrounds it in the composition</li> <li>Only knows: <code>configure</code> \u2192 <code>build_suite</code> \u2192 <code>cleanup</code></li> </ul> <p>A rack-level L11 test is not a different kind of thing \u2014 it is more atoms composed together. Leaves, sequences, parallel nodes, and full hardware devices are all instances of <code>TestComponent</code>; the framework is fractal. A <code>TestComponent</code> is simultaneously a claim about hardware and a procedure for verifying that claim: evidence at scale N\u22121 becomes a verdict at scale N. The DUT tree and the test tree are one structure.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-chemistry-mental-model","title":"The Chemistry Mental Model","text":"Level Framework Construct Atom <code>TestComponent</code> (leaf via <code>self.test()</code>) Molecule <code>self.sequence()</code> (ordered, bails on failure), <code>self.parallel()</code> (concurrent, collects all) Organelle <code>TestComponent</code> subclass (encapsulates state + lifecycle, produces atoms via <code>build_suite()</code>) Organism Full rack/system-level suite Emulsion L10+JBOG/JBOD \u2014 two molecules behaving as unified system under test <p>Key property: No level needs to know about the levels above it. Composition is the only coupling mechanism and the framework is contractually obligated to provide it.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-three-terminal-states","title":"The Three Terminal States","text":"<p>Every run of a <code>TestComponent</code> resolves to one of three peer states \u2014 no silent exits, no hidden fourth category:</p> <ol> <li>Pass \u2014 the test method returned <code>PASS</code> explicitly, bare or as <code>PASS(evidence)</code> carrying its readings. Cannot accidentally pass: the framework checks <code>isinstance(result, Verdict)</code>, and a <code>Verdict</code> is only obtained by importing <code>PASS</code> or calling it, so a string, a truthy value, or a parsed config cannot forge it. A method that declares its evidence with <code>@evidence(...)</code> cannot pass without carrying it.</li> <li>Fail \u2014 the test method raised a <code>TestFailure</code>. Structured articulation of what the hardware did wrong, or (for a plain exception) a loud contract violation signal that the developer didn't model this failure mode.</li> <li>Aborted \u2014 no verdict was produced. Two causes:</li> <li>Harness-level: an external integration hook (<code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> on the run params) threw, halting the run before a verdict could be reached.</li> <li>Node-local: the test method returned anything other than the <code>PASS</code> sentinel \u2014 a <code>None</code> fall-through or an explicit wrong value (the string <code>\"PASS\"</code> included). No verdict exists, so the framework refuses to forge Fail (or Pass) on its behalf. Siblings are unaffected.</li> </ol> <p>Peer state to Pass/Fail, NOT a flavor of failure.</p> <p>The node-local Aborted cause is a deliberate deviation from the TypeScript original, which folded a missing verdict into the contract violation/Fail path. The port's position: a contract violation that threw produced information (Fail + violation signal); a method that returned wrong produced nothing, and reporting nothing as a verdict would itself be a quiet lie.</p> <p>The tech's recovery protocol differs for each. A Fail says \"check the hardware.\" An Aborted says \"check the harness \u2014 the shop-floor DB, the coordinator, the integration layer \u2014 or the test method that produced no verdict \u2014 not the unit.\" Collapsing these into one category is exactly what the framework refuses to do.</p> <p>A two-layer bug-guard exists alongside the three terminal states: - <code>ContractViolation</code> \u2014 developer contract violation, raised at the lifecycle layer - <code>FrameworkContractViolation</code> \u2014 framework internal contract violated (should be unreachable, indicates a bug in the framework itself)</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#failure-taxonomy","title":"Failure Taxonomy","text":"<p>Root causes the framework must make obvious: 1. DUT failed a check \u2014 <code>TestFailure</code> from a test method or from <code>configure()</code> \u2192 state <code>Fail</code>. A <code>TestFailure</code> from <code>configure()</code> means \"the device did not reach a testable state\"; whether hardware or infrastructure is the cause is not knowable from code \u2014 the human triager decides. 2. Hardware lying \u2014 survey artifacts expose mismatch (claimed success vs actual state) 3. Test method contract violation \u2014 plain exception from a test method or from <code>configure()</code> \u2192 violation signal 4. Integration failure \u2014 <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> from <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> aborts the run. The test may be fine; the harness around it broke (e.g. shopfloor DB unreachable, network congestion). 5. Operator interrupt \u2014 <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> from SIGINT/SIGTERM during a live run. The Floor Technician reads that the operator stopped the rack, not that the harness or the DUT failed.</p> <p>A human must always make the final determination. The framework presents evidence clearly enough that the tech can make the call without a software engineer's help.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-temporal-backbone","title":"The Temporal Backbone","text":"<p>The log artifact is the authoritative chronological record of a test run. Two complementary views: - Structural (inspector tree): what ran, what passed, what failed, how tests compose - Temporal (log backbone): what happened, in what order, what state at each moment</p> <p><code>await utils.capture_artifact(artifact)</code> stitches artifacts onto the timeline via structured pointer log entries. The public <code>Artifact</code> value has no file-writing method, so the framework cannot persist evidence without also recording that pointer.</p> <p>Every full run creates one timestamped directory beneath the configured artifact root. Logging nodes stream a human <code>timeline.log</code> and a versioned Inspector <code>timeline.jsonl</code>; leaves roll into their nearest owning ancestor. Both views are flushed entry by entry, preserving the accepted chronology through interruption. When the root reaches Pass, Fail, or Aborted, the framework atomically writes its versioned <code>RunResult</code> to <code>result.json</code> at that run-directory root. The root timeline's final entry points to the file, so the structural and temporal views remain joined.</p> <p>Timestamp integrity is a system requirement. NTP sync on the test host is not optional. The log backbone is used as a legal instrument in customer disputes (\"did failures start before your 2pm firmware push?\").</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#integration-hooks-the-external-world-contract","title":"Integration Hooks: The External-World Contract","text":"<p><code>before_each</code> and <code>after_each</code> on the run params are how HILT composes with the world outside the test framework \u2014 shop-floor databases, lab coordinators, audit pipelines, any system that needs a \"started\" signal before each test and a \"finished\" signal after.</p> <ul> <li>Registered once at the root <code>run()</code> call, fire around every executed test in the tree (fractal dispatch)</li> <li>Throw from either hook \u2192 run halts \u2192 enclosing node transitions to <code>Aborted</code></li> <li>Pair invariant: <code>after_each</code> fires even when <code>before_each</code> threw, so \"started\" and \"finished\" records stay balanced in the external system. No orphan rows.</li> <li>Hooks receive a narrow context (node name, ancestor chain, scoped logger, env snapshot) \u2014 NOT <code>utils.exec</code> or <code>get_shell</code>. The interface encodes \"integration hooks don't touch the hardware.\"</li> </ul> <p>When a hook throws, the required end state is for the framework to close every in-flight transport across the whole tree immediately (SSH connections destroyed, subprocesses killed). A 30-minute firmware flash in flight when the integration layer halts must NOT continue to mutate the hardware. This is load-bearing for manufacturing test integrity.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-pit-of-success-model","title":"The Pit of Success Model","text":"<p>The correct pattern must be the path of least resistance \u2014 enforced by API shape, not documentation.</p> <p>HILT is deliberately designed so that the right way to write a test is also the easiest way. The framework uses runtime enforcement, import-time structural checks, sensible defaults, and loud signals to guide developers toward honest, evidence-rich tests without requiring them to constantly consult the docs.</p> <p>Here are some of the ways HILT implements this:</p> <ul> <li>No pass by default \u2014 test methods must explicitly return <code>PASS</code>, or <code>PASS(evidence)</code> with the readings that prove it. Reaching the end of the method without doing so is a missing verdict (node-local Aborted); raising a plain exception is a developer contract violation. A method that declares its evidence with <code>@evidence(...)</code> cannot pass without carrying it. Even internal implementations like <code>TestSequence</code> explicitly model the passing condition as (all tests ran AND all tests passed) \u2014 it does not silently pass if nothing threw.</li> <li>Setup is constrained to <code>configure()</code> \u2014 All infrastructure wiring, readiness checks, and context creation must happen in <code>configure()</code>. The framework merges the return value into <code>utils.ctx</code> for the rest of the lifecycle and all descendants, and ctx is read-only from then on: inputs come in through configure, readings go out with the verdict. This prevents hidden state coupling and makes the data flow explicit.</li> <li>Unmodeled failure modes are loud \u2014 Raising anything other than a <code>TestFailure</code> (or subclass) from a test method produces a clear \"CONTRACT VIOLATION\" message. The framework refuses to let an unhandled error silently become a pass.</li> <li><code>use_shell()</code> auto-wires execution routing \u2014 Calling <code>utils.use_shell()</code> in <code>configure()</code> automatically rebinds <code>utils.exec</code> to route through the SSH connection. Child tests and descendants inherit the routing with zero extra code. The SE never has to manually thread SSH interfaces.</li> <li>Composition takes bound methods directly \u2014 <code>self.test(\"name\", self._my_method)</code> works with a plain bound method reference. No wrapping, no binding ceremony.</li> <li>Sensible defaults reduce boilerplate \u2014 <code>self.test(\"name\", self._my_method)</code> defaults to <code>discrete_log_file=False</code>, so its logs automatically roll up into the parent. This gives you one log per logical abstraction instead of one log per screw.</li> <li>Survey and audit hooks belong in the constructor \u2014 Registering them only in <code>__init__</code> makes them part of the test's stable identity (visible on the class and consistent across runs).</li> <li><code>wait_for()</code> requires intent \u2014 The <code>looking_for</code> parameter is mandatory and becomes the primary diagnostic string when a timeout occurs. This nudges developers to write human-readable failure messages.</li> <li>Import-time structural enforcement \u2014 a tier the TypeScript original never had. <code>__init_subclass__</code> on <code>TestComponent</code> verifies at class definition (import time, before any hardware is touched) that the subclass is structurally sound: required lifecycle methods present and async, sealed internals (<code>main</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>_run_lifecycle</code>, the <code>_safe_*</code> family) not overridden. A malformed test refuses to import; it can never reach the floor.</li> </ul> <p>These constraints are not restrictions \u2014 they are guardrails that make it difficult to write ambiguous or incomplete tests while making the correct patterns feel natural.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#why-hilt-rejects-pass-by-default","title":"Why HILT Rejects \"Pass by Default\"","text":"<p>Most test frameworks treat \"the test reached the end without throwing an error\" as a passing result. This is the default mental model the industry has used for decades.</p> <p>HILT rejects it outright.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#the-quiet-lie","title":"The Quiet Lie","text":"<p>When a test passes by default, it has only proven one thing:</p> <p>It did not encounter any of the failure modes the author happened to think of and write explicit checks for.</p> <p>That is not evidence that the system works. It is a quiet lie \u2014 one that accumulates silently until it causes a line stoppage. HILT provides the guardrails that make it difficult to accidentally ship a test that only appears complete. The same conviction governs what a pass carries: a pass without its declared evidence is not a pass.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#cases-where-explicit-pass-modeling-feels-awkward","title":"Cases Where Explicit Pass Modeling Feels Awkward","text":"<p>Here are the situations where forcing an explicit <code>PASS</code> can feel clunky if you are coming from a pass-by-default mentality. In every case below, the job is the same: make the pass condition a readable check the next developer can find and understand. <code>return PASS</code> on its own is syntax, not modeling \u2014 the conditional that gates it is where your intent lives, and where <code>hilt-check</code> (the static-analysis tier) will insist it goes.</p> Situation Why It Feels Awkward How to Model the Pass Condition Long-running / soak tests Success = \"it didn't crash for 8 hours\" Track error count or health samples across the run. <code>if errors == 0 and samples_collected &gt;= expected: return PASS</code> \u2014 the conditional names what \"healthy for 8 hours\" actually means. Event-driven / callback tests Success depends on receiving a callback within time <code>if await wait_for(lambda: callback_fired, looking_for=...): return PASS</code> \u2014 the wait returning truthy IS the success condition, gated explicitly. Performance / load tests Success = \"p99 latency &lt; 50ms and throughput &gt; 10k req/s\" Collect metrics, then: <code>if metrics.p99 &lt; 50 and metrics.throughput &gt; 10_000: return PASS</code> \u2014 both thresholds named in code, not just in your head. Fuzzing / property-based tests Success is probabilistic (\"no counterexample found in 10,000 runs\") <code>if iterations_completed == 10_000 and not counterexamples: return PASS</code> \u2014 both halves matter: you ran the full budget AND found nothing. Best-effort / smoke tests \"It's okay if some optional checks fail\" Define the threshold as a variable then <code>if critical_checks_passed: return PASS</code>. The next reader sees the threshold immediately. Non-deterministic external systems Success depends on flaky third-party behavior After retries: <code>if last_attempt_succeeded: return PASS</code>. The retry loop models the flake tolerance; the final conditional models the verdict."},{"location":"overview.html#why-this-matters-to-floor-technicians","title":"Why This Matters to Floor Technicians","text":"<p>Floor Technicians are the primary consumers of everything you produce. They are the ones who will open the Inspector at 2am when a station is down. They need to answer three questions in under 60 seconds:</p> <ol> <li>What actually happened?</li> <li>Is this a hardware problem, a test problem, or an infrastructure problem?</li> <li>What should I do next?</li> </ol> <p>Pass-by-default tests make all three questions harder. The tech is left guessing at your intent, debugging ambiguous results, and sometimes having to escalate because the evidence is unclear.</p> <p>HILT is built so that the evidence a tech needs is produced automatically and presented clearly \u2014 without requiring them to read your source code or understand your design intent.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#what-hilt-demands","title":"What HILT Demands","text":""},{"location":"overview.html#the-three-tier-enforcement-stack","title":"The three-tier enforcement stack","text":"<p>HILT does not merely prefer explicit passes \u2014 it rejects implicit ones at every tier of the enforcement stack:</p> Tier Mechanism Enforces 1 Runtime IoC wrapping (the child-boundary in <code>lifecycle.py</code>) Verdict contract, failure taxonomy, lifecycle order \u2014 can never be bypassed 2 Import-time structural checks (<code>__init_subclass__</code>) Subclass shape, sealed internals \u2014 fires before hardware is touched 3 Static analysis (<code>hilt-check</code>) Declarative syntax: pure <code>build_suite</code>, gated <code>PASS</code>, immutable-self idiom <p>Rule: nothing that protects hardware or verdict integrity may live only in tier 3.</p> <ul> <li>In the runtime contract: test methods must return the <code>PASS</code> sentinel explicitly. Reaching the end without doing so produces no verdict \u2014 the node renders Aborted, never Pass.</li> <li>In internal implementations: even when all conditions are met (as in <code>TestSequence</code>), the framework still performs an explicit check and refuses to silently pass.</li> <li>At the static-analysis level: returning <code>PASS</code> outside of an <code>if</code> condition is surfaced as a lint error \u2014 the framework wants you to model your success condition deliberately.</li> </ul> <p>This is more work up front. It forces you to think about what \"success\" actually looks like with the same rigor you apply to failure modes.</p> <p>In return, you get tests that mean what they say, clear failure categorization, and automatic structured evidence that a floor technician can trust.</p>"},{"location":"overview.html#where-things-live","title":"Where things live","text":"<p>The concepts above are carried by the modules under <code>src/hilt</code>. Each line says what the module is for, not what it contains; the contracts themselves are stated once, in contracts.md and the pages linked from it.</p> Module What it is for <code>__init__.py</code> The public surface: <code>hilt.__all__</code> is the documented API (api.md); every other name in the package is internal. <code>base.py</code> The atom: <code>TestComponent</code>, the one node every suite is composed from, and the framework-owned lifecycle that runs it. <code>lifecycle.py</code> Tier 1, the kernel of trust: the child boundary that turns what a test method returns into one of the three terminal states. <code>verdict.py</code> The non-forgeable <code>PASS</code> sentinel and the <code>Verdict</code> a test method hands back. <code>errors.py</code> The failure taxonomy: which exception means Fail, which means Aborted, and who is being told. <code>utils.py</code> The per-node surface authored code is handed: exec, ctx, env, logging, measurements, hook registration. <code>evidence.py</code> <code>@evidence</code>: the readings a test method promises to carry with its <code>PASS</code>. <code>measurement.py</code> Parametric measurements: a number with a unit and limits, recorded beside the verdict (measurements.md). <code>run_data.py</code> The JSON-safe view of ctx and verdict evidence that reaches the run record. <code>results.py</code> The immutable evidence of a run and its JSON boundary to the Inspector (inspector-json.md). <code>timeline.py</code> The temporal backbone: the durable per-node records that are a run's authoritative chronology. <code>artifacts.py</code> Point-in-time captures and their framework-owned persistence. <code>hooks.py</code> The integration hooks (surveys, audits, <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code>) and the deliberately narrow contexts they see. <code>executor.py</code> The host-side execution choke point every blocking call passes through. <code>routing.py</code> Topology-aware exec routing: which hop a node's <code>utils.exec</code> travels through. <code>remote.py</code> The remote transport contract the concrete transports implement. <code>ssh.py</code> The SSH transport. <code>mock.py</code> The in-process transport for self-tests: no hardware, no SSH. <code>arp.py</code> MAC-to-IP resolution over the node's current exec route. <code>device_api.py</code> The Device API (CoAP/HTTP) contract: resource requests, not an exec subclass (hil/device-api.md). <code>junit.py</code> The JUnit XML reporter over the run record, for CI systems that read that format (junit-xml.md). <code>cli.py</code> The <code>hilt</code> command line: <code>run</code>, <code>new</code>, <code>inspect</code> (hilt-run.md, hilt-new.md, inspector.md). <code>scaffold.py</code> Writes a gated-<code>PASS</code> suite and its self-test from a class name, so a new suite starts inside the pit of success. <code>inspector_*.py</code> (ten modules) The Floor Technician's Inspector, split reader / model / view. Readers (<code>inspector_json</code>, <code>inspector_run</code>, <code>inspector_timeline</code>, <code>inspector_artifacts</code>) load saved evidence and execute nothing; models (<code>inspector_tree</code>, <code>inspector_selection</code>, <code>inspector_artifact_navigation</code>) turn it into rows and cursors; views (<code>inspector_timeline_view</code>, <code>inspector_artifact_view</code>, <code>inspector_app</code>) render and forward keys, deciding nothing."},{"location":"utilities.html","title":"Utilities","text":""},{"location":"utilities.html#logging-and-the-timeline","title":"Logging and the timeline","text":"<ul> <li><code>utils.logger</code> is scoped per node. Every entry is captured in artifacts.</li> <li><code>discrete_log_file=True</code> (the default on class-based tests) gives the node its own log file; <code>False</code> rolls entries into the parent logger. Inline <code>self.test()</code> bodies default to <code>False</code>.</li> <li>Every full run creates a timestamped directory under the configured artifact root. A logging owner streams both <code>timeline.log</code> for humans and versioned <code>timeline.jsonl</code> for the Inspector, flushing both on every entry. Treat <code>TestComponent.log_path</code> as the authoritative path; directory names are not an API.</li> <li>While a durable timeline is attached, <code>utils.logger.isEnabledFor(...)</code> is true at every level so developer-side guards cannot suppress the chronology. Process logging handlers still apply their own level policy.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"utilities.html#point-in-time-artifacts","title":"Point-in-time artifacts","text":"<p>Construct an immutable <code>Artifact</code> and pass it to <code>utils.capture_artifact()</code>. The method persists the JSON artifact under <code>HILT_ARTIFACT_ROOT</code>, then emits a structured pointer through the same scoped logger that records the surrounding test events. <code>Artifact</code> deliberately has no <code>write()</code> method: a file without its pointer would be absent from the chronology.</p> <pre><code>from hilt import Artifact\n\nsnapshot = Artifact(\n    name=\"pre-flash\", type=\"hardware-survey\", data={\"firmware\": \"2.4.1\", \"healthy\": True}\n)\nartifact_path = await utils.capture_artifact(snapshot)\n</code></pre> <p>Names and types accept letters, numbers, dashes, and underscores. Payloads must be JSON serializable. Capture is async, never overwrites, and returns the absolute path recorded in the pointer entry.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#runresult-navigation","title":"RunResult navigation","text":"<p>Every <code>RunResult</code> carries the node's position in the tree:</p> <ul> <li><code>ancestor_names: list[str]</code>, root-to-this inclusive. Use for programmatic aggregation (DB keys, leaf identity).</li> <li><code>bread_crumbs: str</code>, <code>\"/\".join(ancestor_names)</code> precomputed. Use for display, log lines, error messages.</li> <li><code>summary_tree: SummaryTreeNode</code>, a snapshot of this node's subtree at completion, so an integrator can reconstruct the full Inspector tree from the root's <code>RunResult</code> alone.</li> </ul> <p>The versioned payload is defined in <code>inspector-json.md</code>. A full <code>run()</code> also persists the root payload as <code>result.json</code> in the run's artifact directory. Suites consume the returned <code>RunResult</code> when they need the live value; they never write the canonical Inspector file themselves.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#arpresolver-mac-to-ip","title":"ArpResolver: MAC to IP","text":"<p>Create one via <code>utils.get_arp_resolver()</code> in <code>configure()</code>, call <code>resolve()</code>, and return both the resolved data and the resolver into ctx. Test methods read the IP and the resolver from <code>utils.ctx</code>. <code>arp.ping(utils)</code> checks reachability. <code>arp.reacquire(utils)</code> after anything that may change the IP (reboot, flash, DHCP churn) returns the new address and the resolver remembers it, so a later <code>ping</code> dials the address it last resolved while ctx stays as <code>configure()</code> left it. Carry the new address as evidence on the verdict if the Floor Technician should see it.</p> <p><code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> is not a <code>TestFailure</code>. Catch it in <code>configure</code> and re-raise as <code>TestFailure</code> so a missing ARP entry is a verdict. Uncaught, it is a developer bug: the suite did not model the failure mode.</p> <pre><code>arp = utils.get_arp_resolver(mac=self._mac)\ntry:\n    resolved = await arp.resolve()\nexcept ArpResolutionFailure as error:\n    raise TestFailure(\n        f\"{error.mac} did not appear on the network\", error.mac, error.host\n    ) from error\nreturn {**resolved, \"arp\": arp}\n</code></pre> <p>The resolver is scoped to the node's current exec route and its cache is keyed by the routing label, so a device composed under a gateway resolves through the hop with no change to the device's code. <code>hilt run ...::DutDevice</code> reads the test host's ARP table; <code>hilt run ...::Gateway</code> runs the same class through the hop. See <code>examples/networked_device.py</code>.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#ssh-use_shell-and-get_shell","title":"SSH: <code>use_shell</code> and <code>get_shell</code>","text":"<p><code>use_shell</code> answers \"where does <code>utils.exec</code> go\": it rebinds the routing for this node and its descendants. <code>get_shell</code> hands you an additional interface without touching the routing. Both are framework-managed; no <code>cleanup()</code> override is needed, and the shell is disconnected after the post-cleanup survey.</p> <p>Open the device's shell in <code>configure()</code> with <code>use_shell</code> when one connection must be shared across all of the device's leaves. See <code>examples/remote_device_ssh.py</code>. The pi-hole dogfood suite (<code>examples/pi_hole_server.py</code>) is the same class-owned hop plus phase-boundary surveys that tail service logs for the Floor Technician.</p> <p>Each <code>utils.exec</code> over the hop opens its own SSH session, and an sshd grants a limited number per connection (<code>MaxSessions</code>, 10 by default). A suite that fans out wider than that under one hop should pass <code>max_sessions</code> at or below the device's limit: <code>await utils.use_shell(host=..., username=..., max_sessions=8)</code>. Commands past the bound wait their turn instead of being refused with <code>open failed</code>.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#tunneling","title":"Tunneling","text":"<p>Pass the <code>Shell</code> an earlier <code>get_shell()</code> returned as the <code>via</code> argument to the next one to tunnel the new connection through it.</p> <pre><code>jump = await utils.get_shell(host=jump_host, username=jump_user)\ntarget = await utils.get_shell(host=target_host, username=target_user, via=jump)\n</code></pre> <p>See <code>examples/ssh_tunneling.py</code>.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#serial-console-use_serial-and-get_serial","title":"Serial console: <code>use_serial</code> and <code>get_serial</code>","text":"<p><code>use_serial(device=...)</code> is <code>use_shell(host=...)</code> for a console reached by its device path: it opens the port, binds <code>utils.exec</code> to it for this node and its descendants, and the framework closes the port after the post-cleanup survey and from the abort sweep. <code>get_serial(device=...)</code> hands you an additional console without touching the routing. Both return a <code>Shell</code>; <code>use_serial()</code> with no arguments reads the active route from any slot, and the declaring form is admitted only in <code>configure()</code>, under the same <code>ContractViolation</code> as <code>use_shell</code>. No <code>cleanup()</code> override is needed and no transport is imported.</p> <pre><code>async def configure(self, utils: TestUtils) -&gt; dict[str, Any]:\n    await utils.use_serial(\n        device=utils.env.require(\"SERIAL_DEVICE\"),\n        baud=utils.env.int(\"SERIAL_BAUD\", default=115200),\n    )\n    return {}\n</code></pre> <p>The line settings are the transport's: <code>prompt</code> (a regex; <code>None</code> is a bare POSIX prompt), <code>login_prompt</code> / <code>password_prompt</code> with <code>username</code> / <code>password</code> for a console that logs in, <code>login_timeout</code> for connect, <code>prompt_timeout</code> for how long one <code>exec</code> waits for the prompt before settling <code>exit_code == -1</code> with the text so far as evidence. A console's <code>stderr</code> is transport-generated only (<code>\"prompt timeout\"</code>, <code>\"transport closed\"</code>); everything the DUT writes lands in <code>stdout</code>. The contract is <code>docs/hil/serial.md</code>.</p> <p>A console behind a hop (a console server, a Pi with a USB-UART) is the same call: pass an SSH <code>Shell</code> as <code>via</code>, or declare <code>use_shell(host=...)</code> on the parent and let the console inherit the route. The device path then names a node on the hop box and the bytes bridge through it (<code>bridge_command</code> overrides the default <code>stty</code> + <code>cat</code> bridge). A serial console accepts no other kind of hop; passing one is a <code>TypeError</code> naming the call. The policy-only shapes (<code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) belong to <code>use_shell</code>. <code>pyserial-asyncio</code> is the <code>open-hilt[serial]</code> extra; a local open without it raises <code>ModuleNotFoundError</code> naming the install.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#device-api-coap-and-http","title":"Device API: CoAP and HTTP","text":"<p>A device API is not <code>exec</code> with a URL. Open a session from <code>configure()</code> with <code>utils.get_http</code> or <code>utils.get_coap</code>, then <code>request</code> a typed <code>DeviceRequest</code>. The response's <code>status</code> is data; HTTP 4xx/5xx is not an exception. Catch <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> when the transfer itself did not complete (timeout, refused connection) and model it as a <code>TestFailure</code>.</p> <p>Retries default to zero and each declared attempt is logged. POST refuses retry unless <code>retry_non_idempotent=True</code>. Attempt logs keep header names and withhold every value unless the author lists that name in <code>evidence_headers</code>. Abort closes the session; it does not invent a shutdown PUT.</p> <pre><code>api = await utils.get_http(base_url=\"https://dut.local\")\nresponse = await api.request(DeviceRequest(method=\"GET\", path=\"/whoami\"))\nif response.status != 200:\n    raise TestFailure(\"whoami rejected\", 200, response.status)\nreturn PASS({\"status\": response.status})\n</code></pre> <p>The backends stay off the public surface, the way <code>SshInterface</code> does.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#wait_for","title":"<code>wait_for</code>","text":"<p><code>looking_for</code> is required and must be plain language: it is the Floor Technician's primary diagnostic string when a wait times out. Both placements raise <code>TestFailure</code>; where the call sits decides what the timeout means, and that is a lifecycle question answered on the idioms page. The call returns the predicate's truthy value (<code>True</code> stays <code>True</code>), so a claim that needs the observation returns it from the predicate instead of writing into a closure. The runnable <code>examples/wait_for_patterns.py</code> shows both placements and how polling observations flow through scoped logs, surveys, artifacts, <code>run_data</code>, and the root <code>summary_tree</code> without another evidence API.</p>"},{"location":"utilities.html#utilsexec","title":"<code>utils.exec</code>","text":"<p>Never throws. Returns <code>stdout</code>, <code>stderr</code>, and <code>exit_code</code>; output is bounded and truncation is recorded as evidence. Where it lands is the parent's decision (idioms, root 4).</p>"},{"location":"hil/index.html","title":"HIL interface design notes","text":"<p>Phase 9 gives each hardware-in-the-loop interface its own contract, written to the same diligence as <code>docs/contracts.md</code>, and signed by Ty before any implementation begins. This page states the three standing rulings that govern every note below once, so each note can cite them instead of repeating them.</p>"},{"location":"hil/index.html#the-three-rulings","title":"The three rulings","text":"<ol> <li>New contracts, not <code>exec</code> subclasses. <code>exec</code> is a command contract: a string    goes in, an <code>ExecResult</code> comes out, and a non-zero exit is data for the test    method to judge. A GPIO pin level, a PWM duty cycle, a CAN frame, an I2C/SPI    transaction, and a CoAP/HTTP resource are not commands \u2014 none of them fit the    shape of \"run this string and collect stdout/stderr/exit_code.\" Coercing one    into <code>exec</code> would either lose information (a frame is not text) or invite the    test author to shell out to a protocol library instead of calling a typed    method. Each interface below gets its own contract, in the same shape as    <code>docs/contracts.md</code>: a named contract, the framework's obligation, the    developer's obligation, and the consequence of getting it wrong.</li> <li>The framework owns lifecycle and hooks, unforked. Connected-flag    ownership, the idempotent <code>cancel_in_flight</code> halt, <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code>    dispatch and the pair invariant, <code>use_survey</code>/<code>use_audit</code> asymmetry (survey    swallows, audit does not), and the In-Flight Termination Contract belong to    <code>src/hilt/lifecycle.py</code>, <code>src/hilt/hooks.py</code>, and the transport base in    <code>src/hilt/remote.py</code>. A new interface contract governs transfer, error    taxonomy, timing, and safe-state for its own protocol \u2014 it does not reopen how    the framework sequences <code>configure</code> \u2192 <code>build_suite</code> \u2192 <code>cleanup</code>, how a hook    pair fires, or how a hard abort reaches a resource. Hardware whose behavior    cannot be expressed under those existing contracts is a finding to bring back    to Ty, not a license to grow a parallel lifecycle for one interface.</li> <li>Ty signs the design note; Zavala signs the implementation. Each note below    is <code>DRAFT</code> until Ty records his signature on its issue. No implementation MR    may open, and no reference implementation (#98) may build against a shape,    until the note it depends on carries that signature. An implementation may    not outrun its approved note.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"hil/index.html#the-notes","title":"The notes","text":"Interface Note Issue Status GPIO gpio.md #93 SIGNED 2026-09-07 PWM pwm.md #94 SIGNED 2026-09-08 CAN bus can.md #95 SIGNED 2026-09-09 I2C / SPI i2c-spi.md #96 SIGNED 2026-09-09 Device API (CoAP/HTTP) device-api.md #97 SIGNED 2026-09-09 Serial (UART) serial.md #91 SIGNED 2026-09-10 <p>Every note this page introduces is now signed and on <code>main</code>. Each landed as an independent MR carrying its note and the required <code>mkdocs.yml</code> navigation entry, so the docs checks passed standalone on that branch, following the scope ruling on #94. A filename here becomes a link only after the file exists on <code>main</code>, so a note still in flight would otherwise carry a broken link on every branch but the one that adds the target.</p> <p>Serial/UART is Cayde's vertical (ruled by Ty as both a local peer of SSH and the same transport reached through a <code>via</code> hop). It is listed here for navigation only \u2014 its note lives at <code>docs/hil/serial.md</code> and is not one of the five notes this page introduces.</p>"},{"location":"hil/index.html#what-every-note-holds-in-common","title":"What every note holds in common","text":"<ul> <li>A <code>Status: DRAFT \u2014 awaiting Ty's signature on #N</code> header. The note is a   proposal until that line changes.</li> <li>Provisional interface and type names, clearly marked as provisional. Naming is   not the thing being signed off; the obligations are. A name may still change   between sign-off and implementation without reopening the ruling, provided the   obligations it names do not move.</li> <li>A statement that lifecycle and hook ownership are unchanged, made concrete for   that hardware \u2014 not a restatement of ruling 2 in the abstract, but what it   means for this protocol's bring-up, teardown, and abort path.</li> <li>A failure-vocabulary mapping onto the taxonomy already in <code>docs/contracts.md</code>   and <code>src/hilt/errors.py</code> (<code>TestFailure</code>, <code>ContractViolation</code>,   <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code>, <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>, <code>MissingVerdict</code>). No note   invents a new terminal state or a new exception root; Aborted and Fail keep   their existing meanings.</li> <li>A description of how a mock backend and a real backend both satisfy the same   contract \u2014 the pattern <code>RemoteInterface</code>/<code>MockTransport</code>/<code>SshInterface</code>   already establishes for <code>exec</code>, carried over to the new protocol.</li> <li>A statement of how evidence is recorded, tied to the Evidence Contract   (<code>PASS(evidence)</code>, <code>@evidence</code>) and, where the interface produces a measurable   quantity, a reference to the parametric-measurements protocol under design in   issue #85 rather than a restatement of it.</li> <li>A description of what the Floor Technician sees in the timeline when the   framework takes a safe-state action on that interface.</li> </ul> <p>None of these notes changes <code>hilt.__all__</code>. Every name in them is provisional until an implementation issue is raised and signed off in its own right, per <code>docs/api-stability.md</code>.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html","title":"Design note: CAN bus interface contract","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty approved this note in !128 on 2026-09-09; recorded on #95</p> <p>A CAN frame is not a command string. This note states the transfer, filter, bus-off, timing, and silent-bus obligations that make a controller fit HILT's existing lifecycle. The three HIL rulings govern it. Ty's approval of the actual note signs the obligations below. Implementation requires its own approved issue and Zavala's review under API stability.</p> <p>All names below (<code>CanInterface</code>, <code>CanFrame</code>, <code>CanFilter</code>, <code>CanBusOff</code>, <code>CanOperationFailure</code>, <code>MockCan</code>, and the method names) are provisional. This note adds no public Python name and changes no stored record. The legacy framework and language server supply no CAN behavior to carry forward; the migration context is the preserved lifecycle and evidence contracts, with a new hardware contract for Ty to judge.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#why-not-an-exec-subclass","title":"Why not an <code>exec</code> subclass","text":"<p>A CAN frame has an arbitration identifier, an extended-id flag, a remote flag, and a payload of bytes. None of that is a shell command. Forcing frames through <code>exec</code> would mean either shelling out to <code>cansend</code>/<code>candump</code> (lossy, racy, and blind to bus-off) or smuggling a controller inside a <code>RemoteInterface</code> subclass that answers <code>_exec</code> with a fake dialect \u2014 exactly the parallel-lifecycle-in-disguise ruling 1 forbids. A frame that has been stringified has already lost the identifier width, the ACK, and the error counters. <code>CanInterface</code> is its own ABC, structurally parallel to <code>RemoteInterface</code> (<code>src/hilt/remote.py</code>) and to the signed <code>GpioInterface</code>: it owns <code>connect</code> / <code>disconnect</code> / <code>cancel_in_flight</code> in the same shape, and adds frame-shaped operations in place of <code>exec</code>.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#the-can-frame-transfer-contract","title":"The CAN Frame Transfer Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Present CAN as its own typed interface under HIL ruling 1. <code>CanInterface</code> is not an <code>exec</code> subclass and does not change where <code>utils.exec</code> routes. A handle is bound to one controller, one bitrate (or bitrate pair, for a backend that also speaks CAN FD), and a declared payload ceiling before it can send or receive.</p> <ul> <li>A provisional <code>CanFrame</code> carries <code>arbitration_id</code> (non-negative int),   <code>extended</code> (11-bit vs 29-bit), <code>rtr</code> (remote transmission request), and   <code>data</code> (<code>bytes</code>). Classic CAN admits 0\u20138 data bytes. A CAN-FD backend   declares a larger ceiling (up to 64) and a bitrate switch; it does not   earn a second interface or a second lifecycle. Reject a negative id, an   id that does not fit the declared width, a payload longer than the   ceiling, a non-<code>bytes</code> payload, and a remote frame that also carries   data, at the call site, before the controller is touched.</li> <li><code>send(frame)</code> places one data or remote frame on the wire and waits for   the on-bus ACK bit. That ACK is evidence that a receiver on the bus   acknowledged the frame. It is not evidence that the DUT parsed it, acted   on it, or is even the node that ACKed. A send that the controller cannot   represent is refused; a send the bus does not ACK within the backend's   declared transmit ceiling is an operational failure, not a forged   success. The interface does not retry.</li> <li><code>receive(deadline=...)</code> returns the next frame that passes this handle's   declared filters, or fails if none arrives before the deadline. The   deadline is positive and finite seconds; booleans and non-finite values   are refused. There is no GPIO-style stability window: a CAN frame is   atomic. A timeout does not invent a frame, does not return empty <code>data</code>   with a sentinel id, and does not raise <code>TestFailure</code> \u2014 see the failure   vocabulary. Software matching on top of hardware filters is allowed;   dropping a frame the hardware already accepted is not a protocol error.</li> <li>One in-flight send and one in-flight receive per handle. A second <code>send</code>   or <code>receive</code> is refused while one is active; it is not queued to surprise   the next lifecycle phase. Framework termination takes precedence over   this admission rule.</li> <li>Each operation records the requested frame (id, flags, payload length;   payload bytes when they are JSON-safe and within evidence bounds) and   whether the controller confirmed the outcome. An unconfirmed send never   becomes the last-successful frame in a cache the next test method reads   as truth.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare the controller, bitrate, payload ceiling, and filters in <code>configure()</code>. Obtain the framework-managed handle through a provisional <code>utils.get_can(...)</code>; return the handle and static inputs through configure's ctx. Keep <code>self</code> immutable, <code>build_suite()</code> pure, and later ctx reads read-only. Judge the DUT with frames actually received (or with an independent observation), never by treating a successful <code>send</code> ACK as a passing device.</p> <p>Consequence: The Floor Technician can tell the frame the suite asked to put on the wire, whether the bus ACKed it, and what the DUT sent back. A missing ACK cannot quietly become a delivered command in a record that claims otherwise.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#the-can-filter-contract","title":"The CAN Filter Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Acceptance filters are part of the handle's identity, declared before the first <code>receive</code>, not discovered by dumping the bus into the test method.</p> <ul> <li>A provisional <code>CanFilter</code> names an arbitration-id base, a mask, and   whether it matches standard, extended, or both. Hardware filters are   programmed at bring-up. A backend that cannot install the declared set   refuses the handle before any traffic is observed, naming the filters it   cannot honour.</li> <li>Filters are immutable for the handle's lifetime. Changing the mask   mid-run is the CAN instance of flipping a GPIO pin's direction live:   <code>TypeError</code> at the call site. A suite that needs a different window opens   a different declared handle.</li> <li>An open handle whose filters would accept every frame on a production   bus is legal but must be explicit \u2014 a wildcard filter is a declaration,   not a default. The default with no filters declared is \"receive   nothing,\" so an author who forgot to say what they are listening for   does not silently subscribe to the whole network.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: State the filters that belong to this node. Do not compensate for a missing filter by busy-looping <code>receive</code> and discarding ids in suite code; that turns the test method into a sniffer and hides the contract from the Floor Technician.</p> <p>Consequence: The record shows which ids this node was allowed to see. A pass that depended on a frame the handle was not declared to accept is not reconstructable from the artifacts.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#the-can-bus-off-and-recovery-contract","title":"The CAN Bus-Off and Recovery Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Bus-off is a controller state, not a DUT verdict. When the transmit error counter crosses the bus-off threshold the controller stops driving the bus. That event is reported as an operational failure (<code>CanBusOff</code>, a <code>HiltError</code>) naming the handle and the last confirmed error counters if the backend can read them.</p> <ul> <li>Automatic recovery that resumes transmitting during the same run is   refused. A controller whose driver auto-recovers in the background would   put frames on the wire after the suite, or the abort path, has stopped   sending \u2014 the CAN instance of a detached PWM ramp. Bring-up may enable   a backend's recovery into silent/stopped, never into active TX, unless   the suite calls a provisional <code>recover()</code> deliberately.</li> <li><code>recover()</code> is a new operation, not an implicit retry of the failed   <code>send</code>. It is refused while a send or receive is in flight, refused after   <code>cancel_in_flight</code> has started, and records whether the controller   re-entered the declared active state. Failure to recover is the same   operational class, not a <code>TestFailure</code>.</li> <li>Error-passive and warning thresholds, when the backend exposes them, are   logged as evidence of controller health. They do not change control   flow. Only bus-off (unable to transmit) is a failure of <code>send</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Catch <code>CanBusOff</code> and decide what it means. A DUT that storms the bus, a terminator that fell off, and a second tester fighting this node all produce the same controller state. The framework must not choose among those. Do not treat recover-and-retry inside the test method as a substitute for naming the failure.</p> <p>Consequence: Bus-off cannot masquerade as a green run, and it cannot masquerade as a DUT Fail the author never wrote. The Floor Technician sees a controller that stopped, not a verdict someone else invented.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#the-can-safe-state-and-timing-contract","title":"The CAN Safe-State and Timing Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: The safe state is silence \u2014 no further frames originating from this handle \u2014 enforced at the resource by the existing termination sweep. This specializes the signed GPIO ownership and cut rules and the signed PWM disabled-output termination. It introduces no CAN halt signal, event loop, lifecycle runner, or integration hook, and it does not invent a shutdown frame.</p> <ul> <li>Register ownership as soon as acquisition can put a frame on the wire, so   partial <code>configure()</code> failure cannot orphan a transmitter. Bring-up   establishes and confirms the declared silent/stopped state before   admitting the first <code>send</code>.</li> <li>An integration-hook failure or operator interrupt silences every open   CAN handle. A node deadline silences only resources opened by the   bounded subtree, under both <code>fail_after</code> (Fail) and <code>abort_after</code>   (node-local Aborted). An inherited controller or borrowed hop remains   its owner's resource; unrelated siblings are untouched.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight()</code> is idempotent. It aborts a pending send or receive   at the controller, attempts to enter the declared silent state under the   backend's latency ceiling, and records whether silence was confirmed. A   repeated call cannot resume TX or replay the last frame. A closed handle   remains closed; there is no automatic reconnect.</li> <li>The framework does not transmit a \"safe\" or \"shutdown\" frame on abort.   A DUT that needs a specific last frame gets it from authored   <code>cleanup()</code>, which the hard cut does not wait for. Inventing a frame at   abort would be a stimulus the suite never declared, written into a   record the technician might trust.</li> <li>Transmit ACK ceiling, receive deadline, and bring-up/silence ceiling are   each positive finite backend-declared bounds, separate from the node's   <code>fail_after</code> / <code>abort_after</code>. An operation timeout names that operation   and its ceiling. It does not manufacture the <code>timeout</code> evidence reserved   for the winning node clock in the   Time Budget Contract. A longer   receive wait cannot extend the node clock.</li> <li>A backend whose shared adapter would let one node's silence or filter   change starve another owner's handle must refuse the conflicting   acquisition before either owner sends. It cannot sacrifice Group   Isolation to offer more nodes on one stick.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Select a fixture for which \"this node stops transmitting\" is the intended safe state. Declare shared adapters honestly. Keep any required shutdown frame in <code>cleanup()</code>; rely on framework ownership for termination. Never manually sequence another node's configure or cleanup.</p> <p>Consequence: Ending the test stops this node's frames and records what could be confirmed. A technician is never asked to infer bus safety from a disconnected handle, from Pass/Fail/Aborted alone, or from a shutdown frame nobody authored.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#lifecycle-and-hook-ownership-unchanged","title":"Lifecycle and hook ownership: unchanged","text":"<p>The HIL lifecycle ruling applies unchanged. Concretely:</p> <ul> <li>Bring-up happens the same way <code>use_shell</code> brings up SSH: a <code>configure()</code>   call obtains a <code>CanInterface</code> handle (provisionally <code>utils.get_can(...)</code>,   mirroring <code>get_shell</code> / <code>get_gpio</code> / <code>get_pwm</code>), and the framework's   existing bookkeeping tracks what this node opened versus what it   inherited.</li> <li>Teardown order is the framework's: <code>cancel_in_flight</code> on every opened   <code>CanInterface</code> fires as part of the same In-Flight Termination Contract   sweep that already closes <code>RemoteInterface</code> transports and silences   signed GPIO/PWM resources when <code>RunAbort</code> fires.</li> <li><code>use_survey</code> / <code>use_audit</code> remain the only hooks a suite registers to   observe the bus; a periodic audit that receives a watchdog frame and   raises <code>TestFailure</code> on an unexpected payload is the existing audit   contract, not a new hook kind.</li> <li><code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> remain the integrator's narrow context and   still cannot touch hardware \u2014 a CAN controller is exactly the kind of   thing those hooks are barred from reaching.</li> </ul> <p>Hardware that needs the controller to run its own concurrent RX loop outside survey/audit, or that cannot honor a hard <code>cancel_in_flight</code>, is a finding for Ty \u2014 not a reason to give CAN its own lifecycle.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#failure-vocabulary","title":"Failure vocabulary","text":"<p>No new terminal state is introduced, and no new exception root. <code>CanOperationFailure</code> and <code>CanBusOff</code> derive from <code>HiltError</code>, not <code>TestFailure</code>, for the same reason GPIO's <code>PinSettleFailure</code> and PWM's <code>PwmOperationFailure</code> do: failure to put a frame on the wire, or a controller that has gone bus-off, does not identify the DUT as the cause.</p> Situation Raised as Terminal state Invalid frame, filter, deadline, or conflicting in-flight use <code>TypeError</code> / <code>ValueError</code> at the call site <code>ContractViolation</code> (Fail, <code>bug: true</code>) if it escapes Bus does not ACK a send within the transmit ceiling; receive deadline expires with no matching frame; silence cannot be confirmed at bring-up <code>CanOperationFailure</code> (provisional <code>HiltError</code>) naming the handle, operation, requested frame or filter, and ceiling Fail when the author catches it and states the verdict; a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (<code>bug: true</code>) when it escapes Controller enters bus-off <code>CanBusOff</code> (provisional <code>HiltError</code>) As above. Never an automatic DUT Fail Backend cannot reach the adapter at bring-up <code>ConnectionError</code> from the interface's own <code>connect</code> Absorbed the way <code>RemoteInterface</code> connect failures are absorbed today Silence cannot be confirmed during the safe-state sweep No exception; the run is already ending Terminal state unchanged; timeline records the unconfirmed silence, naming the handle. Never a success line Integration hook throws while frames are in flight Framework raises <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> Aborted; run-level halt silences every open <code>CanInterface</code> Operator SIGINT/SIGTERM mid-run Framework raises <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> Aborted; same run-level sweep A node's time budget expires while it holds a handle Framework settles the node per its declared budget <code>fail_after</code> \u2192 Fail, <code>abort_after</code> \u2192 node-local Aborted; either way only that subtree's handles are silenced Test returns without a verdict Existing <code>MissingVerdict</code> Node-local Aborted. A completed <code>send</code> cannot supply the missing verdict <p>Unknown is represented explicitly. A timed-out <code>receive</code> is not an empty frame. An unconfirmed silence is not a quiet bus.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#mock-and-real-backends-one-contract-two-implementations","title":"Mock and real backends: one contract, two implementations","text":"<p>The pattern is the one <code>RemoteInterface</code> / <code>MockTransport</code> / <code>SshInterface</code> already proves, and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse: <code>CanInterface</code> is the ABC; a real backend (SocketCAN, a USB-CAN adapter, a vendor SDK) and a <code>MockCan</code> test double both implement the same <code>connect</code> / <code>_send</code> / <code>_receive</code> / <code>_cancel_in_flight</code> methods. Self-tests exercise both against a shared contract suite so a bug in either backend is caught by the same assertions.</p> <p>Required proofs: refuse unrepresentable frames before TX; exclusive in-flight send/receive; immutable filters; wildcard-vs-declared receive; ACK timeout; receive deadline with no invented frame; bus-off stops TX and does not auto-resume; <code>recover()</code> is explicit; partial <code>configure()</code> cannot orphan a transmitter; node cuts leave an independent sibling transmitting; repeated <code>cancel_in_flight</code> cannot replay a frame; unconfirmed silence produces only unconfirmed evidence. <code>MockCan</code> schedules <code>park</code>-style holds analogous to <code>MockTransport.park</code>, so lifecycle tests can abort mid-ACK without hardware. A suite author never constructs either backend directly: <code>utils.get_can(...)</code> resolves to whichever backend the run was configured with.</p> <p>Real-backend tests must verify frames through independent observation (a second SocketCAN interface, a logger); mock ACKs cannot establish that a DUT saw the payload.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#evidence","title":"Evidence","text":"<p>Frames that matter to the verdict travel with it exactly as the Evidence Contract already requires: <code>PASS({\"response_id\": frame.arbitration_id})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={\"response_id\": ...})</code>, declared up front with <code>@evidence(...)</code> when the method promises to carry it. Commanded frames are stimulus, not measurements. A payload that is numeric in the problem domain (RPM on a J1939 PGN, a voltage packed in two bytes) follows whatever protocol issue #85 settled in <code>docs/measurements.md</code>, referenced there rather than restated here. This note neither fills the reserved <code>measurements</code> slot nor decodes anyone's application protocol.</p> <p>Every handle declaration \u2014 bitrate, payload ceiling, filters, silent-state \u2014 is logged through <code>utils.logger</code> at configuration time, the same way <code>ExecRouting</code> logs a connection's provenance. Sends, receives, bus-off, recover, and silence attempts use that scoped logger to distinguish requested, ACKed, received, interrupted, and unconfirmed outcomes.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#what-the-floor-technician-sees","title":"What the Floor Technician sees","text":"<p>When a handle is silenced, the node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> (and its <code>timeline.log</code> twin) carries a log entry at the scope of the node that owned it, naming the handle and whether silence was confirmed, immediately before that node's terminal entry \u2014 the same temporal-backbone placement the In-Flight Termination Contract already gives a hard-closed SSH transport and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse for idle/disable. The terminal entry is not always Aborted: a run-level halt and an <code>abort_after</code> expiry both settle Aborted, a <code>fail_after</code> expiry settles Fail, and the silence sweep is owed under every one of them.</p> <p><code>result.json</code>'s <code>summary_tree</code> shows whichever state applies; there is no CAN-specific field, because a safe-state action is evidence of what the framework did to protect the bus, not a verdict of its own. A technician reading the timeline sees why the run ended and \u2014 from the log line \u2014 that this node stopped transmitting. Where the sweep could not confirm silence, the timeline says exactly that, naming the handle. An unconfirmed silence never leaves a success line behind.</p>"},{"location":"hil/can.html#signed-decisions-recorded-on-95","title":"Signed decisions recorded on #95","text":"<p>The draft asks for a ruling on these concrete obligations before implementation:</p> <ol> <li>Frames as a typed value (id, extended, rtr, payload bytes), not an    <code>exec</code> string; Classic CAN 0\u20138 bytes, CAN-FD as a backend-declared    ceiling under the same contract, not a fork.</li> <li><code>send</code> confirms the on-bus ACK bit only; a DUT response is a later    <code>receive</code>. No silent retry.</li> <li>Filters declared in <code>configure()</code>, immutable for the handle's lifetime;    the no-filter default is receive-nothing, not subscribe-everything.</li> <li>Bus-off is a <code>HiltError</code> operational condition; no automatic TX    resume during the run; <code>recover()</code> is an explicit later operation.</li> <li>Abort safe-state is silence, not a framework-invented shutdown frame.    Authored <code>cleanup()</code> may send a last frame; the hard cut does not wait    for it and does not replace it.</li> <li>Shared adapters refuse conflicting ownership when one node's silence    would starve another, same Group Isolation posture as signed PWM.</li> </ol> <p>The lifecycle, verdict, and evidence contracts above are constraints on that decision. If a required backend cannot satisfy them, the finding belongs on #95 with the affected guarantee named. It does not earn an alternate lifecycle or an exception enforced only by a linter.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html","title":"Design note: Device API (CoAP/HTTP) interface contract","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty approved this note in !130 on 2026-09-09; recorded on #97</p> <p>A CoAP or HTTP resource is not a command string. This note states the request and response shape, retry-as-evidence, timing, error taxonomy, and closed-session obligations that make a device API fit HILT's existing lifecycle. The three HIL rulings govern it. Ty's approval of the actual note signs the obligations below. Implementation requires its own approved issue and Zavala's review under API stability.</p> <p>This is the interface most likely to be waved through as \"just <code>exec</code> with a URL.\" It is not. An HTTP call needs its own contract, error taxonomy, and safe-state, same as a pin or a bus.</p> <p>All names below (<code>DeviceApiInterface</code>, <code>HttpInterface</code>, <code>CoapInterface</code>, <code>DeviceRequest</code>, <code>DeviceResponse</code>, <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code>, <code>MockDeviceApi</code>, and the method names) are provisional. This note adds no public Python name and changes no stored record. The legacy <code>CoapInterface</code> is a design reference only \u2014 see below. The migration context is the preserved lifecycle and evidence contracts, with a new hardware contract for Ty to judge.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#why-not-an-exec-subclass","title":"Why not an <code>exec</code> subclass","text":"<p><code>exec</code> is a command contract: a string goes in, an <code>ExecResult</code> comes out, and a non-zero exit is data for the test method to judge. A device-API call is a typed resource operation: method, path, payload bytes, status, headers or CoAP options, and a body. None of that is a shell command.</p> <p>Forcing it through <code>exec</code> would mean either shelling out to <code>curl</code> / <code>wget</code> / the <code>coap</code> CLI (lossy, racy, and blind to in-flight cancellation) or smuggling an HTTP client inside a <code>RemoteInterface</code> subclass that answers <code>_exec</code> with a fake dialect \u2014 exactly the parallel-lifecycle-in-disguise ruling 1 forbids. A URL that has been stringified into <code>curl -X POST ...</code> has already lost the status as a first-class value, the header set, whether the body was truncated, and whether the client retried. <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> is its own ABC, structurally parallel to <code>RemoteInterface</code> (<code>src/hilt/remote.py</code>) and to the signed <code>GpioInterface</code> / <code>PwmInterface</code>: it owns <code>connect</code> / <code>disconnect</code> / <code>cancel_in_flight</code> in the same shape, and adds request-shaped operations in place of <code>exec</code>.</p> <p>The legacy TypeScript <code>CoapInterface</code> wrapped the <code>coap</code> CLI through <code>utils.exec</code> because the UDP-only <code>node-coap</code> package could not speak <code>coaps+tcp://</code> (RFC 8323). That was a toolchain constraint, not a contract. This port does not carry the CLI wrapper forward. A backend that can only shell out to <code>curl</code> or <code>coap</code> is a finding for Ty, not the shape of the interface.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#the-device-request-contract","title":"The Device Request Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Present CoAP and HTTP as one resource-request contract under HIL ruling 1. <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> is not an <code>exec</code> subclass and does not change where <code>utils.exec</code> routes. A handle is bound to one scheme, one base URL, and a declared timing envelope before it can request.</p> <ul> <li>HTTP and CoAP share the request/response shape (method, path, payload,   status, body). They are not one handle type. An <code>HttpInterface</code> cannot   speak CoAP; a <code>CoapInterface</code> cannot follow HTTP redirects. Scheme is   identity, fixed for the handle's lifetime: <code>http</code> / <code>https</code> for HTTP,   <code>coap</code> / <code>coaps</code> / <code>coaps+tcp</code> for CoAP. Flipping the scheme live is the   Device API instance of flipping a GPIO pin's direction.</li> <li>A provisional <code>DeviceRequest</code> carries <code>method</code> (GET, PUT, POST, DELETE;   CoAP may add the methods its backend declares), <code>path</code> (appended to the   declared base URL, never a second origin), <code>payload</code> (<code>bytes</code> or absent),   protocol options (HTTP headers, CoAP options), and <code>evidence_headers</code>   (the set of request header or option names whose values may appear   in the attempt log; default empty). For HTTP the name is the field   name, matched case-insensitively. For CoAP the name is the registered   option name (<code>Uri-Path</code>, <code>Content-Format</code>); an option number is not a   name and is refused at the call site. PATCH is not in that method set;   whether the first backend declares it as a non-idempotent sibling of   POST is a decision for Ty below, not a silent widening. Reject a   non-<code>bytes</code> payload, a path that is an absolute URL, a method the   backend did not declare, and a header or option name that is not a   string, at the call site, before the adapter is touched. Booleans are   not integers; a <code>dict</code> passed as <code>payload</code> is not a JSON body. The approved   convenience on #155 adds keyword-only <code>json=value</code>: serialize once to UTF-8   bytes and default the HTTP <code>Content-Type</code> to <code>application/json</code>, preserving an   explicitly supplied content type case-insensitively. Omission sends no JSON;   <code>json=None</code> sends null. Combining it with a non-<code>None</code> <code>payload</code> (including   empty bytes) is refused, as are unserializable values and non-finite numbers.   The wire payload remains bytes, and logs still record lengths, never body contents.</li> <li>A provisional <code>DeviceResponse</code> carries <code>status</code> (an HTTP status integer,   or a CoAP code as the dotted string <code>2.05</code> \u2014 see <code>CoapCode</code>), protocol   options (headers / CoAP options), and <code>body</code> (<code>bytes</code>). The wire integer   (<code>class &lt;&lt; 5 | detail</code>, so Content is <code>69</code>) stays inside the transport.   Issue #156.   JSON parsing is a convenience on top of those bytes, not the contract:   a body that is not JSON is still a successful transfer of bytes. The   interface does not invent an empty body, a 200, or a CoAP 2.05 to stand   in for a missing response.   Issue #155 adds <code>json()</code> and <code>parse(response_type=\"json\")</code> to decode those   bytes explicitly; <code>parse(response_type=\"yaml\")</code> uses <code>yaml.safe_load</code> and   requires the optional <code>open-hilt[yaml]</code> extra. Parser errors propagate unchanged,   unsupported formats raise <code>ValueError</code>, and a missing YAML extra raises   <code>ImportError</code>. Neither method changes status or bytes or interprets a status   as a verdict. A malformed body remains a completed transfer.</li> <li>Status is data. HTTP 4xx / 5xx and CoAP 4.xx / 5.xx are fields on   the response, the way a non-zero <code>exit_code</code> is a field on <code>ExecResult</code>.   The interface does not raise <code>TestFailure</code> because a resource returned   404, and it does not raise because a resource returned 500. The test   method judges the status. Transport failure, protocol violation, and   timeout are the operational exceptions; a well-formed error response is   not one of them.</li> <li><code>request(req) -&gt; DeviceResponse</code> performs one application-level attempt   (see retries below). One handle carries one request at a time, in call   order. A <code>request</code> that arrives while another is on the wire waits its   turn; it does not collide, and it does not fail. A handle shared by the   leaves of a <code>parallel</code> therefore serialises them: the claims stay   independent, their requests do not, and a latency measured around   <code>request</code> on that handle includes the wait for siblings. A leaf that   must measure the DUT alone takes its own handle. The wait is bounded by   the node clock, not by the request ceiling, because a sibling ahead in   the queue is not the DUT being slow. Framework termination takes   precedence over the queue: a handle cancelled or closed while a request   waited its turn never sends it (see the closed-session contract below).   The only refusal left is re-entrancy \u2014 a backend's <code>_exchange</code> calling   <code>request</code> on the handle it is already mid-attempt on \u2014 and that is a   <code>ValueError</code> at the call site, a developer bug, not a queue.</li> <li>Each operation records the requested method, path, payload length,   request-header names, whether the adapter confirmed a response, the   status if one arrived, and the body length. Request-header values   stay out of that record unless the author listed the name in   <code>evidence_headers</code>. Response protocol options \u2014 HTTP response headers,   CoAP response options \u2014 are not in that list. Their values are never   recorded by the framework; carrying one into evidence is the author's   act, the same as a body. <code>evidence_headers</code> does not govern them. An   unconfirmed request never becomes the last-successful response in a   cache the next test method reads as truth. Unknown is explicit.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare the scheme, base URL, and protocol parameters in <code>configure()</code>. Obtain the framework-managed handle through a provisional <code>utils.get_http(...)</code> or <code>utils.get_coap(...)</code>; return the handle and static inputs through configure's ctx. Keep <code>self</code> immutable, <code>build_suite()</code> pure, and later ctx reads read-only. Judge the DUT with the status and body actually returned (or with an independent observation), never by treating a 2xx as a passing device.</p> <p>Consequence: The Floor Technician can tell which resource was addressed, what was sent, what status came back, and whether the transfer completed. A 404 cannot quietly become a missing payload in a record that claims the call succeeded, and a 200 cannot quietly become a verdict.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#the-retry-as-evidence-contract","title":"The Retry-as-Evidence Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: A retry is stimulus the DUT saw. Hiding it lies to the Floor Technician.</p> <ul> <li>Application-level retries default to zero. A handle that retries   without a declaration is refused at the call site. Each declared   attempt \u2014 including the first \u2014 is logged through the scoped logger   with attempt index, outcome (response status, operational failure, or   cancelled), and delay before the next attempt when one is scheduled.</li> <li>The retry budget is a count of application-level attempts, not a   duration. Delay between attempts is positive finite seconds, declared   with the count. Booleans and non-finite values are refused. The node's   <code>fail_after</code> / <code>abort_after</code> still bounds the whole sequence; a retry   loop cannot extend the node clock.</li> <li>Non-idempotent methods (POST, and any other method the backend declares   non-idempotent) refuse application retry unless the author sets an   explicit <code>retry_non_idempotent=True</code> on that call. GET / PUT / DELETE   may retry when a count is declared. The interface does not guess   idempotency from the path.</li> <li>Protocol-level reliability is not an application retry. HTTP/TCP   retransmits and CoAP CON retransmits belong to the backend's declared   request ceiling. CON retransmits that actually go on the wire are   counted in the attempt's evidence (the DUT saw them) but they do not   consume the application retry budget. A backend whose CON loop cannot   be bounded by the request ceiling, or cannot be cut by   <code>cancel_in_flight</code>, is a finding for Ty.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight</code> stops the in-flight attempt and the retry loop.   A cancelled retry does not start the next attempt. The record names how   many attempts ran and that the remainder were not started.</li> <li>One logical request holds the handle's turn from its first attempt to   its last. A retry never interleaves with a sibling's request, so the   attempt log for one request reads as one request.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Leave retries at zero unless this bench has a named reason to repeat a request. When retries are declared, treat the attempt log as part of the evidence that will be read after a Fail \u2014 especially for POST. Do not wrap <code>request()</code> in a suite-owned retry loop to dodge the declaration; that is the same lie with worse artifacts.</p> <p>Consequence: The timeline shows every request the DUT actually received from this handle. A pass that depended on the third try cannot be reconstructed as a single clean GET.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#the-closed-session-and-timing-contract","title":"The Closed-Session and Timing Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: The safe state is a closed session \u2014 no further requests originating from this handle \u2014 enforced at the resource by the existing termination sweep. This specializes the signed GPIO ownership and cut rules and the signed PWM disabled-output termination. It introduces no Device API halt signal, event loop, lifecycle runner, or integration hook, and it does not invent a shutdown request.</p> <ul> <li>Register ownership as soon as acquisition can put a request on the   wire, so partial <code>configure()</code> failure cannot orphan a session.   Bring-up establishes and confirms the session (TLS handshake, CoAP   identity) before admitting the first <code>request</code>.</li> <li>An integration-hook failure or operator interrupt closes every open   Device API handle. A node deadline closes only resources opened by the   bounded subtree, under both <code>fail_after</code> (Fail) and <code>abort_after</code>   (node-local Aborted). An inherited adapter or borrowed hop remains its   owner's resource; unrelated siblings are untouched.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight()</code> is idempotent. It aborts the in-flight request at   the socket (HTTP: cancel the stream and close the connection; CoAP:   stop CON retransmits; a RST or connection-close is the protocol cancel,   analogous to I2C STOP, not a resource write), attempts the closed   state under the backend's latency ceiling, and records whether close   was confirmed. A repeated call cannot start a new request or replay the   last one. A closed handle remains closed; there is no automatic   reconnect.</li> <li>A request waiting its turn behind the aborted one is not sent. When its   turn comes on a cancelled or closed handle it fails with   <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> naming that the handle was cancelled or   closed while the request was queued \u2014 an operational failure the author   catches and models as a verdict, never a <code>ValueError</code> and never a   silent request on a dead session. <code>disconnect()</code> treats waiters the same   way.</li> <li>The framework does not transmit a \"safe\" PUT, DELETE, or CoAP action   on abort. A DUT that needs a specific last request gets it from   authored <code>cleanup()</code>, which the hard cut does not wait for. Inventing   a <code>/shutdown</code> write at abort would be a stimulus the suite never   declared, written into a record the technician might trust.</li> <li>Request ceiling (including protocol-level CON/TCP reliability), retry   delay, bring-up/close ceiling, and the node's <code>fail_after</code> /   <code>abort_after</code> are separate clocks. An operation timeout names that   operation and its ceiling. It does not manufacture the <code>timeout</code>   evidence reserved for the winning node clock in the   Time Budget Contract. A long   poll cannot extend the node clock. The request ceiling starts when the   request takes its turn on the handle, not when it was queued: the wait   behind a sibling is bounded by the node clock alone.</li> <li>A backend whose shared session would let one node's close starve   another owner's in-flight request must refuse the conflicting   acquisition before either owner requests. It cannot sacrifice Group   Isolation to offer more nodes on one socket. Serialising requests on   one handle is not that: the handle has one owner, the node whose   <code>configure()</code> opened it, and the leaves queuing on it are that owner's   subtree, closed together when that owner's clock expires.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Select a fixture for which \"this node stops talking\" is the intended safe state. Declare shared sessions honestly. Keep any required shutdown request in <code>cleanup()</code>; rely on framework ownership for termination. Never manually sequence another node's configure or cleanup.</p> <p>Consequence: Ending the test stops this node's requests and records what could be confirmed. A technician is never asked to infer that a session is idle from Pass/Fail/Aborted alone, or from a DELETE nobody authored.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#redirects-credentials-and-the-legacy-action-helper","title":"Redirects, credentials, and the legacy action helper","text":"<p>Framework obligation:</p> <ul> <li>HTTP redirects are a declared policy, default do not follow.   Same-origin follow is an opt-in on the handle. Cross-host follow is   refused: a 302 to a second origin is data on the response, not a   silent second request, and it must not carry the original credentials.   A backend that auto-follows across hosts is refused at bring-up.</li> <li>Authentication material (Bearer tokens, basic passwords, client   certificates, CoAP DTLS PSKs) is declared in <code>configure()</code> and never   reaches evidence. Provenance names the credential path or slot, never   the value \u2014 the same containment SSH already proves. URL userinfo in   the declared base URL is withheld the same way.</li> <li>Attempt logs keep every request-header name and withhold every   value unless the author lists that name in <code>evidence_headers</code> on   the <code>DeviceRequest</code>. Default is empty: the safe case costs the author   no thought. A secret-name allowlist cannot deliver \"never\" \u2014   <code>X-Device-Token</code>, <code>Api-Key</code>, and the next DUT's favorite all miss it.   Matching is case-insensitive for HTTP field names. For CoAP, the name   is the registered option name; an option number is not a name and is   refused at the call site \u2014 a guess that silently matches nothing would   look like a working redaction. Opting <code>Authorization</code> in is the   author's leak to stop, not a framework rewrite. Response protocol   option values are never recorded by the framework. <code>evidence_headers</code>   is a request-side opt-in; it does not cover <code>Set-Cookie</code>,   <code>WWW-Authenticate</code>, or a CoAP response option. Carrying a response   option into evidence is the author's act, the same as a body.   Response bodies still go to evidence when the author puts them there;   secrets in a body are the author's leak to stop, not a framework   rewrite of the payload.</li> <li>TLS / DTLS verification is on by default. Skipping it is a lab-only   declaration analogous to SSH <code>skip_host_verification</code>, logged as such   at bring-up.</li> <li>The legacy <code>performAction</code> helper (PUT an action id, poll until a   K2-specific status set) is application protocol. It is not part of   this contract. Authors who need a long-running device action compose   <code>request()</code> with the existing <code>wait_for</code>. Porting that helper into   <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> would bake one vendor's action DSL into the   framework the way decoding a J1939 PGN would bake an application   protocol into CAN.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Do not follow redirects in suite code to dodge the handle policy. Do not log tokens. Do not opt a secret request header into <code>evidence_headers</code> unless the Floor Technician is meant to read that value. Do not treat <code>evidence_headers</code> as covering response headers or CoAP response options; those values never enter the attempt log, and putting one in a verdict is the author's act. Do not ask the framework for a <code>perform_action</code> sugar that hides the poll.</p> <p>Consequence: A redirect cannot launder a second origin into a record that still names the declared base URL. An attempt log cannot carry a bearer token off the bench unless the author opted that header in. A <code>Set-Cookie</code> or <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> cannot walk into the attempt log because <code>evidence_headers</code> was assumed to cover the response. A K2 action poll is visible as the requests it actually made.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#lifecycle-and-hook-ownership-unchanged","title":"Lifecycle and hook ownership: unchanged","text":"<p>The HIL lifecycle ruling applies unchanged. Concretely:</p> <ul> <li>Bring-up happens the same way <code>use_shell</code> brings up SSH: <code>configure()</code>   obtains a handle (provisionally <code>utils.get_http(...)</code> /   <code>utils.get_coap(...)</code>, mirroring <code>get_shell</code> / <code>get_gpio</code> / <code>get_pwm</code>),   and the framework's bookkeeping tracks what this node opened versus   what it inherited.</li> <li>Teardown order is the framework's: <code>cancel_in_flight</code> fires as part of   the same In-Flight Termination Contract sweep that already closes   <code>RemoteInterface</code> transports and silences signed GPIO/PWM resources.</li> <li><code>use_survey</code> / <code>use_audit</code> remain the observation hooks. A periodic   audit that GETs a health resource and raises <code>TestFailure</code> on an   unexpected status or body is the existing audit contract.</li> <li><code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> remain the integrator's narrow context and   still cannot touch hardware.</li> </ul> <p>Hardware that needs the client to run its own concurrent receive loop outside survey/audit, that cannot honor a hard <code>cancel_in_flight</code> (a request that keeps running on the device after the socket is cut is the device's problem; a client that cannot cut the socket is a finding), or that requires the CLI-wrapper shape the legacy <code>CoapInterface</code> used, is a finding for Ty \u2014 not a reason to give Device API its own lifecycle.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#failure-vocabulary","title":"Failure vocabulary","text":"<p>No new terminal state is introduced, and no new exception root. <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> derives from <code>HiltError</code>, not <code>TestFailure</code>, for the same reason GPIO's <code>PinSettleFailure</code> and PWM's <code>PwmOperationFailure</code> do: failure to complete a transfer does not identify the DUT as the cause.</p> Situation Raised as Terminal state Invalid method, path, payload, or retry declaration; <code>request</code> re-entered from inside the handle's own in-flight request <code>TypeError</code> / <code>ValueError</code> at the call site <code>ContractViolation</code> (Fail, <code>bug: true</code>) naming the exception if it escapes A second <code>request</code> while one is on the wire No exception: it waits its turn, logged as queued Whatever verdict the author writes; the measured latency includes the wait Handle cancelled or closed while a request waited its turn <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> naming the handle, method, path, and that it was queued, never sent Fail when the author catches it and states the verdict; a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (<code>bug: true</code>) when it escapes HTTP 4xx / 5xx or CoAP 4.xx / 5.xx on a well-formed response No exception: status and body are the result Whatever verdict the author writes. The interface does not guess Fail Connection refused, TLS/DTLS handshake failure, protocol violation, request exceeds its ceiling, close cannot be confirmed at bring-up <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> (provisional <code>HiltError</code>) naming the handle, operation, method, path, and ceiling Fail when the author catches it and states the verdict; a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (<code>bug: true</code>) when it escapes Backend cannot reach the adapter at bring-up <code>ConnectionError</code> from the interface's own <code>connect</code> Absorbed the way <code>RemoteInterface</code> connect failures are absorbed today Close cannot be confirmed during the safe-state sweep No exception; the run is already ending Terminal state unchanged; timeline records the unconfirmed close, naming the handle. Never a success line Integration hook throws mid-request Framework raises <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> Aborted; run-level halt closes every open handle Operator SIGINT/SIGTERM mid-run Framework raises <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> Aborted; same run-level sweep A node's time budget expires while it holds a handle Framework settles the node per its declared budget <code>fail_after</code> \u2192 Fail, <code>abort_after</code> \u2192 node-local Aborted; either way only that subtree's handles are closed Test returns without a verdict Existing <code>MissingVerdict</code> Node-local Aborted. A completed <code>request</code> cannot supply the missing verdict <p>Unknown is represented explicitly. A timed-out request is not a 200 with an empty body. An unconfirmed close is not an idle session.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#mock-and-real-backends-one-contract-two-implementations","title":"Mock and real backends: one contract, two implementations","text":"<p>The pattern is the one <code>RemoteInterface</code> / <code>MockTransport</code> / <code>SshInterface</code> already proves, and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse: <code>DeviceApiInterface</code> is the ABC; <code>HttpInterface</code> and <code>CoapInterface</code> are real backends; <code>MockDeviceApi</code> is the test double. Self-tests exercise mock and real against a shared contract suite so a bug in either backend is caught by the same assertions.</p> <p>Required proofs: refuse a non-<code>bytes</code> payload and an absolute-URL path before the adapter is touched; concurrent requests on one handle run one at a time in call order, and a cancel or close mid-queue fails the waiters as <code>DeviceApiOperationFailure</code> without sending them; a re-entered <code>request</code> is refused rather than deadlocked; status-as-data (4xx is a response, not an exception); retries default to zero and each declared attempt is evidenced; non-idempotent retry refused unless opted in; <code>cancel_in_flight</code> stops the retry loop; redirects do not follow cross-host; request-header values never appear in evidence unless the author opted that name into <code>evidence_headers</code>; a CoAP option number is refused as an <code>evidence_headers</code> name; response option values never appear in the attempt log; partial <code>configure()</code> cannot orphan a session; node cuts leave a sibling's in-flight request untouched; repeated <code>cancel_in_flight</code> cannot replay a POST; unconfirmed close produces only unconfirmed evidence. <code>MockDeviceApi</code> schedules <code>park</code>-style holds analogous to <code>MockTransport.park</code>, so lifecycle tests can abort mid-request without a server. A suite author never constructs either backend directly: <code>utils.get_http</code> / <code>utils.get_coap</code> resolve to whichever backend the run was configured with.</p> <p>Real-backend tests must verify transfers through independent observation (a second client, a capturing proxy); mock statuses cannot establish that a DUT saw the body.</p> <p>Core install stays dependency-light. HTTP and CoAP clients land as optional extras on the implementation issue; this note does not pick the library.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#evidence","title":"Evidence","text":"<p>Responses that matter to the verdict travel with it exactly as the Evidence Contract already requires: <code>PASS({\"status\": 200, \"whoami\": ...})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={\"status\": 404})</code>, declared with <code>@evidence(...)</code> when the method promises to carry it. Commanded requests are stimulus, not measurements. A body field that is parametric in the problem domain (a temperature in a JSON object, a firmware version) follows whatever protocol issue #85 settled in <code>docs/measurements.md</code>, referenced there rather than restated here. This note neither fills the reserved <code>measurements</code> slot nor decodes anyone's application protocol.</p> <p>Every handle declaration \u2014 scheme, base URL, retry policy, redirect policy, verification posture, ceilings \u2014 is logged through <code>utils.logger</code> at configuration time. Requests, retries, operational failures, and close attempts use that scoped logger to distinguish requested, responded, interrupted, and unconfirmed outcomes. Attempt index and status belong in that log even when the body does not. Request-header names belong there too; their values do not, unless the author listed that name in <code>evidence_headers</code>. Response option values do not belong there at all.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#what-the-floor-technician-sees","title":"What the Floor Technician sees","text":"<p>When a handle is closed, the node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> (and its <code>timeline.log</code> twin) carries a log entry at the scope of the node that owned it, naming the handle and whether close was confirmed, immediately before that node's terminal entry \u2014 the same temporal-backbone placement the In-Flight Termination Contract already gives a hard-closed SSH transport and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse for idle/disable. The terminal entry is not always Aborted: a run-level halt and an <code>abort_after</code> expiry both settle Aborted, a <code>fail_after</code> expiry settles Fail, and the close sweep is owed under every one of them.</p> <p><code>result.json</code>'s <code>summary_tree</code> shows whichever state applies; there is no Device API-specific field, because a safe-state action is evidence of what the framework did to protect the session, not a verdict of its own. A technician reading the timeline sees why the run ended and \u2014 from the log line \u2014 that this node stopped requesting. Where the sweep could not confirm close, the timeline says exactly that, naming the handle. An unconfirmed close never leaves a success line behind. Retry lines, when present, sit with the request that produced them, so a Fail that landed on the third attempt cannot be read as a single call.</p>"},{"location":"hil/device-api.html#signed-decisions-recorded-on-97","title":"Signed decisions recorded on #97","text":"<p>Ty signed these obligations:</p> <ol> <li>One resource-request contract, two backends. HTTP and CoAP share    method/path/payload/status/body. They are not one handle type, and    they are not <code>exec</code> with a URL. Status (including 4xx/5xx) is data.</li> <li>Typed <code>DeviceRequest</code> / <code>DeviceResponse</code>. Payload is <code>bytes</code>. Path is    relative to the declared base URL. JSON parsing is convenience, not    the contract.</li> <li>Retries are evidence. Default is zero. Each attempt is logged.    Non-idempotent retry is refused unless opted in. Abort cancels the    in-flight attempt and the remainder of the loop. CoAP CON retransmits    are protocol-level, bounded by the request ceiling, and counted in    that attempt's evidence.</li> <li>Legacy <code>CoapInterface</code> is reference only. The CLI-through-<code>exec</code>    wrapper is the trap this note closes. Legacy <code>performAction</code> is    application protocol and stays out of the framework; authors compose    <code>request()</code> with <code>wait_for</code>.</li> <li>Abort safe-state is a closed session (socket cancel / CON stop /    connection-close), not a framework-invented shutdown request.    Authored <code>cleanup()</code> may send a last request; the hard cut does not    wait for it and does not replace it.</li> <li>Redirects default to do-not-follow; cross-host follow is refused.    Shared sessions refuse conflicting ownership, same Group Isolation    posture as signed PWM. Attempt logs keep request-header names and    withhold every value unless the author lists that name in    <code>evidence_headers</code> (default empty). A secret-name allowlist cannot    keep <code>X-Device-Token</code> out of an artifact. Opting <code>Authorization</code> in    is the author's leak. For CoAP the name is the registered option    name, not an option number. Response option values are never    recorded by the framework; carrying one into evidence is the    author's act, the same as a body.</li> <li>PATCH stays undeclared. The declared HTTP methods are GET, PUT, POST,    DELETE. POST refuses application retry unless opted in. PATCH is the    same double-stimulus, and the signature of 2026-09-09 did not choose    between the two branches this decision offered, so it takes the branch    that adds nothing to the public surface: PATCH is refused at the call    site as an undeclared method. Declaring it is a later note against this    contract, not a widening of the first backend.</li> </ol> <p>The lifecycle, verdict, and evidence contracts above are constraints on that decision. If a required backend cannot satisfy them, the finding belongs on #97 with the affected guarantee named. It does not earn an alternate lifecycle or an exception enforced only by a linter.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html","title":"Design note: GPIO interface contract","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty ruled the open questions on #93 on 2026-09-07</p> <p>This note states the developer-framework contract for digital I/O pins \u2014 direction, level, debounce, safe-state on abort, the error taxonomy for a pin that will not settle, and timing guarantees. It is written to the diligence of <code>docs/contracts.md</code> and governed by the three rulings in <code>docs/hil/index.md</code>. All names below (<code>GpioInterface</code>, <code>GpioPin</code>, <code>PinDirection</code>, <code>PinLevel</code>, <code>PinSettleFailure</code>) are provisional: they name the shape Ty signed off, not a promised spelling.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#why-not-an-exec-subclass","title":"Why not an <code>exec</code> subclass","text":"<p>A GPIO pin does not run commands. \"Set this pin high\" and \"read this pin's level\" are typed operations on a fixed set of physical lines, not strings handed to a shell. Forcing pin control through <code>exec</code> would mean either shelling out to <code>sysfs</code>/<code>libgpiod</code> command-line tools per call (slow, racy, and impossible to debounce cleanly) or smuggling a pin controller inside a <code>RemoteInterface</code> subclass that answers <code>_exec</code> with a fake shell dialect \u2014 exactly the parallel-lifecycle-in-disguise ruling 1 forbids. <code>GpioInterface</code> is its own ABC, structurally parallel to <code>RemoteInterface</code> (<code>src/hilt/remote.py</code>) but not a subclass of it: it owns <code>connect</code>/ <code>disconnect</code>/<code>cancel_in_flight</code> in the same shape, and adds pin-shaped operations in place of <code>exec</code>.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#the-gpio-interface-contract","title":"The GPIO Interface Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>GpioInterface</code> presents a fixed set of named pins, each with a declared direction and idle level, and guarantees the following regardless of backend:</p> <ul> <li>Direction and level. Every pin is declared <code>PinDirection.OUT</code> or   <code>PinDirection.IN</code> before first use; direction is fixed for the pin's   lifetime \u2014 a suite that needs the other direction opens a different   declared pin, it does not flip one live. <code>set_level(pin, PinLevel.HIGH |   PinLevel.LOW)</code> and <code>read_level(pin) -&gt; PinLevel</code> are the only level   operations. <code>PinLevel</code> carries three members, not two: <code>HIGH</code>, <code>LOW</code>, and   <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> \u2014 the last meaning the controller could not be reached for   this read. A two-valued return would have to answer that case with a level   nobody measured, and there is no honest choice between them.   Setting an input pin or reading an output-only pin (on hardware   that cannot read back its own output) is a <code>TypeError</code> at the call site, not   a silent no-op \u2014 the same posture <code>RemoteInterface.exec</code> takes toward a   disconnected transport, caught before it can be mistaken for a hardware   reading.</li> <li>Debounce. A read on an input pin names two quantities, never one: a   stability window \u2014 how long the level must hold unchanged for the   reading to count \u2014 and an acquisition deadline \u2014 how long the framework   keeps looking for such a stretch. Both are seconds, both validated positive   and finite, and the deadline must exceed the window. One number cannot carry   both jobs: a transition halfway through a window leaves no complete stable   stretch before that same instant, so a single <code>settle</code> can demand an outcome   it has also made unreachable. Given the two, the framework samples at an   interval no coarser than a tenth of the window and returns the first level   that holds for a full window. A pin still chattering when the deadline   passes does not return a guessed value \u2014 it raises (see the failure   vocabulary below). Both defaults are per-backend but always explicit in the   resolved call. Debounce is symmetric: it is not a filter for \"the level I   expected,\" it is a filter for \"the level that stopped moving.\"</li> <li>Safe-state on abort. Every declared output pin carries a required idle   level, given at declaration time, not inferred from its first <code>set_level</code>   call. Two different events drive pins to idle, and this note keeps them   apart because the contracts do:<ul> <li>The run-level halt \u2014 an integration hook throw, or an operator   SIGINT/SIGTERM \u2014 drives every output pin that every open   <code>GpioInterface</code> holds to its declared idle level before any interface   reports <code>cancel_in_flight</code> complete. The whole bench is coming down.</li> <li>A node's time budget expiring is not that halt. Deadline Aborted is   node-local: it never fires the shared run halt and never cascades past   the bounded subtree (<code>docs/contracts.md</code>, Time Budget Contract). It   drives only the pins the cut subtree itself opened; an inherited   controller or an inherited hop is never in that owned set, and a   sibling outside the subtree is untouched under the Group Isolation   Contract. On a rack of thirty units, one node overrunning its clock must   not idle the other twenty-nine.   The sweep is owed under both budget names. <code>fail_after</code> renders Fail and   <code>abort_after</code> renders node-local Aborted; the verdict differs, the safe   state does not. This is the GPIO-specific instance of the In-Flight   Termination Contract's \"cancel the resource, not just the coroutine\": for a   relay driving mains power or a reset line holding a DUT in a known state,   leaving the last-written level in place across an abort is the failure mode   this contract exists to prevent. Input pins have no safe-state action; they   carry no risk from being read after abort, and <code>cancel_in_flight</code> stops   accepting reads immediately.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Timing guarantees. <code>set_level</code> and <code>read_level</code> (after debounce settles)   complete within a bounded, backend-declared latency ceiling \u2014 the interface   states its worst-case pin-toggle latency so a suite author can reason about   whether a protocol timing requirement (a reset pulse width, a chip-select   setup time) is achievable at all through this backend before writing the   test. A call that cannot complete within that ceiling is a   backend-operation timeout \u2014 the controller gave up \u2014 and it is reported as   the operational failure below. It is not the node's clock expiring, and the   record must never conflate the two: a suite that declared a generous   <code>fail_after</code> has not thereby reclassified a controller that timed out in   milliseconds, and a node deadline firing during a pin call has not made the   controller at fault.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation:</p> <ul> <li>Declare every pin's direction and, for outputs, its idle level at   configuration time \u2014 in <code>configure()</code>, the same place <code>use_shell</code> is called.   A pin whose idle level is not knowable (\"depends on what the last test left   it at\") is a design defect in the test, not something the framework can   paper over.</li> <li>State both debounce quantities deliberately. The defaults are not \"right for   every signal\" \u2014 a mechanical relay and a solid-state one settle on different   timescales, and a noisy line often needs a strict window with a generous   deadline. The author who knows the hardware states both numbers.</li> <li>Catch <code>PinSettleFailure</code> and decide what it means. It is a <code>HiltError</code>,   not a <code>TestFailure</code>, and that ancestry is the whole point: a pin that will   not stop moving does not by itself say whether the DUT is faulty, the   harness is noisy, or the probe is loose. The author models it into a verdict   or the child boundary classifies the escape as the bug it is \u2014 a mode the   suite never handled.</li> <li>Handle <code>PinLevel.UNAVAILABLE</code> at every read. It is a real state of the   bench, not defensive padding: the controller was unreachable and no   measurement exists. Compare against the member you mean   (<code>if level is PinLevel.LOW:</code>), never against its negation \u2014   <code>if level != PinLevel.HIGH:</code> quietly reads an unreachable controller as a   low line, and a technician acting on that verdict believes a wire is dead   when nothing confirmed it.</li> </ul> <p>Consequence: A relay left energized, a reset line left asserted, or a power-enable pin left high across an aborted run is a safety and equipment- damage risk, not merely a test artifact. Declaring idle levels up front and enforcing them on every abort path \u2014 not just a clean <code>cleanup()</code> \u2014 is what makes GPIO safe to use on live hardware under this framework.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#lifecycle-and-hook-ownership-unchanged","title":"Lifecycle and hook ownership: unchanged","text":"<p><code>GpioInterface</code> does not run its own event loop, does not register its own <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code>, and does not own a competing halt signal. Concretely:</p> <ul> <li>Bring-up happens the same way <code>use_shell</code> brings up SSH: a <code>configure()</code>   call obtains a <code>GpioInterface</code> handle (provisionally <code>utils.get_gpio(...)</code>,   mirroring <code>get_shell</code>), and the framework's existing <code>ExecRouting</code>-style   bookkeeping tracks what this node opened versus what it inherited.</li> <li>Teardown order is the framework's, not the interface's: <code>cancel_in_flight</code>   on every opened <code>GpioInterface</code> fires as part of the same In-Flight   Termination Contract sweep that already closes <code>RemoteInterface</code> transports   when <code>RunAbort</code> fires, not on a separate GPIO-specific abort path.</li> <li><code>use_survey</code>/<code>use_audit</code> are the only hooks a suite registers to observe pin   state; a periodic audit that reads a watchdog pin and raises <code>TestFailure</code>   on an unexpected level is exactly the audit contract's existing shape   (thermal-runaway-style monitoring), not a new hook kind.</li> <li><code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> remain the integrator's narrow context   (<code>BeforeEachContext</code>/<code>AfterEachContext</code>) and still cannot touch hardware \u2014   a GPIO pin is exactly the kind of thing those hooks are barred from reaching,   same as <code>exec</code> and <code>get_shell</code> today.</li> </ul> <p>Hardware that needs a GPIO controller to run its own concurrent polling loop outside the audit/survey pattern, or that cannot honor a hard <code>cancel_in_flight</code>, is a finding for Ty \u2014 not a reason to give GPIO its own lifecycle.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#failure-vocabulary","title":"Failure vocabulary","text":"Situation Raised as Terminal state A pin never settles within its acquisition deadline <code>PinSettleFailure</code> (provisional; a <code>HiltError</code>, deliberately not a <code>TestFailure</code> \u2014 the exact posture <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> holds, <code>src/hilt/errors.py</code>) Fail when the author catches it and states the verdict; a <code>ContractViolation</code>-wrapped Fail (<code>bug: true</code>) when it escapes, because the suite never modeled the mode A pin call exceeds the backend's declared latency ceiling <code>PinSettleFailure</code>, naming the backend operation As above. Never recorded as a node time-budget expiry Direction/level operation misused (write to an input, read an output-only pin) <code>TypeError</code> at the call site <code>ContractViolation</code> (Fail, <code>bug: true</code>) \u2014 same posture as <code>ExecRouting</code>'s connection-pair <code>TypeError</code>s Backend cannot reach the controller during a read No exception: <code>read_level</code> returns <code>PinLevel.UNAVAILABLE</code> Whatever verdict the author writes for that state. An unhandled third member is not a framework error \u2014 it is a reading the suite declined to judge Backend cannot reach the controller at bring-up <code>ConnectionError</code> from the interface's own <code>connect</code> Absorbed the way <code>RemoteInterface</code>'s connect failures are absorbed today \u2014 not a bug, a hardware-unreachable condition for the test method to judge An idle drive cannot be confirmed during the safe-state sweep No exception; the run is already ending The node's terminal state is unchanged, and the timeline records the failure to confirm, naming the pin. A sweep that could not verify idle never leaves a success line behind Integration hook throws while pins are held mid-toggle Framework raises <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> Aborted; the run-level halt drives every open <code>GpioInterface</code> to idle, per the In-Flight Termination Contract Operator SIGINT/SIGTERM mid-run Framework raises <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> Aborted; same run-level sweep A node's time budget expires while it holds pins Framework settles the node per its declared budget <code>fail_after</code> \u2192 Fail, <code>abort_after</code> \u2192 node-local Aborted; either way only the pins that subtree opened are driven to idle <p>No new terminal state is introduced, and no new exception root. <code>PinSettleFailure</code> is an operational condition rather than a verdict, which is why it takes <code>HiltError</code> and not <code>TestFailure</code>: \"this pin will not stop moving\" does not tell anyone whether the DUT is faulty, the harness is noisy, or the probe is loose, and the framework must not choose among those on the author's behalf. That is the epistemic position <code>ArpResolutionFailure</code> already occupies. <code>PinLevel.UNAVAILABLE</code> is likewise data and never control flow \u2014 a reading the author judges, exactly as a non-zero exit code from <code>exec</code> is.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#mock-and-real-backends-one-contract-two-implementations","title":"Mock and real backends: one contract, two implementations","text":"<p>The pattern is the one <code>RemoteInterface</code>/<code>MockTransport</code>/<code>SshInterface</code> already proves for <code>exec</code>: <code>GpioInterface</code> is the ABC; a real backend (provisionally targeting <code>libgpiod</code> or a vendor SDK) and a <code>MockGpio</code> test double both implement the same <code>connect</code>/<code>_set_level</code>/<code>_read_level</code>/ <code>_cancel_in_flight</code> methods, and self-tests exercise both against a shared contract-suite of assertions (declare pin, set level, read level, abort mid- toggle, verify idle drive) so a bug in either backend is caught by the same tests. <code>MockGpio</code> schedules <code>park</code>-style holds analogous to <code>MockTransport.park</code>, so lifecycle tests can assert that an abort arriving mid-debounce still drives idle levels without a real controller on the bench. A suite author never constructs either backend directly, mirroring <code>use_shell</code>/<code>get_shell</code>: a provisional <code>utils.get_gpio(...)</code> call resolves to whichever backend the run was configured with.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#evidence","title":"Evidence","text":"<p>A pin read that matters to the verdict travels with it exactly as the Evidence Contract already requires: <code>PASS({\"reset_pin_level\": level})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={\"reset_pin_level\": level})</code>, declared up front with <code>@evidence(...)</code> when the method promises to carry it. A GPIO level itself is not a parametric measurement (there is no unit, limit, or continuous scale to it \u2014 it is boolean-shaped evidence), so it is recorded as a plain evidence entry, not through the <code>#85</code> measurements protocol. Where a GPIO-adjacent reading is parametric \u2014 a pulse-width or duty-cycle-like timing measured off a pin transition \u2014 that measurement follows whatever protocol issue #85 settles on, referenced there rather than re-specified here.</p> <p>Every pin declaration, direction, and idle-level assignment is logged through <code>utils.logger</code> at configuration time, the same way <code>ExecRouting</code> logs a connection's provenance lines \u2014 so the Floor Technician can see what idle state was promised without opening the suite source.</p>"},{"location":"hil/gpio.html#what-the-floor-technician-sees","title":"What the Floor Technician sees","text":"<p>When a pin is driven to its idle level, the node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> (and its human-readable <code>timeline.log</code> twin) carries a log entry at the scope of the node that owned the pin, naming which pin was driven and to what level, immediately before that node's terminal entry \u2014 the same temporal-backbone placement the In-Flight Termination Contract already gives a hard-closed SSH transport. The terminal entry is not always Aborted: a run-level halt and an <code>abort_after</code> expiry both settle the node Aborted, a <code>fail_after</code> expiry settles it Fail, and the idle sweep is owed under every one of them. <code>result.json</code>'s <code>summary_tree</code> shows whichever state applies; there is no separate GPIO-specific field, because a safe-state action is evidence of what the framework did to protect the hardware, not a verdict of its own. A technician reading the timeline sees why the run ended (from <code>failure</code>, naming the operator, the integration hook that threw, or the clock that fired) and \u2014 from the log line \u2014 that the reset/enable/relay pin the suite declared was left in its documented idle state, not in whatever level the test last happened to set.</p> <p>Where the sweep could not confirm a pin reached its idle level, the timeline says exactly that, naming the pin. This is the most load-bearing line in the note. A technician reaching into a rack trusts a record that says a relay was de-energized, and a success line written for a drive nobody confirmed is how someone puts a hand on a line believing it dead. An unconfirmed idle is recorded as unconfirmed.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html","title":"Design note: I2C/SPI interface contract","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty approved this note in !129 on 2026-09-09; recorded on #96</p> <p>I2C and SPI carry register transactions, not command strings. This note states the transfer, addressing, arbitration, timing, and released-bus obligations that make both buses fit HILT's existing lifecycle. They are two interfaces under one contract shape, not one type with a protocol flag. The three HIL rulings govern both. Ty's approval of the actual note signs the obligations below. Implementation requires its own approved issue and Zavala's review under API stability.</p> <p>All names below (<code>I2cInterface</code>, <code>SpiInterface</code>, <code>I2cAddress</code>, <code>SpiMode</code>, <code>BusArbitrationFailure</code>, <code>BusOperationFailure</code>, <code>MockI2c</code>, <code>MockSpi</code>, and the method names) are provisional. This note adds no public Python name and changes no stored record. The legacy framework and language server supply no I2C/SPI behavior to carry forward; the migration context is the preserved lifecycle and evidence contracts, with a new hardware contract for Ty to judge.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#why-not-an-exec-subclass","title":"Why not an <code>exec</code> subclass","text":"<p>A register read is an addressed transaction: a 7- or 10-bit I2C address and a register pointer, or a SPI chip-select plus a command byte, then a bounded payload. None of that is a shell command. Forcing it through <code>exec</code> would mean shelling out to <code>i2cget</code>/<code>spidev</code> tools (lossy, racy, and blind to a stuck-low SDA) or smuggling a controller inside a <code>RemoteInterface</code> subclass that answers <code>_exec</code> with a fake dialect \u2014 exactly the parallel-lifecycle-in-disguise ruling 1 forbids. <code>I2cInterface</code> and <code>SpiInterface</code> are their own ABCs, structurally parallel to <code>RemoteInterface</code> (<code>src/hilt/remote.py</code>) and to the signed <code>GpioInterface</code> / <code>PwmInterface</code>: each owns <code>connect</code> / <code>disconnect</code> / <code>cancel_in_flight</code> in the same shape, and adds transaction-shaped operations in place of <code>exec</code>.</p> <p>They are not subclasses of each other. An I2C NACK is not a SPI 0xFF. A shared \"bus transfer\" ABC that pretended otherwise would lose the arbitration and chip-select contracts this note exists to name.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#the-bus-transaction-contract","title":"The Bus Transaction Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Present each bus as its own typed interface under HIL ruling 1. Neither is an <code>exec</code> subclass; neither changes where <code>utils.exec</code> routes. A handle is bound to one adapter, one protocol, and a declared timing envelope before it can transfer.</p> <ul> <li>A transaction is atomic at the wire: I2C from START through STOP; SPI   for the whole time chip-select is asserted. The interface does not expose   a pile of unbound <code>read()</code> / <code>write()</code> calls that another node, or an   abort, can interleave mid-address.</li> <li>One in-flight transaction per handle. A second transfer is refused while   one is active; it is not queued to surprise the next lifecycle phase.   Framework termination takes precedence over this admission rule.</li> <li>Each operation records the requested address or chip-select, the   direction, the payload length, and whether the controller confirmed the   outcome. An unconfirmed transfer never becomes the last-successful value   in a cache the next test method reads as truth. Unknown is explicit.</li> <li>Payload is <code>bytes</code>. Reject a non-<code>bytes</code> payload, a negative length, and   a length above the backend-declared ceiling, at the call site, before the   adapter is touched. Booleans are not integers.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare the adapter, protocol parameters, and every device this node will address in <code>configure()</code>. Obtain the framework-managed handle through a provisional <code>utils.get_i2c(...)</code> or <code>utils.get_spi(...)</code>; return the handle and static inputs through configure's ctx. Keep <code>self</code> immutable, <code>build_suite()</code> pure, and later ctx reads read-only. Judge the DUT with bytes actually returned (or with an independent observation), never by treating a successful ACK or a full-duplex clock-out as a passing device.</p> <p>Consequence: The Floor Technician can tell which device was addressed, what was written, what came back, and whether the transaction completed. A NACK or a short read cannot quietly become a register value in a record that claims otherwise.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#the-i2c-addressing-and-arbitration-contract","title":"The I2C Addressing and Arbitration Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: I2C identity is an address, not a path.</p> <ul> <li>A provisional <code>I2cAddress</code> carries the 7-bit or 10-bit address and the   width. Reject an address that does not fit the declared width. 8-bit   \"address plus R/W\" encodings are refused: the R/W bit is the transfer's,   not the handle's.</li> <li>Provisional <code>write(address, data)</code>, <code>read(address, n)</code>, and   <code>write_read(address, tx, n)</code> each emit one START\u2013STOP transaction.   <code>write_read</code> is a repeated-START, not two transactions the abort path   could split. There is no \"hold the bus across calls\" mode.</li> <li>NACK on address (no device ACKed) and NACK on data (device ACKed   the address then rejected a byte) are distinct operational failures,   named in the error. Neither is a <code>TestFailure</code>. The interface does not   retry, does not clock out dummy bytes after a NACK, and does not return   a truncated payload as success.</li> <li>Arbitration loss is an operational failure (<code>BusArbitrationFailure</code>).   Another master won the bus. The interface stops, does not retry, and   does not invent the bytes it did not finish sending. A backend whose   driver auto-retries arbitration in the background is refused at bring-up:   that is the I2C instance of a detached PWM ramp.</li> <li>Clock stretching is the slave's right within the backend-declared stretch   ceiling. Exceeding that ceiling is an operational timeout of the   transaction, not a node time-budget expiry.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Name every address this node will talk to at configure time when the set is known. Catch address-NACK, data-NACK, and arbitration loss separately if they mean different things on this bench (missing DUT vs. register reject vs. a second tester). Do not busy-loop a NACK into existence of a device.</p> <p>Consequence: A missing part, a device that refused a register, and a bus fight are three records, not one \"I2C failed\" blob.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#the-spi-chip-select-and-mode-contract","title":"The SPI Chip-Select and Mode Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: SPI identity is a chip-select plus a mode, not an address.</p> <ul> <li>A provisional <code>SpiDevice</code> declares the chip-select line, <code>SpiMode</code>   (CPOL/CPHA 0\u20133), bit order, and bits-per-word. Mode is fixed for the   handle's lifetime; flipping CPOL live is the SPI instance of flipping a   GPIO pin's direction.</li> <li>Provisional <code>transfer(tx) -&gt; rx</code> asserts chip-select, clocks <code>tx</code>,   returns <code>rx</code> of the same length, and deasserts chip-select. Full-duplex   is the default shape. A backend that can only half-duplex still returns   <code>bytes</code> of that length; it does not return <code>None</code> for the unobserved   side.</li> <li>There is no SPI NACK. MISO idle (typically 0xFF or 0x00) is data.   The framework must not interpret a repeating fill byte as \"device   missing.\" Absence of a device, if the bench can know it, is a GPIO-level   or power-level observation, not a SPI protocol error.</li> <li>Chip-select is owned for the transaction. The interface does not leave   CS asserted across calls so an author can \"optimize\" two transfers. A   multi-message device that requires CS held uses one <code>transfer</code> covering   the whole sequence, or it is a finding for Ty.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare mode, bit order, and chip-select in <code>configure()</code>. Do not decode 0xFF into \"unplugged\" inside the interface call; if that fill is a fault, the test method says so with a <code>TestFailure</code> naming the expected register contents.</p> <p>Consequence: A disconnected MISO cannot be laundered into a protocol exception the Floor Technician would treat like an I2C NACK.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#the-released-bus-and-timing-contract","title":"The Released-Bus and Timing Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: The safe state is a released bus \u2014 I2C lines idle after a STOP, SPI chip-select deasserted and clock at the mode's idle level \u2014 enforced at the resource by the existing termination sweep. This specializes the signed GPIO ownership and cut rules and the signed PWM disabled-output termination. It introduces no bus halt signal, event loop, lifecycle runner, or integration hook.</p> <ul> <li>Register ownership as soon as acquisition can drive a line, so partial   <code>configure()</code> failure cannot leave SDA held low or CS asserted. Bring-up   establishes and confirms the released state before admitting the first   transaction.</li> <li>An integration-hook failure or operator interrupt releases every open   I2C and SPI handle. A node deadline releases only resources opened by   the bounded subtree, under both <code>fail_after</code> (Fail) and <code>abort_after</code>   (node-local Aborted). An inherited adapter remains its owner's resource;   unrelated siblings are untouched.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight()</code> is idempotent. It aborts the in-flight transaction   at the controller (I2C: STOP if a START is open; SPI: deassert CS),   attempts the released state under the backend's latency ceiling, and   records whether release was confirmed. A repeated call cannot start a   new transaction or replay the last write. A closed handle remains   closed.</li> <li>The framework does not invent a \"safe\" register write on abort (clearing   an output register, putting a DAC to zero). A DUT that needs a specific   last write gets it from authored <code>cleanup()</code>, which the hard cut does   not wait for. Inventing a write at abort would be a stimulus the suite   never declared.</li> <li>Transaction ceiling (including I2C stretch bound) and bring-up/release   ceiling are each positive finite backend-declared bounds, separate from   the node's <code>fail_after</code> / <code>abort_after</code>. An operation timeout names that   operation and its ceiling. It does not manufacture the <code>timeout</code>   evidence reserved for the winning node clock in the   Time Budget Contract.</li> <li>A backend whose shared adapter would let one node's STOP or CS-deassert   starve another owner's in-flight transaction must refuse the conflicting   acquisition before either owner transfers. It cannot sacrifice Group   Isolation to offer more nodes on one bus.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Select a fixture for which \"this node releases the bus\" is the intended safe state. Declare shared adapters honestly. Keep any required shutdown write in <code>cleanup()</code>; rely on framework ownership for termination.</p> <p>Consequence: Ending the test stops this node's transactions and records what could be confirmed. A technician is never asked to infer that SDA is free, or that CS is high, from Pass/Fail/Aborted alone.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#lifecycle-and-hook-ownership-unchanged","title":"Lifecycle and hook ownership: unchanged","text":"<p>The HIL lifecycle ruling applies unchanged. Concretely:</p> <ul> <li>Bring-up happens the same way <code>use_shell</code> brings up SSH: <code>configure()</code>   obtains a handle (provisionally <code>utils.get_i2c(...)</code> / <code>utils.get_spi(...)</code>,   mirroring <code>get_shell</code> / <code>get_gpio</code> / <code>get_pwm</code>), and the framework's   bookkeeping tracks what this node opened versus what it inherited.</li> <li>Teardown order is the framework's: <code>cancel_in_flight</code> fires as part of   the same In-Flight Termination Contract sweep that already closes   <code>RemoteInterface</code> transports and silences signed GPIO/PWM resources.</li> <li><code>use_survey</code> / <code>use_audit</code> remain the observation hooks. A periodic   audit that reads a status register and raises <code>TestFailure</code> on an   unexpected value is the existing audit contract.</li> <li><code>before_each</code> / <code>after_each</code> remain the integrator's narrow context and   still cannot touch hardware.</li> </ul> <p>Hardware that needs the controller to run its own concurrent transfer loop outside survey/audit, or that cannot honor a hard <code>cancel_in_flight</code> (cannot issue STOP, cannot deassert CS), is a finding for Ty \u2014 not a reason to give either bus its own lifecycle.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#failure-vocabulary","title":"Failure vocabulary","text":"<p>No new terminal state is introduced, and no new exception root. <code>BusOperationFailure</code> and <code>BusArbitrationFailure</code> derive from <code>HiltError</code>, not <code>TestFailure</code>, for the same reason GPIO's <code>PinSettleFailure</code> and PWM's <code>PwmOperationFailure</code> do: failure to complete a transaction does not identify the DUT as the cause.</p> Situation Raised as Terminal state Invalid address, mode, payload, or conflicting in-flight use <code>TypeError</code> / <code>ValueError</code> at the call site <code>ContractViolation</code> (Fail, <code>bug: true</code>) if it escapes I2C NACK on address or data; transaction exceeds its ceiling (including stretch); SPI transfer exceeds its ceiling; release cannot be confirmed at bring-up <code>BusOperationFailure</code> (provisional <code>HiltError</code>) naming the handle, operation, address or CS, and ceiling Fail when the author catches it and states the verdict; a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail (<code>bug: true</code>) when it escapes I2C arbitration loss <code>BusArbitrationFailure</code> (provisional <code>HiltError</code>) As above. Never an automatic DUT Fail SPI MISO fill (0xFF / 0x00) No exception: those bytes are the transfer result Whatever verdict the author writes. The interface does not guess \"unplugged\" Backend cannot reach the adapter at bring-up <code>ConnectionError</code> from the interface's own <code>connect</code> Absorbed the way <code>RemoteInterface</code> connect failures are absorbed today Release cannot be confirmed during the safe-state sweep No exception; the run is already ending Terminal state unchanged; timeline records the unconfirmed release, naming the handle. Never a success line Integration hook throws mid-transaction Framework raises <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> Aborted; run-level halt releases every open handle Operator SIGINT/SIGTERM mid-run Framework raises <code>OperatorInterrupt</code> Aborted; same run-level sweep A node's time budget expires while it holds a handle Framework settles the node per its declared budget <code>fail_after</code> \u2192 Fail, <code>abort_after</code> \u2192 node-local Aborted; either way only that subtree's handles are released Test returns without a verdict Existing <code>MissingVerdict</code> Node-local Aborted. A completed transfer cannot supply the missing verdict <p>Unknown is represented explicitly. A NACK is not a zero payload. An unconfirmed STOP is not a free bus.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#mock-and-real-backends-one-contract-two-implementations-per-bus","title":"Mock and real backends: one contract, two implementations per bus","text":"<p>The pattern is the one <code>RemoteInterface</code> / <code>MockTransport</code> / <code>SshInterface</code> already proves, and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse: each ABC has a real backend (<code>smbus2</code> / <code>spidev</code> / a vendor SDK) and a mock (<code>MockI2c</code>, <code>MockSpi</code>). Self-tests exercise both against a shared contract suite.</p> <p>Required proofs, I2C: refuse bad addresses before START; one transaction in flight; address-NACK vs data-NACK named separately; arbitration loss stops and does not auto-retry; <code>write_read</code> is one repeated-START; partial <code>configure()</code> cannot leave SDA held; node cuts leave a sibling's transaction untouched; repeated <code>cancel_in_flight</code> issues at most one STOP and cannot replay a write; unconfirmed release produces only unconfirmed evidence.</p> <p>Required proofs, SPI: mode immutable; <code>transfer</code> length(tx) == length(rx); CS asserted only for that call; fill bytes are data; CS deasserted on abort even mid-clock; exclusive ownership of a shared adapter.</p> <p><code>MockI2c</code> / <code>MockSpi</code> schedule <code>park</code>-style holds analogous to <code>MockTransport.park</code>. A suite author never constructs either backend directly: <code>utils.get_i2c</code> / <code>utils.get_spi</code> resolve to whichever backend the run was configured with. Real-backend tests must verify transactions through independent observation (a second master, a logic analyzer); mock ACKs cannot establish that a DUT saw the register write.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#evidence","title":"Evidence","text":"<p>Register values that matter to the verdict travel with it exactly as the Evidence Contract already requires: <code>PASS({\"whoami\": value})</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence={\"whoami\": value})</code>, declared with <code>@evidence(...)</code> when the method promises to carry it. Commanded writes are stimulus, not measurements. A value that is parametric in the problem domain (a temperature register, a DAC code) follows whatever protocol issue #85 settled in <code>docs/measurements.md</code>, referenced there rather than restated here. This note neither fills the reserved <code>measurements</code> slot nor decodes anyone's application protocol.</p> <p>Every handle declaration \u2014 adapter, addresses or chip-selects, I2C width, SPI mode, ceilings \u2014 is logged through <code>utils.logger</code> at configuration time. Transfers, NACKs, arbitration loss, and release attempts use that scoped logger to distinguish requested, confirmed, interrupted, and unconfirmed outcomes.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#what-the-floor-technician-sees","title":"What the Floor Technician sees","text":"<p>When a handle is released, the node's <code>timeline.jsonl</code> (and its <code>timeline.log</code> twin) carries a log entry at the scope of the node that owned it, naming the handle and whether release was confirmed, immediately before that node's terminal entry \u2014 the same temporal-backbone placement the In-Flight Termination Contract already gives a hard-closed SSH transport and that signed GPIO/PWM reuse for idle/disable. The terminal entry is not always Aborted: a run-level halt and an <code>abort_after</code> expiry both settle Aborted, a <code>fail_after</code> expiry settles Fail, and the release sweep is owed under every one of them.</p> <p><code>result.json</code>'s <code>summary_tree</code> shows whichever state applies; there is no I2C- or SPI-specific field, because a safe-state action is evidence of what the framework did to protect the bus, not a verdict of its own. A technician reading the timeline sees why the run ended and \u2014 from the log line \u2014 that this node released the bus. Where the sweep could not confirm release (SDA still low, CS still asserted), the timeline says exactly that, naming the handle. An unconfirmed release never leaves a success line behind. That is the load-bearing line: a technician reaching into a rack trusts a record that says the bus is free.</p>"},{"location":"hil/i2c-spi.html#signed-decisions-recorded-on-96","title":"Signed decisions recorded on #96","text":"<p>The draft asks for a ruling on these concrete obligations before implementation:</p> <ol> <li>Two interfaces, one contract shape. I2C and SPI are not one type. A    NACK is not a 0xFF fill byte.</li> <li>Transactions are atomic (I2C START\u2013STOP, including <code>write_read</code> as    repeated-START; SPI CS held only for one <code>transfer</code>). No unbound    read/write that another node can interleave.</li> <li>Address-NACK, data-NACK, and arbitration loss are named operational    <code>HiltError</code>s; no automatic retry.</li> <li>SPI MISO fill is data. Device-missing is not a SPI protocol error.</li> <li>Abort safe-state is a released bus (STOP / CS deassert), not a    framework-invented register write. Authored <code>cleanup()</code> may write a    last value; the hard cut does not wait for it and does not replace it.</li> <li>Shared adapters refuse conflicting ownership when one node's release    would starve another, same Group Isolation posture as signed PWM.</li> </ol> <p>The lifecycle, verdict, and evidence contracts above are constraints on that decision. If a required backend cannot satisfy them, the finding belongs on #96 with the affected guarantee named. It does not earn an alternate lifecycle or an exception enforced only by a linter.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html","title":"Design note: PWM interface contract","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty approved this note in !125 on 2026-09-08; recorded on #94</p> <p>PWM controls a repeating output through its frequency and duty cycle. This note states the transfer, ramp, timing, and disabled-output obligations that make that control fit HILT's existing lifecycle. The three HIL rulings govern it. Ty's approval of the actual note signs the obligations below. Implementation requires its own approved issue and Zavala's review under API stability.</p> <p>All names below (<code>PwmInterface</code>, <code>PwmChannel</code>, <code>PwmOperationFailure</code>, <code>MockPwm</code>, and the method names) are provisional. This note adds no public Python name and changes no stored record. The legacy framework and language server supply no PWM behavior to carry forward; the migration context is the preserved lifecycle and evidence contracts, with a signed hardware contract for a later implementation.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#the-pwm-transfer-contract","title":"The PWM Transfer Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: Present PWM as its own typed interface under HIL ruling 1. <code>PwmInterface</code> is not an <code>exec</code> subclass and does not change where <code>utils.exec</code> routes. Each named channel has a declared output polarity, frequency range, duty-cycle resolution, and disabled-output behavior before it can be enabled.</p> <ul> <li>Frequency is in hertz, positive and finite. Duty cycle is the fraction of   the period spent in the declared active polarity, from <code>0.0</code> through <code>1.0</code>   inclusive. Reject booleans, non-numbers, non-finite values, out-of-range   values, and unsupported frequency/duty pairs at runtime before any write.   A fraction and a percentage are not interchangeable: <code>50</code> is invalid.</li> <li>A provisional <code>set_output(channel, frequency_hz=..., duty_cycle=...)</code>   deliberately enables or updates the output. It validates the complete pair   before applying either member. The backend must describe its supported   pairs and update behavior, including shared timing resources; it cannot   silently clamp, round, or momentarily apply an undeclared combination.   A value the hardware cannot represent is refused, with the supported   resolution named, so the author can request a different value deliberately.</li> <li><code>disable(channel)</code> stops output generation. Neither a zero duty cycle nor   a lost controller connection counts as confirmation that output is disabled.   The inactive electrical behavior of a disabled channel is part of its   declaration; disabled does not claim that an attached load has lost power.</li> <li>Each operation records the requested setting and whether the controller   confirmed applying it. An acknowledgement is evidence of controller state,   not an independent measurement of the waveform at the DUT. A failure or   timeout leaves the unconfirmed setting explicitly unknown, never cached as   the last successful setting.</li> <li>One operation owns a channel at a time. A second write or ramp is refused   while one is active; it is not queued to surprise the next lifecycle phase.   Framework termination takes precedence over this admission rule. A backend   whose shared timer would let one channel's update or disable alter another   owner's channel must refuse the conflicting acquisition before touching   either owner's output. It cannot sacrifice Group Isolation to offer more channels.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Declare the channel, polarity, supported settings, and disabled behavior in <code>configure()</code>. Obtain its framework-managed handle through a provisional <code>utils.get_pwm(...)</code>; return the handle and static inputs through configure's ctx. Keep <code>self</code> immutable, <code>build_suite()</code> pure, and later ctx reads read-only. Judge the DUT with actual observations, rather than treating a successful output request as a passing test.</p> <p>Consequence: The Floor Technician can distinguish the stimulus requested, the controller's acknowledgement, and the DUT's response. A rejected request cannot quietly become a different stimulus in a record that claims otherwise.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#the-pwm-ramp-and-timing-contract","title":"The PWM Ramp and Timing Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: A provisional <code>ramp(channel, target_duty_cycle=..., duration=...)</code> changes duty at a fixed frequency. The channel must already be enabled with a confirmed starting setting. Duration is positive finite seconds, with booleans refused by the same numeric rule as transfer; target duty follows the transfer validation above. A frequency ramp is outside this proposal.</p> <ul> <li>The requested trajectory is linear from the confirmed starting duty to the   target over the declared duration. The backend states its update interval,   duty resolution, and worst-case scheduling deviation. Quantized intermediate   steps must remain within those declared bounds, progress monotonically, and   reach the representable target; they must not overshoot. Before starting,   refuse a trajectory that cannot meet the backend's declared envelope.</li> <li>Ramp duration and backend-operation deadline are separate quantities. The   former describes the requested change; the latter bounds how long the   controller may take to confirm it. As in the signed GPIO   stability-window/acquisition-deadline distinction,   one number cannot describe both the work and the allowance to finish it.   The resolved ramp completion deadline must exceed its duration and include   the backend's declared scheduling and acknowledgement allowances. Both   values appear in evidence before the ramp starts.</li> <li>Ordinary output updates, disable operations, and bring-up also have positive   finite backend-declared latency ceilings. An operation timeout names that   operation and its ceiling. It does not manufacture the <code>timeout</code> evidence   reserved for the winning node clock in the   Time Budget Contract.</li> <li>The node's <code>fail_after</code> or <code>abort_after</code> bounds an awaited ramp like any   other work. A longer ramp or backend deadline cannot extend that clock.   Cancellation interrupts the resource's ramp; it does not wait for the target   duty or a graceful ramp-down before disabling output.</li> <li>A ramp settles only when its completion is confirmed or a failure/cut has   been recorded. An operational failure stops further ramp writes and attempts   bounded disable of that channel before settlement; failed confirmation stays   explicit. It cannot leave a detached task changing the output after it   settles. Blocking SDK work uses the existing framework executor   boundary. A late native completion must not re-enable or rewrite an output   after the framework has disabled it.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Choose a supported trajectory and name the node budget for what expiry means to this test. Do not create a suite-owned timing loop, suppress <code>CancelledError</code>, or use an output ramp as a substitute for measuring the DUT's response. Catch operational failures and model their meaning at the test boundary.</p> <p>Consequence: A completed ramp means the declared controller trajectory completed within its stated envelope. An interrupted ramp carries the point last confirmed and an explicit interruption, not a fabricated endpoint. The record keeps a slow controller distinct from a node that spent its budget.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#the-pwm-safe-state-contract","title":"The PWM Safe-State Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: The safe state is output disabled, enforced at the resource by the existing termination sweep. This specializes the signed GPIO ownership and cut rules; it introduces no PWM halt signal, event loop, lifecycle runner, or integration hook.</p> <ul> <li>Register ownership as soon as acquisition can affect a channel, so partial   <code>configure()</code> failure cannot orphan an output. Bring-up establishes and   confirms disabled state before admitting the first enable operation.   Failure to establish it refuses use and records the unconfirmed state.</li> <li>An integration-hook failure or operator interrupt disables every output   owned by every open PWM interface. A node deadline disables only resources   opened by the bounded subtree, under both <code>fail_after</code> (Fail) and   <code>abort_after</code> (node-local Aborted). An inherited controller or borrowed hop   remains its owner's resource; unrelated siblings are untouched.</li> <li>Normal teardown disables owned outputs as well. The framework retains its   existing order: bounded authored cleanup and post-cleanup survey, then   release of owned resources before their borrowed transport. On a hard cut,   disable starts immediately through resource termination; it never waits for   authored cleanup to remember the channel.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight()</code> is idempotent and refuses further output work when the   cut begins. It cancels pending ramps at the controller, attempts the disable   under the declared backend bound, and records whether disable was confirmed.   A repeated call cannot restart a ramp or perform an enable. A closed handle   remains closed; there is no automatic reconnect or replay of its last duty.</li> <li>Loss of access can prevent confirmation. As the signed GPIO note requires,   the scoped timeline records that fact with the channel and operation named.   An unconfirmed disable never leaves a success line behind or rewrites the   original terminal state. A backend unable to stop autonomous output through   the existing resource-termination boundary is a finding for Ty before it   can be admitted; closing its control socket alone does not satisfy this note.</li> </ul> <p>Developer obligation: Select a fixture for which the declared disabled output is the intended safe state. Declare shared controller resources honestly. Keep restoration in <code>cleanup()</code> but rely on framework ownership for termination; never manually sequence another node's configure or cleanup.</p> <p>Consequence: Ending the test stops its output work and records what could be confirmed. A technician is never asked to infer electrical safety from a disconnected handle or from Pass, Fail, or Aborted alone.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#lifecycle-hooks-and-failure-vocabulary","title":"Lifecycle, hooks, and failure vocabulary","text":"<p>The HIL lifecycle ruling applies unchanged. Framework bookkeeping owns connection flags, acquisition, release, and halt dispatch. <code>before_each</code>/<code>after_each</code> keep their pair invariant and their narrow context without hardware access. Constructor-registered surveys and audits remain the observation hooks: survey errors are logged and swallowed, audit errors fail their own scope, and <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> is never swallowed. PWM transfer methods return operational results, never <code>PASS</code>.</p> <p>The proposed <code>PwmOperationFailure</code> derives from <code>HiltError</code>, not <code>TestFailure</code>, for the same reason GPIO's <code>PinSettleFailure</code> does: failure to apply a stimulus does not identify the DUT as the cause. It names the channel, operation, requested setting, last confirmed setting if known, and any backend ceiling that expired. Unknown is represented explicitly, never as zero duty or disabled output.</p> Situation Signal and existing classification Invalid setting, unsupported trajectory, or conflicting channel use <code>TypeError</code> for a wrong type, <code>ValueError</code> for an invalid value or unsupported request; rejected before writes. Escaping configure/test code becomes a <code>ContractViolation</code>: Fail with the <code>CONTRACT VIOLATION</code> banner and <code>bug: true</code>. Controller unavailable during bring-up or an output request; backend operation exceeds its ceiling <code>PwmOperationFailure</code>; the author handles the condition and may raise a modeled <code>TestFailure</code>. An unmodeled escape through configure/test code is a <code>ContractViolation</code> Fail, not an automatic hardware verdict. No silent retry or assumed setting. Independent observation shows the DUT missed its declared requirement Author raises <code>TestFailure(..., evidence=...)</code>; Fail under the existing verdict contract. Disable cannot be confirmed during finalization Scoped evidence names the unconfirmed channel; the established terminal state is preserved, as in GPIO's safe-state sweep. Integration hook throws or the operator interrupts Framework-owned <code>ExternalIntegrationFailure</code> or <code>OperatorInterrupt</code>; run-level Aborted and the full resource sweep. Node budget expires during transfer or ramp The winning clock supplies its existing structured evidence: <code>fail_after</code> produces Fail, <code>abort_after</code> node-local Aborted. Only the owned resources within the cut are disabled. Test returns without a verdict Existing <code>MissingVerdict</code>, node-local Aborted. A completed PWM operation cannot supply the missing verdict. <p>This is one proposed operational exception under the existing root, not a new terminal state. Cancellation still propagates and outranks an attempted late return or substituted error at the child boundary.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#evidence-and-backend-acceptance","title":"Evidence and backend acceptance","text":"<p>Configuration logs name the channel, owner, polarity, disabled behavior, supported resolution, and timing envelope. Transfers and ramps use the same scoped logger to distinguish requested, confirmed, interrupted, and unconfirmed outcomes. The safe-state entry names its cause and confirmation before evidence closes. <code>timeline.log</code> and <code>timeline.jsonl</code> remain the owning node's two views; <code>result.json</code> retains the existing state and failure shape.</p> <p>Readings that establish a verdict travel as JSON-safe <code>PASS(evidence)</code> or <code>TestFailure(..., evidence=...)</code>, with <code>@evidence(...)</code> when a pass owes those keys. Commanded frequency and duty are stimulus settings, not measured frequency and duty. Actual parametric observations follow the signed #85 measurement design, whose implementation remains</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#86-this-note-neither-fills-the-reserved-measurements-slot-nor-redefines","title":"86; this note neither fills the reserved <code>measurements</code> slot nor redefines","text":"<p>units, limits, or verdict evaluation. In-flight observations use the scoped log or <code>capture_artifact</code>, since a cancelled leaf has no verdict evidence.</p> <p><code>MockPwm</code> and a real backend must face the same contract suite, following the <code>RemoteInterface</code>/<code>MockTransport</code> precedent without inheriting command semantics. Required proofs include boundary/unsupported values before writes, atomic pair updates, exclusive channel ownership and shared-timer refusal, representable ramps within declared bounds, partial acquisition failure, normal teardown, and interruption at every ramp boundary. Parked operations must prove repeated cancellation cannot allow a late enable, node cuts leave an independent sibling unchanged, and unavailable disable produces only unconfirmed evidence. Real-backend tests must verify output behavior through independent observation; mock acknowledgements cannot establish it.</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#signed-decisions-recorded-on-94","title":"Signed decisions recorded on #94","text":"<p>Ty approved these obligations with the note in !125 on 2026-09-08. The signature is also recorded on #94:</p> <ol> <li>Explicit frequency/duty pairs with refusal of unrepresentable requests,    rather than implicit rounding; fixed polarity and declared disabled behavior.</li> <li>Linear duty-only ramps at fixed frequency, with a backend-declared timing    envelope and a separate completion deadline; no detached ramp after settlement.</li> <li>Exclusive channel operations and refusal of independent ownership when    shared hardware cannot preserve node-local isolation and disable semantics.</li> <li>Operational write/bring-up failures as a modeled <code>HiltError</code> condition,    with unconfirmed finalization recorded under the signed GPIO rule. GPIO's    typed <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> read is not silently extended into a PWM write result.</li> </ol> <p>The lifecycle, verdict, and evidence contracts above are constraints on that decision. If a required backend cannot satisfy them, the finding belongs on</p>"},{"location":"hil/pwm.html#94-with-the-affected-guarantee-named-it-does-not-earn-an-alternate-lifecycle","title":"94 with the affected guarantee named. It does not earn an alternate lifecycle","text":"<p>or an exception enforced only by a linter.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html","title":"Serial/UART console transport","text":"<p>Status: SIGNED \u2014 Ty, 2026-09-10 (\"all approved\", recorded on #91). Implementation lands with the MR that carries this status change.</p> <p>Amendment SIGNED \u2014 Ty, 2026-10-18 (#150, merged as !184): the Serial Turn Contract below changes the transport's concurrency contract (one command on the wire at a time, queued in call order). Ty ruled \"queue\", not \"refuse\".</p> <p>Ty ruled both placements on 2026-09-05: <code>SerialInterface</code> as a local peer of <code>SshInterface</code> (console header on the HILT host), and the same transport reached through the existing <code>via</code> hop (a console server, or a Pi with a USB-UART, driven over SSH). One transport, two placements. This note specifies the transport's contract once \u2014 prompt detection, timeouts, line discipline, exit-status recovery, output bounding, safe-state on abort \u2014 and gives <code>via</code> composition its own section, per Ty's scoping note on the issue. Serial is a transport, not a new HIL contract: it slots in beside <code>SshInterface</code> behind <code>RemoteInterface</code>, matching <code>remote.py</code>'s own framing (\"SSH, and later serial/UART, are peers behind this class\"). Blocked on #90 (closed): the shared transport-contract suite this design must pass before any implementation MR opens.</p> <p><code>SerialInterface(RemoteInterface)</code> opens a local device node (<code>/dev/ttyUSB0</code>, a Windows <code>COM</code> port) via <code>pyserial-asyncio</code>; it never dials a socket. <code>address</code> is <code>None</code> \u2014 matching <code>RemoteInterface</code>'s own note that \"a UART on a local device node has no address\" \u2014 so <code>connect()</code> rejects <code>host</code>/<code>port</code> with <code>TypeError</code>, same as any unaddressed transport. <code>endpoint</code> is the device basename.</p> <p>A suite reaches it the way it reaches SSH: <code>utils.use_serial(device=...)</code> in <code>configure()</code> declares the console as the node's exec route, <code>utils.get_serial</code> opens one as an auxiliary endpoint, both hand back a <code>Shell</code>, and the framework owns the close (see \"Routing\" below). The transport itself is constructed only by the router and the self-tests.</p> <pre><code>await utils.use_serial(device=\"/dev/ttyUSB0\", baud=115200)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#serial-prompt-contract","title":"Serial Prompt Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: A console is a byte stream, not a request/response protocol \u2014 <code>exec</code> recognizes a command's end by the shell's prompt reappearing, never by an EOF or a status arriving on the wire. <code>prompt</code> (a regex, default a bare POSIX prompt) is matched against the tail of decoded output; <code>login_prompt</code> / <code>password_prompt</code>, when configured, are answered during <code>connect()</code> before the transport reads until <code>prompt</code> matches \u2014 a console already at a shell is unaffected. <code>login_timeout</code> (default 10s, connect-time only) bounds that resolution; expiry raises <code>ConnectionError</code> naming the last bytes seen, <code>connect()</code> raising whatever <code>_connect</code> raises, matching <code>RemoteInterface</code>. <code>prompt_timeout</code> (default 30s, fixed at construction) bounds how long <code>exec</code> waits for the prompt after sending a command \u2014 the transport's own safety net, not a substitute for <code>fail_after</code>/<code>abort_after</code>: a wedged console is a resource the transport cannot leave parked forever even with no ancestor budget declared. <code>prompt_timeout</code> and an ancestor's <code>fail_after</code>/<code>abort_after</code> are independent clocks racing the same await; whichever fires first decides the state, exactly as the Time Budget Contract already specifies. An echoed line unchanged across two read cycles is named in the eventual timeout's message, distinguishing \"wedged, frozen\" from \"slow, still producing bytes\" without the Floor Technician opening the suite.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Supply <code>prompt</code> (and <code>login_prompt</code>/<code>password_prompt</code> where the console logs in) at construction for anything but a bare POSIX shell \u2014 guessing a prompt at runtime is how a stuck read gets misread as a fast command. Set <code>prompt_timeout</code> shorter than any <code>fail_after</code> wrapping this node's <code>exec</code> calls when a wedge should surface on its own evidence line rather than only as an ancestor's <code>TimeoutEvidence</code>.</p> <p>Consequence: <code>exec</code> never raises for a command's own completion. Expiry settles like a killed command \u2014 <code>ExecResult.settled(partial_output, \"prompt timeout\", None)</code>, <code>exit_code == -1</code>, partial output kept as evidence \u2014 never an exception the suite must catch. If the harness's own clock wins the race instead, the coroutine is cancelled (<code>CancelledError</code> propagates, never swallowed) and <code>cancel_in_flight</code> runs the Serial Abort Contract below.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#serial-line-discipline-contract","title":"Serial Line Discipline Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: The wire is raw bytes with none of an SSH channel's structure (separate stdout/stderr, a real exit status). <code>_exec</code> normalizes before matching the prompt or handing bytes to <code>ExecResult.settled</code>: input is written as bare <code>\\r</code> (real-terminal <code>Enter</code>, since some bootloader shells ignore <code>\\n</code> alone), output CRLF is normalized to <code>\\n</code>; exactly one leading echoed line matching the command just sent is stripped before that normalization (strip-if-present \u2014 a console with echo already off is unaffected); ANSI/VT100 escapes are stripped on the accumulated decoded buffer, so an escape split across two reads still resolves and a colorized prompt cannot defeat the <code>prompt</code> regex; decoding is UTF-8 with <code>errors=\"replace\"</code>, matching <code>ExecResult.settled</code>'s existing path for every transport.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Read <code>ExecResult.stdout</code> with <code>\\n</code> line endings and no local echo, the same as any other transport's result \u2014 do not special-case serial output in suite code for line endings or echo.</p> <p>Consequence: A suite calling <code>result.stdout.splitlines()</code> does not need to know the transport was serial. A DUT's binary or non-UTF-8 output is evidence, not a dead channel.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#serial-exit-status-contract","title":"Serial Exit-Status Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: A raw console has no exit-status side-channel \u2014 no equivalent of asyncssh's <code>process.exit_status</code>. The transport appends a sentinel to what it sends and parses it back out of the reply:</p> <pre><code>&lt;command&gt;; echo \"__HILT_RC__=$?\"\n</code></pre> <p>Once the prompt reappears, the transport scans for <code>__HILT_RC__=&lt;digits&gt;</code>, strips that one line from <code>ExecResult.stdout</code>, and parses the digits as <code>exit_code</code>. A sentinel that never appears \u2014 echo suppression failed, the shell died mid-command \u2014 settles as <code>exit_code == -1</code> via <code>ExecResult.settled(..., None)</code>, the same \"no real status exists\" path every transport shares, with the raw captured text kept as evidence rather than discarded: the documented <code>-1</code> case (\"killed by a signal, or aborted before an exit status existed\") with a lost sentinel standing in for the signal-kill. Stdout and the sentinel-bearing reply together are bounded by <code>hilt.executor.BoundedCapture</code> and <code>DEFAULT_EXEC_OUTPUT_BUDGET</code> unmodified \u2014 the same named ceiling <code>LocalExec</code> and <code>SshInterface</code> already share. Hitting the budget sets <code>truncated=True</code>, stops accepting bytes, and triggers the Serial Abort Contract below (there is no process to SIGTERM on the other end of a UART, only a wire to interrupt); the port stays open afterward, matching \"the SSH session stays up\" for the equivalent case.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Read <code>ExecResult.stderr</code> as transport-generated messages only (<code>\"prompt timeout\"</code>, <code>\"transport closed\"</code>) \u2014 never a slice of the DUT's own output. A console has one combined byte stream, not separate stdout/stderr descriptors; everything the DUT writes settles into <code>stdout</code>. A suite ported from an SSH-backed console that greps <code>stderr</code> for DUT error text needs to read <code>stdout</code> instead \u2014 the one place the two transports are not drop-in interchangeable.</p> <p>Consequence: Every <code>ExecResult</code> this transport produces is a real, bounded value a suite can treat exactly like any other transport's result \u2014 a missing or truncated status is evidence, never a raised exception.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#serial-abort-contract","title":"Serial Abort Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>cancel_in_flight</code> is the load-bearing halt (\"kill every in-flight operation as hard as the protocol allows... a later <code>exec</code> rejects\"). A serial line has no socket to destroy the way <code>SshInterface._abort_client</code> does, so the sequence is protocol-shaped: send a <code>BREAK</code> condition (a real UART primitive) to interrupt whatever runs in the foreground on most bootloader and kernel consoles; send <code>Ctrl-C</code> (<code>\\x03</code>) for a foreground shell command a BREAK alone misses; close the port unconditionally, whether or not the prompt reappeared after those two \u2014 no graceful wait, matching SSH's \"graceful hangup is not used on either path\" and the In-Flight Termination Contract's \"closed HARD immediately... not at the next polling boundary.\" This contract does not fork that one; it names what \"closed hard\" means on a protocol with no socket to abort. The connected flag clears even if the BREAK/Ctrl-C sends themselves raise, same <code>try</code>/<code>finally</code> pattern as <code>RemoteInterface.cancel_in_flight</code>.</p> <p>Developer obligation: None beyond the existing <code>RemoteInterface</code> contract \u2014 abort is framework-owned and idempotent; suite code never calls <code>cancel_in_flight</code> itself.</p> <p>Consequence: Any <code>exec</code> still parked when the sequence runs settles with <code>stderr</code> naming <code>\"transport closed\"</code> \u2014 the same cause string <code>_name_transport_death</code> gives SSH, so a Floor Technician's grep for a dead resource does not need to know which transport produced it. An <code>exec</code> still queued behind it settles the same way without ever reaching the wire (Serial Turn Contract below). A 30-minute console session in progress when the run aborts does not keep mutating hardware after the sequence completes.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#serial-turn-contract","title":"Serial Turn Contract","text":"<p>Framework obligation: <code>exec</code> on one <code>SerialInterface</code> runs one command at a time, in call order. A console carries one byte stream and no channel per caller \u2014 unlike an SSH session, which opens a channel per <code>exec</code> \u2014 so two callers writing back to back would both read the same reply. The transport holds an <code>asyncio.Lock</code> around each command's write-and-read; a caller that arrives while another is on the wire waits its turn, and waiters are served in the order they called. This is the turn a shared <code>DeviceApi</code> handle takes per request (bench finding F13), applied to the console, and it is what makes a <code>use_serial</code> route safe under <code>self.parallel()</code>: the leaves run concurrently, their commands go out one at a time.</p> <p><code>prompt_timeout</code> bounds only the command's own wait for the prompt once it is on the wire, never its time in the queue \u2014 a sibling ahead in the queue is not the DUT being slow, and the node clock bounds the whole wait. A command still queued when <code>cancel_in_flight</code> or <code>disconnect</code> lands never reaches the wire: it settles <code>exit_code == -1</code> with <code>stderr</code> <code>\"transport closed\"</code>, the same evidence an in-flight command gets from the Serial Abort Contract. The halt itself takes no turn \u2014 BREAK, Ctrl-C, and the close go out past whatever holds the lock, so the halt never waits.</p> <p>Developer obligation: Know what the leaves share. Queued leaves share one shell, so its state travels between them: a <code>cd</code>, an exported variable, or a background job started in one leaf is visible to the next. That is the cost particular to a console \u2014 a <code>DeviceApi</code> handle shares a session, not a shell. Claims that need isolation want one console each (<code>get_serial(device=...)</code> per leaf, or one <code>use_serial</code> per child suite), and a latency measured around <code>utils.exec</code> on a shared console includes the wait for siblings.</p> <p>Consequence: The F13 shape on a console \u2014 one <code>use_serial(device=...)</code> in <code>configure()</code>, <code>utils.exec</code> in every <code>parallel</code> leaf \u2014 comes back with each leaf's own verdict. Before this contract the second caller's read collided with the first's: <code>utils.exec</code> raised <code>asyncio.StreamReader</code>'s <code>RuntimeError</code> through the node, a false <code>ContractViolation</code> beside a false <code>\"prompt timeout\"</code> on a board that was healthy (issue #150).</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#error-taxonomy-mapping","title":"Error-taxonomy mapping","text":"<p>The transport raises nothing shaped like a verdict \u2014 only <code>ConnectionError</code> (protocol-level reject, absorbed by <code>TestUtils.exec</code>/<code>Shell.exec</code> into <code>exit_code == -1</code>) or whatever <code>_connect</code> propagates from a failed login.</p> <ul> <li><code>exec</code> while not connected \u2192 <code>ConnectionError</code>, absorbed to <code>-1</code>.</li> <li>Login/prompt never resolves at connect \u2192 <code>ConnectionError</code> from <code>_connect</code>.   A suite that wraps <code>connect()</code> in <code>configure()</code> and re-raises as <code>TestFailure</code>   makes the hardware-vs-infrastructure call itself \u2014 the framework never guesses   (Terminal State Contract: \"not knowable from code... the human triager's call\").</li> <li>Prompt timeout / sentinel not found \u2192 <code>ExecResult</code> with <code>exit_code == -1</code>,   raw text kept as evidence; a suite treating this as a Fail raises <code>TestFailure</code>   from the reading, not the transport.</li> <li><code>cancel_in_flight</code> / abort \u2192 parked <code>exec</code> settles <code>-1</code>,   <code>stderr=\"transport closed\"</code> \u2014 framework-decided, never a suite choice.</li> <li>A suite bug (bad regex type, wrong argument) \u2192 propagates uncaught into   <code>TestComponent</code>'s existing bug boundary: unmodeled exception \u2192 <code>ContractViolation</code>,   \"CONTRACT VIOLATION IN <code>&lt;name&gt;</code>\".</li> </ul> <p>Nothing here adds a new type to <code>hilt.errors</code>. The transport speaks the two vocabularies every <code>RemoteInterface</code> already speaks \u2014 <code>ConnectionError</code> at the protocol layer, <code>ExecResult</code> at the command layer.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#via-composition-placement-2","title":"Via composition (placement 2)","text":"<p>The device path names a node on the remote box, not the HILT host, so <code>SerialInterface</code> cannot open it as a local file descriptor the way placement 1 does. The resolution reuses the seam <code>RemoteInterface</code> already has for this: <code>hop</code> and <code>endpoint</code> let a transport name its place in the topology independent of how its bytes move (\"an owned via hop and a borrowed tunnel answer alike\"). <code>SerialInterface</code> takes a <code>via: SshInterface | None</code> constructor argument, structurally matching <code>SshInterface</code>'s own <code>via</code> (SSH only \u2014 a console has no other kind of hop): when given, the \"wire\" is not a local pyserial handle but the stdin/stdout of a bridging command run through the hop's own <code>exec</code> and kept open as a long-lived remote process. Every contract above \u2014 prompt, timeouts, line discipline, sentinel, abort \u2014 operates on that bytestream identically; placement only changes where the bytes originate. <code>cancel_in_flight</code> for placement 2 sends BREAK/Ctrl-C into the bridging process's stdin, then hard-cancels that exec through the hop \u2014 never the hop's own <code>cancel_in_flight()</code>, which would take down every other consumer of that SSH connection (Exec Routing Contract: a router \"must not disconnect, cancel, or collapse its hop\").</p> <p>Borrowed hops. When the exec router supplies the hop \u2014 an inherited <code>use_shell</code> route, or an explicit <code>via=jump</code> <code>Shell</code> \u2014 that hop is already live and belongs to the router, so the console takes it through <code>tunnel=</code> rather than <code>via=</code>: it must arrive connected, a failed bridge leaves it standing, and <code>disconnect</code> closes only the bridge. <code>via</code> and <code>tunnel</code> are mutually exclusive, the same two channels <code>SshInterface</code> has, with the same ownership rule.</p> <p>Routing line. <code>route_label</code>'s existing chain-walk needs no new rendering code: placement 1 renders <code>exec routing: via ttyUSB0 (declared via use_serial)</code> \u2014 no network in the picture; placement 2 renders <code>exec routing: via bastion -&gt; console-pi -&gt; ttyUSB0 (declared via use_serial)</code> \u2014 test-host-first, exactly as any SSH chain renders today.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#routing-use_serial-and-get_serial","title":"Routing: <code>use_serial</code> and <code>get_serial</code>","text":"<p><code>TestUtils.use_serial</code> and <code>TestUtils.get_serial</code> are <code>use_shell</code> and <code>get_shell</code> for a device path. A device path and baud rate are not a <code>host</code>/<code>username</code> pair, so the console has its own request (<code>SerialOpenRequest</code>) and its own connector seam (<code>SerialConnector</code>, the mirror of <code>SshConnector</code>), and the router treats what comes back exactly as it treats an SSH remote: <code>ExecRouter.for_ssh</code> attaches it, the <code>opened</code> set records it, <code>disconnect_opened</code> closes it after the post-cleanup survey, and the run-level abort sweep hard-closes it on an operator Ctrl-C. The parameters are the transport's constructor arguments (<code>device</code>, <code>baud</code>, <code>prompt</code>, <code>login_prompt</code>, <code>password_prompt</code>, <code>username</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>login_timeout</code>, <code>prompt_timeout</code>, <code>bridge_command</code>, <code>output_budget</code>) plus the routing trio <code>via</code>, <code>inherit</code>, <code>propagate</code>.</p> <p>Two shapes, not four: <code>use_serial(device=...)</code> opens and binds; <code>use_serial()</code> with no arguments reads the active route. The policy-only shapes (<code>inherit=False</code>, <code>propagate=False</code>) stay with <code>use_shell</code>, and <code>use_serial</code> refuses them with a <code>TypeError</code> that says so. The declaring form is admitted only while <code>configure()</code> runs, under the same <code>ContractViolation</code> as <code>use_shell</code>, and <code>hilt-check</code>'s <code>hilt/shell-in-configure</code> reads <code>use_serial(...)</code> with arguments the same way. <code>via</code> must resolve to an SSH hop (a <code>Shell</code> from <code>use_shell</code> or <code>get_shell</code>): a console has no other tunnel to borrow, and the connector names <code>use_serial</code> in the <code>TypeError</code> when handed anything else \u2014 naming the hop it was handed and the <code>inherit=False</code> escape, since under a <code>use_serial</code> parent that hop was inherited, not passed. A local <code>Shell</code> as <code>via</code> is a direct open on the test host, as it is for <code>use_shell</code>. The route is shared by the node and its descendants, so <code>parallel</code> leaves under it take turns on the one console (Serial Turn Contract).</p> <p><code>bench/suites/serial_console.py</code> is the shape in use: one <code>use_serial</code> in <code>configure()</code>, <code>utils.exec</code> in every claim, no handle in ctx, no <code>cleanup()</code>.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#packaging","title":"Packaging","text":"<p><code>pyserial-asyncio</code> is checked for Apache-2.0 compatibility at implementation time and lands as an optional extra: <code>pip install open-hilt[serial]</code>. Core <code>hilt</code> gains no new dependency and <code>import hilt</code> never imports <code>hilt.serial</code>: the default connector imports it on the first <code>use_serial</code> / <code>get_serial</code>, and a local open without the extra raises <code>ModuleNotFoundError</code> naming the install. <code>SerialInterface</code> stays off <code>hilt.__all__</code>, same policy as <code>RemoteInterface</code>, <code>SshInterface</code>, and <code>JunitXml</code> today (\"stay private by test-pinned ruling\") \u2014 the promised way to a console is <code>use_serial</code> / <code>get_serial</code>; self-tests and the transport-contract suite import the module directly.</p>"},{"location":"hil/serial.html#conformance-the-shared-transport-contract-suite","title":"Conformance: the shared transport-contract suite","text":"<p><code>tests/test_transport_contract.py</code> parametrizes every assertion over a <code>TRANSPORTS</code> tuple of fixtures; \"serial/UART is the next name on that list\" per the suite's own docstring. A <code>serial_peer</code> fixture joins <code>mock_peer</code> and <code>ssh_peer</code>, backed by a pty-based fake console: <code>pty.openpty()</code> gives a real master/slave pair, and a small in-process script on the slave end plays a login-and-shell console \u2014 echoing input, answering <code>success</code>/<code>fail</code>/<code>block</code> (parked until cancelled) the same way the other two fixtures do, and emitting the <code>__HILT_RC__=</code> sentinel itself so the suite exercises real parsing rather than a stub. This keeps the suite honest about actual line discipline (real CRLF, a real echo) while staying fully in-process \u2014 no UART hardware required for <code>make check</code>. <code>wait_in_flight</code> follows the existing <code>_wait_ssh_in_flight</code> shape (a short sleep) since the parked command is a real subprocess, not a future the fixture can poll.</p> <p>Every existing assertion applies unchanged: connect/disconnect idempotence, exec-never-throws, <code>cancel_in_flight</code> idempotence and the <code>\"transport closed\"</code> cause name, connected-flag ownership. Adding this fixture and naming it in <code>TRANSPORTS</code> is the entire integration cost the suite's docstring promises (\"adding a transport is adding a fixture\").</p>"}]}